const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"London. An ante-chamber in the palace.","id":0},{"t":"sp","who":"PROLOGUE","lines":["I COME no more to make you laugh: things now","That bear a weighty and a serious brow,","Sad, high, and working, full of state and woe,","Such noble scenes as draw the eye to flow,","We now present. Those that can pity, here","May, if they think it well, let fall a tear;","The subject will deserve it. Such as give","Their money out of hope they may believe,","May here find truth too. Those that come to see","Only a show or two, and so agree","The play may pass, if they be still and willing,","I'll undertake may see away their shilling","Richly in two short hours. Only they","That come to hear a merry bawdy play,","A noise of targets, or to see a fellow","In a long motley coat guarded with yellow,","Will be deceiv'd; for, gentle hearers, know,","To rank our chosen truth with such a show","As fool and fight is, beside forfeiting","Our own brains, and the opinion that we bring","To make that only true we now intend,","Will leave us never an understanding friend.","Therefore, for goodness' sake, and as you are known","The first and happiest hearers of the town,","Be sad, as we would make ye; think ye see","The very persons of our noble story","As they were living; think you see them great,","And follow'd with the general throng and sweat","Of thousand friends; then, in a moment, see","How soon this mightiness meets misery;","And if you can be merry then, I'll say","A man may weep upon his wedding-day."],"id":1,"m":"I have not come to make you laugh this time. We now present serious, weighty scenes, sad and lofty, full of state affairs and grief, noble scenes that will bring tears to your eyes. Those of you who can feel pity may shed a tear here if you like; the subject deserves it. Those who pay hoping to see something true will find truth here too. Those who come only for a spectacle or two, and are willing to let the play pass, I promise will get their shilling's worth in two short hours. Only those who come to hear a merry, bawdy play, the clash of shields, or a clown in a long motley coat trimmed with yellow will be disappointed. For, gentle audience, if we mixed our chosen true story with such foolery and fighting, we would throw away our own wits and our aim of showing only what is true, and no thinking person would stay our friend. So, for goodness' sake, and since you are known as the best and most discerning audience in the city, be as sad as we want to make you. Imagine you see the real people of our noble story as if they were alive; see them great, followed by crowds of thousands of sweating friends; then see how in a moment this greatness meets misery. And if you can be merry then, I'll say a man can weep on his wedding day."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Duke of Norfolk at one door; at the other, the Duke of Buckingham and the Lord Abergavenny.","id":2},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Good morrow, and well met. How have ye done","Since last we saw in France?"],"id":3,"m":"Good morning, and well met. How have you been since we last saw each other in France?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["I thank your Grace,","Healthful; and ever since a fresh admirer","Of what I saw there."],"id":4,"m":"Healthy, thank you, your Grace, and ever since then a fresh admirer of what I saw there."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["An untimely ague","Stay'd me a prisoner in my chamber when","Those suns of glory, those two lights of men,","Met in the vale of Andren."],"id":5,"m":"A badly timed fever kept me a prisoner in my room when those two glorious suns, those two shining kings, met in the valley of Andren."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["'Twixt Guynes and Arde.","I was then present, saw them salute on horseback;","Beheld them, when they lighted, how they clung","In their embracement, as they grew together;","Which had they, what four thron'd ones could have weigh'd","Such a compounded one?"],"id":6,"m":"Between Guisnes and Ardres. I was there. I saw them greet each other on horseback, and when they dismounted, how they hugged, clinging together as if they had grown into one. If they had become one, what four kings could have outweighed such a compound?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["All the whole time","I was my chamber's prisoner."],"id":7,"m":"The whole time I was a prisoner in my room."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Then you lost","The view of earthly glory. Men might say,","Till this time pomp was single, but now married","To one above itself. Each following day","Became the next day's master, till the last","Made former wonders its. To-day the French,","All clinquant, all in gold, like heathen gods,","Shone down the English; and, to-morrow, they","Made Britain India: every man that stood","Show'd like a mine. Their dwarfish pages were","As cherubins, all gilt; the madams too,","Not us'd to toil, did almost sweat to bear","The pride upon them, that their very labour","Was to them as a painting. Now this masque","Was cried incomparable; and the ensuing night","Made it a fool and beggar. The two kings,","Equal in lustre, were now best, now worst,","As presence did present them; him in eye,","Still him in praise; and, being present both,","'Twas said they saw but one; and no discerner","Durst wag his tongue in censure. When these suns—","For so they phrase 'em—by their heralds challeng'd","The noble spirits to arms, they did perform","Beyond thought's compass, that former fabulous story,","Being now seen possible enough, got credit,","That Bevis was believ'd."],"id":8,"m":"Then you missed the sight of earthly glory. You could say that until then splendour had been single, but now it married something greater than itself. Each day outdid the one before, until the last one gathered up all the earlier wonders as its own. One day the French, all glittering, all in gold like pagan gods, outshone the English; the next, the English made Britain look like India: every man standing there shone like a gold mine. Their little pages were like cherubs, all gilded; the ladies too, not used to work, almost sweated under the weight of their finery, so that the effort of carrying it made them blush as if painted. One night's masque was called beyond compare, and the next night's made it look poor and foolish. The two kings, equal in splendour, were now one the best, now the other, depending on who was in sight; whichever you were looking at got the praise, and when both were present people said they saw only one, and no critic dared say a word against either. When these two suns, as they called them, had their heralds challenge the nobles to joust, they performed beyond belief, so that old fabulous legends, now seen to be possible, were believed, and even the tale of Bevis was credited."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["O, you go far!"],"id":9,"m":"Oh, you exaggerate!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["As I belong to worship and affect","In honour honesty, the tract of ev'rything","Would by a good discourser lose some life,","Which action's self was tongue to. All was royal;","To the disposing of it nought rebell'd,","Order gave each thing view; the office did","Distinctly his full function."],"id":10,"m":"On my honour as a gentleman who loves honesty, the telling of every detail would lose some life in the mouth of even a good storyteller, because the event itself spoke better than words. Everything was royal; nothing went against the arrangements; good order made everything visible; every official carried out his duty exactly."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Who did guide,","I mean, who set the body and the limbs","Of this great sport together, as you guess?"],"id":11,"m":"Who was in charge? I mean, who put together the body and limbs of this great entertainment, do you think?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["One, certes, that promises no element","In such a business."],"id":12,"m":"Someone, certainly, whom you would not expect to have any part in such business."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["I pray you, who, my lord?"],"id":13,"m":"Who, please, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["All this was ord'red by the good discretion","Of the right reverend Cardinal of York."],"id":14,"m":"All this was arranged by the wise judgement of the right reverend Cardinal of York."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["The devil speed him! no man's pie is freed","From his ambitious finger. What had he","To do in these fierce vanities? I wonder","That such a keech can with his very bulk","Take up the rays o' th' beneficial sun,","And keep it from the earth."],"id":15,"m":"The devil take him! No one's pie is safe from his ambitious finger. What business did he have in these wild vanities? I wonder how such a lump of fat can block the rays of the life-giving sun with his bulk and keep them from the earth."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Surely, sir,","There's in him stuff that puts him to these ends;","For, being not propp'd by ancestry, whose grace","Chalks successors their way, nor call'd upon","For high feats done to the crown; neither allied","To eminent assistants; but, spider-like,","Out of his self-drawing web, he gives us note,","The force of his own merit makes his way;","A gift that heaven gives for him, which buys","A place next to the King."],"id":16,"m":"Surely, sir, he has qualities that drive him to these ends. He isn't supported by noble ancestry, whose favour marks out the way for heirs; he isn't called on for great services to the crown; he isn't connected to powerful allies. Like a spider spinning a web out of itself, he shows us that the force of his own merit makes his way, a gift from heaven that buys him the place next to the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABERGAVENNY","lines":["I cannot tell","What heaven hath given him,—let some graver eye","Pierce into that; but I can see his pride","Peep through each part of him. Whence has he that?","If not from hell, the devil is a niggard,","Or has given all before, and he begins","A new hell in himself."],"id":17,"m":"I can't say what heaven has given him; let some wiser eye look into that. But I can see his pride peeping out of every part of him. Where did he get it? If not from hell, then the devil is a miser, or has given all his pride away already, and Wolsey is starting a new hell in himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Why the devil,","Upon this French going out, took he upon him,","Without the privity o' the King, to appoint","Who should attend on him? He makes up the file","Of all the gentry; for the most part such","To whom as great a charge as little honour","He meant to lay upon; and his own letter,","The honourable board of council out,","Must fetch him in the papers."],"id":18,"m":"Why the devil, for this French trip, did he take it on himself, without the King's knowledge, to choose who should go with him? He drew up the list of all the gentry, mostly men he meant to load with great expense and little honour; and his own letter, without the honourable council being consulted, was enough to summon whoever he named in his papers."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABERGAVENNY","lines":["I do know","Kinsmen of mine, three at the least, that have","By this so sicken'd their estates, that never","They shall abound as formerly."],"id":19,"m":"I know kinsmen of mine, at least three, who have so ruined their estates by this that they will never be as rich as before."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["O, many","Have broke their backs with laying manors on 'em","For this great journey. What did this vanity","But minister communication of","A most poor issue?"],"id":20,"m":"Oh, many have broken their backs by wearing whole estates on their backs for this great journey. And what did this vanity achieve but talks that came to a very poor result?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Grievingly I think","The peace between the French and us not values","The cost that did conclude it."],"id":21,"m":"Sadly I think the peace between France and us is not worth what it cost to make."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Every man,","After the hideous storm that follow'd, was","A thing inspir'd; and, not consulting, broke","Into a general prophecy, that this tempest,","Dashing the garment of this peace, aboded","The sudden breach on't."],"id":22,"m":"After the terrible storm that followed, every man became a prophet; without consulting each other, they all predicted that this storm, tearing the robe of this peace, foretold that it would soon be broken."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Which is budded out;","For France hath flaw'd the league, and hath attach'd","Our merchants' goods at Bordeaux."],"id":23,"m":"And so it has begun; France has broken the treaty and has seized our merchants' goods at Bordeaux."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABERGAVENNY","lines":["Is it therefore","The ambassador is silenc'd?"],"id":24,"m":"Is that why the French ambassador has been silenced?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Marry, is't."],"id":25,"m":"Indeed it is."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABERGAVENNY","lines":["A proper title of a peace, and purchas'd","At a superfluous rate!"],"id":26,"m":"A fine sort of peace, and bought at an excessive price!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Why, all this business","Our reverend Cardinal carried."],"id":27,"m":"And our reverend Cardinal managed all this business."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Like it your Grace,","The state takes notice of the private difference","Betwixt you and the Cardinal. I advise you—","And take it from a heart that wishes towards you","Honour and plenteous safety—that you read","The Cardinal's malice and his potency","Together, to consider further that","What his high hatred would effect wants not","A minister in his power. You know his nature,","That he's revengeful, and I know his sword","Hath a sharp edge; it's long, and, 't may be said,","It reaches far, and where 'twill not extend,","Thither he darts it. Bosom up my counsel,","You'll find it wholesome. Lo, where comes that rock","That I advise your shunning."],"id":28,"m":"If your Grace pleases, the government has noticed the private quarrel between you and the Cardinal. I advise you, and take this from a heart that wishes you honour and safety, to weigh the Cardinal's malice and his power together, and to consider that whatever his great hatred wants done, he has someone in his power to do it. You know his nature, that he is revengeful, and I know his sword has a sharp edge; it is long, and you might say it reaches far, and where it won't reach he throws it. Keep my advice close; you'll find it healthy. Look, here comes the rock I advise you to steer around."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Cardinal Wolsey, the purse borne before him, certain of the Guard, and two Secretaries, with papers. The Cardinal in his passage fixeth his eye on Buckingham, and Buckingham on him, both full of disdain.","id":29},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["The Duke of Buckingham's surveyor, ha?","Where's his examination?"],"id":30,"m":"The Duke of Buckingham's surveyor, ha? Where is the record of his questioning?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECRETARY","lines":["Here, so please you."],"id":31,"m":"Here, if you please."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Is he in person ready?"],"id":32,"m":"Is he here in person, ready?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECRETARY","lines":["Ay, please your Grace."],"id":33,"m":"Yes, if it please your Grace."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Well, we shall then know more; and Buckingham","Shall lessen this big look."],"id":34,"m":"Good, then we'll learn more, and Buckingham will have to lower that proud look."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Wolsey and his train.","id":35},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["This butcher's cur is venom-mouth'd, and I","Have not the power to muzzle him; therefore best","Not wake him in his slumber. A beggar's book","Outworths a noble's blood."],"id":36,"m":"This butcher's dog has a poisonous mouth, and I don't have the power to muzzle him; so it's best not to wake him while he sleeps. A beggar's book learning is worth more than a noble's blood these days."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["What, are you chaf'd?","Ask God for temp'rance; that's the appliance only","Which your disease requires."],"id":37,"m":"What, are you angry? Ask God for self-control; that's the only medicine your illness needs."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["I read in 's looks","Matter against me, and his eye revil'd","Me as his abject object. At this instant","He bores me with some trick. He's gone to the King;","I'll follow, and outstare him."],"id":38,"m":"I read in his looks something against me; his eye scorned me as something low and contemptible. At this moment he is working some trick against me. He's gone to the King; I'll follow and stare him down."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Stay, my lord,","And let your reason with your choler question","What 'tis you go about. To climb steep hills","Requires slow pace at first. Anger is like","A full hot horse, who being allow'd his way,","Self-mettle tires him. Not a man in England","Can advise me like you; be to yourself","As you would to your friend."],"id":39,"m":"Wait, my lord, and let your reason question your anger about what you are doing. To climb steep hills you must go slowly at first. Anger is like a hot-blooded horse: let it have its head and its own spirit tires it out. No man in England can advise me as well as you can; be to yourself what you would be to a friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["I'll to the King,","And from a mouth of honour quite cry down","This Ipswich fellow's insolence, or proclaim","There's difference in no persons."],"id":40,"m":"I'll go to the King, and with the voice of a nobleman shout down this Ipswich fellow's insolence, or else declare that there is no difference in rank between people."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Be advis'd;","Heat not a furnace for your foe so hot","That it do singe yourself. We may outrun,","By violent swiftness, that which we run at,","And lose by over-running. Know you not,","The fire that mounts the liquor till 't run o'er,","In seeming to augment it wastes it? Be advis'd.","I say again, there is no English soul","More stronger to direct you than yourself,","If with the sap of reason you would quench,","Or but allay, the fire of passion."],"id":41,"m":"Be careful. Don't heat the furnace for your enemy so hot that it scorches you. We can overshoot what we are running at by running too fast, and lose by overrunning. Don't you know that the fire that makes liquid boil over seems to increase it but actually wastes it? Be careful. I say again, there is no English soul better able to guide you than yourself, if you would quench, or just cool, the fire of passion with the sap of reason."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Sir,","I am thankful to you; and I'll go along","By your prescription; but this top-proud fellow,","Whom from the flow of gall I name not, but","From sincere motions, by intelligence,","And proofs as clear as founts in July when","We see each grain of gravel, I do know","To be corrupt and treasonous."],"id":42,"m":"Sir, I am thankful to you, and I'll follow your advice. But this arrogant fellow, whom I call so not out of bitterness but from honest motives, I know from information and proofs as clear as springs in July, when we can see every grain of gravel, to be corrupt and treasonous."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Say not \"treasonous.\""],"id":43,"m":"Don't say \"treasonous.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["To the King I'll say't, and make my vouch as strong","As shore of rock. Attend. This holy fox,","Or wolf, or both,—for he is equal ravenous","As he is subtle, and as prone to mischief","As able to perform't; his mind and place","Infecting one another, yea, reciprocally—","Only to show his pomp as well in France","As here at home, suggests the King our master","To this last costly treaty, the interview,","That swallowed so much treasure, and like a glass","Did break i' the rinsing."],"id":44,"m":"I'll say it to the King, and make my claim as firm as a rocky shore. Listen. This holy fox, or wolf, or both, for he is as greedy as he is cunning, and as ready for mischief as he is able to carry it out, his mind and his position infecting each other, just to show off his splendour in France as well as at home, urged the King our master into this last expensive treaty, the meeting that swallowed so much treasure and broke like a glass in the rinsing."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Faith, and so it did."],"id":45,"m":"Indeed it did."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Pray, give me favour, sir. This cunning Cardinal","The articles o' the combination drew","As himself pleas'd; and they were ratified","As he cried \"Thus let be,\" to as much end","As give a crutch to the dead. But our count-cardinal","Has done this, and 'tis well; for worthy Wolsey,","Who cannot err, he did it. Now this follows,—","Which, as I take it, is a kind of puppy","To the old dam, treason,—Charles the Emperor,","Under pretence to see the Queen his aunt,—","For 'twas indeed his colour, but he came","To whisper Wolsey,—here makes visitation.","His fears were, that the interview betwixt","England and France might, through their amity,","Breed him some prejudice; for from this league","Peep'd harms that menac'd him. He privily","Deals with our Cardinal; and, as I trow,—","Which I do well, for I am sure the Emperor","Paid ere he promis'd; whereby his suit was granted","Ere it was ask'd—but when the way was made,","And pav'd with gold, the Emperor thus desir'd,","That he would please to alter the King's course,","And break the foresaid peace. Let the King know,","As soon he shall by me, that thus the Cardinal","Does buy and sell his honour as he pleases","And for his own advantage."],"id":46,"m":"Please, let me go on, sir. This cunning Cardinal drew up the terms of the alliance just as he liked, and they were ratified as soon as he said \"Let it be so,\" with as much use as giving a crutch to a dead man. But our cardinal-count did it, so it must be good, for worthy Wolsey, who can't make mistakes, did it. Now this follows, which I take to be a puppy of the old bitch treason: Charles the Emperor, pretending to visit his aunt the Queen, though that was only his excuse, came to whisper with Wolsey. He feared that the meeting between England and France might, through their friendship, do him harm, since threats to him peeped out of this alliance. He deals secretly with our Cardinal, and, as I believe, and I'm sure of it, for the Emperor paid before he promised, so his request was granted before it was asked, when the way had been made and paved with gold, the Emperor asked that Wolsey turn the King's course and break that peace. Let the King know, as he soon will from me, that this is how the Cardinal buys and sells the King's honour as he likes, for his own profit."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["I am sorry","To hear this of him; and could wish he were","Something mistaken in't."],"id":47,"m":"I'm sorry to hear this about him, and I wish there were some mistake in it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["No, not a syllable:","I do pronounce him in that very shape","He shall appear in proof."],"id":48,"m":"No, not a syllable. I describe him exactly as he will be proved to be."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Brandon, a Sergeant-at-arms before him, and two or three of the Guard.","id":49},{"t":"sp","who":"BRANDON","lines":["Your office, sergeant; execute it."],"id":50,"m":"Do your duty, sergeant; carry it out."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERGEANT","lines":["Sir,","My lord the Duke of Buckingham, and Earl","Of Hereford, Stafford, and Northampton, I","Arrest thee of high treason, in the name","Of our most sovereign king."],"id":51,"m":"Sir, my lord the Duke of Buckingham, Earl of Hereford, Stafford and Northampton, I arrest you for high treason, in the name of our most sovereign king."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Lo, you, my lord,","The net has fall'n upon me! I shall perish","Under device and practice."],"id":52,"m":"There, my lord, you see: the net has fallen on me! I shall die through plotting and trickery."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRANDON","lines":["I am sorry","To see you ta'en from liberty, to look on","The business present. 'Tis his Highness' pleasure","You shall to the Tower."],"id":53,"m":"I am sorry to see you lose your liberty and to watch this business. It is his Highness's wish that you go to the Tower."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["It will help nothing","To plead mine innocence; for that dye is on me","Which makes my whit'st part black. The will of Heaven","Be done in this and all things! I obey.","O my Lord Abergavenny, fare you well!"],"id":54,"m":"It will do no good to plead my innocence, for I am stained with a dye that makes my whitest part black. God's will be done in this and in everything! I obey. Oh, my Lord Abergavenny, farewell!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRANDON","lines":["Nay, he must bear you company.","[To Abergavenny] The King","Is pleas'd you shall to the Tower, till you know","How he determines further."],"id":55,"m":"No, he must go with you. (To Abergavenny) The King wishes you to go to the Tower until you learn what he decides next."},{"t":"sp","who":"ABERGAVENNY","lines":["As the Duke said,","The will of Heaven be done, and the King's pleasure","By me obey'd!"],"id":56,"m":"As the Duke said, God's will be done, and I obey the King's wishes!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRANDON","lines":["Here is warrant from","The King to attach Lord Montacute, and the bodies","Of the Duke's confessor, John de la Car,","One Gilbert Peck, his chancellor,—"],"id":57,"m":"Here is a warrant from the King to arrest Lord Montacute, and the persons of the Duke's confessor, John de la Car, and one Gilbert Peck, his chancellor —"},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["So, so;","These are the limbs o' the plot. No more, I hope?"],"id":58,"m":"So, so; these are the limbs of the plot. No more, I hope?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRANDON","lines":["A monk o' the Chartreux."],"id":59,"m":"A Carthusian monk."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["O, Nicholas Hopkins?"],"id":60,"m":"Oh, Nicholas Hopkins?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRANDON","lines":["He."],"id":61,"m":"Him."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["My surveyor is false; the o'er-great Cardinal","Hath show'd him gold; my life is spann'd already.","I am the shadow of poor Buckingham,","Whose figure even this instant cloud puts on,","By dark'ning my clear sun. My lord, farewell."],"id":62,"m":"My surveyor has betrayed me; the overmighty Cardinal has shown him gold. My life is already measured out. I am now only the shadow of poor Buckingham, whose shape this sudden cloud takes on by darkening my bright sun. My lord, farewell."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":63},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"London. The council-chamber.","id":64},{"t":"sd","text":"Cornets. Enter the King, leaning on the Cardinal's shoulder, the Nobles, and Sir Thomas Lovell; the Cardinal places himself under the King's feet on his right side.","id":65},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["My life itself, and the best heart of it,","Thanks you for this great care. I stood i' the level","Of a full-charg'd confederacy, and give thanks","To you that chok'd it. Let be call'd before us","That gentleman of Buckingham's; in person","I'll hear his confessions justify;","And point by point the treasons of his master","He shall again relate."],"id":66,"m":"My life itself, and the very core of it, thanks you for this great care. I stood in the firing line of a fully loaded conspiracy, and I thank you for choking it off. Have that gentleman of Buckingham's called before us; I'll hear him confirm his confession in person, and he shall repeat, point by point, his master's treasons."},{"t":"sd","text":"A noise within, crying \"Room for the Queen!\" Enter Queen Katherine, ushered by the Duke of Norfolk, and the Duke of Suffolk; she kneels. The King riseth from his state, takes her up, kisses and placeth her by him.","id":67},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Nay, we must longer kneel; I am a suitor."],"id":68,"m":"No, I must kneel longer; I have a request to make."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Arise, and take place by us. Half your suit","Never name to us, you have half our power;","The other moiety, ere you ask, is given.","Repeat your will and take it."],"id":69,"m":"Get up and sit beside us. Never mention half your request to us: you have half our power. The other half is granted before you ask. Say what you want and take it."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Thank your Majesty.","That you would love yourself, and in that love","Not unconsidered leave your honour, nor","The dignity of your office, is the point","Of my petition."],"id":70,"m":"Thank you, your Majesty. My request is that you love yourself, and in that love do not neglect your honour or the dignity of your office."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Lady mine, proceed."],"id":71,"m":"Go on, my lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["I am solicited, not by a few,","And those of true condition, that your subjects","Are in great grievance. There have been commissions","Sent down among 'em, which hath flaw'd the heart","Of all their loyalties; wherein, although,","My good Lord Cardinal, they vent reproaches","Most bitterly on you, as putter on","Of these exactions, yet the King our master—","Whose honour Heaven shield from soil!—even he escapes not","Language unmannerly, yea, such which breaks","The sides of loyalty, and almost appears","In loud rebellion."],"id":72,"m":"I am being petitioned, not by a few people, and those honest ones, that your subjects are in great distress. Commissions have been sent among them that have cracked the heart of their loyalty. In this, although they pour bitter reproaches on you, my good Lord Cardinal, as the one who urged these demands, yet even the King our master, whose honour heaven keep from stain, does not escape rude language, language that breaks the bounds of loyalty and almost appears as open rebellion."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Not \"almost appears,\"","It doth appear; for, upon these taxations,","The clothiers all, not able to maintain","The many to them longing, have put off","The spinsters, carders, fullers, weavers, who,","Unfit for other life, compell'd by hunger","And lack of other means, in desperate manner","Daring the event to the teeth, are all in uproar,","And danger serves among them."],"id":73,"m":"Not \"almost appears\"; it does appear. Because of these taxes, the cloth-makers, unable to keep their many workers, have laid off the spinners, carders, fullers and weavers. Fit for no other work, driven by hunger and lack of other means, they are desperately defying the consequences and are all in uproar, and danger is among them."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Taxation!","Wherein? and what taxation? My Lord Cardinal,","You that are blam'd for it alike with us,","Know you of this taxation?"],"id":74,"m":"Taxation! On what? What taxation? My Lord Cardinal, you who are blamed for it together with us, do you know about this taxation?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Please you, sir,","I know but of a single part, in aught","Pertains to the state, and front but in that file","Where others tell steps with me."],"id":75,"m":"If you please, sir, I know only my own part of what concerns the state, and I only walk in the front of a line where others keep step with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["No, my lord?","You know no more than others? But you frame","Things that are known alike, which are not wholesome","To those which would not know them, and yet must","Perforce be their acquaintance. These exactions,","Whereof my sovereign would have note, they are","Most pestilent to the hearing; and, to bear 'em,","The back is sacrifice to the load. They say","They are devis'd by you; or else you suffer","Too hard an exclamation."],"id":76,"m":"No, my lord? You know no more than others? But you devise things that everyone then knows, which are harmful to those who would rather not know them but are forced to become familiar with them. These demands, which my sovereign wants to hear about, are poisonous even to hear of, and bearing them breaks the back under the load. They say you devised them; if not, you are blamed too harshly."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Still exaction!","The nature of it? In what kind, let's know,","Is this exaction?"],"id":77,"m":"Demands again! What kind? Let us know what sort of demand this is."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["I am much too venturous","In tempting of your patience; but am bold'ned","Under your promis'd pardon. The subjects' grief","Comes through commissions, which compels from each","The sixth part of his substance, to be levied","Without delay; and the pretence for this","Is nam'd, your wars in France. This makes bold mouths;","Tongues spit their duties out, and cold hearts freeze","Allegiance in them; their curses now","Live where their prayers did; and it's come to pass","This tractable obedience is a slave","To each incensed will. I would your Highness","Would give it quick consideration, for","There is no primer business."],"id":78,"m":"I am too bold in testing your patience, but your promised pardon gives me courage. The subjects' grievance comes from commissions that force each man to pay a sixth of all he owns, to be collected without delay, and the reason given is your wars in France. This makes mouths bold; tongues spit out their duty, and cold hearts freeze their allegiance. Their curses now live where their prayers used to, and it has come to this: their willing obedience has become a slave to every angry impulse. I wish your Highness would consider it quickly, for there is no more urgent business."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["By my life,","This is against our pleasure."],"id":79,"m":"On my life, this is against our wishes."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["And for me,","I have no further gone in this than by","A single voice; and that not pass'd me but","By learned approbation of the judges. If I am","Traduc'd by ignorant tongues, which neither know","My faculties nor person, yet will be","The chronicles of my doing, let me say","'Tis but the fate of place, and the rough brake","That virtue must go through. We must not stint","Our necessary actions, in the fear","To cope malicious censurers; which ever,","As ravenous fishes, do a vessel follow","That is new-trimm'd, but benefit no further","Than vainly longing. What we oft do best,","By sick interpreters, once weak ones, is","Not ours, or not allow'd; what worst, as oft,","Hitting a grosser quality, is cried up","For our best act. If we shall stand still,","In fear our motion will be mock'd or carp'd at,","We should take root here where we sit, or sit","State-statues only."],"id":80,"m":"As for me, I went no further in this than casting a single vote, and that only with the learned approval of the judges. If I am slandered by ignorant tongues that know neither my abilities nor me, yet set themselves up as historians of what I do, let me say that this is simply the fate of high office, the thorny thicket that virtue must go through. We must not hold back necessary actions for fear of facing malicious critics, who always follow like greedy fish behind a newly fitted ship but gain nothing except useless longing. What we often do best, sick or weak interpreters say is not ours or not good; what we do worst, because it appeals to cruder minds, is often praised as our best act. If we stand still for fear our actions will be mocked or criticised, we should take root where we sit, or sit like statues of statesmen."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Things done well,","And with a care, exempt themselves from fear;","Things done without example, in their issue","Are to be fear'd. Have you a precedent","Of this commission? I believe, not any.","We must not rend our subjects from our laws,","And stick them in our will. Sixth part of each?","A trembling contribution! Why, we take","From every tree lop, bark, and part o' the timber;","And, though we leave it with a root, thus hack'd,","The air will drink the sap. To every county","Where this is question'd send our letters, with","Free pardon to each man that has deni'd","The force of this commission. Pray, look to't;","I put it to your care."],"id":81,"m":"Things done well and carefully have nothing to fear; things done without precedent are to be feared in their results. Do you have a precedent for this commission? I believe not. We must not tear our subjects away from our laws and fix them on our mere will. A sixth of everything? A contribution to make men tremble! Why, it is as if we took from every tree its branches, bark and part of the trunk; though we leave the root, the tree so hacked will have its sap drunk by the air. Send our letters to every county where this is disputed, with a free pardon for every man who has refused this commission. Please see to it; I leave it in your care."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["A word with you. [To the Secretary, aside]","Let there be letters writ to every shire,","Of the King's grace and pardon. The grieved commons","Hardly conceive of me; let it be nois'd","That through our intercession this revokement","And pardon comes. I shall anon advise you","Further in the proceeding."],"id":82,"m":"A word with you. (To the Secretary, aside) Have letters written to every county about the King's grace and pardon. The aggrieved common people think badly of me; let it be spread about that this repeal and pardon came about through my pleading. I'll give you further instructions soon."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Secretary.","id":83},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Surveyor.","id":84},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["I am sorry that the Duke of Buckingham","Is run in your displeasure."],"id":85,"m":"I am sorry that the Duke of Buckingham has fallen out of your favour."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["It grieves many.","The gentleman is learn'd, and a most rare speaker;","To nature none more bound; his training such","That he may furnish and instruct great teachers,","And never seek for aid out of himself. Yet see,","When these so noble benefits shall prove","Not well dispos'd, the mind growing once corrupt,","They turn to vicious forms, ten times more ugly","Than ever they were fair. This man so complete,","Who was enroll'd 'mongst wonders, and when we,","Almost with ravish'd list'ning, could not find","His hour of speech a minute; he, my lady,","Hath into monstrous habits put the graces","That once were his, and is become as black","As if besmear'd in hell. Sit by us; you shall hear—","This was his gentleman in trust—of him","Things to strike honour sad. Bid him recount","The fore-recited practices, whereof","We cannot feel too little, hear too much."],"id":86,"m":"It grieves many. The gentleman is learned and a remarkably fine speaker, more gifted by nature than anyone; his education is such that he could teach great teachers and never need help from outside himself. Yet see: when such noble gifts are badly used and the mind once becomes corrupt, they turn into vicious forms ten times uglier than they were ever beautiful. This man, so complete, who was counted among the wonders, whom we listened to almost entranced so that an hour of his speech seemed a minute, has, my lady, turned the graces that were once his into monstrous habits and has become as black as if smeared in hell. Sit beside us; you shall hear things about him, from this man who was his trusted servant, that would sadden honour itself. Tell him to repeat the plots he already described, which we cannot be too little moved by, nor hear too much of."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Stand forth, and with bold spirit relate what you,","Most like a careful subject, have collected","Out of the Duke of Buckingham."],"id":87,"m":"Step forward, and boldly tell what you, like a careful subject, have gathered about the Duke of Buckingham."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Speak freely."],"id":88,"m":"Speak freely."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURVEYOR","lines":["First, it was usual with him, every day","It would infect his speech, that if the King","Should without issue die, he'll carry it so","To make the sceptre his. These very words","I've heard him utter to his son-in-law,","Lord Abergavenny; to whom by oath he menac'd","Revenge upon the Cardinal."],"id":89,"m":"First, it was his habit, every day it tainted his talk, to say that if the King died without children he would manage things so as to make the sceptre his. I've heard him say these very words to his son-in-law, Lord Abergavenny, to whom he swore he would take revenge on the Cardinal."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Please your Highness, note","This dangerous conception in this point.","Not friended by his wish, to your high person","His will is most malignant; and it stretches","Beyond you, to your friends."],"id":90,"m":"If your Highness pleases, note this dangerous idea. Since his wish isn't granted, his will is deeply malicious toward your royal person, and it reaches beyond you to your friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["My learn'd Lord Cardinal,","Deliver all with charity."],"id":91,"m":"My learned Lord Cardinal, tell everything with charity."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Speak on.","How grounded he his title to the crown?","Upon our fail? To this point hast thou heard him","At any time speak aught?"],"id":92,"m":"Go on. On what did he base his claim to the crown? On our having no heir? Have you ever heard him say anything about that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SURVEYOR","lines":["He was brought to this","By a vain prophecy of Nicholas Henton."],"id":93,"m":"He was led to this by an empty prophecy of Nicholas Henton."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["What was that Henton?"],"id":94,"m":"Who was this Henton?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SURVEYOR","lines":["Sir, a Chartreux friar,","His confessor; who fed him every minute","With words of sovereignty."],"id":95,"m":"Sir, a Carthusian friar, his confessor, who fed him every minute with talk of becoming king."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["How know'st thou this?"],"id":96,"m":"How do you know this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SURVEYOR","lines":["Not long before your Highness sped to France,","The Duke being at the Rose, within the parish","Saint Lawrence Poultney, did of me demand","What was the speech among the Londoners","Concerning the French journey. I repli'd,","Men fear the French would prove perfidious,","To the King's danger. Presently the Duke","Said, 'twas the fear, indeed; and that he doubted","'Twould prove the verity of certain words","Spoke by a holy monk, \"that oft,\" says he,","\"Hath sent to me, wishing me to permit","John de la Car, my chaplain, a choice hour","To hear from him a matter of some moment;","Whom after under the confession's seal","He solemnly had sworn, that what he spoke","My chaplain to no creature living but","To me should utter, with demure confidence","This pausingly ensu'd: 'Neither the King nor's heirs,","Tell you the Duke, shall prosper. Bid him strive","To gain the love o' the commonalty. The Duke","Shall govern England.\"'"],"id":97,"m":"Not long before your Highness went to France, the Duke, at the Rose in the parish of Saint Lawrence Poultney, asked me what the Londoners were saying about the French journey. I replied that people feared the French would prove treacherous, endangering the King. At once the Duke said that was indeed the fear, and he suspected it would prove the truth of certain words spoken by a holy monk. \"He has often sent to me,\" he said, \"asking me to allow my chaplain, John de la Car, a convenient hour to hear something important from him. After he had made my chaplain swear solemnly, under the seal of confession, to tell what he said to no living creature but me, he said slowly and with grave confidence: 'Neither the King nor his heirs, tell the Duke, shall prosper. Tell him to work to win the love of the common people. The Duke shall govern England.'\""},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["If I know you well,","You were the Duke's surveyor, and lost your office","On the complaint o' the tenants. Take good heed","You charge not in your spleen a noble person","And spoil your nobler soul; I say, take heed;","Yes, heartily beseech you."],"id":98,"m":"If I know you rightly, you were the Duke's surveyor and lost your post because his tenants complained. Be careful not to accuse a noble person out of spite and ruin your own nobler soul. I say, be careful; yes, I beg you with all my heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Let him on.","Go forward."],"id":99,"m":"Let him go on. Continue."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURVEYOR","lines":["On my soul, I'll speak but truth.","I told my lord the Duke, by the devil's illusions","The monk might be deceiv'd; and that 'twas dangerous for him","To ruminate on this so far, until","It forg'd him some design; which, being believ'd,","It was much like to do. He answer'd, \"Tush,","It can do me no damage;\" adding further","That, had the King in his last sickness fail'd,","The Cardinal's and Sir Thomas Lovell's heads","Should have gone off."],"id":100,"m":"On my soul, I'll speak only the truth. I told my lord the Duke that the monk might have been deceived by the devil's tricks, and that it was dangerous for him to brood on this so long that it led him into some plot, which, if he believed it, it was very likely to do. He answered, \"Nonsense, it can do me no harm,\" and added that if the King had died in his last illness, the Cardinal's and Sir Thomas Lovell's heads would have come off."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Ha! what, so rank? Ah ha!","There's mischief in this man. Canst thou say further?"],"id":101,"m":"Ha! What, as foul as that? Aha! There's mischief in this man. Can you tell more?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SURVEYOR","lines":["I can, my liege."],"id":102,"m":"I can, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Proceed."],"id":103,"m":"Go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURVEYOR","lines":["Being at Greenwich,","After your Highness had reprov'd the Duke","About Sir William Bulmer,—"],"id":104,"m":"At Greenwich, after your Highness had rebuked the Duke about Sir William Bulmer —"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I remember","Of such a time; being my sworn servant,","The Duke retain'd him his. But on; what hence?"],"id":105,"m":"I remember that time; Bulmer was my sworn servant and the Duke took him into his own service. But go on; what came of it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SURVEYOR","lines":["\"If,\" quoth he, \"I for this had been committed,\"","—As, to the Tower, I thought,—\"I would have play'd","The part my father meant to act upon","The usurper Richard; who, being at Salisbury,","Made suit to come in 's presence; which if granted,","As he made semblance of his duty, would","Have put his knife into him.\""],"id":106,"m":"\"If,\" he said, \"I had been imprisoned for this,\" in the Tower, I took it, \"I would have played the part my father meant to play against the usurper Richard. When Richard was at Salisbury, my father asked to come into his presence; if that had been granted, he would have pretended to pay his respects and put his knife into him.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["A giant traitor!"],"id":107,"m":"A monstrous traitor!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Now, madam, may his Highness live in freedom,","And this man out of prison?"],"id":108,"m":"Now, madam, can his Highness live safely with this man out of prison?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["God mend all!"],"id":109,"m":"God set everything right!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["There's something more would out of thee; what say'st?"],"id":110,"m":"There's something more you want to say; what is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SURVEYOR","lines":["After \"the Duke his father,\" with \"the knife,\"","He stretch'd him, and, with one hand on his dagger,","Another spread on 's breast, mounting his eyes,","He did discharge a horrible oath; whose tenour","Was, were he evil us'd, he would outgo","His father by as much as a performance","Does an irresolute purpose."],"id":111,"m":"After saying \"the Duke his father\" and \"the knife,\" he drew himself up, one hand on his dagger and the other spread on his chest, lifted his eyes, and swore a horrible oath, the gist of which was that if he were badly treated he would outdo his father as much as a deed outdoes an uncertain intention."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["There's his period,","To sheathe his knife in us. He is attach'd.","Call him to present trial. If he may","Find mercy in the law, 'tis his; if none,","Let him not seek 't of us. By day and night,","He's traitor to th' height."],"id":112,"m":"That's his aim: to sheathe his knife in us. He is under arrest. Bring him to trial at once. If he can find mercy in the law, he may have it; if not, let him not look for it from us. By day and night, he is a traitor to the highest degree."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":113},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"III","loc":"An ante-chamber in the palace.","id":114},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Lord Chamberlain and Lord Sandys.","id":115},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Is't possible the spells of France should juggle","Men into such strange mysteries?"],"id":116,"m":"Is it possible that the spells of France can trick men into such strange fashions?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SANDYS","lines":["New customs,","Though they be never so ridiculous,","Nay, let 'em be unmanly, yet are follow'd."],"id":117,"m":"New customs, however ridiculous, even unmanly ones, are still followed."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["As far as I see, all the good our English","Have got by the late voyage is but merely","A fit or two o' the face; but they are shrewd ones;","For when they hold 'em, you would swear directly","Their very noses had been counsellors","To Pepin or Clotharius, they keep state so."],"id":118,"m":"As far as I can see, all the good our Englishmen have got from the recent trip is a grimace or two; but they're sharp ones, for when they put them on you'd swear their very noses had been advisers to Pepin or Clotharius, they look so grand."},{"t":"sp","who":"SANDYS","lines":["They have all new legs, and lame ones. One would take it,","That never saw 'em pace before, the spavin","Or springhalt reign'd among 'em."],"id":119,"m":"They all have new ways of walking, and lame ones. Anyone who had never seen them walk before would think they all suffered from horse diseases like spavin or stringhalt."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Death! my lord,","Their clothes are after such a pagan cut too,","That, sure, they've worn out Christendom."],"id":120,"m":"Death! My lord, their clothes are so outlandishly cut, too, that they must have worn out all Christian fashions."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Thomas Lovell.","id":121},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","cont":1,"lines":["How now!","What news, Sir Thomas Lovell?"],"id":122,"m":"Hello! What's the news, Sir Thomas Lovell?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["Faith, my lord,","I hear of none, but the new proclamation","That's clapp'd upon the court-gate."],"id":123,"m":"Indeed, my lord, I've heard none except the new proclamation nailed up on the court gate."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["What is't for?"],"id":124,"m":"What is it for?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["The reformation of our travell'd gallants,","That fill the court with quarrels, talk, and tailors."],"id":125,"m":"The reform of our well-travelled young dandies, who fill the court with quarrels, chatter and tailors."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["I'm glad 'tis there. Now I would pray our monsieurs","To think an English courtier may be wise,","And never see the Louvre."],"id":126,"m":"I'm glad it's there. Now I'd ask our \"monsieurs\" to believe an English courtier can be wise without ever seeing the Louvre."},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["They must either,","For so run the conditions, leave those remnants","Of fool and feather that they got in France,","With all their honourable points of ignorance","Pertaining thereunto, as fights and fireworks,","Abusing better men than they can be,","Out of a foreign wisdom, renouncing clean","The faith they have in tennis and tall stockings,","Short blist'red breeches, and those types of travel,","And understand again like honest men,","Or pack to their old playfellows. There, I take it,","They may, \"cum privilegio,\" wear away","The lag end of their lewdness and be laugh'd at."],"id":127,"m":"By the terms of it, they must either give up the scraps of folly and feathers they picked up in France, with all the honourable bits of ignorance that go with them, such as duelling and fireworks and insulting better men than themselves with foreign wisdom; renounce completely their faith in tennis, tall stockings, short puffed breeches and those other badges of travel, and behave like honest men again; or else pack off to their old playmates. There, I suppose, they may, \"with privilege,\" wear out the last of their lewdness and be laughed at."},{"t":"sp","who":"SANDYS","lines":["'Tis time to give 'em physic, their diseases","Are grown so catching."],"id":128,"m":"It's time to give them medicine; their diseases have become so catching."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["What a loss our ladies","Will have of these trim vanities!"],"id":129,"m":"What a loss our ladies will suffer from these smart vanities!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["Ay, marry,","There will be woe indeed, lords; the sly whoresons","Have got a speeding trick to lay down ladies.","A French song and a fiddle has no fellow."],"id":130,"m":"Yes indeed, there will be grief, lords; the sly rascals have a quick trick for laying ladies down. A French song and a fiddle have no equal."},{"t":"sp","who":"SANDYS","lines":["The devil fiddle 'em! I am glad they are going,","For, sure, there's no converting of 'em. Now","An honest country lord, as I am, beaten","A long time out of play, may bring his plainsong","And have an hour of hearing; and, by 'r Lady,","Held current music too."],"id":131,"m":"The devil fiddle them! I'm glad they're going, for there's certainly no converting them. Now an honest country lord like me, kept out of the game for a long time, can bring his plain tune, get an hour's hearing and, by Our Lady, be thought good music too."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Well said, Lord Sandys;","Your colt's tooth is not cast yet."],"id":132,"m":"Well said, Lord Sandys; you still have your colt's tooth, your youthful appetite."},{"t":"sp","who":"SANDYS","lines":["No, my lord;","Nor shall not, while I have a stump."],"id":133,"m":"No, my lord, and I won't lose it while I have a stump left."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Sir Thomas,","Whither were you a-going?"],"id":134,"m":"Sir Thomas, where were you going?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["To the Cardinal's.","Your lordship is a guest too."],"id":135,"m":"To the Cardinal's. Your lordship is a guest too."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["O, 'tis true:","This night he makes a supper, and a great one,","To many lords and ladies; there will be","The beauty of this kingdom, I'll assure you."],"id":136,"m":"Oh, that's true. Tonight he's giving a supper, a great one, for many lords and ladies. All the beauty of this kingdom will be there, I promise you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["That churchman bears a bounteous mind indeed,","A hand as fruitful as the land that feeds us;","His dews fall everywhere."],"id":137,"m":"That churchman has a generous mind indeed, a hand as fruitful as the land that feeds us; his dew falls everywhere."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["No doubt he's noble;","He had a black mouth that said other of him."],"id":138,"m":"No doubt he's noble; anyone who said otherwise would have a slanderous mouth."},{"t":"sp","who":"SANDYS","lines":["He may, my lord; has wherewithal; in him","Sparing would show a worse sin than ill doctrine.","Men of his way should be most liberal;","They are set here for examples."],"id":139,"m":"He can afford it, my lord; he has the means. In him meanness would be a worse sin than false doctrine. Men in his position should be very generous; they are set here as examples."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["True, they are so;","But few now give so great ones. My barge stays;","Your lordship shall along. Come, good Sir Thomas,","We shall be late else; which I would not be,","For I was spoke to, with Sir Henry Guildford,","This night to be comptrollers."],"id":140,"m":"True, they are; but few set such great ones now. My barge is waiting; your lordship shall come with me. Come, good Sir Thomas, or we'll be late, which I don't want to be, because I was asked, along with Sir Henry Guildford, to be master of ceremonies tonight."},{"t":"sp","who":"SANDYS","lines":["I am your lordship's."],"id":141,"m":"I am at your lordship's service."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":142},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"IV","loc":"A Hall in York Place.","id":143},{"t":"sd","text":"Hautboys. A small table under a state for the Cardinal, a longer table for the guests. Then enter Anne Bullen and divers other Ladies and Gentlemen as guests, at one door; at another door, enter Sir Henry Guildford.","id":144},{"t":"sp","who":"GUILDFORD","lines":["Ladies, a general welcome from his Grace","Salutes ye all; this night he dedicates","To fair content and you. None here, he hopes,","In all this noble bevy, has brought with her","One care abroad. He would have all as merry","As, first, good company, good wine, good welcome,","Can make good people."],"id":145,"m":"Ladies, his Grace greets you all with a warm welcome. He dedicates this night to pleasure and to you. He hopes none of this noble company has brought a single care out with her. He wants everyone as merry as good company, good wine and a good welcome can make good people."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lord Chamberlain, Lord Sandys, and Sir Thomas Lovell.","id":146},{"t":"sp","who":"GUILDFORD","cont":1,"lines":["O, my lord, you're tardy;","The very thought of this fair company","Clapp'd wings to me."],"id":147,"m":"Oh, my lord, you're late. The mere thought of this lovely company gave me wings."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["You are young, Sir Harry Guildford."],"id":148,"m":"You are young, Sir Harry Guildford."},{"t":"sp","who":"SANDYS","lines":["Sir Thomas Lovell, had the Cardinal","But half my lay thoughts in him, some of these","Should find a running banquet ere they rested,","I think would better please 'em. By my life,","They are a sweet society of fair ones."],"id":149,"m":"Sir Thomas Lovell, if the Cardinal had only half my worldly thoughts, some of these ladies would get a quick snack of a different kind before they went to bed, which I think would please them more. On my life, they are a sweet company of beauties."},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["O, that your lordship were but now confessor","To one or two of these!"],"id":150,"m":"Oh, if only your lordship were confessor to one or two of them now!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SANDYS","lines":["I would I were;","They should find easy penance."],"id":151,"m":"I wish I were; they'd get an easy penance."},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["Faith, how easy?"],"id":152,"m":"Really? How easy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SANDYS","lines":["As easy as a down-bed would afford it."],"id":153,"m":"As easy as a feather bed could make it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Sweet ladies, will it please you sit? Sir Harry,","Place you that side; I'll take the charge of this.","His Grace is ent'ring. Nay, you must not freeze;","Two women plac'd together makes cold weather.","My Lord Sandys, you are one will keep 'em waking;","Pray, sit between these ladies."],"id":154,"m":"Sweet ladies, will you please sit down? Sir Harry, you seat that side; I'll take charge of this one. His Grace is coming in. No, you mustn't freeze; two women seated together make cold weather. My Lord Sandys, you're one who'll keep them awake; please sit between these ladies."},{"t":"sp","who":"SANDYS","lines":["By my faith,","And thank your lordship. By your leave, sweet ladies.","If I chance to talk a little wild, forgive me;","I had it from my father."],"id":155,"m":"Gladly, and thank you, my lord. With your permission, sweet ladies. If I happen to talk a little wildly, forgive me; I got it from my father."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE BULLEN","lines":["Was he mad, sir?"],"id":156,"m":"Was he mad, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SANDYS","lines":["O, very mad, exceeding mad; in love too;","But he would bite none. Just as I do now,","He would kiss you twenty with a breath."],"id":157,"m":"Oh, very mad, extremely mad, and in love too; but he never bit anyone. Just as I do now, he would kiss you twenty times in one breath."},{"t":"sd","text":"Kisses her.","id":158},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Well said, my lord.","So, now you're fairly seated. Gentlemen,","The penance lies on you, if these fair ladies","Pass away frowning."],"id":159,"m":"Well done, my lord. So, now you're nicely seated. Gentlemen, it will be your fault if these lovely ladies go away frowning."},{"t":"sp","who":"SANDYS","lines":["For my little cure,","Let me alone."],"id":160,"m":"For my small part of the cure, leave it to me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Hautboys. Enter Cardinal Wolsey, and takes his state.","id":161},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["You're welcome, my fair guests. That noble lady","Or gentleman that is not freely merry","Is not my friend. This, to confirm my welcome;","And to you all, good health."],"id":162,"m":"Welcome, my fair guests. Any noble lady or gentleman who isn't freely merry is no friend of mine. This, to confirm my welcome; and good health to you all."},{"t":"sd","text":"Drinks.","id":163},{"t":"sp","who":"SANDYS","lines":["Your Grace is noble.","Let me have such a bowl may hold my thanks,","And save me so much talking."],"id":164,"m":"Your Grace is noble. Give me a bowl big enough to hold my thanks, and save me so much talking."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["My Lord Sandys,","I am beholding to you; cheer your neighbours.","Ladies, you are not merry. Gentlemen,","Whose fault is this?"],"id":165,"m":"My Lord Sandys, I am obliged to you; cheer up your neighbours. Ladies, you aren't merry. Gentlemen, whose fault is this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SANDYS","lines":["The red wine first must rise","In their fair cheeks, my lord; then we shall have 'em","Talk us to silence."],"id":166,"m":"First the red wine must rise into their pretty cheeks, my lord; then they'll talk us into silence."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE BULLEN","lines":["You are a merry gamester,","My Lord Sandys."],"id":167,"m":"You are a merry player, my Lord Sandys."},{"t":"sp","who":"SANDYS","lines":["Yes, if I make my play.","Here's to your ladyship; and pledge it, madam,","For 'tis to such a thing,—"],"id":168,"m":"Yes, if I win my game. Here's to your ladyship; and drink to it, madam, for it's to such a thing —"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE BULLEN","lines":["You cannot show me."],"id":169,"m":"You can't show me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SANDYS","lines":["I told your Grace they would talk anon."],"id":170,"m":"I told your Grace they'd start talking soon."},{"t":"sd","text":"Drum and trumpet, chambers discharged.","id":171},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["What's that?"],"id":172,"m":"What's that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Look out there, some of ye."],"id":173,"m":"Look out there, some of you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Servant.","id":174},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["What warlike voice,","And to what end, is this? Nay, ladies, fear not;","By all the laws of war you're privileg'd."],"id":175,"m":"What warlike sound is this, and what is it for? No, ladies, don't be afraid; by all the laws of war you're protected."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Servant.","id":176},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["How now! what is't?"],"id":177,"m":"Well! What is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["A noble troop of strangers,","For so they seem. They've left their barge and landed,","And hither make, as great ambassadors","From foreign princes."],"id":178,"m":"A noble troop of foreigners, or so they seem. They've left their barge, landed, and are coming here like great ambassadors from foreign princes."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Good Lord Chamberlain,","Go, give 'em welcome; you can speak the French tongue;","And, pray, receive 'em nobly, and conduct 'em","Into our presence, where this heaven of beauty","Shall shine at full upon them. Some attend him."],"id":179,"m":"Good Lord Chamberlain, go and welcome them; you speak French. And please receive them nobly and bring them into our presence, where this heaven of beauty shall shine fully on them. Some of you go with him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Chamberlain, attended. All rise, and tables remov'd.","id":180},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","cont":1,"lines":["You have now a broken banquet; but we'll mend it.","A good digestion to you all; and once more","I shower a welcome on ye. Welcome all!"],"id":181,"m":"Now your banquet has been interrupted, but we'll make up for it. Good digestion to you all, and once again I shower welcome on you. Welcome, all!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Hautboys. Enter the King, and others, as masquers, habited like shepherds, usher'd by the Lord Chamberlain. They pass directly before the Cardinal, and gracefully salute him.","id":182},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","cont":1,"lines":["A noble company! What are their pleasures?"],"id":183,"m":"A noble company! What do they wish?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Because they speak no English, thus they pray'd","To tell your Grace, that, having heard by fame","Of this so noble and so fair assembly","This night to meet here, they could do no less,","Out of the great respect they bear to beauty,","But leave their flocks; and, under your fair conduct,","Crave leave to view these ladies and entreat","An hour of revels with 'em."],"id":184,"m":"Since they speak no English, they asked me to tell your Grace this: having heard by report of this noble and lovely gathering meeting here tonight, they could do no less, out of their great respect for beauty, than leave their flocks; and with your kind permission they ask leave to see these ladies and beg an hour of dancing with them."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Say, Lord Chamberlain,","They have done my poor house grace; for which I pay 'em","A thousand thanks, and pray 'em take their pleasures."],"id":185,"m":"Tell them, Lord Chamberlain, that they have honoured my poor house, for which I give them a thousand thanks, and ask them to enjoy themselves."},{"t":"sd","text":"They choose ladies for the dance. The King chooses Anne Bullen.","id":186},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["The fairest hand I ever touch'd! O beauty,","Till now I never knew thee!"],"id":187,"m":"The fairest hand I ever touched! Oh beauty, I never knew you until now!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Music. Dance.","id":188},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["My lord!"],"id":189,"m":"My lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Your Grace?"],"id":190,"m":"Your Grace?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Pray, tell 'em thus much from me:","There should be one amongst 'em, by his person,","More worthy this place than myself; to whom,","If I but knew him, with my love and duty","I would surrender it."],"id":191,"m":"Please tell them this from me: there must be one among them, by his bearing, more worthy of this seat than I am, and if I only knew who he was I would give it up to him with love and duty."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["I will, my lord."],"id":192,"m":"I will, my lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Whispers the Masquers.","id":193},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["What say they?"],"id":194,"m":"What do they say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Such a one, they all confess,","There is indeed; which they would have your Grace","Find out, and he will take it."],"id":195,"m":"They all admit there is such a one, and they would like your Grace to find him out; then he will take the seat."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Let me see, then.","By all your good leaves, gentlemen; here I'll make","My royal choice."],"id":196,"m":"Let me see, then. With your permission, gentlemen, here I'll make my royal choice."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Ye have found him, Cardinal. [Unmasking]","You hold a fair assembly; you do well, lord.","You are a churchman, or, I'll tell you, Cardinal,","I should judge now unhappily."],"id":197,"m":"You've found him, Cardinal. (Unmasking) You hold a fine gathering; you do well, lord. You are a churchman, or else, I tell you, Cardinal, I would think badly of you now."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["I am glad","Your Grace is grown so pleasant."],"id":198,"m":"I'm glad your Grace has become so merry."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["My Lord Chamberlain,","Prithee come hither. What fair lady's that?"],"id":199,"m":"My Lord Chamberlain, please come here. Who is that lovely lady?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["An't please your Grace, Sir Thomas Bullen's daughter,—","The Viscount Rochford,—one of her Highness' women."],"id":200,"m":"If it please your Grace, Sir Thomas Bullen's daughter, Viscount Rochford's, one of her Highness's ladies-in-waiting."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["By heaven, she is a dainty one. Sweetheart,","I were unmannerly to take you out","And not to kiss you. A health, gentlemen","Let it go round."],"id":201,"m":"By heaven, she is a delicate one. Sweetheart, it would be bad manners to lead you out to dance and not kiss you. A toast, gentlemen; let it go round."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Sir Thomas Lovell, is the banquet ready","I' the privy chamber?"],"id":202,"m":"Sir Thomas Lovell, is the banquet ready in the private room?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["Yes, my lord."],"id":203,"m":"Yes, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Your Grace,","I fear, with dancing is a little heated."],"id":204,"m":"I'm afraid your Grace is a little heated from dancing."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I fear, too much."],"id":205,"m":"Too much, I'm afraid."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["There's fresher air, my lord,","In the next chamber."],"id":206,"m":"There's fresher air in the next room, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Lead in your ladies, every one. Sweet partner,","I must not yet forsake you; let's be merry.","Good my Lord Cardinal, I have half a dozen healths","To drink to these fair ladies, and a measure","To lead 'em once again; and then let's dream","Who's best in favour. Let the music knock it."],"id":207,"m":"Lead in your ladies, every one of you. Sweet partner, I mustn't leave you yet; let's be merry. My good Lord Cardinal, I have half a dozen toasts to drink to these lovely ladies, and a dance to lead them in once more; and then let's dream about who is most in favour. Let the music strike up."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt with trumpets.","id":208},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"Westminster. A street.","id":209},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter two Gentlemen at several doors.","id":210},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Whither away so fast?"],"id":211,"m":"Where are you going so fast?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["O, God save ye!","Even to the hall, to hear what shall become","Of the great Duke of Buckingham."],"id":212,"m":"Oh, God save you! To the hall, to hear what will become of the great Duke of Buckingham."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["I'll save you","That labour, sir. All's now done, but the ceremony","Of bringing back the prisoner."],"id":213,"m":"I'll save you the trouble, sir. It's all over, except for the ceremony of bringing the prisoner back."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["Were you there?"],"id":214,"m":"Were you there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Yes, indeed, was I."],"id":215,"m":"Yes, indeed I was."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["Pray, speak what has happen'd."],"id":216,"m":"Please tell me what happened."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["You may guess quickly what."],"id":217,"m":"You can quickly guess what."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["Is he found guilty?"],"id":218,"m":"Has he been found guilty?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Yes, truly is he, and condemn'd upon't."],"id":219,"m":"Yes, he has, and condemned for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["I am sorry for't."],"id":220,"m":"I'm sorry for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["So are a number more."],"id":221,"m":"So are many more."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["But, pray, how pass'd it?"],"id":222,"m":"But please, how did it go?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["I'll tell you in a little. The great Duke","Came to the bar; where to his accusations","He pleaded still not guilty and alleged","Many sharp reasons to defeat the law.","The King's attorney on the contrary","Urg'd on the examinations, proofs, confessions","Of divers witnesses; which the Duke desir'd","To have brought viva voce to his face;","At which appear'd against him his surveyor;","Sir Gilbert Peck his chancellor; and John Car,","Confessor to him, with that devil-monk,","Hopkins, that made this mischief."],"id":223,"m":"I'll tell you briefly. The great Duke came to the bar, where he kept pleading not guilty to the charges and offered many sharp arguments to defeat the law. The King's attorney, on the other side, pressed the statements, proofs and confessions of various witnesses, whom the Duke asked to have brought to his face to speak in person. Then his surveyor appeared against him, Sir Gilbert Peck his chancellor, and John Car his confessor, along with that devil of a monk, Hopkins, who caused this mischief."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["That was he","That fed him with his prophecies?"],"id":224,"m":"Was he the one who fed him with prophecies?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["The same.","All these accus'd him strongly; which he fain","Would have flung from him, but, indeed, he could not.","And so his peers, upon this evidence,","Have found him guilty of high treason. Much","He spoke, and learnedly, for life; but all","Was either pitied in him or forgotten."],"id":225,"m":"The same. All of these accused him strongly, and he would gladly have thrown off their charges, but in truth he couldn't. So his fellow peers, on this evidence, found him guilty of high treason. He spoke much, and learnedly, for his life, but it was all either pitied or ignored."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["After all this, how did he bear himself?"],"id":226,"m":"After all this, how did he behave?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["When he was brought again to the bar, to hear","His knell rung out, his judgment, he was stirr'd","With such an agony, he sweat extremely,","And something spoke in choler, ill, and hasty.","But he fell to himself again, and sweetly","In all the rest show'd a most noble patience."],"id":227,"m":"When he was brought back to the bar to hear his death knell rung, his sentence, he was so shaken with agony that he sweated heavily and said something angry, bitter and rash. But he recovered himself, and for the rest showed a most noble and gentle patience."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["I do not think he fears death."],"id":228,"m":"I don't think he's afraid of death."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Sure, he does not;","He never was so womanish. The cause","He may a little grieve at."],"id":229,"m":"Surely he isn't; he was never so womanish. He may grieve a little at the cause of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["Certainly","The Cardinal is the end of this."],"id":230,"m":"Surely the Cardinal is behind this."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["'Tis likely,","By all conjectures: first, Kildare's attainder,","Then deputy of Ireland; who remov'd,","Earl Surrey was sent thither, and in haste too,","Lest he should help his father."],"id":231,"m":"It's likely, by every guess. First there was the condemnation of Kildare, then Deputy of Ireland; once he was removed, the Earl of Surrey was sent there, and in a hurry too, so that he couldn't help his father-in-law."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["That trick of state","Was a deep envious one."],"id":232,"m":"That political trick was a deep and spiteful one."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["At his return","No doubt he will requite it. This is noted,","And generally, whoever the King favours,","The Cardinal instantly will find employment,","And far enough from court too."],"id":233,"m":"No doubt he will pay it back when he returns. People have noticed that, as a rule, whoever the King favours, the Cardinal will at once find a job for, and far enough from court too."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["All the commons","Hate him perniciously, and, o' my conscience,","Wish him ten fathom deep. This duke as much","They love and dote on; call him bounteous Buckingham,","The mirror of all courtesy,—"],"id":234,"m":"All the common people hate him deadly and, on my conscience, wish him sixty feet under the sea. They love and adore this duke just as much; they call him bountiful Buckingham, the model of all courtesy —"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Buckingham from his arraignment; tipstaves before him; the axe with the edge towards him; halberds on each side; accompanied with Sir Thomas Lovell, Sir Nicholas Vaux, Sir William Sandys, and common people.","id":235},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Stay there, sir,","And see the noble ruin'd man you speak of."],"id":236,"m":"Stop there, sir, and see the noble ruined man you're talking about."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["Let's stand close, and behold him."],"id":237,"m":"Let's stand aside and watch him."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["All good people,","You that thus far have come to pity me,","Hear what I say, and then go home and lose me.","I have this day receiv'd a traitor's judgement,","And by that name must die; yet, Heaven bear witness,","And if I have a conscience, let it sink me,","Even as the axe falls, if I be not faithful!","The law I bear no malice for my death;","'T has done, upon the premises, but justice;","But those that sought it I could wish more Christians.","Be what they will, I heartily forgive 'em;","Yet let 'em look they glory not in mischief,","Nor build their evils on the graves of great men,","For then my guiltless blood must cry against 'em.","For further life in this world I ne'er hope,","Nor will I sue, although the King have mercies","More than I dare make faults. You few that lov'd me","And dare be bold to weep for Buckingham,","His noble friends and fellows, whom to leave","Is only bitter to him, only dying,","Go with me, like good angels, to my end;","And, as the long divorce of steel falls on me,","Make of your prayers one sweet sacrifice,","And lift my soul to heaven. Lead on, o' God's name."],"id":238,"m":"All you good people who have come this far out of pity for me, hear what I say, and then go home and forget me. Today I have received a traitor's sentence and must die under that name; yet heaven be my witness, and if I have a conscience, let it sink me as the axe falls if I am not loyal! I bear the law no grudge for my death; on the evidence it has done only justice. But I wish those who sought it were better Christians. Whoever they are, I forgive them with all my heart; but let them take care not to glory in mischief, nor build their evil on great men's graves, for then my innocent blood will cry out against them. I never hope for more life in this world, nor will I beg for it, although the King has more mercy than I dare commit faults. You few who loved me and dare to weep for Buckingham, his noble friends and companions, whom it is the only bitterness, the only death, to leave, go with me like good angels to my end; and as the long divorce of the steel falls on me, make your prayers one sweet offering and lift my soul to heaven. Lead on, in God's name."},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["I do beseech your Grace, for charity,","If ever any malice in your heart","Were hid against me, now to forgive me frankly."],"id":239,"m":"I beg your Grace, out of charity, if any malice against me was ever hidden in your heart, to forgive me freely now."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Sir Thomas Lovell, I as free forgive you","As I would be forgiven. I forgive all.","There cannot be those numberless offences","'Gainst me, that I cannot take peace with; no black envy","Shall mark my grave. Commend me to his Grace;","And, if he speak of Buckingham, pray, tell him","You met him half in heaven. My vows and prayers","Yet are the King's; and, till my soul forsake,","Shall cry for blessings on him. May he live","Longer than I have time to tell his years!","Ever belov'd and loving may his rule be!","And when old Time shall lead him to his end,","Goodness and he fill up one monument!"],"id":240,"m":"Sir Thomas Lovell, I forgive you as freely as I would be forgiven. I forgive everyone. There cannot be so many offences against me that I cannot make peace with them; no dark hatred shall mark my grave. Give my respects to his Grace, and if he speaks of Buckingham, please tell him you met him half in heaven. My vows and prayers are still the King's, and until my soul leaves me they will cry for blessings on him. May he live longer than I have time to count his years! May his rule be always loved and loving! And when old Time leads him to his end, may goodness and he share one monument!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["To the water side I must conduct your Grace;","Then give my charge up to Sir Nicholas Vaux,","Who undertakes you to your end."],"id":241,"m":"I must take your Grace to the riverside, then hand over my charge to Sir Nicholas Vaux, who will take you to your end."},{"t":"sp","who":"VAUX","lines":["Prepare there,","The Duke is coming. See the barge be ready;","And fit it with such furniture as suits","The greatness of his person."],"id":242,"m":"Get ready there, the Duke is coming. See that the barge is ready, and furnish it with such fittings as suit the greatness of his person."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUCKINGHAM","lines":["Nay, Sir Nicholas,","Let it alone; my state now will but mock me.","When I came hither, I was Lord High Constable","And Duke of Buckingham; now, poor Edward Bohun.","Yet I am richer than my base accusers,","That never knew what truth meant. I now seal it;","And with that blood will make 'em one day groan for't.","My noble father, Henry of Buckingham,","Who first rais'd head against usurping Richard,","Flying for succour to his servant Banister,","Being distress'd, was by that wretch betray'd,","And without trial fell; God's peace be with him!","Henry the Seventh succeeding, truly pitying","My father's loss, like a most royal prince,","Restor'd me to my honours, and, out of ruins,","Made my name once more noble. Now his son,","Henry the Eighth, life, honour, name, and all","That made me happy, at one stroke has taken","For ever from the world. I had my trial,","And, must needs say, a noble one; which makes me","A little happier than my wretched father.","Yet thus far we are one in fortunes: both","Fell by our servants, by those men we lov'd most;","A most unnatural and faithless service.","Heaven has an end in all; yet, you that hear me,","This from a dying man receive as certain:","Where you are liberal of your loves and counsels","Be sure you be not loose; for those you make friends","And give your hearts to, when they once perceive","The least rub in your fortunes, fall away","Like water from ye; never found again","But where they mean to sink ye. All good people,","Pray for me! I must now forsake ye. The last hour","Of my long weary life is come upon me.","Farewell!","And when you would say something that is sad,","Speak how I fell. I have done; and God forgive me!"],"id":243,"m":"No, Sir Nicholas, leave it; my former rank would only mock me now. When I came here I was Lord High Constable and Duke of Buckingham; now I'm poor Edward Bohun. Yet I am richer than my low accusers, who never knew what truth meant. I seal my truth now, and with this blood I'll make them groan for it one day. My noble father, Henry of Buckingham, who first raised an army against the usurper Richard, fled for help to his servant Banister when he was in trouble, was betrayed by that wretch, and died without trial; God's peace be with him! Henry the Seventh, who came after, truly pitying my father's loss, like a most royal prince restored me to my honours and made my name noble again out of the ruins. Now his son, Henry the Eighth, has taken life, honour, name and everything that made me happy from the world for ever at one stroke. I had a trial, and I must say a noble one, which makes me a little happier than my wretched father. Yet in this our fortunes are the same: we both fell through our servants, through the men we loved most, a most unnatural and faithless service. Heaven has a purpose in everything; yet you who hear me, take this as certain from a dying man: where you are generous with your love and advice, be sure you are not careless. Those you make friends of and give your hearts to, once they see the least bump in your fortunes, fall away from you like water and are never found again except where they mean to sink you. All good people, pray for me! I must leave you now. The last hour of my long, weary life has come. Farewell! And when you want to tell a sad story, tell how I fell. I have finished; and God forgive me!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Duke and train.","id":244},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["O, this is full of pity! Sir, it calls,","I fear, too many curses on their heads","That were the authors."],"id":245,"m":"Oh, this is full of pity! Sir, I fear it calls down too many curses on the heads of those who caused it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["If the Duke be guiltless,","'Tis full of woe; yet I can give you inkling","Of an ensuing evil, if it fall,","Greater than this."],"id":246,"m":"If the Duke is innocent, it's full of grief; yet I can give you a hint of an evil to come, if it happens, greater than this."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Good angels keep it from us!","What may it be? You do not doubt my faith, sir?"],"id":247,"m":"Good angels keep it from us! What could it be? You don't doubt my loyalty, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["This secret is so weighty, 'twill require","A strong faith to conceal it."],"id":248,"m":"This secret is so weighty it will need strong loyalty to keep it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Let me have it.","I do not talk much."],"id":249,"m":"Tell me; I don't talk much."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["I am confident;","You shall, sir. Did you not of late days hear","A buzzing of a separation","Between the King and Katherine?"],"id":250,"m":"I trust you; you shall have it, sir. Didn't you hear recently a buzz of rumour about a separation between the King and Katherine?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Yes, but it held not;","For when the King once heard it, out of anger","He sent command to the Lord Mayor straight","To stop the rumour, and allay those tongues","That durst disperse it."],"id":251,"m":"Yes, but it didn't last; for when the King heard it he angrily ordered the Lord Mayor at once to stop the rumour and silence the tongues that dared to spread it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["But that slander, sir,","Is found a truth now; for it grows again","Fresher than e'er it was; and held for certain","The King will venture at it. Either the Cardinal,","Or some about him near, have, out of malice","To the good Queen, possess'd him with a scruple","That will undo her. To confirm this too,","Cardinal Campeius is arriv'd, and lately;","As all think, for this business."],"id":252,"m":"But that slander, sir, has now turned out true, for it is growing again, fresher than ever, and people take it as certain that the King will attempt it. Either the Cardinal or someone close to him, out of malice toward the good Queen, has filled him with a doubt of conscience that will ruin her. To confirm this, Cardinal Campeius has arrived, recently, and everyone thinks it is for this business."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["'Tis the Cardinal;","And merely to revenge him on the Emperor","For not bestowing on him, at his asking,","The archbishopric of Toledo, this is purpos'd."],"id":253,"m":"It's the Cardinal. This is planned merely to take revenge on the Emperor for not giving him the archbishopric of Toledo when he asked for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["I think you have hit the mark; but is't not cruel","That she should feel the smart of this? The Cardinal","Will have his will, and she must fall."],"id":254,"m":"I think you've hit the mark. But isn't it cruel that she should suffer for this? The Cardinal will have his way, and she must fall."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["'Tis woeful.","We are too open here to argue this;","Let's think in private more."],"id":255,"m":"It's sad. We're too exposed here to discuss this; let's think about it more in private."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":256},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"An ante-chamber in the palace.","id":257},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Lord Chamberlain, reading this letter.","id":258},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["\"My lord, the horses your lordship sent for,","with all the care had, I saw well chosen, ridden, and furnish'd.","They were young and handsome, and of the best breed in the north.","When they were ready to set out for London, a man of","my Lord Cardinal's, by commission and main power, took","'em from me, with this reason: His master would be serv'd","before a subject, if not before the King; which stopp'd","our mouths, sir.\" I fear he will indeed. Well, let him have them:","He will have all, I think."],"id":259,"m":"\"My lord, the horses your lordship sent for were, with all possible care, well chosen, broken in and equipped. They were young and handsome, of the best breed in the north. When they were ready to set out for London, a man of my Lord Cardinal's took them from me by warrant and sheer force, with this reason: his master would be served before a subject, if not before the King. That shut our mouths, sir.\" I'm afraid he will be, indeed. Well, let him have them; he'll have everything, I think."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter to the Lord Chamberlain the Dukes of Norfolk and Suffolk.","id":260},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Well met, my Lord Chamberlain."],"id":261,"m":"Well met, my Lord Chamberlain."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Good day to both your Graces."],"id":262,"m":"Good day to both your Graces."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["How is the King employ'd?"],"id":263,"m":"What is the King doing?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["I left him private,","Full of sad thoughts and troubles."],"id":264,"m":"I left him alone, full of sad thoughts and troubles."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["What's the cause?"],"id":265,"m":"What's the cause?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["It seems the marriage with his brother's wife","Has crept too near his conscience."],"id":266,"m":"It seems his marriage to his brother's widow has come too close to his conscience."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["No, his conscience","Has crept too near another lady."],"id":267,"m":"No, his conscience has come too close to another lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["'Tis so.","This is the Cardinal's doing, the king-cardinal.","That blind priest, like the eldest son of Fortune,","Turns what he list. The King will know him one day."],"id":268,"m":"That's it. This is the Cardinal's doing, the king-cardinal. That blind priest, like Fortune's eldest son, turns things whichever way he likes. The King will see him for what he is one day."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Pray God he do! he'll never know himself else."],"id":269,"m":"God grant he does! Otherwise he'll never know himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["How holily he works in all his business!","And with what zeal! for, now he has crack'd the league","Between us and the Emperor, the Queen's great nephew,","He dives into the King's soul, and there scatters","Dangers, doubts, wringing of the conscience,","Fears, and despairs; and all these for his marriage.","And out of all these to restore the King,","He counsels a divorce; a loss of her","That, like a jewel, has hung twenty years","About his neck, yet never lost her lustre;","Of her that loves him with that excellence","That angels love good men with; even of her","That, when the greatest stroke of fortune falls,","Will bless the King. And is not this course pious?"],"id":270,"m":"How holy he is in all his business, and how zealous! Now that he has broken the alliance between us and the Emperor, the Queen's great nephew, he dives into the King's soul and scatters there dangers, doubts, pangs of conscience, fears and despair, all about his marriage. And to rescue the King from all of these, he advises a divorce: losing her who has hung like a jewel round his neck for twenty years without ever losing her shine; her who loves him with the perfect love angels have for good men; her who, even when the heaviest blow of fortune falls, will bless the King. Isn't this a pious course?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Heaven keep me from such counsel! 'Tis most true","These news are everywhere; every tongue speaks 'em,","And every true heart weeps for't. All that dare","Look into these affairs see this main end,","The French king's sister. Heaven will one day open","The King's eyes, that so long have slept upon","This bold bad man."],"id":271,"m":"Heaven keep me from such advice! It's quite true this news is everywhere; every tongue speaks of it and every honest heart weeps for it. Everyone who dares look into these affairs sees the main aim: the French king's sister. Heaven will one day open the King's eyes, which have slept so long on this bold, bad man."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["And free us from his slavery."],"id":272,"m":"And free us from slavery to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["We had need pray,","And heartily, for our deliverance;","Or this imperious man will work us all","From princes into pages. All men's honours","Lie like one lump before him, to be fashion'd","Into what pitch he please."],"id":273,"m":"We need to pray, and heartily, to be delivered; or this domineering man will turn us all from princes into pages. All men's honours lie before him like one lump of clay, to be shaped to whatever level he likes."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["For me, my lords,","I love him not, nor fear him; there's my creed.","As I am made without him, so I'll stand,","If the King please; his curses and his blessings","Touch me alike, they'are breath I not believe in.","I knew him, and I know him; so I leave him","To him that made him proud, the Pope."],"id":274,"m":"As for me, my lords, I neither love nor fear him; that's my creed. Just as I was made without him, so I'll stand, if the King pleases. His curses and his blessings touch me equally; they're breath I don't believe in. I knew him and I know him; so I leave him to the one who made him proud, the Pope."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Let's in;","And with some other business put the King","From these sad thoughts, that work too much upon him.","My lord, you'll bear us company?"],"id":275,"m":"Let's go in, and with some other business distract the King from these sad thoughts that weigh on him too much. My lord, will you come with us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Excuse me,","The King has sent me otherwhere. Besides,","You'll find a most unfit time to disturb him.","Health to your lordships!"],"id":276,"m":"Excuse me, the King has sent me elsewhere. Besides, you'll find it a very bad time to disturb him. Good health to your lordships!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Thanks, my good Lord Chamberlain."],"id":277,"m":"Thanks, my good Lord Chamberlain."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Lord Chamberlain; Norfolk draws the curtain, and discovers the King reading pensively.","id":278},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["How sad he looks! Sure, he is much afflicted."],"id":279,"m":"How sad he looks! He must be very troubled."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Who's there, ha?"],"id":280,"m":"Who's there, ha?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Pray God he be not angry."],"id":281,"m":"God grant he isn't angry."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Who's there, I say? How dare you thrust yourselves","Into my private meditations?","Who am I? ha?"],"id":282,"m":"Who's there, I say? How dare you push yourselves into my private thoughts? Who do you think I am? Ha?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["A gracious king that pardons all offences","Malice ne'er meant. Our breach of duty this way","Is business of estate; in which we come","To know your royal pleasure."],"id":283,"m":"A gracious king who pardons all offences that were not meant in malice. We have broken our duty this way on state business; we come to learn your royal wishes about it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Ye are too bold.","Go to; I'll make ye know your times of business.","Is this an hour for temporal affairs, ha?"],"id":284,"m":"You are too bold. Go on; I'll teach you when the right times for business are. Is this an hour for worldly affairs, ha?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Wolsey and Campeius, with a commission.","id":285},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","cont":1,"lines":["Who's there? My good Lord Cardinal? O my Wolsey,","The quiet of my wounded conscience,","Thou art a cure fit for a King. [To Campeius] You're welcome,","Most learned reverend sir, into our kingdom;","Use us and it. [To Wolsey] My good lord, have great care","I be not found a talker."],"id":286,"m":"Who's there? My good Lord Cardinal? Oh my Wolsey, the comfort of my wounded conscience, you are a cure fit for a king. (To Campeius) You are welcome to our kingdom, most learned reverend sir; make use of us and it. (To Wolsey) My good lord, take great care that I am not found to be all talk."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Sir, you cannot.","I would your Grace would give us but an hour","Of private conference."],"id":287,"m":"Sir, you cannot be. I wish your Grace would give us just an hour of private conversation."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["[To Norfolk and Suffolk] We are busy; go."],"id":288,"m":"(To Norfolk and Suffolk) We are busy; go."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["[Aside to Suffolk] This priest has no pride in him?"],"id":289,"m":"(Aside to Suffolk) This priest has no pride in him, does he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["[Aside to Norfolk] Not to speak of.","I would not be so sick, though, for his place.","But this cannot continue."],"id":290,"m":"(Aside to Norfolk) None worth mentioning. I wouldn't be so sick with pride as he is, though, to have his position. But this can't go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["[Aside to Suffolk] If it do,","I'll venture one have-at-him."],"id":291,"m":"(Aside to Suffolk) If it does, I'll take a swing at him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["[Aside to Norfolk] I another."],"id":292,"m":"(Aside to Norfolk) And I another."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Norfolk and Suffolk.","id":293},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Your Grace has given a precedent of wisdom","Above all princes, in committing freely","Your scruple to the voice of Christendom.","Who can be angry now? What envy reach you?","The Spaniard, tied by blood and favour to her,","Must now confess, if they have any goodness,","The trial just and noble. All the clerks,","I mean the learned ones, in Christian kingdoms","Have their free voices. Rome, the nurse of judgement,","Invited by your noble self, hath sent","One general tongue unto us, this good man,","This just and learned priest, Cardinal Campeius,","Whom once more I present unto your Highness."],"id":294,"m":"Your Grace has given an example of wisdom above all princes by freely submitting your doubt to the judgement of all Christendom. Who can be angry now? What malice can reach you? The Spanish, tied to her by blood and favour, must now admit, if they have any goodness, that the trial is just and noble. All the clergy, I mean the learned ones, in Christian kingdoms are free to give their opinions. Rome, the nurse of judgement, invited by your noble self, has sent us one voice to speak for all, this good man, this just and learned priest, Cardinal Campeius, whom I present to your Highness once more."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["And once more in mine arms I bid him welcome,","And thank the holy conclave for their loves.","They have sent me such a man I would have wish'd for."],"id":295,"m":"And once more I welcome him in my arms, and thank the holy college of cardinals for their love. They have sent me just the man I would have wished for."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMPEIUS","lines":["Your Grace must needs deserve all strangers' loves,","You are so noble. To your Highness' hand","I tender my commission; by whose virtue,","The court of Rome commanding—you, my Lord","Cardinal of York, are join'd with me their servant","In the unpartial judging of this business."],"id":296,"m":"Your Grace must deserve the love of all foreigners, you are so noble. I hand my commission to your Highness; by its authority, at the command of the court of Rome, you, my Lord Cardinal of York, are joined with me, their servant, in judging this business impartially."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Two equal men. The Queen shall be acquainted","Forthwith for what you come. Where's Gardiner?"],"id":297,"m":"Two fair-minded men. The Queen shall be told at once why you have come. Where's Gardiner?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["I know your Majesty has always lov'd her","So dear in heart not to deny her that","A woman of less place might ask by law,","Scholars allow'd freely to argue for her."],"id":298,"m":"I know your Majesty has always loved her so dearly that you won't deny her what a woman of lower rank could claim by law: scholars allowed to argue freely on her behalf."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Ay, and the best she shall have; and my favour","To him that does best; God forbid else. Cardinal,","Prithee, call Gardiner to me, my new secretary.","I find him a fit fellow."],"id":299,"m":"Yes, and she shall have the best, and my favour to whoever does best; God forbid otherwise. Cardinal, please call Gardiner to me, my new secretary. I find him a fit fellow."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Wolsey.","id":300},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Wolsey, with Gardiner.","id":301},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["[Aside to Gardiner] Give me your hand. Much joy and favour to you;","You are the King's now."],"id":302,"m":"(Aside to Gardiner) Give me your hand. Much joy and favour to you; you are the King's man now."},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["[Aside to Wolsey] But to be commanded","For ever by your Grace, whose hand has rais'd me."],"id":303,"m":"(Aside to Wolsey) But always to be commanded by your Grace, whose hand has raised me."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Come hither, Gardiner."],"id":304,"m":"Come here, Gardiner."},{"t":"sd","text":"Walks and whispers.","id":305},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMPEIUS","lines":["My Lord of York, was not one Doctor Pace","In this man's place before him?"],"id":306,"m":"My Lord of York, wasn't a Doctor Pace in this man's position before him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Yes, he was."],"id":307,"m":"Yes, he was."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMPEIUS","lines":["Was he not held a learned man?"],"id":308,"m":"Wasn't he thought a learned man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Yes, surely."],"id":309,"m":"Yes, certainly."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMPEIUS","lines":["Believe me, there's an ill opinion spread then","Even of yourself, Lord Cardinal."],"id":310,"m":"Believe me, then, there's a bad opinion going around about you, Lord Cardinal."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["How! of me?"],"id":311,"m":"What! About me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMPEIUS","lines":["They will not stick to say you envi'd him,","And fearing he would rise, he was so virtuous,","Kept him a foreign man still; which so griev'd him","That he ran mad and died."],"id":312,"m":"They don't hesitate to say you envied him, and, fearing he would rise because he was so virtuous, kept him always abroad on foreign missions, which grieved him so much that he went mad and died."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Heav'n's peace be with him!","That's Christian care enough. For living murmurers","There's places of rebuke. He was a fool,","For he would needs be virtuous. That good fellow,","If I command him, follows my appointment;","I will have none so near else. Learn this, brother,","We live not to be grip'd by meaner persons."],"id":313,"m":"Heaven's peace be with him! That's enough Christian charity. For living grumblers there are places of punishment. He was a fool, because he insisted on being virtuous. That good fellow there follows my orders when I command him; I'll have no one else so close to the King. Learn this, brother: we don't live to be pushed around by lesser people."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Deliver this with modesty to the Queen."],"id":314,"m":"Give this to the Queen with tact."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Gardiner.","id":315},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","cont":1,"lines":["The most convenient place that I can think of","For such receipt of learning is Black-Friars;","There ye shall meet about this weighty business.","My Wolsey, see it furnish'd. O, my lord,","Would it not grieve an able man to leave","So sweet a bedfellow? But, conscience, conscience!","O, 'tis a tender place; and I must leave her."],"id":316,"m":"The most suitable place I can think of to receive such learned men is Blackfriars; you shall meet there about this weighty business. My Wolsey, see it is made ready. Oh, my lord, wouldn't it grieve any capable man to leave so sweet a bedfellow? But conscience, conscience! Oh, it's a tender spot, and I must leave her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":317},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"An ante-chamber of the Queen's apartments.","id":318},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Anne Bullen and an Old Lady.","id":319},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE BULLEN","lines":["Not for that neither. Here's the pang that pinches:","His Highness having liv'd so long with her, and she","So good a lady that no tongue could ever","Pronounce dishonour of her,—by my life,","She never knew harm-doing—O, now, after","So many courses of the sun enthroned,","Still growing in a majesty and pomp, the which","To leave a thousand-fold more bitter than","'Tis sweet at first to acquire,—after this process,","To give her the avaunt, it is a pity","Would move a monster."],"id":320,"m":"Not for that either. Here's the pain that pinches: his Highness has lived so long with her, and she is so good a lady that no tongue could ever speak of dishonour in her; on my life, she never knew how to do harm. Now, after so many years on the throne, always growing in majesty and splendour, which are a thousand times more bitter to lose than they are sweet to gain at first, after all this, to send her packing is a pity that would move a monster."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD LADY","lines":["Hearts of most hard temper","Melt and lament for her."],"id":321,"m":"The hardest hearts melt and grieve for her."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE BULLEN","lines":["O, God's will, much better","She ne'er had known pomp! Though't be temporal,","Yet, if that quarrel, fortune, do divorce","It from the bearer, 'tis a sufferance panging","As soul and body's severing."],"id":322,"m":"Oh, God's will, it would have been much better if she'd never known greatness! Though it's only worldly, if fortune, that quarrelsome thing, divorces it from the one who has it, it is as painful a suffering as the soul's separation from the body."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD LADY","lines":["Alas, poor lady!","She's a stranger now again."],"id":323,"m":"Alas, poor lady! She is a foreigner again now."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE BULLEN","lines":["So much the more","Must pity drop upon her. Verily,","I swear, 'tis better to be lowly born","And range with humble livers in content,","Than to be perk'd up in a glist'ring grief,","And wear a golden sorrow."],"id":324,"m":"All the more reason for pity to fall on her. Truly, I swear it's better to be born humble and live contentedly among humble people than to be perched up in glittering grief and wear a golden sorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD LADY","lines":["Our content","Is our best having."],"id":325,"m":"Contentment is our best possession."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE BULLEN","lines":["By my troth and maidenhead,","I would not be a queen."],"id":326,"m":"By my faith and my virginity, I wouldn't be a queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD LADY","lines":["Beshrew me, I would,","And venture maidenhead for't; and so would you,","For all this spice of your hypocrisy.","You, that have so fair parts of woman on you,","Have too a woman's heart, which ever yet","Affected eminence, wealth, sovereignty;","Which, to say sooth, are blessings; and which gifts,","Saving your mincing, the capacity","Of your soft cheveril conscience would receive,","If you might please to stretch it."],"id":327,"m":"Curse me, I would, and risk my virginity for it; and so would you, for all this flavour of hypocrisy. You, who have such lovely woman's features, have a woman's heart too, which has always wanted high rank, wealth and power; which, to tell the truth, are blessings; and, for all your primness, your soft, stretchy kid-leather conscience would take in those gifts if you cared to stretch it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE BULLEN","lines":["Nay, good troth."],"id":328,"m":"No, truly."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD LADY","lines":["Yes, troth and troth. You would not be a queen?"],"id":329,"m":"Yes, truly, truly. You wouldn't be a queen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE BULLEN","lines":["No, not for all the riches under heaven."],"id":330,"m":"No, not for all the riches under heaven."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD LADY","lines":["'Tis strange. A three-pence bow'd would hire me,","Old as I am, to queen it. But, I pray you,","What think you of a duchess? Have you limbs","To bear that load of title?"],"id":331,"m":"That's strange. A bent threepenny bit would hire me, old as I am, to play the queen. But tell me, what do you think of being a duchess? Do you have the limbs to carry that load of title?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE BULLEN","lines":["No, in truth."],"id":332,"m":"No, honestly."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD LADY","lines":["Then you are weakly made; pluck off a little.","I would not be a young count in your way,","For more than blushing comes to. If your back","Cannot vouchsafe this burden, 'tis too weak","Ever to get a boy."],"id":333,"m":"Then you're weakly built; take a little off. I wouldn't want to be a young earl coming your way, for more than a blush is worth. If your back can't carry this burden, it's too weak ever to get a boy."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE BULLEN","lines":["How you do talk!","I swear again I would not be a queen","For all the world."],"id":334,"m":"How you talk! I swear again I wouldn't be a queen for the whole world."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD LADY","lines":["In faith, for little England","You'd venture an emballing. I myself","Would for Carnarvonshire, although there long'd","No more to the crown but that. Lo, who comes here?"],"id":335,"m":"Honestly, for little England you'd risk being given the orb. I would myself for Carnarvonshire, even if nothing else belonged to the crown. Look, who's coming?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Lord Chamberlain.","id":336},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Good morrow, ladies. What were't worth to know","The secret of your conference?"],"id":337,"m":"Good morning, ladies. What would it cost to know the secret of your conversation?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE BULLEN","lines":["My good lord,","Not your demand; it values not your asking.","Our mistress' sorrows we were pitying."],"id":338,"m":"My good lord, not even the effort of asking; it isn't worth your question. We were pitying our mistress's sorrows."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["It was a gentle business, and becoming","The action of good women. There is hope","All will be well."],"id":339,"m":"That was a kind occupation, and fitting for good women. There is hope that all will be well."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE BULLEN","lines":["Now, I pray God, amen!"],"id":340,"m":"I pray God it will, amen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["You bear a gentle mind, and heavenly blessings","Follow such creatures. That you may, fair lady,","Perceive I speak sincerely, and high note's","Ta'en of your many virtues, the King's Majesty","Commends his good opinion of you, and","Does purpose honour to you no less flowing","Than Marchioness of Pembroke; to which title","A thousand pound a year, annual support,","Out of his grace he adds."],"id":341,"m":"You have a kind heart, and heavenly blessings follow such people. So that you may see I'm sincere, fair lady, and that high notice has been taken of your many virtues: the King's Majesty sends his good opinion of you and intends to give you honour no less than the title of Marchioness of Pembroke, to which title, out of his grace, he adds a thousand pounds a year to support it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE BULLEN","lines":["I do not know","What kind of my obedience I should tender.","More than my all is nothing; nor my prayers","Are not words duly hallowed, nor my wishes","More worth than empty vanities; yet prayers and wishes","Are all I can return. Beseech your lordship,","Vouchsafe to speak my thanks and my obedience,","As from a blushing handmaid, to his Highness;","Whose health and royalty I pray for."],"id":342,"m":"I don't know what kind of obedience I should offer. Even more than everything I have would be nothing; my prayers are not properly blessed words, and my wishes are worth no more than empty vanities; yet prayers and wishes are all I can give back. I beg your lordship to give my thanks and obedience to his Highness, as from a blushing servant, whose health and royal state I pray for."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Lady,","I shall not fail to approve the fair conceit","The King hath of you. [Aside] I have perus'd her well.","Beauty and honour in her are so mingled","That they have caught the King; and who knows yet","But from this lady may proceed a gem","To lighten all this isle? I'll to the King,","And say I spoke with you."],"id":343,"m":"Lady, I shall be sure to confirm the good opinion the King has of you. (Aside) I've studied her well. Beauty and honour are so mixed in her that they have captured the King; and who knows whether from this lady may come a jewel to light up all this island? (To Anne) I'll go to the King and say I spoke with you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Lord Chamberlain.","id":344},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE BULLEN","lines":["My honour'd lord."],"id":345,"m":"My honoured lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD LADY","lines":["Why, this it is: see, see!","I have been begging sixteen years in court,","Am yet a courtier beggarly, nor could","Come pat betwixt too early and too late","For any suit of pounds; and you, O fate!","A very fresh-fish here—fie, fie, fie upon","This compell'd fortune!—have your mouth fill'd up","Before you open it."],"id":346,"m":"Why, that's how it is: look, look! I've been begging at court for sixteen years and am still a beggarly courtier, and I could never hit the right moment between too early and too late for any money request; and you, oh fate, a brand-new fish here, damn this forced fortune, have your mouth filled before you open it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE BULLEN","lines":["This is strange to me."],"id":347,"m":"This is strange to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD LADY","lines":["How tastes it? Is it bitter? Forty pence, no.","There was a lady once, 'tis an old story,","That would not be a queen, that would she not,","For all the mud in Egypt. Have you heard it?"],"id":348,"m":"How does it taste? Is it bitter? I bet forty pence it isn't. There was once a lady, it's an old story, who would not be a queen, she would not, for all the mud in Egypt. Have you heard it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE BULLEN","lines":["Come, you are pleasant."],"id":349,"m":"Come, you're joking."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD LADY","lines":["With your theme, I could","O'ermount the lark. The Marchioness of Pembroke!","A thousand pounds a year for pure respect!","No other obligation! By my life,","That promises moe thousands; Honour's train","Is longer than his foreskirt. By this time","I know your back will bear a duchess. Say,","Are you not stronger than you were?"],"id":350,"m":"With your subject I could fly higher than the lark. Marchioness of Pembroke! A thousand pounds a year just as a mark of respect! No other obligation! On my life, that promises more thousands; honour's train is longer than its front hem. By now I know your back can carry a duchess. Tell me, aren't you stronger than you were?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE BULLEN","lines":["Good lady,","Make yourself mirth with your particular fancy,","And leave me out on't. Would I had no being,","If this salute my blood a jot. It faints me,","To think what follows.","The Queen is comfortless, and we forgetful","In our long absence. Pray, do not deliver","What here you've heard to her."],"id":351,"m":"Good lady, amuse yourself with your own fancies and leave me out of them. I wish I didn't exist if this stirs my blood one bit. It makes me faint to think what comes next. The Queen is without comfort, and we are neglectful in being away so long. Please don't tell her what you've heard here."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD LADY","lines":["What do you think me?"],"id":352,"m":"What do you take me for?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":353},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"IV","loc":"A hall in Black-Friars.","id":354},{"t":"sd","text":"Trumpets, sennet, and cornets. Enter two Vergers, with short silver wands; next them, two Scribes, in the habit of doctors; after them, the Archbishop of Canterbury alone; after him, the Bishops of Lincoln, Ely, Rochester, and Saint Asaph; next them, with some small distance, follows a Gentleman bearing the purse, with the great seal, and a cardinal's hat; then two Priests, bearing each silver cross; then a Gentleman Usher bareheaded, accompanied with a Sergeant-at-arms bearing a silver mace; then two Gentlemen bearing two great silver pillars; after them, side by side, the two Cardinals; two Noblemen with the sword and mace. The King takes place under the cloth of state; the two Cardinals sit under him as judges. The Queen takes place some distance from the King. The Bishops place themselves on each side the court, in manner of consistory; below them, the Scribes. The Lords sit next the Bishops. The rest of the Attendants stand in convenient order about the stage.","id":355},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Whilst our commission from Rome is read,","Let silence be commanded."],"id":356,"m":"While our commission from Rome is read, let silence be ordered."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["What's the need?","It hath already publicly been read,","And on all sides the authority allow'd;","You may, then, spare that time."],"id":357,"m":"What need is there? It has already been read publicly, and its authority accepted by all sides; so you may save that time."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Be't so. Proceed."],"id":358,"m":"So be it. Proceed."},{"t":"sp","who":"SCRIBE","lines":["Say, Henry King of England, come into the court."],"id":359,"m":"Say, \"Henry, King of England, come into the court.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"CRIER","lines":["Henry King of England, etc."],"id":360,"m":"Henry, King of England, and so on."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Here."],"id":361,"m":"Here."},{"t":"sp","who":"SCRIBE","lines":["Say, Katherine Queen of England, come into the court."],"id":362,"m":"Say, \"Katherine, Queen of England, come into the court.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"CRIER","lines":["Katherine Queen of England, etc."],"id":363,"m":"Katherine, Queen of England, and so on."},{"t":"sd","text":"The Queen makes no answer, rises out of her chair, goes about the court, comes to the King, and kneels at his feet; then speaks.","id":364},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Sir, I desire you do me right and justice,","And to bestow your pity on me; for","I am a most poor woman, and a stranger,","Born out of your dominions, having here","No judge indifferent, nor no more assurance","Of equal friendship and proceeding. Alas, sir,","In what have I offended you? What cause","Hath my behaviour given to your displeasure,","That thus you should proceed to put me off","And take your good grace from me? Heaven witness,","I have been to you a true and humble wife,","At all times to your will conformable;","Ever in fear to kindle your dislike,","Yea, subject to your countenance, glad or sorry","As I saw it inclin'd. When was the hour","I ever contradicted your desire,","Or made it not mine too? Or which of your friends","Have I not strove to love, although I knew","He were mine enemy? What friend of mine","That had to him deriv'd your anger, did I","Continue in my liking? nay, gave notice","He was from thence discharg'd? Sir, call to mind","That I have been your wife in this obedience","Upward of twenty years, and have been blest","With many children by you. If, in the course","And process of this time, you can report,","And prove it too, against mine honour aught,","My bond to wedlock, or my love and duty,","Against your sacred person, in God's name,","Turn me away; and let the foul'st contempt","Shut door upon me, and so give me up","To the sharp'st kind of justice. Please you, sir,","The King, your father, was reputed for","A prince most prudent, of an excellent","And unmatch'd wit and judgment; Ferdinand,","My father, King of Spain, was reckon'd one","The wisest prince that there had reign'd by many","A year before; it is not to be question'd","That they had gather'd a wise council to them","Of every realm, that did debate this business,","Who deem'd our marriage lawful; wherefore I humbly","Beseech you, sir, to spare me till I may","Be by my friends in Spain advis'd, whose counsel","I will implore. If not, i' the name of God,","Your pleasure be fulfill'd!"],"id":365,"m":"Sir, I ask you to do me right and justice and to take pity on me, for I am a very poor woman and a foreigner, born outside your kingdom, with no impartial judge here and no assurance of fair friendship or fair proceedings. Alas, sir, how have I offended you? What cause has my behaviour given for your displeasure, that you should proceed like this to cast me off and take your good grace from me? Heaven be my witness, I have been a true and humble wife to you, always obedient to your will, always afraid of provoking your dislike, yes, following your expression, glad or sorry as I saw it turn. When did I ever contradict your wishes, or not make them mine too? Which of your friends have I not tried to love, even knowing he was my enemy? Which friend of mine who had earned your anger did I go on liking? No, I let him know he was dismissed. Sir, remember that I have been your wife in this obedience for more than twenty years, and have been blessed with many children by you. If in all that time you can report and prove anything against my honour, my marriage vows, or my love and duty to your sacred person, then in God's name turn me away, let the foulest contempt shut the door on me, and hand me over to the harshest justice. If you please, sir, the King your father was known as a most prudent prince, of excellent, unmatched intelligence and judgement; my father Ferdinand, King of Spain, was thought the wisest prince who had reigned for many years before. There is no question that they gathered wise councillors from each kingdom to debate this business, and these judged our marriage lawful. So I humbly beg you, sir, to spare me until I can be advised by my friends in Spain, whose counsel I will beg for. If not, in God's name, let your will be done!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["You have here, lady,","And of your choice, these reverend fathers; men","Of singular integrity and learning,","Yea, the elect o' the land, who are assembled","To plead your cause. It shall be therefore bootless","That longer you desire the court; as well","For your own quiet, as to rectify","What is unsettled in the King."],"id":366,"m":"Lady, you have here, of your own choosing, these reverend fathers, men of rare integrity and learning, the pick of the land, gathered to plead your cause. So it is useless for you to ask the court for more delay, both for your own peace and to settle what is unsettled in the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMPEIUS","lines":["His Grace","Hath spoken well and justly; therefore, madam,","It's fit this royal session do proceed,","And that, without delay, their arguments","Be now produc'd and heard."],"id":367,"m":"His Grace has spoken well and justly; so, madam, it is right for this royal session to go on, and for their arguments to be presented and heard without delay."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Lord Cardinal,","To you I speak."],"id":368,"m":"Lord Cardinal, I am speaking to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Your pleasure, madam?"],"id":369,"m":"What is your wish, madam?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Sir,","I am about to weep; but, thinking that","We are a queen, or long have dream'd so, certain","The daughter of a king, my drops of tears","I'll turn to sparks of fire."],"id":370,"m":"Sir, I am about to weep; but remembering that I am a queen, or have long dreamed I was, and am certainly a king's daughter, I'll turn my tears into sparks of fire."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Be patient yet."],"id":371,"m":"Be patient still."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["I will, when you are humble; nay, before,","Or God will punish me. I do believe,","Induced by potent circumstances, that","You are mine enemy, and make my challenge","You shall not be my judge; for it is you","Have blown this coal betwixt my lord and me,","Which God's dew quench! Therefore I say again,","I utterly abhor, yea, from my soul","Refuse you for my judge; whom, yet once more,","I hold my most malicious foe, and think not","At all a friend to truth."],"id":372,"m":"I will, when you are humble; no, even before, or God will punish me. I believe, persuaded by strong evidence, that you are my enemy, and I object that you shall not be my judge, for it is you who have blown on this coal between my lord and me; may God's dew put it out! So I say again: I utterly loathe you, and from my soul I refuse you as my judge, you whom once more I consider my most malicious enemy and no friend at all to truth."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["I do profess","You speak not like yourself, who ever yet","Have stood to charity and display'd the effects","Of disposition gentle, and of wisdom","O'ertopping woman's pow'r. Madam, you do me wrong.","I have no spleen against you, nor injustice","For you or any. How far I have proceeded,","Or how far further shall, is warranted","By a commission from the consistory,","Yea, the whole consistory of Rome. You charge me","That I have blown this coal. I do deny it.","The King is present: if it be known to him","That I gainsay my deed, how may he wound,","And worthily, my falsehood! yea, as much","As you have done my truth. If he know","That I am free of your report, he knows","I am not of your wrong. Therefore in him","It lies to cure me; and the cure is, to","Remove these thoughts from you; the which before","His Highness shall speak in, I do beseech","You, gracious madam, to unthink your speaking","And to say so no more."],"id":373,"m":"I declare you are not speaking like yourself; you have always been charitable and shown a gentle nature and a wisdom beyond a woman's. Madam, you wrong me. I have no malice against you, and intend no injustice to you or anyone. How far I have gone, and how much further I shall go, is authorised by a commission from the papal court, the whole court of Rome. You accuse me of blowing on this coal. I deny it. The King is here. If he knows that I am denying what I did, how rightly he could punish my falsehood, as much as you have wounded my truth! If he knows I am innocent of what you say, he knows I am not guilty of wronging you. So it is in his power to cure me, and the cure is to remove these thoughts from you. Before his Highness speaks on this, I beg you, gracious madam, to take back what you said and say it no more."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["My lord, my lord,","I am a simple woman, much too weak","To oppose your cunning. You're meek and humble-mouth'd;","You sign your place and calling, in full seeming,","With meekness and humility; but your heart","Is cramm'd with arrogancy, spleen, and pride.","You have, by fortune and his Highness' favours,","Gone slightly o'er low steps and now are mounted","Where powers are your retainers, and your words,","Domestics to you, serve your will as 't please","Yourself pronounce their office. I must tell you,","You tender more your person's honour than","Your high profession spiritual; that again","I do refuse you for my judge; and here,","Before you all, appeal unto the Pope,","To bring my whole cause 'fore his Holiness,","And to be judg'd by him."],"id":374,"m":"My lord, my lord, I am a simple woman, far too weak to stand against your cunning. You are mild and humble in speech; you mark your position and calling, to all appearances, with meekness and humility; but your heart is crammed with arrogance, malice and pride. Through luck and his Highness's favours you have skipped lightly over the low steps and now sit so high that powerful men are your servants, and your words, like household servants, do whatever you tell them. I must tell you, you care more for your own honour than for your high spiritual calling. So again I refuse you as my judge, and here, before you all, I appeal to the Pope, to bring my whole case before his Holiness and be judged by him."},{"t":"sd","text":"She curtsies to the King, and offers to depart.","id":375},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMPEIUS","lines":["The Queen is obstinate,","Stubborn to justice, apt to accuse it, and","Disdainful to be tried by't; 'tis not well.","She's going away."],"id":376,"m":"The Queen is obstinate, stubborn against justice, quick to accuse it and too proud to be tried by it; that is not good. She is leaving."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Call her again."],"id":377,"m":"Call her back."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRIER","lines":["Katherine Queen of England, come into the court."],"id":378,"m":"Katherine, Queen of England, come into the court."},{"t":"sp","who":"GENTLEMAN USHER","lines":["Madam, you are call'd back."],"id":379,"m":"Madam, you are being called back."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["What need you note it? Pray you keep your way;","When you are call'd, return. Now, the Lord help!","They vex me past my patience. Pray you, pass on.","I will not tarry; no, nor ever more","Upon this business my appearance make","In any of their courts."],"id":380,"m":"Why do you need to notice it? Please go on your way; return when you are called. Now, Lord help me! They provoke me past my patience. Please, go on. I will not stay; no, nor will I ever appear in any of their courts on this business again."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Queen, and her Attendants.","id":381},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Go thy ways, Kate.","That man i' the world who shall report he has","A better wife, let him in nought be trusted,","For speaking false in that. Thou art, alone,","If thy rare qualities, sweet gentleness,","Thy meekness saint-like, wife-like government,","Obeying in commanding, and thy parts","Sovereign and pious else, could speak thee out,","The queen of earthly queens. She's noble born;","And, like her true nobility, she has","Carried herself towards me."],"id":382,"m":"Go your way, Kate. Any man in the world who claims he has a better wife should not be trusted in anything, for lying about that. If your rare qualities, your sweet gentleness, your saintly meekness, your wifely self-command, obedient while commanding, and your other royal and devout qualities could speak for you, you alone would be the queen of earthly queens. She is nobly born, and she has behaved toward me with true nobility."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Most gracious sir,","In humblest manner I require your Highness,","That it shall please you to declare, in hearing","Of all these ears,—for, where I am robb'd and bound,","There must I be unloos'd, although not there","At once and fully satisfied,—whether ever I","Did broach this business to your Highness, or","Laid any scruple in your way, which might","Induce you to the question on't? or ever","Have to you, but with thanks to God for such","A royal lady, spake one the least word that might","Be to the prejudice of her present state,","Or touch of her good person?"],"id":383,"m":"Most gracious sir, I humbly ask your Highness to declare in the hearing of everyone here, for where I was robbed and bound I must be set free there, though not at once fully compensated, whether I ever raised this business with your Highness or put any doubt in your way that might lead you to question your marriage; or whether I ever said to you, except to thank God for so royal a lady, the smallest word that might harm her present position or touch her good person."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["My Lord Cardinal,","I do excuse you; yea, upon mine honour,","I free you from't. You are not to be taught","That you have many enemies, that know not","Why they are so, but, like to village-curs,","Bark when their fellows do: by some of these","The Queen is put in anger. You're excus'd;","But will you be more justifi'd? You ever","Have wish'd the sleeping of this business; never desir'd","It to be stirr'd; but oft have hind'red, oft,","The passages made toward it. On my honour,","I speak my good Lord Cardinal to this point,","And thus far clear him. Now, what mov'd me to't,","I will be bold with time and your attention:","Then mark the inducement. Thus it came; give heed to't:","My conscience first receiv'd a tenderness,","Scruple, and prick, on certain speeches utter'd","By the Bishop of Bayonne, then French ambassador;","Who had been hither sent on the debating","A marriage 'twixt the Duke of Orleans and","Our daughter Mary. I' the progress of this business,","Ere a determinate resolution, he,","I mean the Bishop, did require a respite;","Wherein he might the King his lord advertise","Whether our daughter were legitimate,","Respecting this our marriage with the dowager,","Sometimes our brother's wife. This respite shook","The bosom of my conscience, enter'd me,","Yea, with a splitting power, and made to tremble","The region of my breast; which forc'd such way,","That many maz'd considerings did throng","And press'd in with this caution. First, methought","I stood not in the smile of Heaven; who had","Commanded nature, that my lady's womb,","If it conceiv'd a male child by me, should","Do no more offices of life to't than","The grave does to the dead; for her male issue","Or died where they were made, or shortly after","This world had air'd them. Hence I took a thought","This was a judgement on me; that my kingdom,","Well worthy the best heir o' the world, should not","Be gladded in't by me. Then follows, that","I weigh'd the danger which my realms stood in","By this my issue's fail; and that gave to me","Many a groaning throe. Thus hulling in","The wild sea of my conscience, I did steer","Toward this remedy, whereupon we are","Now present here together; that's to say,","I meant to rectify my conscience,—which","I then did feel full sick, and yet not well,—","By all the reverend fathers of the land","And doctors learn'd. First I began in private","With you, my Lord of Lincoln. You remember","How under my oppression I did reek,","When I first mov'd you."],"id":384,"m":"My Lord Cardinal, I excuse you; yes, on my honour, I clear you of it. You don't need telling that you have many enemies who don't know why they are your enemies, but like village dogs bark when the others do; some of these have made the Queen angry. You are excused. But would you like to be cleared further? You have always wished this business to stay asleep, never wanted it stirred, but often, often blocked the moves toward it. On my honour, I speak for my good Lord Cardinal on this point and so far clear him. Now, as to what moved me to it, I'll take some time and your attention; so mark the cause. It came like this; listen. My conscience first felt a tenderness, a doubt and a prick, at certain words spoken by the Bishop of Bayonne, then the French ambassador, who had been sent here to negotiate a marriage between the Duke of Orleans and our daughter Mary. In the course of this business, before a firm decision, the Bishop asked for a delay in which he could tell the King his master whether our daughter was legitimate, considering our marriage to the widow who was once our brother's wife. This delay shook my conscience to its heart, entered me with a splitting force and made my whole breast tremble; it forced such a way in that many bewildered thoughts crowded in after this warning. First, I thought I was not in Heaven's favour, which had ordered nature that if my lady's womb conceived a male child by me it should do no more to keep it alive than the grave does for the dead; for her sons either died in the womb or shortly after this world had given them air. So I thought this was a judgement on me: that my kingdom, worthy of the best heir in the world, should not be made glad by one from me. Then I considered the danger my kingdoms were in through my lack of an heir, and that gave me many groaning pangs. So, drifting on the wild sea of my conscience, I steered toward this remedy, which is why we are here together; that is, I meant to set right my conscience, which I then felt was very sick and still is not well, with the help of all the reverend fathers of the land and learned doctors. I began first in private with you, my Lord of Lincoln. You remember how I sweated under my burden when I first raised it with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LINCOLN","lines":["Very well, my liege."],"id":385,"m":"Very well, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I have spoke long; be pleas'd yourself to say","How far you satisfi'd me."],"id":386,"m":"I have spoken long; please say yourself how far you satisfied me."},{"t":"sp","who":"LINCOLN","lines":["So please your Highness,","The question did at first so stagger me,","Bearing a state of mighty moment in't","And consequence of dread, that I committed","The daring'st counsel which I had to doubt;","And did entreat your Highness to this course","Which you are running here."],"id":387,"m":"If it please your Highness, the question at first so staggered me, involving such a weighty matter and such fearful consequences, that I doubted even my boldest advice, and urged your Highness to take the course you are now following here."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["I then mov'd you,","My Lord of Canterbury; and got your leave","To make this present summons. Unsolicited","I left no reverend person in this court;","But by particular consent proceeded","Under your hands and seals. Therefore, go on;","For no dislike i' the world against the person","Of the good queen, but the sharp thorny points","Of my alleged reasons, drives this forward.","Prove but our marriage lawful, by my life","And kingly dignity, we are contented","To wear our mortal state to come with her,","Katherine our queen, before the primest creature","That's paragon'd o' the world."],"id":388,"m":"Then I raised it with you, my Lord of Canterbury, and got your permission to call this present hearing. I left no reverend person in this court unasked, but went ahead with the agreement of each, under your signatures and seals. So go on; for it is no dislike in the world of the good Queen's person, but the sharp, thorny points of the reasons I have given, that drives this forward. Only prove our marriage lawful, and on my life and royal dignity we are content to spend the rest of our mortal life with her, Katherine our queen, rather than with the most perfect creature in the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMPEIUS","lines":["So please your Highness,","The Queen being absent, 'tis a needful fitness","That we adjourn this court till further day.","Meanwhile must be an earnest motion","Made to the Queen, to call back her appeal","She intends unto his Holiness."],"id":389,"m":"If it please your Highness, since the Queen is absent, it is necessary and proper that we adjourn this court until a later day. Meanwhile an earnest appeal must be made to the Queen to withdraw the appeal she intends to make to his Holiness."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["[Aside] I may perceive","These Cardinals trifle with me; I abhor","This dilatory sloth and tricks of Rome.","My learn'd and well-beloved servant, Cranmer,","Prithee, return. With thy approach, I know,","My comfort comes along.—Break up the court!","I say, set on."],"id":390,"m":"(Aside) I can see these Cardinals are toying with me; I hate this delaying laziness and these tricks of Rome. My learned and well-loved servant Cranmer, please come back. I know my comfort comes with you. (Aloud) Break up the court! I say, go on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt in manner as they enter'd.","id":391},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"London. The Queen's apartments.","id":392},{"t":"sd","text":"The Queen and her women, as at work.","id":393},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Take thy lute, wench; my soul grows sad with troubles.","Sing, and disperse 'em, if thou canst. Leave working."],"id":394,"m":"Take your lute, girl; my soul grows sad with troubles. Sing, and scatter them if you can. Stop working."},{"t":"sp","who":"GENTLEWOMAN","lines":["~Orpheus with his lute made trees","~And the mountain tops that freeze","~Bow themselves when he did sing.","~To his music plants and flowers","~Ever sprung; as sun and showers","~There had made a lasting spring.","~Every thing that heard him play,","~Even the billows of the sea,","~Hung their heads, and then lay by.","~In sweet music is such art,","~Killing care and grief of heart","~Fall asleep, or hearing, die."],"id":395,"m":"Orpheus with his lute made trees and frozen mountain tops bow down when he sang. Plants and flowers always sprang up to his music, as if sun and showers had made an everlasting spring there. Everything that heard him play, even the waves of the sea, hung their heads and then lay still. In sweet music there is such art that care and heartache, hearing it, fall asleep or die."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Gentleman.","id":396},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["How now!"],"id":397,"m":"What is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GENTLEMAN","lines":["An't please your Grace, the two great Cardinals","Wait in the presence."],"id":398,"m":"If it please your Grace, the two great Cardinals are waiting in the audience room."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Would they speak with me?"],"id":399,"m":"Do they wish to speak with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GENTLEMAN","lines":["They will'd me say so, madam."],"id":400,"m":"They told me to say so, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Pray their Graces","To come near. [Exit Gentleman] What can be their business","With me, a poor weak woman, fallen from favour?","I do not like their coming. Now I think on't,","They should be good men, their affairs as righteous.","But all hoods make not monks."],"id":401,"m":"Ask their Graces to come in. (The Gentleman leaves) What business can they have with me, a poor weak woman fallen out of favour? I don't like their coming. Now that I think of it, they ought to be good men, and their business righteous. But a hood doesn't make a monk."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the two Cardinals, Wolsey and Campeius.","id":402},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Peace to your Highness!"],"id":403,"m":"Peace to your Highness!"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Your Graces find me here part of housewife;","I would be all, against the worst may happen.","What are your pleasures with me, reverend lords?"],"id":404,"m":"Your Graces find me here partly a housewife; I'd like to be wholly one, in case the worst happens. What do you want with me, reverend lords?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["May it please you, noble madam, to withdraw","Into your private chamber, we shall give you","The full cause of our coming."],"id":405,"m":"If it please you, noble madam, to withdraw into your private room, we will tell you the full reason for our coming."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Speak it here;","There's nothing I have done yet, o' my conscience,","Deserves a corner. Would all other women","Could speak this with as free a soul as I do!","My lords, I care not, so much I am happy","Above a number, if my actions","Were tried by every tongue, every eye saw 'em,","Envy and base opinion set against 'em,","I know my life so even. If your business","Seek me out, and that way I am wife in,","Out with it boldly. Truth loves open dealing."],"id":406,"m":"Say it here. Nothing I have done yet, on my conscience, needs a corner to hide in. I wish all other women could say this with as clear a soul as I do! My lords, I don't care, and in this I am happier than many, if my actions were judged by every tongue and seen by every eye, with malice and low opinion set against them; I know my life is so blameless. If your business is about me and my position as a wife, out with it boldly. Truth loves plain dealing."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Tanta est erga te mentis integritas, regina serenissima,—"],"id":407,"m":"Most serene queen, so upright is our intention toward you —"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["O, good my lord, no Latin;","I am not such a truant since my coming,","As not to know the language I have liv'd in.","A strange tongue makes my cause more strange, suspicious;","Pray, speak in English. Here are some will thank you,","If you speak truth, for their poor mistress' sake.","Believe me, she has had much wrong. Lord Cardinal,","The willing'st sin I ever yet committed","May be absolv'd in English."],"id":408,"m":"Oh, my good lord, no Latin. I haven't been so idle since I came here as not to know the language I have lived in. A foreign tongue makes my case seem stranger and more suspicious. Please speak English. There are people here who will thank you for their poor mistress's sake, if you speak the truth. Believe me, she has been greatly wronged. Lord Cardinal, the most deliberate sin I ever committed can be absolved in English."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Noble lady,","I am sorry my integrity should breed,","And service to his Majesty and you,","So deep suspicion, where all faith was meant.","We come not by the way of accusation","To taint that honour every good tongue blesses,","Nor to betray you any way to sorrow;","You have too much, good lady; but to know","How you stand minded in the weighty difference","Between the King and you; and to deliver,","Like free and honest men, our just opinions","And comforts to your cause."],"id":409,"m":"Noble lady, I am sorry that my honesty and my service to his Majesty and you should cause such deep suspicion when only good faith was meant. We don't come to accuse you or to stain the honour every good tongue blesses, nor to lead you into sorrow in any way; you have too much, good lady. We come to learn your mind on the serious dispute between the King and you, and to give you, as free and honest men, our sincere opinions and comfort for your cause."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMPEIUS","lines":["Most honour'd madam,","My Lord of York, out of his noble nature,","Zeal and obedience he still bore your Grace,","Forgetting, like a good man, your late censure","Both of his truth and him, which was too far,","Offers, as I do, in a sign of peace,","His service and his counsel."],"id":410,"m":"Most honoured madam, my Lord of York, out of his noble nature and the devotion and obedience he has always had for your Grace, forgetting like a good man your recent attack on his honesty and himself, which went too far, offers, as I do, his service and advice as a sign of peace."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["[Aside] To betray me.—","My lords, I thank you both for your good wills.","Ye speak like honest men; pray God, ye prove so!","But how to make ye suddenly an answer,","In such a point of weight, so near mine honour,—","More near my life, I fear,—with my weak wit,","And to such men of gravity and learning,","In truth I know not. I was set at work","Among my maids; full little, God knows, looking","Either for such men or such business.","For her sake that I have been,—for I feel","The last fit of my greatness—good your Graces,","Let me have time and counsel for my cause.","Alas, I am a woman, friendless, hopeless!"],"id":411,"m":"(Aside) To betray me. (Aloud) My lords, I thank you both for your good will. You speak like honest men; God grant you prove to be! But how to give you a quick answer on such a serious matter, so close to my honour, and closer to my life, I fear, with my weak wits, and to such grave and learned men, I really don't know. I was working among my maids, little expecting, God knows, such men or such business. For the sake of what I have been, for I feel the last throes of my greatness, good your Graces, let me have time and counsel for my cause. Alas, I am a woman, friendless and hopeless!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Madam, you wrong the King's love with these fears.","Your hopes and friends are infinite."],"id":412,"m":"Madam, you wrong the King's love with these fears. Your hopes and friends are countless."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["In England","But little for my profit. Can you think, lords,","That any Englishman dare give me counsel?","Or be a known friend, 'gainst his Highness' pleasure,","Though he be grown so desperate to be honest,","And live a subject? Nay, forsooth; my friends,","They that much weigh out my afflictions,","They that my trust must grow to, live not here;","They are, as all my other comforts, far hence","In mine own country, lords."],"id":413,"m":"In England they are of little use to me. Can you believe, lords, that any Englishman would dare give me advice, or be known as my friend against his Highness's wishes, even if he had become desperate enough to be honest, and still live as a subject? No, indeed. My friends, those who truly weigh my troubles, those I can trust, don't live here; they are, like all my other comforts, far away in my own country, lords."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMPEIUS","lines":["I would your Grace","Would leave your griefs, and take my counsel."],"id":414,"m":"I wish your Grace would put aside your grief and take my advice."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["How, sir?"],"id":415,"m":"What is it, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMPEIUS","lines":["Put your main cause into the King's protection;","He's loving and most gracious. 'Twill be much","Both for your honour better and your cause;","For if the trial of the law o'ertake ye,","You'll part away disgrac'd."],"id":416,"m":"Place your whole case under the King's protection. He is loving and most gracious. It will be much better both for your honour and for your cause, for if the law's judgement overtakes you, you'll leave in disgrace."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["He tells you rightly."],"id":417,"m":"He tells you correctly."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Ye tell me what ye wish for both,—my ruin.","Is this your Christian counsel? Out upon ye!","Heaven is above all yet; there sits a judge","That no king can corrupt."],"id":418,"m":"You tell me what you both want: my ruin. Is this your Christian advice? Shame on you! Heaven is still above everything; there sits a judge no king can corrupt."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMPEIUS","lines":["Your rage mistakes us."],"id":419,"m":"Your anger misjudges us."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["The more shame for ye! Holy men I thought ye,","Upon my soul, two reverend cardinal virtues;","But cardinal sins and hollow hearts I fear ye.","Mend 'em, for shame, my lords! Is this your comfort,","The cordial that ye bring a wretched lady,","A woman lost among ye, laugh'd at, scorn'd?","I will not wish ye half my miseries;","I have more charity; but say, I warn'd ye.","Take heed, for heaven's sake, take heed, lest at once","The burden of my sorrows fall upon ye."],"id":420,"m":"The more shame on you! I thought you were holy men, on my soul, two reverend cardinal virtues; but I fear you are cardinal sins and hollow hearts. Mend them, for shame, my lords! Is this your comfort, the medicine you bring a miserable lady, a woman lost among you, laughed at and scorned? I won't wish you half my miseries; I have more charity than that. But remember, I warned you. Take care, for heaven's sake, take care that the whole weight of my sorrows doesn't fall on you at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Madam, this is a mere distraction;","You turn the good we offer into envy."],"id":421,"m":"Madam, this is sheer madness; you turn the good we offer into malice."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Ye turn me into nothing. Woe upon ye","And all such false professors! Would you have me—","If you have any justice, any pity;","If ye be anything but churchmen's habits—","Put my sick cause into his hands that hates me?","Alas, he's banish'd me his bed already,","His love, too, long ago! I am old, my lords,","And all the fellowship I hold now with him","Is only my obedience. What can happen","To me above this wretchedness? All your studies","Make me a curse like this."],"id":422,"m":"You turn me into nothing. A curse on you and on all such false believers! Would you have me, if you have any justice or pity, if you are anything more than churchmen's robes, put my sick case into the hands of the man who hates me? Alas, he has already banished me from his bed, and from his love long ago! I am old, my lords, and the only bond I have with him now is my obedience. What can happen to me worse than this misery? All your efforts bring me this kind of curse."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMPEIUS","lines":["Your fears are worse."],"id":423,"m":"Your fears make it worse than it is."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Have I liv'd thus long—let me speak myself,","Since virtue finds no friends—a wife, a true one?","A woman, I dare say without vain-glory,","Never yet branded with suspicion?","Have I with all my full affections","Still met the King? lov'd him next Heav'n? obey'd him?","Been, out of fondness, superstitious to him?","Almost forgot my prayers to content him?","And am I thus rewarded! 'Tis not well, lords.","Bring me a constant woman to her husband,","One that ne'er dream'd a joy beyond his pleasure;","And to that woman, when she has done most,","Yet will I add an honour,—a great patience."],"id":424,"m":"Have I lived so long, let me speak for myself, since virtue finds no friends, as a wife, a true one? A woman, I dare say without boasting, never once branded with suspicion? Have I always met the King with all my love? Loved him next to Heaven? Obeyed him? Out of fondness, almost worshipped him? Almost forgotten my prayers to please him? And am I rewarded like this? It isn't right, lords. Show me a woman faithful to her husband, one who never dreamed of any joy beyond his pleasure, and to that woman, when she has done her utmost, I will still add one honour more: a great patience."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Madam, you wander from the good we aim at."],"id":425,"m":"Madam, you are straying from the good we intend."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["My lord, I dare not make myself so guilty,","To give up willingly that noble title","Your master wed me to. Nothing but death","Shall e'er divorce my dignities."],"id":426,"m":"My lord, I dare not make myself so guilty as to give up willingly the noble title your master married me to. Nothing but death shall ever divorce me from my dignities."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Pray hear me."],"id":427,"m":"Please hear me."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Would I had never trod this English earth,","Or felt the flatteries that grow upon it!","Ye have angels' faces, but Heaven knows your hearts.","What will become of me now, wretched lady!","I am the most unhappy woman living.","Alas, poor wenches, where are now your fortunes!","Shipwreck'd upon a kingdom, where no pity,","No friends, no hope; no kindred weep for me;","Almost no grave allow'd me. Like the lily,","That once was mistress of the field and flourish'd,","I'll hang my head and perish."],"id":428,"m":"I wish I had never set foot on this English soil, or felt the flattery that grows on it! You have angels' faces, but Heaven knows your hearts. What will become of me now, wretched lady? I am the unhappiest woman alive. Alas, poor girls, where are your fortunes now! Shipwrecked in a kingdom where there is no pity, no friends, no hope; no relatives weep for me; I'm hardly allowed a grave. Like the lily that was once mistress of the field and flourished, I'll hang my head and die."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["If your Grace","Could but be brought to know our ends are honest,","You'd feel more comfort. Why should we, good lady,","Upon what cause, wrong you? Alas, our places,","The way of our profession is against it;","We are to cure such sorrows, not to sow 'em.","For goodness' sake, consider what you do;","How you may hurt yourself, ay, utterly","Grow from the King's acquaintance, by this carriage.","The hearts of princes kiss obedience,","So much they love it; but to stubborn spirits","They swell, and grow as terrible as storms.","I know you have a gentle, noble temper,","A soul as even as a calm; pray, think us","Those we profess, peacemakers, friends, and servants."],"id":429,"m":"If your Grace could only be brought to see that our purposes are honest, you would feel more comfort. Why should we wrong you, good lady, and for what reason? Alas, our positions and the nature of our calling are against it; we are meant to cure such sorrows, not to sow them. For goodness' sake, think what you are doing: how you may harm yourself, yes, and lose the King's acquaintance completely by behaving like this. The hearts of princes kiss obedience, they love it so much; but against stubborn spirits they swell and grow as terrible as storms. I know you have a gentle, noble temper, a soul as smooth as a calm sea; please think of us as what we claim to be, peacemakers, friends and servants."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAMPEIUS","lines":["Madam, you'll find it so. You wrong your virtues","With these weak women's fears. A noble spirit","As yours was, put into you, ever casts","Such doubts, as false coin, from it. The King loves you;","Beware you lose it not. For us, if you please","To trust us in your business, we are ready","To use our utmost studies in your service."],"id":430,"m":"Madam, you'll find it so. You wrong your virtues with these weak womanish fears. A noble spirit such as was put into you always throws out such doubts like counterfeit coins. The King loves you; take care you don't lose that love. As for us, if you will trust us in your business, we are ready to do our very best in your service."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Do what ye will, my lords; and, pray, forgive me","If I have us'd myself unmannerly;","You know I am a woman, lacking wit","To make a seemly answer to such persons.","Pray, do my service to his Majesty;","He has my heart yet, and shall have my prayers","While I shall have my life. Come, reverend fathers,","Bestow your counsels on me. She now begs,","That little thought, when she set footing here,","She should have bought her dignities so dear."],"id":431,"m":"Do what you will, my lords, and please forgive me if I have behaved rudely; you know I am a woman, without the wit to give a proper answer to men like you. Please give my respects to his Majesty; he still has my heart, and shall have my prayers as long as I live. Come, reverend fathers, give me your advice. She who now begs little thought, when she first set foot here, that she would pay so dearly for her honours."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":432},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"Ante-chamber to the King's apartment.","id":433},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Duke of Norfolk, the Duke of Suffolk, the Earl of Surrey, and the Lord Chamberlain.","id":434},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["If you will now unite in your complaints","And force them with a constancy, the Cardinal","Cannot stand under them. If you omit","The offer of this time, I cannot promise","But that you shall sustain moe new disgraces,","With these you bear already."],"id":435,"m":"If you will now join together in your complaints and press them steadily, the Cardinal can't withstand them. If you miss the chance this moment offers, I can't promise that you won't suffer more new humiliations on top of the ones you already bear."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["I am joyful","To meet the least occasion that may give me","Remembrance of my father-in-law, the Duke,","To be reveng'd on him."],"id":436,"m":"I'm glad of the smallest chance that reminds me of my father-in-law, the Duke, so I can take revenge on him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Which of the peers","Have uncontemn'd gone by him, or at least","Strangely neglected? When did he regard","The stamp of nobleness in any person","Out of himself?"],"id":437,"m":"Which of the peers has he not treated with contempt, or at least strangely ignored? When did he ever respect the mark of nobility in anyone but himself?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["My lords, you speak your pleasures.","What he deserves of you and me I know;","What we can do to him, though now the time","Gives way to us, I much fear. If you cannot","Bar his access to the King, never attempt","Anything on him; for he hath a witchcraft","Over the King in 's tongue."],"id":438,"m":"My lords, you say what you like. I know what he deserves from you and me; what we can actually do to him, though the time now favours us, I very much doubt. If you can't stop him getting to the King, never try anything against him, for his tongue has a kind of witchcraft over the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["O, fear him not;","His spell in that is out. The King hath found","Matter against him that for ever mars","The honey of his language. No, he's settled,","Not to come off, in his displeasure."],"id":439,"m":"Oh, don't fear him; his spell is broken. The King has found something against him that spoils the honey of his words for ever. No, he's fixed in the King's displeasure, never to get out of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["Sir,","I should be glad to hear such news as this","Once every hour."],"id":440,"m":"Sir, I'd be glad to hear news like this every hour."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Believe it, this is true.","In the divorce his contrary proceedings","Are all unfolded; wherein he appears","As I would wish mine enemy."],"id":441,"m":"Believe me, it's true. His double-dealing over the divorce has all come out, and in it he looks just as I'd wish my enemy to look."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["How came","His practices to light?"],"id":442,"m":"How did his schemes come to light?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Most strangely."],"id":443,"m":"Very strangely."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["O, how, how?"],"id":444,"m":"Oh, how, how?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["The Cardinal's letters to the Pope miscarried,","And came to the eye o' the King; wherein was read,","How that the Cardinal did entreat his Holiness","To stay the judgement o' the divorce; for if","It did take place, \"I do\" quoth he \"perceive","My king is tangled in affection to","A creature of the Queen's, Lady Anne Bullen.\""],"id":445,"m":"The Cardinal's letters to the Pope went astray and came before the King's eyes. In them he read that the Cardinal begged his Holiness to hold back the judgement on the divorce, \"for if it goes ahead,\" he said, \"I see my King is entangled in love with one of the Queen's women, Lady Anne Bullen.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["Has the King this?"],"id":446,"m":"Does the King have this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Believe it."],"id":447,"m":"Believe it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["Will this work?"],"id":448,"m":"Will this do the trick?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["The King in this perceives him, how he coasts","And hedges his own way. But in this point","All his tricks founder, and he brings his physic","After his patient's death. The King already","Hath married the fair lady."],"id":449,"m":"In this the King sees how he steers round and hedges his own way. But here all his tricks run aground, and he brings his medicine after the patient has died. The King has already married the fair lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["Would he had!"],"id":450,"m":"I wish he had!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["May you be happy in your wish, my lord!","For, I profess, you have it."],"id":451,"m":"May you be happy in your wish, my lord! For I declare you have it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["Now, all my joy","Trace the conjunction!"],"id":452,"m":"Now may all my joy follow this union!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["My amen to't!"],"id":453,"m":"My amen to that!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["All men's!"],"id":454,"m":"Everyone's!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["There's order given for her coronation.","Marry, this is yet but young, and may be left","To some ears unrecounted. But, my lords,","She is a gallant creature, and complete","In mind and feature. I persuade me, from her","Will fall some blessing to this land, which shall","In it be memoriz'd."],"id":455,"m":"Orders have been given for her coronation. Indeed, this is still new and may be kept from some ears. But, my lords, she is a splendid creature, perfect in mind and looks. I am convinced some blessing will come from her to this land, which will be remembered for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["But, will the King","Digest this letter of the Cardinal's?","The Lord forbid!"],"id":456,"m":"But will the King swallow this letter of the Cardinal's? God forbid!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Marry, amen!"],"id":457,"m":"Amen to that!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["No, no;","There be moe wasps that buzz about his nose","Will make this sting the sooner. Cardinal Campeius","Is stolen away to Rome; hath ta'en no leave;","He's left the cause o' the King unhandled, and","Is posted, as the agent of our Cardinal,","To second all his plot. I do assure you","The King cried \"Ha!\" at this."],"id":458,"m":"No, no; there are more wasps buzzing round his nose that will make this sting sooner. Cardinal Campeius has slipped away to Rome without taking his leave; he has left the King's case undealt with and has rushed off as our Cardinal's agent to back up his whole plot. I assure you the King cried \"Ha!\" at this."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Now, God incense him,","And let him cry \"Ha!\" louder!"],"id":459,"m":"Now God enrage him, and let him cry \"Ha!\" louder!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["But, my lord,","When returns Cranmer?"],"id":460,"m":"But, my lord, when will Cranmer return?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["He is return'd in his opinions; which","Have satisfied the King for his divorce,","Together with all famous colleges","Almost in Christendom. Shortly, I believe,","His second marriage shall be publish'd, and","Her coronation. Katherine no more","Shall be call'd Queen, but Princess Dowager","And widow to Prince Arthur."],"id":461,"m":"He has returned in his opinions, which have satisfied the King on his divorce, together with nearly all the famous universities in Christendom. Soon, I believe, his second marriage will be announced, and her coronation. Katherine will no longer be called Queen, but Princess Dowager, widow of Prince Arthur."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["This same Cranmer's","A worthy fellow, and hath ta'en much pain","In the King's business."],"id":462,"m":"This Cranmer is a worthy fellow, and has taken great pains over the King's business."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["He has; and we shall see him","For it an archbishop."],"id":463,"m":"He has, and we'll see him made an archbishop for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["So I hear."],"id":464,"m":"So I hear."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["'Tis so."],"id":465,"m":"It's true."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Wolsey and Cromwell.","id":466},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","cont":1,"lines":["The Cardinal!"],"id":467,"m":"The Cardinal!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Observe, observe, he's moody."],"id":468,"m":"Watch, watch; he's in a gloomy mood."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["The packet, Cromwell,","Gave't you the King?"],"id":469,"m":"The packet, Cromwell: did you give it to the King?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["To his own hand, in 's bedchamber."],"id":470,"m":"Into his own hand, in his bedroom."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Look'd he o' the inside of the paper?"],"id":471,"m":"Did he look inside the papers?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["Presently","He did unseal them; and the first he view'd,","He did it with a serious mind; a heed","Was in his countenance. You he bade","Attend him here this morning."],"id":472,"m":"He unsealed them at once, and he read the first one with a serious mind; there was close attention in his face. He told you to wait on him here this morning."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Is he ready","To come abroad?"],"id":473,"m":"Is he ready to come out?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["I think, by this he is."],"id":474,"m":"I think he is by now."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Leave me awhile."],"id":475,"m":"Leave me for a while."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Cromwell.","id":476},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","cont":1,"lines":["[Aside] It shall be to the Duchess of Alencon,","The French king's sister; he shall marry her.","Anne Bullen! No; I'll no Anne Bullens for him;","There's more in't than fair visage. Bullen!","No, we'll no Bullens. Speedily I wish","To hear from Rome. The Marchioness of Pembroke!"],"id":477,"m":"(Aside) It shall be the Duchess of Alencon, the French king's sister; he shall marry her. Anne Bullen! No, I'll have no Anne Bullens for him; there's more to it than a pretty face. Bullen! No, we'll have no Bullens. I wish I could hear from Rome quickly. The Marchioness of Pembroke!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["He's discontented."],"id":478,"m":"He's displeased."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["May be, he hears the King","Does whet his anger to him."],"id":479,"m":"Maybe he hears the King is sharpening his anger against him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["Sharp enough,","Lord, for thy justice!"],"id":480,"m":"Sharp enough, Lord, for your justice!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["[Aside] The late queen's gentlewoman, a knight's daughter,","To be her mistress' mistress! the Queen's queen!","This candle burns not clear: 'tis I must snuff it;","Then out it goes. What though I know her virtuous","And well deserving? yet I know her for","A spleeny Lutheran; and not wholesome to","Our cause, that she should lie i' the bosom of","Our hard-rul'd King. Again, there is sprung up","An heretic, an arch one, Cranmer; one","Hath crawl'd into the favour of the King,","And is his oracle."],"id":481,"m":"(Aside) The late queen's lady-in-waiting, a knight's daughter, to become her mistress's mistress, the Queen's queen! This candle doesn't burn clearly; I must snuff it, and then out it goes. What if I know she is virtuous and deserving? I also know she is a hot-headed Lutheran, and it isn't good for our cause that she should lie in the arms of our hard-to-manage King. And again, a heretic has sprung up, an arch-heretic, Cranmer, who has crawled into the King's favour and is his oracle."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["He's vex'd at something."],"id":482,"m":"He's upset about something."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the King, reading a schedule, and Lovell.","id":483},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["I would 'twere something that would fret the string,","The master-cord on 's heart!"],"id":484,"m":"I wish it were something that would snap the string, the master-cord of his heart!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["The King, the King!"],"id":485,"m":"The King, the King!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["What piles of wealth hath he accumulated","To his own portion! and what expense by the hour","Seems to flow from him! How, i' the name of thrift,","Does he rake this together! Now, my lords,","Saw you the Cardinal?"],"id":486,"m":"What heaps of wealth he has piled up for himself! And what spending seems to pour from him every hour! How, in the name of thrift, does he rake all this together? Now, my lords, have you seen the Cardinal?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["My lord, we have","Stood here observing him. Some strange commotion","Is in his brain; he bites his lip, and starts;","Stops on a sudden, looks upon the ground,","Then lays his finger on his temple; straight","Springs out into fast gait; then stops again,","Strikes his breast hard; and anon he casts","His eye against the moon. In most strange postures","We have seen him set himself."],"id":487,"m":"My lord, we've stood here watching him. Some strange disturbance is in his brain. He bites his lip and starts; stops suddenly, looks at the ground, then puts his finger to his temple; then sets off walking fast; stops again, strikes his chest hard; and then gazes up at the moon. We've seen him put himself into very strange postures."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["It may well be;","There is a mutiny in 's mind. This morning","Papers of state he sent me to peruse,","As I requir'd; and wot you what I found","There,—on my conscience, put unwittingly?","Forsooth, an inventory, thus importing","The several parcels of his plate, his treasure,","Rich stuffs, and ornaments of household; which","I find at such proud rate, that it out-speaks","Possession of a subject."],"id":488,"m":"That may well be; there's a mutiny in his mind. This morning he sent me state papers to read, as I had asked; and do you know what I found there, put in, on my conscience, by mistake? An inventory, listing the various pieces of his silver plate, his treasure, rich fabrics and household ornaments, which I find valued so high that it goes far beyond what a subject should own."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["It's Heaven's will!","Some spirit put this paper in the packet,","To bless your eye withal."],"id":489,"m":"It's Heaven's will! Some spirit put this paper in the packet to bless your eye with it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["If we did think","His contemplation were above the earth,","And fix'd on spiritual object, he should still","Dwell in his musings; but I am afraid","His thinkings are below the moon, not worth","His serious considering."],"id":490,"m":"If we thought his mind was above the earth and fixed on spiritual things, he could go on with his musing; but I'm afraid his thoughts are below the moon, on worldly things, not worth his serious attention."},{"t":"sd","text":"King takes his seat; whispers Lovell, who goes to the Cardinal.","id":491},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Heaven forgive me!","Ever God bless your Highness!"],"id":492,"m":"Heaven forgive me! God bless your Highness always!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Good my lord,","You are full of heavenly stuff, and bear the inventory","Of your best graces in your mind; the which","You were now running o'er. You have scarce time","To steal from spiritual leisure a brief span","To keep your earthly audit. Sure, in that","I deem you an ill husband, and am glad","To have you therein my companion."],"id":493,"m":"My good lord, you are full of heavenly matters and carry the inventory of your best graces in your mind, which you were just now going over. You hardly have time to steal a moment from spiritual leisure to keep your earthly accounts. I certainly think you a poor manager there, and I'm glad to have you as my fellow in that."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Sir,","For holy offices I have a time; a time","To think upon the part of business which","I bear i' the state; and Nature does require","Her times of preservation, which perforce","I, her frail son, amongst my brethren mortal,","Must give my tendance to."],"id":494,"m":"Sir, I have a time for holy duties, a time to think about my share of state business; and Nature needs her times of rest and food, which I, her frail son, like my fellow mortals, must necessarily attend to."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["You have said well."],"id":495,"m":"You have spoken well."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["And ever may your Highness yoke together,","As I will lend you cause, my doing well","With my well saying!"],"id":496,"m":"And may your Highness always link my doing well with my speaking well, as I will give you cause to!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["'Tis well said again;","And 'tis a kind of good deed to say well;","And yet words are no deeds. My father lov'd you;","He said he did; and with his deed did crown","His word upon you. Since I had my office,","I have kept you next my heart; have not alone","Employ'd you where high profits might come home,","But par'd my present havings, to bestow","My bounties upon you."],"id":497,"m":"That's well said again, and speaking well is a kind of good deed; and yet words are not deeds. My father loved you; he said he did, and crowned his words with deeds for you. Since I have been king I have kept you closest to my heart; I have not only employed you where great profits could come your way, but cut back my own wealth to give my bounty to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["[Aside] What should this mean?"],"id":498,"m":"(Aside) What does this mean?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["[Aside] The Lord increase this business!"],"id":499,"m":"(Aside) Lord, make this business grow!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Have I not made you","The prime man of the state? I pray you, tell me,","If what I now pronounce you have found true","And, if you may confess it, say withal,","If you are bound to us or no. What say you?"],"id":500,"m":"Haven't I made you the first man of the state? Please tell me whether you have found what I say to be true, and if you will admit it, say also whether you are bound to us or not. What do you say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["My sovereign, I confess your royal graces,","Shower'd on me daily, have been more than could","My studied purposes requite, which went","Beyond all man's endeavours. My endeavours","Have ever come too short of my desires,","Yet fil'd with my abilities. Mine own ends","Have been mine so that evermore they pointed","To the good of your most sacred person and","The profit of the state. For your great graces","Heap'd upon me, poor undeserver, I","Can nothing render but allegiant thanks,","My prayers to heaven for you, my loyalty,","Which ever has and ever shall be growing,","Till death, that winter, kill it."],"id":501,"m":"My sovereign, I admit your royal favours, showered on me daily, have been more than my most careful efforts could repay, and they were beyond any man's efforts. My efforts have always fallen short of my wishes, yet matched my abilities. My own aims have been mine only in that they always pointed to the good of your most sacred person and the profit of the state. For your great favours heaped on me, a poor undeserving man, I can give nothing back but loyal thanks, my prayers to heaven for you, and my loyalty, which has always grown and always will, until death, that winter, kills it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Fairly answer'd.","A loyal and obedient subject is","Therein illustrated. The honour of it","Does pay the act of it, as i' the contrary,","The foulness is the punishment. I presume","That, as my hand has open'd bounty to you,","My heart dropp'd love, my pow'r rain'd honour, more","On you than any, so your hand and heart,","Your brain, and every function of your power,","Should, notwithstanding that your bond of duty,","As 'twere in love's particular, be more","To me, your friend, than any."],"id":502,"m":"A fair answer. It shows a loyal and obedient subject. The honour of loyalty is its own reward, just as the shame of disloyalty is its punishment. I take it that, since my hand has opened bounty to you, my heart dropped love and my power rained honour on you more than on anyone, so your hand and heart, your brain and every power you have should, besides your general bond of duty, be more devoted to me, as your friend in a particular love, than to anyone."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["I do profess","That for your Highness' good I ever labour'd","More than mine own, that am, have, and will be—","Though all the world should crack their duty to you,","And throw it from their soul; though perils did","Abound, as thick as thought could make 'em, and","Appear in forms more horrid,—yet my duty,","As doth a rock against the chiding flood,","Should the approach of this wild river break,","And stand unshaken yours."],"id":503,"m":"I declare that I have always worked for your Highness's good more than my own; and that I am, and have been, and will be, though all the world should break its duty to you and throw it out of their souls, though dangers were as thick as thought could make them and appeared in more horrible forms, yet my duty, like a rock against the raging flood, will break the onrush of this wild river and stand unshaken, yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["'Tis nobly spoken.","Take notice, lords, he has a loyal breast,","For you have seen him open't. Read o'er this;"],"id":504,"m":"That's nobly spoken. Take note, lords, he has a loyal heart, for you've seen him open it. Read this through;"},{"t":"sd","text":"Giving him papers.","id":505},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","cont":1,"lines":["And, after, this; and then to breakfast with","What appetite you have."],"id":506,"m":"and afterwards this; and then go to breakfast with whatever appetite you have."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit King, frowning upon Cardinal Wolsey; the Nobles throng after him, smiling and whispering.","id":507},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["What should this mean?","What sudden anger's this? How have I reap'd it?","He parted frowning from me, as if ruin","Leap'd from his eyes. So looks the chafed lion","Upon the daring huntsman that has gall'd him;","Then makes him nothing. I must read this paper;","I fear, the story of his anger. 'Tis so;","This paper has undone me. 'Tis the account","Of all that world of wealth I have drawn together","For mine own ends; indeed, to gain the popedom","And fee my friends in Rome. O negligence,","Fit for a fool to fall by! What cross devil","Made me put this main secret in the packet","I sent the King? Is there no way to cure this?","No new device to beat this from his brains?","I know 'twill stir him strongly; yet I know","A way, if it take right, in spite of fortune,","Will bring me off again. What's this? \"To the Pope!\"","The letter, as I live, with all the business","I writ to 's Holiness. Nay then, farewell!","I have touch'd the highest point of all my greatness;","And from that full meridian of my glory,","I haste now to my setting. I shall fall","Like a bright exhalation in the evening,","And no man see me more."],"id":508,"m":"What can this mean? What sudden anger is this? How have I earned it? He left me frowning, as if ruin leapt from his eyes. That's how the enraged lion looks at the bold hunter who has wounded him, before he destroys him. I must read this paper; the story of his anger, I fear. That's it: this paper has ruined me. It's the account of all that world of wealth I have gathered for my own ends, in fact to win the papacy and pay my friends in Rome. Oh, carelessness fit for a fool to fall by! What spiteful devil made me put this vital secret in the packet I sent the King? Is there no way to cure this? No new trick to knock it out of his head? I know it will anger him greatly; yet I know a way that, if it works, will get me off again in spite of fortune. What's this? \"To the Pope!\" The letter, as I live, with all the business I wrote to his Holiness. Well then, farewell! I have reached the highest point of all my greatness, and from that full noon of my glory I now hurry to my setting. I shall fall like a shooting star in the evening, and no one will see me again."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter to Wolsey, the Dukes of Norfolk and Suffolk, the Earl of Surrey, and the Lord Chamberlain.","id":509},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Hear the King's pleasure, Cardinal! who commands you","To render up the great seal presently","Into our hands; and to confine yourself","To Asher House, my Lord of Winchester's,","Till you hear further from his Highness."],"id":510,"m":"Hear the King's wishes, Cardinal! He commands you to hand over the great seal to us at once, and to confine yourself to Asher House, my Lord of Winchester's, until you hear more from his Highness."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Stay!","Where's your commission, lords? Words cannot carry","Authority so weighty."],"id":511,"m":"Wait! Where is your written commission, lords? Words alone can't carry such weighty authority."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Who dares cross 'em,","Bearing the King's will from his mouth expressly?"],"id":512,"m":"Who dares go against them when they bring the King's will straight from his mouth?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Till I find more than will or words to do it,","I mean your malice, know, officious lords,","I dare and must deny it. Now I feel","Of what coarse metal ye are moulded, envy.","How eagerly ye follow my disgraces,","As if it fed ye! and how sleek and wanton","Ye appear in every thing may bring my ruin!","Follow your envious courses, men of malice!","You have Christian warrant for 'em, and, no doubt,","In time will find their fit rewards. That seal","You ask with such a violence, the King,","Mine and your master, with his own hand gave me,","Bade me enjoy it, with the place and honours,","During my life; and, to confirm his goodness,","Tied it by letters-patents. Now, who'll take it?"],"id":513,"m":"Until I find more than will and words to do it, I mean your malice, know, you meddling lords, I dare and must refuse. Now I feel what coarse metal you are made of: envy. How eagerly you follow my disgrace, as if it fed you! And how smooth and gleeful you look at anything that may bring my ruin! Follow your envious ways, men of malice! You have Christian authority for them, and no doubt in time they will find their proper rewards. That seal you demand so violently, the King, my master and yours, gave me with his own hand and told me to enjoy it, with the office and honours, for life; and to confirm his goodness, he bound it by letters patent. Now who'll take it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["The King, that gave it."],"id":514,"m":"The King, who gave it."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["It must be himself, then."],"id":515,"m":"Then it must be himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["Thou art a proud traitor, priest."],"id":516,"m":"You are a proud traitor, priest."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Proud lord, thou liest!","Within these forty hours Surrey durst better","Have burnt that tongue than said so."],"id":517,"m":"Proud lord, you lie! Forty hours ago Surrey would rather have burnt that tongue than said so."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["Thy ambition,","Thou scarlet sin, robb'd this bewailing land","Of noble Buckingham, my father-in-law.","The heads of all thy brother cardinals,","With thee and all thy best parts bound together,","Weigh'd not a hair of his. Plague of your policy!","You sent me deputy for Ireland,","Far from his succour, from the King, from all","That might have mercy on the fault thou gav'st him;","Whilst your great goodness, out of holy pity,","Absolv'd him with an axe."],"id":518,"m":"Your ambition, you scarlet sin, robbed this mourning land of noble Buckingham, my father-in-law. The heads of all your fellow cardinals, together with you and all your best qualities, weren't worth one of his hairs. A plague on your scheming! You sent me as deputy to Ireland, far from helping him, far from the King, far from everyone who might have shown mercy for the crime you charged him with, while your great goodness, out of holy pity, absolved him with an axe."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["This, and all else","This talking lord can lay upon my credit,","I answer is most false. The Duke by law","Found his deserts. How innocent I was","From any private malice in his end,","His noble jury and foul cause can witness.","If I lov'd many words, lord, I should tell you","You have as little honesty as honour,","That in the way of loyalty and truth","Toward the King, my ever royal master,","Dare mate a sounder man than Surrey can be","And all that love his follies."],"id":519,"m":"This, and everything else this talkative lord can blame me for, I answer is completely false. The Duke got what he deserved by law. His noble jury and his foul case can witness how free I was of any private malice in his death. If I liked long speeches, lord, I would tell you that you have as little honesty as honour, and that in loyalty and truth toward the King, my always royal master, I dare match a sounder man than Surrey can be, and all who love his follies."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["By my soul,","Your long coat, priest, protects you; thou shouldst feel","My sword i' the life-blood of thee else. My lords,","Can ye endure to hear this arrogance?","And from this fellow? If we live thus tamely,","To be thus jaded by a piece of scarlet,","Farewell nobility! Let his Grace go forward","And dare us with his cap like larks."],"id":520,"m":"By my soul, priest, your long robe protects you; otherwise you'd feel my sword in your life-blood. My lords, can you bear to hear this arrogance, and from this fellow? If we live this tamely, to be treated like old horses by a bit of scarlet cloth, farewell nobility! Let his Grace go on and dazzle us with his red hat like larks caught with a mirror."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["All goodness","Is poison to thy stomach."],"id":521,"m":"All goodness is poison to your stomach."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["Yes, that goodness","Of gleaning all the land's wealth into one,","Into your own hands, Cardinal, by extortion;","The goodness of your intercepted packets","You writ to the Pope against the King. Your goodness,","Since you provoke me, shall be most notorious.","My Lord of Norfolk, as you are truly noble,","As you respect the common good, the state","Of our despis'd nobility, our issues,","Who, if he live, will scarce be gentlemen,","Produce the grand sum of his sins, the articles","Collected from his life. I'll startle you","Worse than the sacring bell, when the brown wench","Lay kissing in your arms, Lord Cardinal."],"id":522,"m":"Yes, that goodness of gathering all the land's wealth into one place, your own hands, Cardinal, by extortion; the goodness of your intercepted letters that you wrote to the Pope against the King. Since you provoke me, your goodness shall be made well known. My Lord of Norfolk, as you are truly noble, as you care for the common good, the state of our despised nobility and our children, who will hardly be gentlemen if he lives, bring out the grand total of his sins, the charges gathered from his life. I'll startle you worse than the sacring bell at Mass did when the brown-skinned girl lay kissing in your arms, Lord Cardinal."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["How much, methinks, I could despise this man,","But that I am bound in charity against it!"],"id":523,"m":"How much, I think, I could despise this man, if charity didn't forbid it!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Those articles, my lord, are in the King's hand:","But, thus much, they are foul ones."],"id":524,"m":"Those charges, my lord, are in the King's hands; but this much I can say: they are foul ones."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["So much fairer","And spotless shall mine innocence arise,","When the King knows my truth."],"id":525,"m":"All the fairer and more spotless my innocence will rise when the King knows my truth."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["This cannot save you.","I thank my memory, I yet remember","Some of these articles; and out they shall.","Now, if you can blush and cry \"guilty,\" Cardinal,","You'll show a little honesty."],"id":526,"m":"That can't save you. Thanks to my memory, I still remember some of these charges, and out they'll come. Now, if you can blush and cry \"guilty,\" Cardinal, you'll show a little honesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Speak on, sir;","I dare your worst objections. If I blush,","It is to see a nobleman want manners."],"id":527,"m":"Go on, sir; I defy your worst accusations. If I blush, it's to see a nobleman without manners."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["I had rather want those than my head. Have at you!","First, that, without the King's assent or knowledge,","You wrought to be a legate; by which power","You maim'd the jurisdiction of all bishops."],"id":528,"m":"I'd rather lack those than my head. Here goes! First, that without the King's agreement or knowledge you worked to be made papal legate, by which power you crippled the authority of all the bishops."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Then, that in all you writ to Rome, or else","To foreign princes, \"Ego et Rex meus\"","Was still inscrib'd; in which you brought the King","To be your servant."],"id":529,"m":"Then, that in everything you wrote to Rome or to foreign princes, \"Ego et Rex meus,\" \"I and my King,\" was always written, by which you made the King your servant."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Then, that, without the knowledge","Either of king or council, when you went","Ambassador to the Emperor, you made bold","To carry into Flanders the great seal."],"id":530,"m":"Then, that without the knowledge of either king or council, when you went as ambassador to the Emperor, you dared to carry the great seal into Flanders."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["Item, you sent a large commission","To Gregory de Cassado, to conclude,","Without the King's will or the state's allowance,","A league between his Highness and Ferrara."],"id":531,"m":"Item: you sent a full commission to Gregory de Cassado to conclude, without the King's will or the state's approval, an alliance between his Highness and Ferrara."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["That, out of mere ambition, you have caus'd","Your holy hat to be stamp'd on the King's coin."],"id":532,"m":"That, out of sheer ambition, you had your holy hat stamped on the King's coins."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["Then, that you have sent innumerable substance—","By what means got, I leave to your own conscience—","To furnish Rome, and to prepare the ways","You have for dignities; to the mere undoing","Of all the kingdom. Many more there are;","Which, since they are of you, and odious,","I will not taint my mouth with."],"id":533,"m":"Then, that you have sent countless riches, how gained I leave to your own conscience, to supply Rome and smooth your way to higher honours, to the utter ruin of the whole kingdom. There are many more, which, since they are yours and hateful, I won't stain my mouth with."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["O my lord,","Press not a falling man too far! 'tis virtue.","His faults lie open to the laws; let them,","Not you, correct him. My heart weeps to see him","So little of his great self."],"id":534,"m":"Oh, my lord, don't press a falling man too hard! It's a virtue to spare him. His faults are open to the law; let the law correct him, not you. My heart weeps to see him so shrunk from his great self."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["I forgive him."],"id":535,"m":"I forgive him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Lord Cardinal, the King's further pleasure is,","Because all those things you have done of late","By your power legatine within this kingdom,","Fall into the compass of a praemunire,","That therefore such a writ be sued against you;","To forfeit all your goods, lands, tenements,","Chattels, and whatsoever, and to be","Out of the King's protection. This is my charge."],"id":536,"m":"Lord Cardinal, the King's further wish is this: because everything you have recently done in this kingdom by your power as legate falls within the scope of a praemunire, a writ of that kind shall be issued against you, so that you forfeit all your goods, lands, houses, possessions and everything else, and are put outside the King's protection. That is my charge."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["And so we'll leave you to your meditations","How to live better. For your stubborn answer","About the giving back the great seal to us,","The King shall know it, and, no doubt, shall thank you.","So fare you well, my little good Lord Cardinal."],"id":537,"m":"And so we'll leave you to think about how to live better. As for your stubborn answer about giving back the great seal to us, the King shall hear of it and no doubt will thank you. So farewell, my little good Lord Cardinal."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Wolsey.","id":538},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["So farewell to the little good you bear me.","Farewell! a long farewell, to all my greatness!","This is the state of man: to-day he puts forth","The tender leaves of hopes; to-morrow blossoms,","And bears his blushing honours thick upon him;","The third day comes a frost, a killing frost,","And, when he thinks, good easy man, full surely","His greatness is a-ripening, nips his root,","And then he falls, as I do. I have ventur'd,","Like little wanton boys that swim on bladders,","This many summers in a sea of glory,","But far beyond my depth. My high-blown pride","At length broke under me, and now has left me,","Weary and old with service, to the mercy","Of a rude stream, that must for ever hide me.","Vain pomp and glory of this world, I hate ye!","I feel my heart new open'd. O, how wretched","Is that poor man that hangs on princes' favours!","There is, betwixt that smile we would aspire to,","That sweet aspect of princes, and their ruin,","More pangs and fears than wars or women have;","And when he falls, he falls like Lucifer,","Never to hope again."],"id":539,"m":"So farewell to the little good you wish me. Farewell, a long farewell, to all my greatness! This is the state of man: today he puts out the tender leaves of hope; tomorrow he blossoms and wears his blushing honours thick on him; the third day comes a frost, a killing frost, and just when he thinks, the good trusting man, that his greatness is surely ripening, it nips his root, and then he falls, as I do. Like little reckless boys who swim on inflated bladders, I have ventured many summers in a sea of glory, but far out of my depth. My swollen pride finally burst under me, and now has left me, weary and old with service, to the mercy of a rough current that must hide me for ever. Empty pomp and glory of this world, I hate you! I feel my heart opened anew. Oh, how wretched is the poor man who depends on princes' favours! Between the smile we long for, the sweet look of princes, and the ruin they can bring, there are more pangs and fears than wars or women cause; and when he falls, he falls like Lucifer, never to hope again."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Cromwell, standing amazed.","id":540},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","cont":1,"lines":["Why, how now, Cromwell!"],"id":541,"m":"Why, what's the matter, Cromwell?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["I have no power to speak, sir."],"id":542,"m":"I can't speak, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["What, amaz'd","At my misfortunes? Can thy spirit wonder","A great man should decline? Nay, an you weep,","I am fallen indeed."],"id":543,"m":"What, stunned by my misfortunes? Can your spirit wonder that a great man should fall? No, if you weep, I am fallen indeed."},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["How does your Grace?"],"id":544,"m":"How is your Grace?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Why, well,","Never so truly happy, my good Cromwell.","I know myself now; and I feel within me","A peace above all earthly dignities,","A still and quiet conscience. The King has cur'd me,","I humbly thank his Grace; and from these shoulders,","These ruin'd pillars, out of pity, taken","A load would sink a navy, too much honour.","O, 'tis a burden, Cromwell, 'tis a burden","Too heavy for a man that hopes for heaven!"],"id":545,"m":"Why, well; never so truly happy, my good Cromwell. I know myself now, and I feel within me a peace above all earthly honours, a calm and quiet conscience. The King has cured me, I humbly thank his Grace, and out of pity has taken from these shoulders, these ruined pillars, a load that would sink a navy: too much honour. Oh, it's a burden, Cromwell, a burden too heavy for a man who hopes for heaven!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["I am glad your Grace has made that right use of it."],"id":546,"m":"I'm glad your Grace has used it so rightly."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["I hope I have. I am able now, methinks,","Out of a fortitude of soul I feel,","To endure more miseries and greater far","Than my weak-hearted enemies dare offer.","What news abroad?"],"id":547,"m":"I hope I have. I think I am now able, through a strength of soul I feel, to endure more and far greater miseries than my faint-hearted enemies dare inflict. What news is there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["The heaviest and the worst","Is your displeasure with the King."],"id":548,"m":"The heaviest and the worst is that you are out of favour with the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["God bless him!"],"id":549,"m":"God bless him!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["The next is, that Sir Thomas More is chosen","Lord Chancellor in your place."],"id":550,"m":"The next is that Sir Thomas More has been chosen Lord Chancellor in your place."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["That's somewhat sudden;","But he's a learned man. May he continue","Long in his Highness' favour, and do justice","For truth's sake and his conscience; that his bones,","When he has run his course and sleeps in blessings,","May have a tomb of orphans' tears wept on 'em!","What more?"],"id":551,"m":"That's rather sudden; but he's a learned man. May he stay long in his Highness's favour and do justice for truth's and conscience's sake, so that when he has run his course and sleeps in blessings, his bones may have a tomb of orphans' tears wept on them! What else?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["That Cranmer is return'd with welcome,","Install'd Lord Archbishop of Canterbury."],"id":552,"m":"That Cranmer has returned and been welcomed, and installed as Lord Archbishop of Canterbury."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["That's news indeed."],"id":553,"m":"That's news indeed."},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["Last, that the Lady Anne,","Whom the King hath in secrecy long married,","This day was view'd in open as his queen,","Going to chapel; and the voice is now","Only about her coronation."],"id":554,"m":"Lastly, that Lady Anne, whom the King married in secret long ago, was seen in public today as his queen, going to chapel; and now all the talk is of her coronation."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["There was the weight that pull'd me down.","O Cromwell,","The King has gone beyond me! All my glories","In that one woman I have lost for ever.","No sun shall ever usher forth mine honours,","Or gild again the noble troops that waited","Upon my smiles. Go, get thee from me, Cromwell!","I am a poor fallen man, unworthy now","To be thy lord and master. Seek the King!","That sun, I pray, may never set! I have told him","What and how true thou art. He will advance thee;","Some little memory of me will stir him—","I know his noble nature—not to let","Thy hopeful service perish too. Good Cromwell,","Neglect him not; make use now, and provide","For thine own future safety."],"id":555,"m":"That was the weight that pulled me down. Oh, Cromwell, the King has outstripped me! In that one woman I have lost all my glories for ever. No sun will ever usher in my honours again, or gild the noble crowds that waited on my smiles. Go, leave me, Cromwell! I am a poor fallen man, unworthy now to be your lord and master. Go to the King, that sun, which I pray may never set! I have told him what you are and how loyal. He will promote you; some small memory of me will move him, I know his noble nature, not to let your promising service die too. Good Cromwell, don't neglect him; take your chance now and provide for your own future safety."},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["O my lord,","Must I, then, leave you? Must I needs forgo","So good, so noble, and so true a master?","Bear witness, all that have not hearts of iron,","With what a sorrow Cromwell leaves his lord.","The King shall have my service; but my prayers","For ever and for ever shall be yours."],"id":556,"m":"Oh, my lord, must I leave you, then? Must I give up so good, so noble and so true a master? Bear witness, all who don't have hearts of iron, how sorrowfully Cromwell leaves his lord. The King shall have my service, but my prayers shall be yours for ever and ever."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["Cromwell, I did not think to shed a tear","In all my miseries; but thou hast forc'd me,","Out of thy honest truth, to play the woman.","Let's dry our eyes; and thus far hear me, Cromwell;","And when I am forgotten, as I shall be,","And sleep in dull cold marble, where no mention","Of me more must be heard of, say, I taught thee;","Say, Wolsey, that once trod the ways of glory,","And sounded all the depths and shoals of honour,","Found thee a way, out of his wreck, to rise in;","A sure and safe one, though thy master miss'd it.","Mark but my fall, and that that ruin'd me.","Cromwell, I charge thee, fling away ambition!","By that sin fell the angels; how can man, then,","The image of his Maker, hope to win by it?","Love thyself last. Cherish those hearts that hate thee;","Corruption wins not more than honesty.","Still in thy right hand carry gentle peace,","To silence envious tongues. Be just, and fear not;","Let all the ends thou aim'st at be thy country's,","Thy God's, and truth's; then if thou fall'st, O Cromwell,","Thou fall'st a blessed martyr! Serve the King!","And, prithee, lead me in.","There take an inventory of all I have,","To the last penny; 'tis the King's. My robe,","And my integrity to Heaven, is all","I dare now call mine own. O Cromwell, Cromwell!","Had I but serv'd my God with half the zeal","I serv'd my king, He would not in mine age","Have left me naked to mine enemies."],"id":557,"m":"Cromwell, I didn't think I would shed a tear in all my miseries; but your honest loyalty has forced me to weep like a woman. Let's dry our eyes, and hear me this far, Cromwell: when I am forgotten, as I shall be, and sleep in dull cold marble where I must never be mentioned again, say I taught you. Say that Wolsey, who once walked the paths of glory and sounded all the depths and shallows of honour, found you a way to rise out of his wreck, a sure and safe one, though your master missed it. Just note my fall and what ruined me. Cromwell, I charge you, throw away ambition! By that sin the angels fell; how then can man, made in his Maker's image, hope to gain by it? Love yourself last. Cherish the hearts that hate you; corruption gains no more than honesty. Always carry gentle peace in your right hand to silence envious tongues. Be just and fear nothing. Let everything you aim at be for your country, your God and truth; then if you fall, Cromwell, you fall a blessed martyr! Serve the King! And please, lead me in. Take an inventory there of everything I own, to the last penny; it's the King's. My robe and my honesty before Heaven are all I now dare call my own. Oh Cromwell, Cromwell! If I had only served my God with half the zeal I served my king, He would not have left me naked to my enemies in my old age."},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["Good sir, have patience."],"id":558,"m":"Good sir, be patient."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOLSEY","lines":["So I have. Farewell","The hopes of court! My hopes in heaven do dwell."],"id":559,"m":"So I am. Farewell, hopes of court! My hopes now live in heaven."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":560},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"A street in Westminster.","id":561},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter two Gentlemen, meeting one another.","id":562},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["You're well met once again."],"id":563,"m":"Well met once again."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["So are you."],"id":564,"m":"And you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["You come to take your stand here, and behold","The Lady Anne pass from her coronation?"],"id":565,"m":"You've come to take your place here and watch Lady Anne pass on her way back from her coronation?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["'Tis all my business. At our last encounter,","The Duke of Buckingham came from his trial."],"id":566,"m":"That's all my business. When we last met, the Duke of Buckingham was coming from his trial."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["'Tis very true; but that time offer'd sorrow;","This, general joy."],"id":567,"m":"Very true; but that time brought sorrow, this one general joy."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["'Tis well. The citizens,","I am sure, have shown at full their royal minds—","As, let 'em have their rights, they are ever forward—","In celebration of this day with shows,","Pageants, and sights of honour."],"id":568,"m":"Good. The citizens, I'm sure, have shown their loyal spirit to the full, for, to give them their due, they are always eager, celebrating this day with shows, pageants and honourable displays."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Never greater,","Nor, I'll assure you, better taken, sir."],"id":569,"m":"Never greater, nor, I assure you, better received, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["May I be bold to ask what that contains,","That paper in your hand?"],"id":570,"m":"May I be so bold as to ask what that paper in your hand contains?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Yes; 'tis the list","Of those that claim their offices this day","By custom of the coronation.","The Duke of Suffolk is the first, and claims","To be High Steward; next, the Duke of Norfolk,","He to be Earl Marshal. You may read the rest."],"id":571,"m":"Yes; it's the list of those who claim their offices today by custom of the coronation. The Duke of Suffolk is first, and claims to be High Steward; next the Duke of Norfolk, to be Earl Marshal. You can read the rest."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["I thank you, sir; had I not known those customs,","I should have been beholding to your paper.","But, I beseech you, what's become of Katherine,","The Princess Dowager? How goes her business?"],"id":572,"m":"Thank you, sir; if I didn't know those customs I'd be grateful for your paper. But please, what has become of Katherine, the Princess Dowager? How is her business going?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["That I can tell you too. The Archbishop","Of Canterbury, accompanied with other","Learned and reverend fathers of his order,","Held a late court at Dunstable, six miles off","From Ampthill where the Princess lay; to which","She was often cited by them, but appear'd not;","And, to be short, for not appearance and","The King's late scruple, by the main assent","Of all these learned men she was divorc'd,","And the late marriage made of none effect;","Since which she was remov'd to Kimbolton,","Where she remains now sick."],"id":573,"m":"I can tell you that too. The Archbishop of Canterbury, with other learned and reverend fathers of his order, recently held a court at Dunstable, six miles from Ampthill where the Princess was staying. She was summoned to it many times but did not appear. To be brief, because she did not appear and because of the King's recent doubt of conscience, by the general agreement of all these learned men she was divorced and her marriage declared void. Since then she has been moved to Kimbolton, where she now lies ill."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["Alas, good lady!"],"id":574,"m":"Alas, good lady!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Trumpets.","id":575},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","cont":1,"lines":["The trumpets sound; stand close, the Queen is coming."],"id":576,"m":"The trumpets are sounding; stand back, the Queen is coming."},{"t":"sd","text":"Hautboys.","id":577},{"t":"sd","text":"The order of the coronation. 1. A lively flourish of trumpets. 2. Then, Two Judges. 3. Lord Chancellor, with purse and mace before him. 4. Choristers, singing. Music. 5. Mayor of London, bearing the mace. Then Garter, in his coat of arms, and on his head he wore a gilt copper crown. 6. Marquess Dorset, bearing a sceptre of gold, on his head a demi-coronal of gold. With him, the Earl of Surrey, bearing the rod of silver with the dove, crowned with an earl's coronet. Collars of SS. 7. Duke of Suffolk, in his robe of estate, his coronet on his head, bearing a long white wand, as high steward. With him, The Duke of Norfolk, with the rod of marshalship, a coronet on his head. Collars of SS. 8. A canopy borne by four of the Cinque-ports; under it, the Queen in her robe, in her hair richly adorned with pearl, crowned. On each side her, the Bishops of London and Winchester. 9. The old Duchess of Norfolk, in a coronal of gold, wrought with flowers, bearing the Queen's train. 10. Certain Ladies or Countesses, with plain circlets of gold without flowers.","id":578},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt, first passing over the stage in order and state, and then a great flourish of trumpets.","id":579},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["A royal train, believe me. These I know.","Who's that that bears the sceptre?"],"id":580,"m":"A royal procession, believe me. I know these. Who's that carrying the sceptre?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Marquess Dorset;","And that the Earl of Surrey, with the rod."],"id":581,"m":"Marquess Dorset; and that's the Earl of Surrey, with the rod."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["A bold brave gentleman. That should be","The Duke of Suffolk?"],"id":582,"m":"A bold, brave gentleman. That must be the Duke of Suffolk?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["'Tis the same: High Steward."],"id":583,"m":"The same: the High Steward."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["And that my Lord of Norfolk?"],"id":584,"m":"And that's my Lord of Norfolk?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Yes."],"id":585,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["Heaven bless thee! [Looking on the Queen]","Thou hast the sweetest face I ever look'd on.","Sir, as I have a soul, she is an angel;","Our king has all the Indies in his arms,","And more and richer, when he strains that lady.","I cannot blame his conscience."],"id":586,"m":"(Looking at the Queen) Heaven bless you! You have the sweetest face I ever saw. Sir, as I have a soul, she is an angel. Our king holds all the Indies in his arms, and more and richer, when he hugs that lady. I can't blame his conscience."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["They that bear","The cloth of honour over her, are four barons","Of the Cinque-ports."],"id":587,"m":"The men carrying the canopy of honour over her are four barons of the Cinque Ports."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["Those men are happy; and so are all are near her.","I take it, she that carries up the train","Is that old noble lady, Duchess of Norfolk."],"id":588,"m":"Those men are lucky, and so is everyone near her. I take it the one carrying her train is that old noble lady, the Duchess of Norfolk."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["It is; and all the rest are countesses."],"id":589,"m":"It is; and all the rest are countesses."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["Their coronets say so. These are stars indeed;","And sometimes falling ones."],"id":590,"m":"Their coronets say so. These are stars indeed, and sometimes falling ones."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["No more of that."],"id":591,"m":"No more of that."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit the last of the procession.","id":592},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a third Gentleman.","id":593},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","cont":1,"lines":["God save you, sir! Where have you been broiling?"],"id":594,"m":"God save you, sir! Where have you been roasting?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","lines":["Among the crowds i' the Abbey, where a finger","Could not be wedg'd in more. I am stifled","With the mere rankness of their joy."],"id":595,"m":"In the crowds in the Abbey, where you couldn't have wedged in another finger. I'm stifled by the sheer stink of their joy."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["You saw the ceremony?"],"id":596,"m":"You saw the ceremony?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","lines":["That I did."],"id":597,"m":"I did."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["How was it?"],"id":598,"m":"How was it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","lines":["Well worth the seeing."],"id":599,"m":"Well worth seeing."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["Good sir, speak it to us."],"id":600,"m":"Good sir, tell us about it."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","lines":["As well as I am able. The rich stream","Of lords and ladies, having brought the Queen","To a prepar'd place in the choir, fell of","A distance from her; while her Grace sat down","To rest a while, some half an hour or so,","In a rich chair of state, opposing freely","The beauty of her person to the people,—","Believe me, sir, she is the goodliest woman","That ever lay by man;—which when the people","Had the full view of, such a noise arose","As the shrouds make at sea in a stiff tempest,","As loud, and to as many tunes. Hats, cloaks,—","Doublets, I think,—flew up; and had their faces","Been loose, this day they had been lost. Such joy","I never saw before. Great-belli'd women,","That had not half a week to go, like rams","In the old time of war, would shake the press","And make 'em reel before 'em. No man living","Could say \"This is my wife\" there; all were woven","So strangely in one piece."],"id":601,"m":"As well as I can. The rich stream of lords and ladies, having brought the Queen to a place prepared in the choir, drew back a little from her, while her Grace sat down to rest for half an hour or so in a rich chair of state, freely showing the beauty of her person to the people. Believe me, sir, she is the finest woman who ever lay beside a man. When the people got a full view of her, such a noise went up as the rigging makes at sea in a hard storm, as loud and in as many tunes. Hats, cloaks, even doublets I think, flew into the air; and if their faces had been loose, they'd have lost them today. I never saw such joy. Heavily pregnant women with less than half a week to go would shake the crowd like battering rams in old wars and make people reel in front of them. No man alive could say \"This is my wife\" there; they were all so strangely woven into one piece."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["But what follow'd?"],"id":602,"m":"But what happened next?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","lines":["At length her Grace rose, and with modest paces","Came to the altar; where she kneel'd, and saintlike","Cast her fair eyes to heaven and pray'd devoutly;","Then rose again and bow'd her to the people,","When by the Archbishop of Canterbury","She had all the royal makings of a queen,","As holy oil, Edward Confessor's crown,","The rod, and bird of peace, and all such emblems","Laid nobly on her; which perform'd, the choir,","With all the choicest music of the kingdom,","Together sung \"Te Deum.\" So she parted,","And with the same full state pac'd back again","To York Place, where the feast is held."],"id":603,"m":"At last her Grace rose and with modest steps came to the altar, where she knelt and, like a saint, raised her lovely eyes to heaven and prayed devoutly. Then she rose again and bowed to the people, and the Archbishop of Canterbury nobly gave her all the royal marks of a queen: the holy oil, Edward the Confessor's crown, the rod, the dove of peace and all such emblems. When that was done, the choir, with all the finest musicians in the kingdom, sang the \"Te Deum\" together. Then she left, and with the same full ceremony walked back to York Place, where the feast is being held."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Sir,","You must no more call it York Place, that's past;","For, since the Cardinal fell, that title's lost.","'Tis now the King's, and call'd Whitehall."],"id":604,"m":"Sir, you mustn't call it York Place any more; that's over. Since the Cardinal fell that name is lost. It's the King's now, and called Whitehall."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","lines":["I know it;","But 'tis so lately alter'd, that the old name","Is fresh about me."],"id":605,"m":"I know; but it was changed so recently that the old name is still fresh with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["What two reverend bishops","Were those that went on each side of the Queen?"],"id":606,"m":"Who were the two reverend bishops walking on either side of the Queen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","lines":["Stokesly and Gardiner; the one of Winchester,","Newly preferr'd from the King's secretary;","The other, London."],"id":607,"m":"Stokesley and Gardiner; the one is Bishop of Winchester, newly promoted from the King's secretary; the other, of London."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["He of Winchester","Is held no great good lover of the Archbishop's,","The virtuous Cranmer."],"id":608,"m":"The Bishop of Winchester is said to be no great friend of the Archbishop, the virtuous Cranmer."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","lines":["All the land knows that.","However, yet there is no great breach; when it comes,","Cranmer will find a friend will not shrink from him."],"id":609,"m":"The whole country knows that. Still, there's no great split yet; when it comes, Cranmer will find a friend who won't shrink from him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["Who may that be, I pray you?"],"id":610,"m":"Who might that be, please?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","lines":["Thomas Cromwell;","A man in much esteem with the King, and truly","A worthy friend. The King has made him master","O' the jewel house,","And one, already, of the privy council."],"id":611,"m":"Thomas Cromwell, a man highly regarded by the King, and truly a worthy friend. The King has made him Master of the Jewel House, and already a member of the Privy Council."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["He will deserve more."],"id":612,"m":"He'll deserve more."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","lines":["Yes, without all doubt.","Come, gentlemen, ye shall go my way, which","Is to the court, and there ye shall be my guests;","Something I can command. As I walk thither,","I'll tell ye more."],"id":613,"m":"Yes, without any doubt. Come, gentlemen, you shall come my way, which is to court, and there you'll be my guests; I have some influence there. As we walk, I'll tell you more."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST AND SECOND GENTLEMEN","lines":["You may command us, sir."],"id":614,"m":"We are at your service, sir."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":615},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"Kimbolton.","id":616},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Katherine, Dowager, sick; led between Griffith, her gentleman usher, and Patience, her woman.","id":617},{"t":"sp","who":"GRIFFITH","lines":["How does your Grace?"],"id":618,"m":"How is your Grace?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["O Griffith, sick to death!","My legs, like loaden branches, bow to the earth,","Willing to leave their burden. Reach a chair.","So; now, methinks, I feel a little ease.","Didst thou not tell me, Griffith, as thou led'st me,","That the great child of honour, Cardinal Wolsey,","Was dead?"],"id":619,"m":"Oh, Griffith, sick to death! My legs bend to the earth like loaded branches, wanting to drop their burden. Bring a chair. There; now I think I feel a little easier. Didn't you tell me, Griffith, as you led me, that the great child of honour, Cardinal Wolsey, was dead?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRIFFITH","lines":["Yes, madam; but I think your Grace,","Out of the pain you suffer'd, gave no ear to't."],"id":620,"m":"Yes, madam; but I think your Grace, because of the pain you were in, didn't listen."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Prithee, good Griffith, tell me how he died.","If well, he stepp'd before me, happily","For my example."],"id":621,"m":"Please, good Griffith, tell me how he died. If he died well, he went before me happily, as an example to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRIFFITH","lines":["Well, the voice goes, madam:","For after the stout Earl Northumberland","Arrested him at York, and brought him forward,","As a man sorely tainted, to his answer,","He fell sick suddenly, and grew so ill","He could not sit his mule."],"id":622,"m":"Well, so people say, madam. After the bold Earl of Northumberland arrested him at York and brought him south, as a man badly disgraced, to answer the charges, he fell suddenly ill and grew so sick he couldn't sit on his mule."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Alas, poor man!"],"id":623,"m":"Alas, poor man!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRIFFITH","lines":["At last, with easy roads, he came to Leicester,","Lodg'd in the abbey; where the reverend abbot,","With all his covent, honourably receiv'd him;","To whom he gave these words: \"O, father abbot,","An old man, broken with the storms of state,","Is come to lay his weary bones among ye;","Give him a little earth for charity!\"","So went to bed, where eagerly his sickness","Pursu'd him still; and, three nights after this,","About the hour of eight, which he himself","Foretold should be his last, full of repentance,","Continual meditations, tears, and sorrows,","He gave his honours to the world again,","His blessed part to heaven, and slept in peace."],"id":624,"m":"At last, travelling in easy stages, he came to Leicester and lodged in the abbey, where the reverend abbot and all his monks received him with honour. He said to the abbot: \"Oh, father abbot, an old man broken by the storms of state has come to lay his weary bones among you; give him a little earth, for charity!\" So he went to bed, where his sickness kept pursuing him fiercely; and three nights later, about eight o'clock, the hour he himself had predicted would be his last, full of repentance, constant prayer, tears and sorrow, he gave his honours back to the world and his blessed soul to heaven, and slept in peace."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["So may he rest; his faults lie gently on him!","Yet thus far, Griffith, give me leave to speak him,","And yet with charity. He was a man","Of an unbounded stomach, ever ranking","Himself with princes; one that, by suggestion,","Tied all the kingdom. Simony was fair-play;","His own opinion was his law; i' the presence","He would say untruths; and be ever double","Both in his words and meaning. He was never,","But where he meant to ruin, pitiful.","His promises were, as he then was, mighty;","But his performance, as he is now, nothing.","Of his own body he was ill, and gave","The clergy ill example."],"id":625,"m":"So may he rest; may his faults lie lightly on him! Yet let me say this much of him, Griffith, and still with charity. He was a man of boundless pride, always ranking himself with princes; one who by his scheming bound the whole kingdom. He thought selling church offices fair play; his own opinion was his law; in the King's presence he would tell lies, and he was always two-faced in his words and meaning. He never showed pity except where he meant to destroy. His promises were mighty, as he was then; but his performance was nothing, as he is now. He was immoral in his body, and set the clergy a bad example."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRIFFITH","lines":["Noble madam,","Men's evil manners live in brass; their virtues","We write in water. May it please your Highness","To hear me speak his good now?"],"id":626,"m":"Noble madam, men's bad deeds live on in brass; their virtues we write in water. Would it please your Highness to hear me speak of his good qualities now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Yes, good Griffith;","I were malicious else."],"id":627,"m":"Yes, good Griffith; otherwise I would be malicious."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRIFFITH","lines":["This Cardinal,","Though from an humble stock, undoubtedly","Was fashion'd to much honour from his cradle.","He was a scholar, and a ripe and good one;","Exceeding wise, fair-spoken, and persuading;","Lofty and sour to them that lov'd him not,","But to those men that sought him, sweet as summer.","And though he were unsatisfied in getting,","Which was a sin, yet in bestowing, madam,","He was most princely: ever witness for him","Those twins of learning that he rais'd in you,","Ipswich and Oxford! one of which fell with him,","Unwilling to outlive the good that did it;","The other, though unfinish'd, yet so famous,","So excellent in art, and still so rising,","That Christendom shall ever speak his virtue.","His overthrow heap'd happiness upon him;","For then, and not till then, he felt himself,","And found the blessedness of being little;","And, to add greater honours to his age","Than man could give him, he died fearing God."],"id":628,"m":"This Cardinal, though from humble stock, was undoubtedly made for great honour from his cradle. He was a scholar, a mature and good one; extremely wise, well-spoken and persuasive; haughty and sour to those who didn't love him, but sweet as summer to those who sought him out. And though he was never satisfied in getting wealth, which was a sin, yet in giving, madam, he was most princely. Let those twin colleges of learning he founded always bear witness for him, Ipswich and Oxford! One of them fell with him, unwilling to outlive the good man who made it; the other, though unfinished, is already so famous, so excellent in learning and still so growing, that Christendom will always speak of his virtue. His fall heaped happiness on him, for then, and not before, he knew himself and found the blessing of being small; and, to add greater honour to his old age than any man could give him, he died fearing God."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["After my death I wish no other herald,","No other speaker of my living actions,","To keep mine honour from corruption,","But such an honest chronicler as Griffith.","Whom I most hated living, thou hast made me,","With thy religious truth and modesty,","Now in his ashes honour. Peace be with him!","Patience, be near me still, and set me lower:","I have not long to trouble thee. Good Griffith,","Cause the musicians play me that sad note","I nam'd my knell, whilst I sit meditating","On that celestial harmony I go to."],"id":629,"m":"After my death I want no other herald, no other reporter of my life's actions to keep my honour from decay, than an honest chronicler like Griffith. With your devout truthfulness and fairness, you have made me now honour in his ashes the man I most hated while he lived. Peace be with him! Patience, stay close to me, and lay me lower; I won't trouble you much longer. Good Griffith, have the musicians play me that sad tune I named my funeral bell, while I sit thinking about the heavenly harmony I am going to."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sad and solemn music.","id":630},{"t":"sp","who":"GRIFFITH","lines":["She is asleep. Good wench, let's sit down quiet,","For fear we wake her; softly, gentle Patience."],"id":631,"m":"She's asleep. Good girl, let's sit quietly so we don't wake her; softly, gentle Patience."},{"t":"sd","text":"The vision. Enter, solemnly tripping one after another, six personages, clad in white robes, wearing on their heads garlands of bays, and golden vizards on their faces; branches of bays or palm in their hands. They first congee unto her, then dance; and, at certain changes, the first two hold a spare garland over her head; at which the other four make reverent curtsies. Then the two that held the garland deliver the same to the other next two, who observe the same order in their changes, and holding the garland over her head; which done, they deliver the same garland to the last two, who likewise observe the same order; at which, as it were by inspiration, she makes in her sleep signs of rejoicing, and holdeth up her hands to heaven: and so in their dancing vanish, carrying the garland with them. The music continues.","id":632},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Spirits of peace, where are ye? Are ye all gone,","And leave me here in wretchedness behind ye?"],"id":633,"m":"Spirits of peace, where are you? Have you all gone and left me behind here in misery?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRIFFITH","lines":["Madam, we are here."],"id":634,"m":"Madam, we are here."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["It is not you I call for.","Saw ye none enter since I slept?"],"id":635,"m":"It's not you I'm calling for. Did you see no one come in while I slept?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRIFFITH","lines":["None, madam."],"id":636,"m":"No one, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["No? Saw you not, even now, a blessed troop","Invite me to a banquet; whose bright faces","Cast thousand beams upon me, like the sun?","They promis'd me eternal happiness,","And brought me garlands, Griffith, which I feel","I am not worthy yet to wear. I shall, assuredly."],"id":637,"m":"No? Didn't you see just now a blessed company inviting me to a banquet, whose bright faces shone a thousand beams on me like the sun? They promised me eternal happiness and brought me garlands, Griffith, which I feel I am not yet worthy to wear. I surely shall be."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRIFFITH","lines":["I am most joyful, madam, such good dreams","Possess your fancy."],"id":638,"m":"I am very glad, madam, that such good dreams fill your mind."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Bid the music leave,","They are harsh and heavy to me."],"id":639,"m":"Tell the music to stop; it is harsh and heavy to me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Music ceases.","id":640},{"t":"sp","who":"PATIENCE","lines":["Do you note","How much her Grace is alter'd on the sudden?","How long her face is drawn! How pale she looks,","And of an earthly cold! Mark her eyes!"],"id":641,"m":"Do you see how suddenly her Grace has changed? How drawn her face is! How pale she looks, and cold as earth! Look at her eyes!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRIFFITH","lines":["She is going, wench. Pray, pray."],"id":642,"m":"She is dying, girl. Pray, pray."},{"t":"sp","who":"PATIENCE","lines":["Heaven comfort her!"],"id":643,"m":"Heaven comfort her!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":644},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["An't like your Grace,—"],"id":645,"m":"If it please your Grace —"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["You are a saucy fellow.","Deserve we no more reverence?"],"id":646,"m":"You are an insolent fellow. Do we deserve no more respect?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRIFFITH","lines":["You are to blame,","Knowing she will not lose her wonted greatness,","To use so rude behaviour. Go to, kneel."],"id":647,"m":"You're at fault to behave so rudely, knowing she won't give up her customary rank. Come on, kneel."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["I humbly do entreat your Highness' pardon;","My haste made me unmannerly. There is staying","A gentleman, sent from the King, to see you."],"id":648,"m":"I humbly beg your Highness's pardon; my hurry made me rude. A gentleman sent by the King is waiting to see you."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Admit him entrance, Griffith; but this fellow","Let me ne'er see again."],"id":649,"m":"Let him in, Griffith; but let me never see this fellow again."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Messenger.","id":650},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Capucius.","id":651},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","cont":1,"lines":["If my sight fail not,","You should be lord ambassador from the Emperor,","My royal nephew, and your name Capucius."],"id":652,"m":"If my eyesight doesn't fail me, you must be the ambassador from the Emperor, my royal nephew, and your name is Capucius."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPUCIUS","lines":["Madam, the same; your servant."],"id":653,"m":"The same, madam; your servant."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["O, my lord,","The times and titles now are alter'd strangely","With me since first you knew me. But, I pray you,","What is your pleasure with me?"],"id":654,"m":"Oh, my lord, times and titles have changed strangely for me since you first knew me. But please, what do you want with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPUCIUS","lines":["Noble lady,","First, mine own service to your Grace; the next,","The King's request that I would visit you,","Who grieves much for your weakness, and by me","Sends you his princely commendations,","And heartily entreats you take good comfort."],"id":655,"m":"Noble lady, first, to offer my own service to your Grace; next, the King asked me to visit you. He is much grieved by your weakness, sends you his royal greetings through me, and earnestly begs you to take comfort."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["O my good lord, that comfort comes too late;","'Tis like a pardon after execution.","That gentle physic, given in time, had cur'd me;","But now I am past all comforts here, but prayers.","How does his Highness?"],"id":656,"m":"Oh, my good lord, that comfort comes too late; it's like a pardon after the execution. That gentle medicine, given in time, would have cured me; but now I am beyond all comforts here except prayers. How is his Highness?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPUCIUS","lines":["Madam, in good health."],"id":657,"m":"In good health, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["So may he ever do! and ever flourish,","When I shall dwell with worms, and my poor name","Banish'd the kingdom! Patience, is that letter,","I caused you write, yet sent away?"],"id":658,"m":"May he always be so, and always flourish, when I live with worms and my poor name is banished from the kingdom! Patience, has that letter I had you write been sent yet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATIENCE","lines":["No, madam."],"id":659,"m":"No, madam."},{"t":"sd","text":"Giving it to Katherine.","id":660},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["Sir, I most humbly pray you to deliver","This to my lord the King."],"id":661,"m":"Sir, I most humbly ask you to deliver this to my lord the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPUCIUS","lines":["Most willing, madam."],"id":662,"m":"Most willingly, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["In which I have commended to his goodness","The model of our chaste loves, his young daughter;","The dews of heaven fall thick in blessings on her!","Beseeching him to give her virtuous breeding,—","She is young, and of a noble modest nature,","I hope she will deserve well,—and a little","To love her for her mother's sake, that lov'd him,","Heaven knows how dearly. My next poor petition","Is, that his noble Grace would have some pity","Upon my wretched women, that so long","Have follow'd both my fortunes faithfully;","Of which there is not one, I dare avow,","And now I should not lie, but will deserve,","For virtue and true beauty of the soul,","For honesty and decent carriage,","A right good husband; let him be a noble;","And, sure, those men are happy that shall have 'em.","The last is, for my men,—they are the poorest,","But poverty could never draw 'em from me—","That they may have their wages duly paid 'em,","And something over to remember me by.","If Heaven had pleas'd to have given me longer life","And able means, we had not parted thus.","These are the whole contents; and, good my lord,","By that you love the dearest in this world,","As you wish Christian peace to souls departed,","Stand these poor people's friend, and urge the King","To do me this last right."],"id":663,"m":"In it I have commended to his goodness the image of our chaste love, his young daughter, may the dews of heaven fall thick with blessings on her, begging him to give her a virtuous upbringing. She is young, and of a noble, modest nature; I hope she will turn out well. And I ask him to love her a little for her mother's sake, who loved him, heaven knows how dearly. My next poor request is that his noble Grace take pity on my wretched women, who have followed me faithfully for so long through good fortune and bad. There is not one of them, I dare swear, and I should not lie now, who will not deserve a really good husband, for virtue and true beauty of soul, for chastity and good behaviour; let him be a nobleman; and surely the men who get them will be happy. The last is for my menservants, who are very poor, though poverty could never draw them away from me: that they may have their wages properly paid, and something extra to remember me by. If Heaven had been pleased to give me longer life and the means, we would not have parted like this. That is all it contains. And, my good lord, by whatever you love most in this world, as you wish Christian peace to departed souls, be a friend to these poor people and urge the King to do me this last justice."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPUCIUS","lines":["By heaven, I will,","Or let me lose the fashion of a man!"],"id":664,"m":"By heaven, I will, or let me lose the shape of a man!"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","lines":["I thank you, honest lord. Remember me","In all humility unto his Highness.","Say his long trouble now is passing","Out of this world; tell him, in death I bless'd him,","For so I will. Mine eyes grow dim. Farewell,","My lord. Griffith, farewell. Nay, Patience,","You must not leave me yet. I must to bed;","Call in more women. When I am dead, good wench,","Let me be us'd with honour. Strew me over","With maiden flowers, that all the world may know","I was a chaste wife to my grave. Embalm me,","Then lay me forth. Although unqueen'd, yet like","A queen, and daughter to a king, inter me.","I can no more."],"id":665,"m":"Thank you, honest lord. Remember me in all humility to his Highness. Tell him the one who troubled him so long is now passing out of this world; tell him I blessed him in death, for so I will. My eyes are growing dim. Farewell, my lord. Griffith, farewell. No, Patience, you mustn't leave me yet. I must go to bed; call in more women. When I am dead, good girl, let me be treated with honour. Scatter maiden flowers over me, so the whole world may know I was a chaste wife to my grave. Embalm me, then lay me out. Though I am no longer queen, bury me like a queen and a king's daughter. I can say no more."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt, leading Katherine.","id":666},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"A gallery in the palace.","id":667},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Gardiner, Bishop of Winchester, a page with a torch before him, met by Sir Thomas Lovell.","id":668},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["It's one o'clock, boy, is't not?"],"id":669,"m":"It's one o'clock, boy, isn't it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["It hath struck."],"id":670,"m":"It has struck one."},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["These should be hours for necessities,","Not for delights; times to repair our nature","With comforting repose, and not for us","To waste these times. Good hour of night, Sir Thomas!","Whither so late?"],"id":671,"m":"These should be hours for needs, not pleasures; times to restore our bodies with comforting rest, not for us to waste. Good night, Sir Thomas! Where are you going so late?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["Came you from the King, my lord?"],"id":672,"m":"Have you come from the King, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["I did, Sir Thomas; and left him at primero","With the Duke of Suffolk."],"id":673,"m":"I have, Sir Thomas, and left him playing primero with the Duke of Suffolk."},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["I must to him too,","Before he go to bed. I'll take my leave."],"id":674,"m":"I must go to him too, before he goes to bed. I'll say goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["Not yet, Sir Thomas Lovell. What's the matter?","It seems you are in haste. An if there be","No great offence belongs to't, give your friend","Some touch of your late business. Affairs, that walk,","As they say spirits do, at midnight, have","In them a wilder nature than the business","That seeks despatch by day."],"id":675,"m":"Not yet, Sir Thomas Lovell. What's the matter? You seem in a hurry. If there's no great harm in it, give your friend some hint of your business tonight. Affairs that walk at midnight, as they say ghosts do, have a wilder nature than business that is done by day."},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["My lord, I love you;","And durst commend a secret to your ear","Much weightier than this work. The Queen's in labour,","They say in great extremity; and fear'd","She'll with the labour end."],"id":676,"m":"My lord, I love you, and I'd dare trust a secret to your ear much weightier than this errand. The Queen is in labour, they say in great danger, and they fear she will die in childbirth."},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["The fruit she goes with","I pray for heartily, that it may find","Good time, and live; but for the stock, Sir Thomas,","I wish it grubb'd up now."],"id":677,"m":"I pray with all my heart that the fruit she carries may be born safely and live; but as for the tree, Sir Thomas, I wish it were dug up now."},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["Methinks I could","Cry thee amen; and yet my conscience says","She's a good creature, and, sweet lady, does","Deserve our better wishes."],"id":678,"m":"I think I could say amen to that; and yet my conscience tells me she's a good creature, and, sweet lady, deserves better wishes from us."},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["But, sir, sir,","Hear me, Sir Thomas. You're a gentleman","Of mine own way; I know you wise, religious;","And, let me tell you, it will ne'er be well,","'Twill not, Sir Thomas Lovell, take't of me,","Till Cranmer, Cromwell, her two hands, and she,","Sleep in their graves."],"id":679,"m":"But sir, sir, listen to me, Sir Thomas. You are a gentleman of my own persuasion; I know you are wise and religious. Let me tell you, it will never be well, it won't, Sir Thomas Lovell, take it from me, until Cranmer, Cromwell, her two hands, and she herself sleep in their graves."},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["Now, sir, you speak of two","The most remark'd i' the kingdom. As for Cromwell,","Beside that of the jewel house, is made master","O' the rolls, and the King's secretary; further, sir,","Stands in the gap and trade of moe preferments,","With which the time will load him. The Archbishop","Is the King's hand and tongue; and who dare speak","One syllable against him?"],"id":680,"m":"Now, sir, you're speaking of the two most prominent men in the kingdom. As for Cromwell, besides the Jewel House, he's been made Master of the Rolls and the King's secretary; and further, sir, he stands in the path of more promotions, which time will load on him. The Archbishop is the King's hand and tongue; who dares say one word against him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["Yes, yes, Sir Thomas,","There are that dare; and I myself have ventur'd","To speak my mind of him: and indeed this day,","Sir, I may tell it you, I think I have","Incens'd the lords o' the council, that he is,","For so I know he is, they know he is,","A most arch heretic, a pestilence","That does infect the land; with which they moved","Have broken with the King, who hath so far","Given ear to our complaint, of his great grace","And princely care foreseeing those fell mischiefs","Our reasons laid before him, hath commanded","To-morrow morning to the council-board","He be convented. He's a rank weed, Sir Thomas,","And we must root him out. From your affairs","I hinder you too long. Good-night, Sir Thomas."],"id":681,"m":"Yes, yes, Sir Thomas, there are those who dare, and I myself have risked speaking my mind about him. Indeed, sir, I can tell you, today I think I have convinced the lords of the council that he is, for I know he is, and they know he is, a most arch-heretic, a plague infecting the land. Moved by this, they have raised it with the King, who has listened so far to our complaint that, out of his great grace and royal care, foreseeing the deadly harm our arguments set before him, he has ordered him to be summoned before the council tomorrow morning. He's a rank weed, Sir Thomas, and we must root him out. I'm keeping you too long from your business. Good night, Sir Thomas."},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["Many good-nights, my lord! I rest your servant."],"id":682,"m":"Many good nights, my lord! I remain your servant."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Gardiner and Page.","id":683},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the King and Suffolk.","id":684},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Charles, I will play no more to-night.","My mind's not on't; you are too hard for me."],"id":685,"m":"Charles, I won't play any more tonight. My mind isn't on it; you're too good for me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Sir, I did never win of you before."],"id":686,"m":"Sir, I never beat you before."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["But little, Charles;","Nor shall not, when my fancy's on my play.","Now, Lovell, from the Queen what is the news?"],"id":687,"m":"Only a little, Charles; and you won't when my mind is on my game. Now, Lovell, what news from the Queen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["I could not personally deliver to her","What you commanded me, but by her woman","I sent your message; who return'd her thanks","In the great'st humbleness, and desir'd your Highness","Most heartily to pray for her."],"id":688,"m":"I couldn't deliver your message to her in person, but I sent it by her woman, who brought back her thanks in the greatest humility and asked your Highness most earnestly to pray for her."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["What say'st thou, ha?","To pray for her? What, is she crying out?"],"id":689,"m":"What are you saying, ha? To pray for her? What, is she crying out in labour?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["So said her woman; and that her suff'rance made","Almost each pang a death."],"id":690,"m":"So her woman said, and that her suffering made almost every pang a death."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Alas, good lady!"],"id":691,"m":"Alas, good lady!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["God safely quit her of her burden, and","With gentle travail, to the gladding of","Your Highness with an heir!"],"id":692,"m":"May God deliver her safely of her burden, with an easy labour, and gladden your Highness with an heir!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["'Tis midnight, Charles;","Prithee, to bed; and in thy prayers remember","The estate of my poor queen. Leave me alone;","For I must think of that which company","Will not be friendly to."],"id":693,"m":"It's midnight, Charles; please go to bed, and remember my poor queen's condition in your prayers. Leave me alone, for I must think of something company will not help."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["I wish your Highness","A quiet night; and my good mistress will","Remember in my prayers."],"id":694,"m":"I wish your Highness a peaceful night, and I will remember my good mistress in my prayers."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Charles, good-night."],"id":695,"m":"Good night, Charles."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Suffolk.","id":696},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Anthony Denny.","id":697},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","cont":1,"lines":["Well, sir, what follows?"],"id":698,"m":"Well, sir, what is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DENNY","lines":["Sir, I have brought my lord the Archbishop,","As you commanded me."],"id":699,"m":"Sir, I have brought my lord the Archbishop, as you ordered."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Ha! Canterbury?"],"id":700,"m":"Ha! Canterbury?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DENNY","lines":["Ay, my good lord."],"id":701,"m":"Yes, my good lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["'Tis true; where is he, Denny?"],"id":702,"m":"That's right; where is he, Denny?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DENNY","lines":["He attends your Highness' pleasure."],"id":703,"m":"He awaits your Highness's pleasure."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Bring him to us."],"id":704,"m":"Bring him to us."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Denny.","id":705},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["[Aside] This is about that which the bishop spake.","I am happily come hither."],"id":706,"m":"(Aside) This is about what the bishop was talking about. I've come here at a lucky moment."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Denny, with Cranmer.","id":707},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Avoid the gallery. [Lovell seems to stay]","Ha! I have said. Be gone.","What!"],"id":708,"m":"Clear the gallery. (Lovell seems to stay) Ha! I've said so. Go. What!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Lovell and Denny.","id":709},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["[Aside] I am fearful; wherefore frowns he thus?","'Tis his aspect of terror. All's not well."],"id":710,"m":"(Aside) I'm afraid; why is he frowning like this? It's his look of terror. All is not well."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["How now, my lord! you do desire to know","Wherefore I sent for you."],"id":711,"m":"Well, my lord! You want to know why I sent for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["[Kneeling] It is my duty","To attend your Highness' pleasure."],"id":712,"m":"(Kneeling) It is my duty to wait on your Highness's wishes."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Pray you, arise,","My good and gracious Lord of Canterbury.","Come, you and I must walk a turn together;","I have news to tell you. Come, come, me your hand.","Ah, my good lord, I grieve at what I speak,","And am right sorry to repeat what follows.","I have, and most unwillingly, of late","Heard many grievous, I do say, my lord,","Grievous complaints of you; which, being consider'd,","Have mov'd us and our council, that you shall","This morning come before us; where, I know,","You cannot with such freedom purge yourself","But that, till further trial in those charges","Which will require your answer, you must take","Your patience to you, and be well contented","To make your house our Tower. You a brother of us,","It fits we thus proceed, or else no witness","Would come against you."],"id":713,"m":"Please rise, my good and gracious Lord of Canterbury. Come, you and I must take a walk together; I have news to tell you. Come, come, give me your hand. Ah, my good lord, I grieve at what I'm saying, and am truly sorry to repeat what follows. Recently, and very unwillingly, I have heard many serious, I say, my lord, serious complaints about you. Having considered them, we and our council have decided that you shall come before us this morning; where, I know, you cannot clear yourself so freely but that, until there is a further trial of the charges you must answer, you must be patient and be content to make your home in our Tower. Since you are one of our council, it is right we proceed this way, or else no witness would dare come forward against you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["[Kneeling] I humbly thank your Highness;","And am right glad to catch this good occasion","Most throughly to be winnowed, where my chaff","And corn shall fly asunder; for, I know,","There's none stands under more calumnious tongues","Than I myself, poor man."],"id":714,"m":"(Kneeling) I humbly thank your Highness, and am very glad of this good chance to be thoroughly winnowed, so that my chaff and grain will fly apart; for I know no one suffers under more slanderous tongues than I do, poor man."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Stand up, good Canterbury!","Thy truth and thy integrity is rooted","In us, thy friend. Give me thy hand, stand up;","Prithee, let's walk. Now, by my holidame,","What manner of man are you? My lord, I look'd","You would have given me your petition, that","I should have ta'en some pains to bring together","Yourself and your accusers; and to have heard you,","Without indurance, further."],"id":715,"m":"Stand up, good Canterbury! Your truth and integrity are rooted in us, your friend. Give me your hand; stand up. Please, let's walk. Now, by Our Lady, what kind of man are you? My lord, I expected you to petition me to take some trouble to bring you and your accusers face to face, and to hear you further without imprisonment."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["Most dread liege,","The good I stand on is my truth and honesty.","If they shall fail, I, with mine enemies,","Will triumph o'er my person; which I weigh not,","Being of those virtues vacant. I fear nothing","What can be said against me."],"id":716,"m":"Most feared sovereign, the good I stand on is my truth and honesty. If they fail, I will join my enemies in triumphing over my person, which I don't value if it lacks those virtues. I fear nothing that can be said against me."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Know you not","How your state stands i' th' world, with the whole world?","Your enemies are many, and not small; their practices","Must bear the same proportion; and not ever","The justice and the truth o' the question carries","The due o' the verdict with it. At what ease","Might corrupt minds procure knaves as corrupt","To swear against you? Such things have been done.","You are potently oppos'd, and with a malice","Of as great size. Ween you of better luck,","I mean, in perjur'd witness, than your Master,","Whose minister you are, whiles here He liv'd","Upon this naughty earth? Go to, go to!","You take a precipice for no leap of danger,","And woo your own destruction."],"id":717,"m":"Don't you know how you stand in the world, with the whole world? Your enemies are many, and not small; their plots must be just as great. And the justice and truth of a case do not always bring the verdict it deserves. How easily corrupt minds could find rogues just as corrupt to swear against you! Such things have been done. You are powerfully opposed, and with malice just as great. Do you expect better luck with perjured witnesses than your Master, whose minister you are, had while He lived on this wicked earth? Come on, come on! You take a cliff edge for no dangerous leap, and court your own destruction."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["God and your Majesty","Protect mine innocence, or I fall into","The trap is laid for me!"],"id":718,"m":"God and your Majesty protect my innocence, or I fall into the trap that has been set for me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Be of good cheer;","They shall no more prevail than we give way to.","Keep comfort to you; and this morning see","You do appear before them. If they shall chance,","In charging you with matters, to commit you,","The best persuasions to the contrary","Fail not to use, and with what vehemency","The occasion shall instruct you. If entreaties","Will render you no remedy, this ring","Deliver them, and your appeal to us","There make before them. Look, the good man weeps!","He's honest, on mine honour. God's blest mother!","I swear he is true-hearted; and a soul","None better in my kingdom. Get you gone,","And do as I have bid you."],"id":719,"m":"Cheer up; they shall get no further than we allow. Keep your courage, and see that you appear before them this morning. If, in charging you, they happen to commit you to prison, don't fail to use the best arguments against it, as forcefully as the moment teaches you. If pleading gets you no remedy, give them this ring and make your appeal to us there in front of them. Look, the good man is weeping! He's honest, on my honour. By God's blessed mother, I swear he is true-hearted, and there's no better soul in my kingdom. Go now, and do as I've told you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Cranmer.","id":720},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","cont":1,"lines":["He has strangled his language in his tears."],"id":721,"m":"His tears have choked his words."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Old Lady, Lovell following.","id":722},{"t":"sp","who":"GENTLEMAN","lines":["[Within] Come back! What mean you?"],"id":723,"m":"(Within) Come back! What do you mean by it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD LADY","lines":["I'll not come back; the tidings that I bring","Will make my boldness manners. Now, good angels","Fly o'er thy royal head, and shade thy person","Under their blessed wings!"],"id":724,"m":"I won't come back; the news I bring will turn my boldness into good manners. Now may good angels fly over your royal head and shade you under their blessed wings!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Now, by thy looks","I guess thy message. Is the Queen deliver'd?","Say ay; and of a boy."],"id":725,"m":"Now, from your looks I guess your message. Has the Queen given birth? Say yes, and to a boy."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD LADY","lines":["Ay, ay, my liege;","And of a lovely boy. The God of Heaven","Both now and ever bless her! 'tis a girl,","Promises boys hereafter. Sir, your queen","Desires your visitation, and to be","Acquainted with this stranger. 'Tis as like you","As cherry is to cherry."],"id":726,"m":"Yes, yes, my lord, and to a lovely boy. May the God of Heaven bless her now and always! It's a girl, which promises boys later. Sir, your queen wants you to visit her and meet this newcomer. It's as like you as one cherry is to another."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Lovell!"],"id":727,"m":"Lovell!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LOVELL","lines":["Sir?"],"id":728,"m":"Sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Give her an hundred marks. I'll to the Queen."],"id":729,"m":"Give her a hundred marks. I'm going to the Queen."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":730},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD LADY","lines":["An hundred marks! By this light, I'll ha' more.","An ordinary groom is for such payment.","I will have more, or scold it out of him.","Said I for this, the girl was like to him?","I will have more, or else unsay't; and now,","While it is hot, I'll put it to the issue."],"id":731,"m":"A hundred marks! By this daylight, I'll have more. That's payment for an ordinary servant. I'll have more, or scold it out of him. Did I say the girl looked like him for this? I'll have more, or else take it back; and now, while it's hot, I'll settle it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":732},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"Lobby before the council-chamber.","id":733},{"t":"sd","text":"Pursuivants, Pages, etc., attending. Enter Cranmer, Archbishop of Canterbury.","id":734},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["I hope I am not too late; and yet the gentleman,","That was sent to me from the council, pray'd me","To make great haste. All fast? what means this? Ho!","Who waits there? Sure, you know me?"],"id":735,"m":"I hope I'm not too late; and yet the gentleman the council sent me begged me to hurry. All locked? What does this mean? Hello! Who's on duty there? Surely you know me?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Keeper.","id":736},{"t":"sp","who":"KEEPER","lines":["Yes, my lord;","But yet I cannot help you."],"id":737,"m":"Yes, my lord; but still I can't help you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["Why?"],"id":738,"m":"Why?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KEEPER","lines":["Your Grace must wait till you be call'd for."],"id":739,"m":"Your Grace must wait until you are called."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Doctor Butts.","id":740},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["So."],"id":741,"m":"So."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUTTS","lines":["[Aside] This is a piece of malice. I am glad","I came this way so happily; the King","Shall understand it presently."],"id":742,"m":"(Aside) This is a piece of malice. I'm glad I happened to come this way; the King shall know of it at once."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":743},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["[Aside] 'Tis Butts,","The King's physician. As he pass'd along,","How earnestly he cast his eyes upon me!","Pray Heaven, he sound not my disgrace! For certain,","This is of purpose laid by some that hate me—","God turn their hearts! I never sought their malice—","To quench mine honour; they would shame to make me","Wait else at door, a fellow-counsellor,","'Mong boys, grooms, and lackeys. But their pleasures","Must be fulfill'd, and I attend with patience."],"id":744,"m":"(Aside) That's Butts, the King's physician. How intently he looked at me as he passed! Heaven grant he isn't sounding out my disgrace! Surely this has been arranged on purpose by some who hate me, God change their hearts, I never sought their malice, to snuff out my honour; otherwise they'd be ashamed to make me, a fellow councillor, wait at the door among boys, grooms and footmen. But their wishes must be carried out, and I'll wait patiently."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the King and Butts, at a window above.","id":745},{"t":"sp","who":"BUTTS","lines":["I'll show your Grace the strangest sight—"],"id":746,"m":"I'll show your Grace the strangest sight —"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["What's that, Butts?"],"id":747,"m":"What's that, Butts?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BUTTS","lines":["I think your Highness saw this many a day."],"id":748,"m":"I think your Highness hasn't seen this for many a day."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Body o' me, where is it?"],"id":749,"m":"Bless me, where is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BUTTS","lines":["There, my lord,","The high promotion of his Grace of Canterbury;","Who holds his state at door, 'mongst pursuivants,","Pages, and footboys."],"id":750,"m":"There, my lord: the high promotion of his Grace of Canterbury, who holds court at the door among messengers, pages and footboys."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Ha! 'tis he, indeed.","Is this the honour they do one another?","'Tis well there's one above 'em yet. I had thought","They had parted so much honesty among 'em,","At least, good manners, as not thus to suffer","A man of his place, and so near our favour,","To dance attendance on their lordships' pleasures,","And at the door too, like a post with packets.","By holy Mary, Butts, there's knavery.","Let 'em alone, and draw the curtain close;","We shall hear more anon."],"id":751,"m":"Ha! It's him indeed. Is this the honour they pay one another? It's a good thing there's still one above them. I'd have thought they had enough honesty among them, or at least good manners, not to let a man of his rank, so close to our favour, dance attendance on their lordships' pleasure, and at the door too, like a messenger with letters. By holy Mary, Butts, there's villainy here. Leave them alone, and draw the curtain shut; we'll hear more soon."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":752},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"III","loc":"The council-chamber.","id":753},{"t":"sd","text":"A council-table brought in with chairs and stools, and placed under the state. Enter Lord Chancellor; places himself at the upper end of the table on the left hand, a seat being left void above him, as for Canterbury's seat. Duke of Suffolk, Duke of Norfolk, Surrey, Lord Chamberlain, Gardiner, seat themselves in order on each side. Cromwell at lower end, as secretary. Keeper at the door.","id":754},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHANCELLOR","lines":["Speak to the business, master secretary.","Why are we met in council?"],"id":755,"m":"Come to the business, master secretary. Why are we meeting in council?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["Please your honours,","The chief cause concerns his Grace of Canterbury."],"id":756,"m":"If it please your honours, the main matter concerns his Grace of Canterbury."},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["Has he had knowledge of it?"],"id":757,"m":"Has he been told of it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["Yes."],"id":758,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Who waits there?"],"id":759,"m":"Who's on duty there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KEEPER","lines":["Without, my noble lords?"],"id":760,"m":"Outside, my noble lords?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["Yes."],"id":761,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"KEEPER","lines":["My Lord Archbishop;","And has done half an hour, to know your pleasures."],"id":762,"m":"My Lord Archbishop, and he has been for half an hour, waiting to know your wishes."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHANCELLOR","lines":["Let him come in."],"id":763,"m":"Let him come in."},{"t":"sp","who":"KEEPER","lines":["Your Grace may enter now."],"id":764,"m":"Your Grace may come in now."},{"t":"sd","text":"Cranmer approaches the council-table.","id":765},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHANCELLOR","lines":["My good Lord Archbishop, I'm very sorry","To sit here at this present, and behold","That chair stand empty; but we all are men,","In our own natures frail, and capable","Of our flesh; few are angels: out of which frailty","And want of wisdom, you, that best should teach us,","Have misdemean'd yourself, and not a little,","Toward the King first, then his laws, in filling","The whole realm, by your teaching and your chaplains,","For so we are inform'd, with new opinions","Divers and dangerous, which are heresies","And, not reform'd, may prove pernicious."],"id":766,"m":"My good Lord Archbishop, I'm very sorry to sit here now and see that chair stand empty. But we are all men, frail by nature and subject to the weakness of the flesh; few are angels. Out of that frailty and lack of wisdom, you, who ought most to teach us, have behaved wrongly, and not a little, toward the King first and then his laws, by filling the whole kingdom, through your teaching and your chaplains, as we are informed, with new opinions, varied and dangerous, which are heresies and, if not reformed, may prove deadly."},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["Which reformation must be sudden too,","My noble lords; for those that tame wild horses","Pace 'em not in their hands to make 'em gentle,","But stop their mouth with stubborn bits and spur 'em","Till they obey the manage. If we suffer,","Out of our easiness and childish pity","To one man's honour, this contagious sickness,","Farewell all physic! And what follows then?","Commotions, uproars, with a general taint","Of the whole state; as, of late days, our neighbours,","The upper Germany, can dearly witness,","Yet freshly pitied in our memories."],"id":767,"m":"And that reform must be quick too, my noble lords. People who tame wild horses don't walk them about in their hands to make them gentle; they stop their mouths with hard bits and spur them until they obey their training. If, out of our softness and childish pity for one man's honour, we allow this infectious sickness, farewell to all medicine! And what follows then? Riots and uproars, with the whole state infected, as our neighbours in upper Germany can bitterly witness, still freshly pitied in our memories."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["My good lords, hitherto in all the progress","Both of my life and office, I have labour'd,","And with no little study, that my teaching","And the strong course of my authority","Might go one way, and safely; and the end","Was ever, to do well; nor is there living,","I speak it with a single heart, my lords,","A man that more detests, more stirs against,","Both in his private conscience and his place,","Defacers of a public peace, than I do.","Pray Heaven, the King may never find a heart","With less allegiance in it! Men that make","Envy and crooked malice nourishment","Dare bite the best. I do beseech your lordships,","That, in this case of justice, my accusers,","Be what they will, may stand forth face to face","And freely urge against me."],"id":768,"m":"My good lords, until now, in the whole course of my life and office, I have worked, with no small study, to make my teaching and the firm exercise of my authority go one way, and safely; and my aim was always to do good. There is no man alive, I say it with an honest heart, my lords, who hates more, or works harder against, those who disturb the public peace, both in his private conscience and his office, than I do. I pray Heaven the King may never find a heart with less loyalty in it! Men who feed on envy and crooked malice dare bite the best. I beg your lordships that, in this matter of justice, my accusers, whoever they are, may stand face to face with me and freely make their charges."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Nay, my lord,","That cannot be. You are a counsellor,","And, by that virtue, no man dare accuse you."],"id":769,"m":"No, my lord, that can't be. You are a member of the council, and because of that no man dares accuse you."},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["My lord, because we have business of more moment,","We will be short with you. 'Tis his Highness' pleasure","And our consent, for better trial of you,","From hence you be committed to the Tower;","Where, being but a private man again,","You shall know many dare accuse you boldly,","More than, I fear, you are provided for."],"id":770,"m":"My lord, because we have more important business, we'll be brief with you. It is his Highness's wish, and our decision, for a fuller trial of you, that you be taken from here to the Tower; where, being only a private man again, you'll find many who dare accuse you boldly, more than you are prepared for, I fear."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["Ah, my good Lord of Winchester, I thank you.","You are always my good friend; if your will pass,","I shall both find your lordship judge and juror,","You are so merciful. I see your end;","'Tis my undoing. Love and meekness, lord,","Become a churchman better than ambition.","Win straying souls with modesty again,","Cast none away. That I shall clear myself,","Lay all the weight ye can upon my patience,","I make as little doubt as you do conscience","In doing daily wrongs. I could say more,","But reverence to your calling makes me modest."],"id":771,"m":"Ah, my good Lord of Winchester, I thank you. You are always my good friend; if you get your way, I'll find your lordship both judge and jury, you're so merciful. I see your aim; it's my ruin. Love and meekness, my lord, suit a churchman better than ambition. Win back straying souls with gentleness; cast none away. Lay all the weight you can on my patience; I have as little doubt that I will clear myself as you have conscience in doing daily wrongs. I could say more, but respect for your office keeps me modest."},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["My lord, my lord, you are a sectary,","That's the plain truth. Your painted gloss discovers,","To men that understand you, words and weakness."],"id":772,"m":"My lord, my lord, you are a sectarian; that's the plain truth. To men who understand you, your painted gloss reveals mere words and weakness."},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["My Lord of Winchester, you are a little,","By your good favour, too sharp; men so noble,","However faulty, yet should find respect","For what they have been. 'Tis a cruelty","To load a falling man."],"id":773,"m":"My Lord of Winchester, with respect, you are a little too sharp. Men so noble, however much at fault, should still be respected for what they have been. It is cruel to pile weight on a falling man."},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["Good master secretary,","I cry your honour mercy. You may, worst","Of all this table, say so."],"id":774,"m":"Good master secretary, I beg your honour's pardon. You, least of anyone at this table, have the right to say so."},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["Why, my lord?"],"id":775,"m":"Why, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["Do not I know you for a favourer","Of this new sect? Ye are not sound."],"id":776,"m":"Don't I know you are a supporter of this new sect? You're not sound."},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["Not sound?"],"id":777,"m":"Not sound?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["Not sound, I say."],"id":778,"m":"Not sound, I say."},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["Would you were half so honest!","Men's prayers then would seek you, not their fears."],"id":779,"m":"I wish you were half as honest! Then men would pray for you instead of fearing you."},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["I shall remember this bold language."],"id":780,"m":"I shall remember this bold talk."},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["Do.","Remember your bold life too."],"id":781,"m":"Do. Remember your bold life too."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHANCELLOR","lines":["This is too much.","Forbear, for shame, my lords."],"id":782,"m":"This is too much. Stop, for shame, my lords."},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["I have done."],"id":783,"m":"I've finished."},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["And I."],"id":784,"m":"And I."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHANCELLOR","lines":["Then thus for you, my lord: it stands agreed,","I take it, by all voices, that forthwith","You be convey'd to the Tower a prisoner;","There to remain till the King's further pleasure","Be known unto us. Are you all agreed, lords?"],"id":785,"m":"Then this is for you, my lord: it is agreed, I take it, by everyone, that you be taken to the Tower as a prisoner at once, to stay there until the King's further wishes are made known to us. Are you all agreed, lords?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["We are."],"id":786,"m":"We are."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["Is there no other way of mercy,","But I must needs to the Tower, my lords?"],"id":787,"m":"Is there no other merciful way? Must I go to the Tower, my lords?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["What other","Would you expect? You are strangely troublesome.","Let some o' the guard be ready there."],"id":788,"m":"What other way would you expect? You are strangely troublesome. Have some of the guard ready there."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the guard.","id":789},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["For me?","Must I go like a traitor thither?"],"id":790,"m":"For me? Must I go there like a traitor?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["Receive him,","And see him safe i' the Tower."],"id":791,"m":"Take him, and see him safely in the Tower."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["Stay, good my lords,","I have a little yet to say. Look there, my lords;","By virtue of that ring, I take my cause","Out of the gripes of cruel men, and give it","To a most noble judge, the King my master."],"id":792,"m":"Wait, my good lords, I have a little more to say. Look there, my lords. By the power of that ring, I take my case out of the grip of cruel men and give it to a most noble judge, the King my master."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["This is the King's ring."],"id":793,"m":"This is the King's ring."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["'Tis no counterfeit."],"id":794,"m":"It's no fake."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["'Tis the right ring, by heaven! I told ye all,","When we first put this dangerous stone a-rolling,","'Twould fall upon ourselves."],"id":795,"m":"It's the real ring, by heaven! I told you all, when we first set this dangerous stone rolling, it would fall back on us."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORFOLK","lines":["Do you think, my lords,","The King will suffer but the little finger","Of this man to be vex'd?"],"id":796,"m":"Do you think, my lords, the King will allow even this man's little finger to be harmed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["'Tis now too certain.","How much more is his life in value with him?","Would I were fairly out on't!"],"id":797,"m":"That's now only too certain. How much more must his life be worth to him? I wish I were well out of this!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CROMWELL","lines":["My mind gave me,","In seeking tales and informations","Against this man, whose honesty the devil","And his disciples only envy at,","Ye blew the fire that burns ye. Now have at ye!"],"id":798,"m":"I had a feeling that, in hunting for tales and reports against this man, whose honesty only the devil and his followers envy, you were blowing on the fire that burns you. Now look out!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter King, frowning on them; takes his seat.","id":799},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["Dread sovereign, how much are we bound to Heaven","In daily thanks, that gave us such a prince;","Not only good and wise, but most religious;","One that, in all obedience, makes the Church","The chief aim of his honour; and, to strengthen","That holy duty, out of dear respect,","His royal self in judgement comes to hear","The cause betwixt her and this great offender."],"id":800,"m":"Dread sovereign, how much we owe Heaven daily thanks for giving us such a prince, not only good and wise but most devout, one who in all obedience makes the Church the chief aim of his honour; and who, to strengthen that holy duty, out of loving respect, comes in his royal person to hear the case between her and this great offender."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["You were ever good at sudden commendations,","Bishop of Winchester. But know, I come not","To hear such flattery now, and in my presence;","They are too thin and bare to hide offences.","To me you cannot reach you play the spaniel,","And think with wagging of your tongue to win me;","But, whatsoe'er thou tak'st me for, I'm sure","Thou hast a cruel nature and a bloody.","[To Cranmer] Good man, sit down. Now let me see the proudest","He, that dares most, but wag his finger at thee:","By all that's holy, he had better starve","Than but once think this place becomes thee not."],"id":801,"m":"You were always good at sudden compliments, Bishop of Winchester. But know that I have not come to hear such flattery now, and in my presence; it is too thin and bare to hide offences. You can't reach me; you play the fawning spaniel and think you can win me with a wagging tongue; but whatever you take me for, I'm sure you have a cruel and bloody nature. (To Cranmer) Good man, sit down. Now let me see the proudest of them, the boldest, so much as wag his finger at you. By all that's holy, he would do better to starve than even think once that this place doesn't suit you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["May it please your Grace,—"],"id":802,"m":"May it please your Grace —"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["No, sir, it does not please me.","I had thought I had had men of some understanding","And wisdom of my council; but I find none.","Was it discretion, lords, to let this man,","This good man,—few of you deserve that title,—","This honest man, wait like a lousy footboy","At chamber-door? and one as great as you are?","Why, what a shame was this! Did my commission","Bid ye so far forget yourselves? I gave ye","Power as he was a councillor to try him,—","Not as a groom. There's some of ye, I see,","More out of malice than integrity,","Would try him to the utmost, had ye mean;","Which ye shall never have while I live."],"id":803,"m":"No, sir, it does not please me. I thought I had men of some understanding and wisdom on my council, but I find none. Was it sensible, lords, to let this man, this good man, few of you deserve that title, this honest man, wait like a lice-ridden footboy at the chamber door, and a man as great as you? Why, what a disgrace this was! Did my commission tell you to forget yourselves so far? I gave you power to try him as a councillor, not as a servant. I see some of you, more from malice than honesty, would try him to the utmost if you had the means; which you shall never have while I live."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHANCELLOR","lines":["Thus far,","My most dread sovereign, may it like your Grace","To let my tongue excuse all. What was purpos'd","Concerning his imprisonment was rather,","If there be faith in men, meant for his trial","And fair purgation to the world, than malice,","I'm sure, in me."],"id":804,"m":"My most dread sovereign, may it please your Grace to let my tongue excuse us all this far. What was planned concerning his imprisonment was meant, if men can be trusted, rather for his trial and fair clearing before the world than out of malice, certainly in my case."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Well, well, my lords, respect him;","Take him, and use him well, he's worthy of it.","I will say thus much for him, if a prince","May be beholding to a subject, I","Am, for his love and service, so to him.","Make me no more ado, but all embrace him.","Be friends, for shame, my lords! My Lord of Canterbury,","I have a suit which you must not deny me;","That is, a fair young maid that yet wants baptism,","You must be godfather, and answer for her."],"id":805,"m":"Well, well, my lords, respect him. Accept him and treat him well; he deserves it. I'll say this much for him: if a prince can be indebted to a subject, I am to him, for his love and service. Make no more fuss, but all embrace him. Be friends, for shame, my lords! My Lord of Canterbury, I have a request you mustn't refuse me: there is a fair young girl who still needs baptising; you must be her godfather and answer for her."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["The greatest monarch now alive may glory","In such an honour; how may I deserve it,","That am a poor and humble subject to you?"],"id":806,"m":"The greatest king alive could be proud of such an honour; how can I deserve it, being a poor and humble subject of yours?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Come, come, my lord, you'd spare your spoons.","You shall have two noble partners with you, the old Duchess","of Norfolk and Lady Marquess Dorset. Will these please you?","Once more, my Lord of Winchester, I charge you, embrace and","love this man."],"id":807,"m":"Come, come, my lord, you'd like to save the cost of the christening spoons. You shall have two noble partners with you, the old Duchess of Norfolk and Lady Marquess Dorset. Will these please you? Once more, my Lord of Winchester, I order you: embrace and love this man."},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDINER","lines":["With a true heart","And brother-love I do it."],"id":808,"m":"I do it with a true heart and brotherly love."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["And let Heaven","Witness how dear I hold this confirmation."],"id":809,"m":"And let Heaven witness how dearly I value this pledge."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Good man, those joyful tears show thy true heart.","The common voice, I see, is verified","Of thee, which says thus, \"Do my Lord of Canterbury","A shrewd turn, and he is your friend for ever.\"","Come, lords, we trifle time away; I long","To have this young one made a Christian.","As I have made ye one, lords, one remain;","So I grow stronger, you more honour gain."],"id":810,"m":"Good man, those tears of joy show your true heart. I see the common saying about you is proved true: \"Do my Lord of Canterbury a bad turn, and he is your friend for ever.\" Come, lords, we're wasting time; I long to have this little one made a Christian. As I have made you one, lords, stay one; so I grow stronger, and you gain more honour."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":811},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"IV","loc":"The palace yard.","id":812},{"t":"sd","text":"Noise and tumult within. Enter Porter and his Man.","id":813},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTER","lines":["You'll leave your noise anon, ye rascals; do you take","the court for Paris-garden? Ye rude slaves, leave your gaping."],"id":814,"m":"You'll stop your noise soon, you rascals. Do you take the court for the Paris Garden bear-pit? You rude slaves, stop your bawling."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOICE","lines":["[Within] Good master porter, I belong to the larder."],"id":815,"m":"(Within) Good master porter, I belong to the larder."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTER","lines":["Belong to the gallows, and be hang'd, ye rogue! Is this","a place to roar in? Fetch me a dozen crab-tree staves, and strong","ones; these are but switches to 'em. I'll scratch your heads. You","must be seeing christenings? Do you look for ale and cakes here,","you rude rascals?"],"id":816,"m":"Belong to the gallows and be hanged, you rogue! Is this a place to roar in? Fetch me a dozen crab-apple clubs, and strong ones; these are only switches compared to them. I'll scratch your heads. You must see christenings, must you? Do you expect ale and cakes here, you rude rascals?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTER'S MAN","lines":["Pray, sir, be patient. 'Tis as much impossible—","Unless we sweep 'em from the door with cannons—","To scatter 'em, as 'tis to make 'em sleep","On May-day morning; which will never be.","We may as well push against Paul's, as stir 'em."],"id":817,"m":"Please, sir, be patient. Unless we sweep them from the door with cannons, it's as impossible to scatter them as it is to make them sleep on May Day morning, which will never happen. We might as well push against Saint Paul's as try to move them."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTER","lines":["How got they in, and be hang'd?"],"id":818,"m":"How did they get in, damn them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTER'S MAN","lines":["Alas, I know not: how gets the tide in?","As much as one sound cudgel of four foot—","You see the poor remainder—could distribute,","I made no spare, sir."],"id":819,"m":"Alas, I don't know. How does the tide get in? As much as one good four-foot cudgel could hand out, and you see the poor stump left of it, I didn't hold back, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTER","lines":["You did nothing, sir."],"id":820,"m":"You did nothing, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTER'S MAN","lines":["I am not Samson, nor Sir Guy, nor Colbrand,","To mow 'em down before me; but if I spar'd any","That had a head to hit, either young or old,","He or she, cuckold or cuckold-maker,","Let me ne'er hope to see a chine again;","And that I would not for a cow, God save her!"],"id":821,"m":"I'm not Samson, nor Sir Guy, nor Colbrand the giant, to mow them down before me; but if I spared anyone who had a head to hit, young or old, he or she, cuckold or cuckold-maker, may I never hope to see a joint of meat again; and I wouldn't want that for a cow, God save her!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VOICE","lines":["[Within] Do you hear, master porter?"],"id":822,"m":"(Within) Do you hear, master porter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTER","lines":["I shall be with you presently, good master puppy.—","Keep the door close, sirrah."],"id":823,"m":"I'll be with you in a minute, good master puppy. Keep the door shut, fellow."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTER'S MAN","lines":["What would you have me do?"],"id":824,"m":"What do you want me to do?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTER","lines":["What should you do, but knock 'em down by the dozens? Is this","Moorfields to muster in? Or have we some strange Indian with the","great tool come to court, the women so besiege us? Bless me, what","a fry of fornication is at door! On my Christian conscience, this","one christening will beget a thousand; here will be father,","godfather, and all together."],"id":825,"m":"What should you do but knock them down by the dozen? Is this Moorfields, to drill soldiers in? Or has some strange Indian with a great tool come to court, that the women besiege us like this? Bless me, what a swarm of fornication is at the door! On my Christian conscience, this one christening will breed a thousand; here will be father, godfather and all together."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTER'S MAN","lines":["The spoons will be the bigger, sir. There is a fellow somewhat","near the door, he should be a brazier by his face, for, o' my","conscience, twenty of the dog-days now reign in's nose; all that","stand about him are under the line, they need no other penance:","that fire-drake did I hit three times on the head, and three","times was his nose discharged against me; he stands there, like a","mortar-piece, to blow us. There was a haberdasher's wife of","small wit near him, that rail'd upon me till her pink'd porringer","fell off her head, for kindling such a combustion in the state. I","miss'd the meteor once, and hit that woman; who cried out","\"Clubs!\" when I might see from far some forty truncheoners draw","to her succour, which were the hope o' the Strand, where she was","quartered. They fell on; I made good my place; at length they","came to the broomstaff to me; I defied 'em still; when suddenly a","file of boys behind 'em, loose shot, deliver'd such a shower of","pebbles, that I was fain to draw mine honour in, and let 'em win","the work. The devil was amongst 'em, I think, surely."],"id":826,"m":"The christening spoons will be bigger, sir. There's a fellow near the door who must be a brass-worker by his face, for on my conscience twenty dog-days of heat rule in his nose; everyone standing round him is at the equator and needs no other penance. I hit that fiery dragon on the head three times, and three times his nose fired at me; he stands there like a mortar ready to blow us up. There was a haberdasher's wife of little sense near him who abused me till her decorated hat fell off her head, for starting such a fire in the state. I missed the shooting star once and hit that woman, who shouted \"Clubs!\", and I saw from far off about forty club-carrying apprentices, the flower of the Strand where she lodged, come to her rescue. They charged; I held my ground; at last they came at me with broomsticks; I still defied them; when suddenly a line of boys behind them, skirmishers, let fly such a shower of pebbles that I was glad to withdraw my honour and let them win the fort. The devil was among them, I'm sure."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTER","lines":["These are the youths that thunder at a playhouse, and fight for","bitten apples; that no audience but the tribulation of Tower-hill","or the limbs of Limehouse, their dear brothers, are able to","endure. I have some of 'em in Limbo Patrum, and there they are","like to dance these three days; besides the running banquet of","two beadles that is to come."],"id":827,"m":"These are the young men who thunder at a playhouse and fight over bitten apples, whom no audience can stand except the rabble of Tower Hill or the gangs of Limehouse, their dear brothers. I have some of them locked up in the lockup, and there they're likely to dance for three days, besides the whipping by two beadles still to come."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lord Chamberlain.","id":828},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["Mercy o' me, what a multitude are here!","They grow still too; from all parts they are coming","As if we kept a fair here! Where are these porters,","These lazy knaves? Ye have made a fine hand, fellows.","There's a trim rabble let in. Are all these","Your faithful friends o' the suburbs? We shall have","Great store of room, no doubt, left for the ladies,","When they pass back from the christening."],"id":829,"m":"Mercy on me, what a crowd there is here! And they're still growing; they're coming from everywhere as if we were holding a fair! Where are these porters, these lazy rogues? You've made a fine job of it, fellows. That's a pretty rabble you've let in. Are all these your faithful friends from the suburbs? No doubt we'll have plenty of room left for the ladies when they come back from the christening."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTER","lines":["An't please your honour,","We are but men; and what so many may do,","Not being torn a-pieces, we have done.","An army cannot rule 'em."],"id":830,"m":"If it please your honour, we're only men; and we've done what so few could do without being torn to pieces. An army couldn't control them."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","lines":["As I live,","If the King blame me for't, I'll lay ye all","By the heels, and suddenly; and on your heads","Clap round fines for neglect. Ye're lazy knaves;","And here ye lie baiting of bombards, when","Ye should do service. Hark! the trumpets sound;","They're come already from the christening.","Go, break among the press, and find a way out","To let the troops pass fairly; or I'll find","A Marshalsea shall hold ye play these two months."],"id":831,"m":"As I live, if the King blames me for this, I'll put you all in the stocks, and quickly, and slap heavy fines on your heads for neglect. You're lazy rogues, and here you lie drinking from leather jugs when you should be doing your job. Listen! The trumpets are sounding; they've already come from the christening. Go, push into the crowd and clear a way for the procession to pass properly, or I'll find a Marshalsea prison to keep you amused for the next two months."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTER","lines":["Make way there for the princess."],"id":832,"m":"Make way there for the princess."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTER'S MAN","lines":["You great fellow,","Stand close up, or I'll make your head ache."],"id":833,"m":"You big fellow, stand back, or I'll make your head ache."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTER","lines":["You i' the camlet, get up o' the rail;","I'll peck you o'er the pales else."],"id":834,"m":"You in the camlet cloth, get off the rail, or I'll pitch you over the fence."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":835},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"V","loc":"The palace.","id":836},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter trumpets, sounding; then two Aldermen, Lord Mayor, Garter, Cranmer, Duke of Norfolk with his marshal's staff, Duke of Suffolk, two Noblemen bearing great standing-bowls for the christening-gifts; then four Noblemen bearing a canopy, under which the Duchess of Norfolk, godmother, bearing the child richly habited in a mantle, etc., train borne by a Lady; then follows the Marchioness Dorset, the other godmother, and Ladies. The troop pass once about the stage, and Garter speaks.","id":837},{"t":"sp","who":"GARTER","lines":["Heaven, from thy endless goodness, send prosperous","life, long and ever happy, to the high and mighty Princess of","England, Elizabeth!"],"id":838,"m":"Heaven, from your endless goodness, send a prosperous life, long and always happy, to the high and mighty Princess of England, Elizabeth!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter King and Guard.","id":839},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["[Kneeling] And to your royal Grace, and the good queen,","My noble partners, and myself, thus pray:","All comfort, joy, in this most gracious lady,","Heaven ever laid up to make parents happy,","May hourly fall upon ye!"],"id":840,"m":"(Kneeling) And for your royal Grace and the good queen, my noble partners and I pray this: may all the comfort and joy Heaven ever stored up to make parents happy fall on you hourly through this most gracious lady!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Thank you, good Lord Archbishop.","What is her name?"],"id":841,"m":"Thank you, good Lord Archbishop. What is her name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["Elizabeth."],"id":842,"m":"Elizabeth."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Stand up, lord."],"id":843,"m":"Stand up, lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"The King kisses the child.","id":844},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","cont":1,"lines":["With this kiss take my blessing: God protect thee!","Into whose hand I give thy life."],"id":845,"m":"With this kiss take my blessing: God protect you! Into His hand I give your life."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["Amen."],"id":846,"m":"Amen."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["My noble gossips, ye have been too prodigal.","I thank ye heartily; so shall this lady,","When she has so much English."],"id":847,"m":"My noble godparents, you have been too generous. I thank you heartily, and so will this lady when she has enough English."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["Let me speak, sir,","For Heaven now bids me; and the words I utter","Let none think flattery, for they'll find 'em truth.","This royal infant—Heaven still move about her!—","Though in her cradle, yet now promises","Upon this land a thousand thousand blessings,","Which time shall bring to ripeness. She shall be—","But few now living can behold that goodness—","A pattern to all princes living with her,","And all that shall succeed. Saba was never","More covetous of wisdom and fair virtue","Than this pure soul shall be. All princely graces,","That mould up such a mighty piece as this is,","With all the virtues that attend the good,","Shall still be doubled on her. Truth shall nurse her,","Holy and heavenly thoughts still counsel her.","She shall be lov'd and fear'd: her own shall bless her;","Her foes shake like a field of beaten corn,","And hang their heads with sorrow. Good grows with her.","In her days every man shall eat in safety,","Under his own vine, what he plants, and sing","The merry songs of peace to all his neighbours.","God shall be truly known; and those about her","From her shall read the perfect ways of honour,","And by those claim their greatness, not by blood.","Nor shall this peace sleep with her; but as when","The bird of wonder dies, the maiden phoenix,","Her ashes new create another heir","As great in admiration as herself;","So shall she leave her blessedness to one,","When heaven shall call her from this cloud of darkness,","Who from the sacred ashes of her honour","Shall star-like rise as great in fame as she was,","And so stand fix'd. Peace, plenty, love, truth, terror,","That were the servants to this chosen infant,","Shall then be his, and like a vine grow to him.","Wherever the bright sun of heaven shall shine,","His honour and the greatness of his name","Shall be, and make new nations. He shall flourish,","And, like a mountain cedar, reach his branches","To all the plains about him. Our children's children","Shall see this, and bless Heaven."],"id":848,"m":"Let me speak, sir, for Heaven now commands me; and let no one think my words flattery, for they will find them true. This royal infant, may Heaven always watch over her, though still in her cradle, already promises this land a thousand thousand blessings, which time will bring to ripeness. She shall be, though few now living will see that goodness, a model to all princes of her time and all who come after. The Queen of Sheba was never more eager for wisdom and virtue than this pure soul will be. All the royal graces that shape such a mighty person as this, with all the virtues that go with goodness, shall be doubled in her. Truth shall nurse her; holy and heavenly thoughts shall always guide her. She shall be loved and feared; her own people shall bless her; her enemies shall shake like a field of flattened corn and hang their heads in sorrow. Good grows with her. In her days every man shall eat in safety what he plants under his own vine, and sing merry songs of peace to all his neighbours. God shall be truly known; and those around her shall learn from her the perfect ways of honour, and claim their greatness by those, not by birth. Nor shall this peace die with her; but as when the miraculous bird, the virgin phoenix, dies, her ashes create a new heir as wonderful as herself, so she shall leave her blessedness to one who, when heaven calls her from this cloud of darkness, shall rise like a star from the sacred ashes of her honour, as great in fame as she was, and stay fixed there. Peace, plenty, love, truth and awe, which served this chosen infant, shall then be his and grow round him like a vine. Wherever the bright sun of heaven shines, his honour and the greatness of his name shall be there and found new nations. He shall flourish and, like a mountain cedar, spread his branches over all the plains around him. Our children's children shall see this and bless Heaven."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["Thou speakest wonders."],"id":849,"m":"You speak of wonders."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRANMER","lines":["She shall be, to the happiness of England,","An aged princess; many days shall see her,","And yet no day without a deed to crown it.","Would I had known no more! but she must die,","She must, the saints must have her; yet a virgin,","A most unspotted lily shall she pass","To the ground, and all the world shall mourn her."],"id":850,"m":"She shall be, to England's happiness, an aged princess; many days shall see her, and yet no day without a deed to crown it. I wish I knew no more! But she must die; she must, the saints must have her. Yet she shall go to the grave a virgin, a perfectly spotless lily, and the whole world shall mourn her."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY","lines":["O Lord Archbishop,","Thou hast made me now a man! Never, before","This happy child, did I get anything.","This oracle of comfort has so pleas'd me,","That when I am in heaven I shall desire","To see what this child does, and praise my Maker.","I thank ye all. To you, my good Lord Mayor,","And you, good brethren, I am much beholding;","I have receiv'd much honour by your presence,","And ye shall find me thankful. Lead the way, lords.","Ye must all see the Queen, and she must thank ye,","She will be sick else. This day, no man think","Has business at his house; for all shall stay.","This little one shall make it holiday."],"id":851,"m":"Oh, Lord Archbishop, you have made me a man now! Before this happy child I never fathered anything. This comforting prophecy has pleased me so much that when I am in heaven I shall want to see what this child does, and praise my Maker. I thank you all. To you, my good Lord Mayor, and you, good aldermen, I am much obliged; I have received great honour by your presence, and you shall find me grateful. Lead the way, lords. You must all see the Queen, and she must thank you, or she'll be ill. Let no man think he has business at home today, for everyone shall stay. This little one shall make it a holiday."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":852},{"t":"sp","who":"EPILOGUE","lines":["'Tis ten to one this play can never please","All that are here. Some come to take their ease,","And sleep an act or two; but those, we fear,","We have frighted with our trumpets; so, 'tis clear,","They'll say 'tis nought: others, to hear the city","Abus'd extremely, and to cry \"That's witty!\"","Which we have not done neither: that, I fear,","All the expected good we're like to hear","For this play at this time, is only in","The merciful construction of good women;","For such a one we show'd 'em. If they smile","And say 'twill do, I know, within a while","All the best men are ours; for 'tis ill hap","If they hold when their ladies bid 'em clap."],"id":853,"m":"It's ten to one this play can never please everyone here. Some come to relax and sleep through an act or two, but we fear we have woken those with our trumpets, so clearly they'll say it's worthless; others come to hear the city mocked mercilessly and cry \"That's witty!\", which we haven't done either. So I fear all the praise we are likely to hear for this play at present lies only in the kind judgement of good women, for we showed them such a woman. If they smile and say it will do, I know that before long all the best men will be ours; for it's bad luck if the men hold back when their ladies tell them to clap."}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"King Henry VIII","group":"The court","desc":"The King, seen first leaning on Wolsey's shoulder and last holding his new-born daughter. He thanks Wolsey for uncovering Buckingham's 'treason' and has him tried; he cancels the hated tax when Katherine tells him of it; he falls for Anne Bullen at Wolsey's banquet. He says his conscience was troubled by a French ambassador's question about his daughter's legitimacy and by the deaths of his sons, and brings the divorce to court at Blackfriars. When Wolsey's inventory and letter to the Pope reach him, he tests and then ruins him. By Act 5 he acts alone: he gives Cranmer his ring, watches the council from a window, rebukes it, and makes Cranmer godfather to Elizabeth."},{"name":"Cardinal Wolsey","group":"The Church","desc":"Archbishop of York, Lord Chancellor and papal legate, a butcher's son from Ipswich who has risen by his own ability to stand next to the King. The nobles hate his pride: he arranged the costly Field of the Cloth of Gold, levies a tax without precedent, arrests Buckingham through a dismissed surveyor and sends Surrey to Ireland. He wants the King to divorce Katherine and marry the French king's sister, and calls Anne a 'spleeny Lutheran'. Ruined when his inventory and his letter to the Pope fall into the King's hands, he finds peace in his fall, advises Cromwell to 'fling away ambition', and dies repentant at Leicester Abbey."},{"name":"Queen Katherine (Katherine of Aragon)","group":"The court","desc":"Henry's Spanish queen of twenty years, daughter of Ferdinand of Spain and aunt of the Emperor Charles V. She pleads for the taxed weavers, warns that Buckingham's surveyor may be lying out of spite, and at Blackfriars kneels to the King, refuses Wolsey as her judge and appeals to the Pope. In her rooms she turns on the two cardinals: 'Ye have angels' faces, but heaven knows your hearts.' Divorced and dying at Kimbolton, she hears Griffith praise Wolsey and forgives him, sees a vision of spirits with garlands, and sends the King a last letter for their daughter and her servants."},{"name":"Duke of Buckingham (Edward Stafford)","group":"The nobles","desc":"A great noble, proud of his blood, who hates Wolsey as an upstart and means to accuse him to the King. He is arrested for high treason before he can. His dismissed surveyor reports that he talked of the crown if the King died childless and of stabbing the King as his father meant to stab Richard III. Condemned by his peers, he goes to execution forgiving everyone and blessing the King, and warns the crowd that friends 'fall away like water' at the first sign of ruin."},{"name":"Anne Bullen (Anne Boleyn)","group":"The court","desc":"One of Katherine's ladies, the daughter of Sir Thomas Bullen. The King dances with her at Wolsey's banquet and is captured. She pities Katherine and swears she would not be a queen 'for all the riches under heaven', then is made Marchioness of Pembroke with a thousand pounds a year. Married in secret, she is crowned in 4.1 and gives birth to Elizabeth offstage in 5.1. She speaks only in Acts 1 and 2."},{"name":"Thomas Cranmer","group":"The Church","desc":"The King's 'learned and well-beloved servant', whose opinions from the universities support the divorce. He is made Archbishop of Canterbury and crowns Anne. Gardiner and the council accuse him of heresy and try to send him to the Tower, but the King has warned him and given him a ring to show them. Gentle and tearful, he forgives Gardiner, stands godfather to Elizabeth and ends the play with a prophecy of her reign and of her heir."},{"name":"Duke of Norfolk","group":"The nobles","desc":"A noble who describes the Field of the Cloth of Gold and warns Buckingham not to attack Wolsey too hastily. Later he leads the lords against Wolsey, mocks his 'holy' scheming in the divorce, and delivers the King's order to give up the great seal: 'So fare you well, my little good Lord Cardinal.' He sits on the council that tries Cranmer and carries the marshal's staff at the christening."},{"name":"Duke of Suffolk","group":"The nobles","desc":"Charles Brandon, the King's friend and card partner. He scorns Wolsey ('I love him not, nor fear him'), reports the intercepted letter and the King's 'Ha!', pronounces the praemunire that strips Wolsey of his goods, and serves as High Steward at Anne's coronation."},{"name":"Earl of Surrey","group":"The nobles","desc":"Buckingham's son-in-law, sent to Ireland as deputy so that he could not help him. He wants revenge on Wolsey, calls him 'thou scarlet sin', all but draws his sword on him, and reads out the charges against him with relish."},{"name":"Lord Chamberlain","group":"The nobles","desc":"A courtier who mocks French fashions, hosts at Wolsey's banquet and announces the masquers, and brings Anne her new title. He sees that Wolsey's horses have been taken and that 'he will have all'. When Surrey keeps attacking the fallen Cardinal he says 'Press not a falling man too far'. In 5.4 he scolds the Porter for letting in the crowd."},{"name":"Stephen Gardiner, Bishop of Winchester","group":"The Church","desc":"Wolsey's man, placed by him as the King's new secretary and later Bishop of Winchester. A conservative churchman, he wishes Anne, Cranmer and Cromwell dead, persuades the council that Cranmer is an 'arch heretic', and tries to send him to the Tower. The King exposes his flattery and orders him to embrace Cranmer."},{"name":"Thomas Cromwell","group":"The court","desc":"Wolsey's servant, who weeps at his fall and hears his last advice. He rises in the King's service to become Master of the Jewel House, Master of the Rolls, King's secretary and privy councillor. At the council he defends Cranmer and calls Gardiner's life 'bold'."},{"name":"Cardinal Campeius","group":"The Church","desc":"The papal legate sent from Rome to judge the divorce with Wolsey. He calls Katherine obstinate at Blackfriars, urges her to put her case in the King's hands, and then slips away to Rome without leave, which helps turn the King against both cardinals."},{"name":"Griffith","group":"Katherine's household","desc":"Katherine's gentleman usher. He tells her how Wolsey died at Leicester Abbey and, when she lists Wolsey's faults, answers with his virtues: his learning, his colleges at Ipswich and Oxford and his humble death. 'Men's evil manners live in brass; their virtues we write in water.'"},{"name":"Old Lady","group":"The court","desc":"A bawdy older woman, friend to Anne, who mocks Anne's claim that she would not be queen and envies her quick fortune after sixteen years of fruitless begging at court. In 5.1 she brings the King news of the birth, calls the girl a boy at first, and grumbles that a hundred marks is a groom's reward."},{"name":"Sir Thomas Lovell","group":"The court","desc":"A courtier close to the King. Buckingham's surveyor says Buckingham wanted his head along with Wolsey's. He goes with Buckingham to the river, asks his forgiveness, and in 5.1 hears Gardiner's plot against Cranmer."},{"name":"Lord Sandys","group":"The court","desc":"A merry old courtier who scorns the frenchified gallants, flirts with Anne at Wolsey's banquet and kisses her. As Sir William Sandys he walks with Buckingham to his execution."},{"name":"Capucius","group":"Katherine's household","desc":"Ambassador of the Emperor Charles V, Katherine's nephew. The King sends him to Kimbolton with comfort that comes 'too late'; Katherine gives him her last letter to the King."},{"name":"The three Gentlemen","group":"The people","desc":"Well-informed onlookers who report what happens offstage: Buckingham's trial, the rumour of the divorce, Katherine's divorce at Dunstable, and Anne's coronation in the Abbey. They pity Buckingham and Katherine, admire Anne and blame Wolsey."},{"name":"Doctor Butts","group":"The court","desc":"The King's physician, who sees Cranmer kept waiting at the council door 'among boys, grooms and lackeys' and shows the King from a window above."},{"name":"Surveyor","group":"The nobles","desc":"Buckingham's former estate manager, dismissed after complaints from the tenants, who reports his master's words about the crown, Hopkins's prophecy and a dagger. Katherine warns him not to accuse in spite."},{"name":"Patience","group":"Katherine's household","desc":"Katherine's woman, who sits with her at Kimbolton and sees her face change after the vision."},{"name":"The Porter and his Man","group":"The people","desc":"Keepers of the palace gate on christening day, who fail to hold back a cheering London crowd and describe the fight at the door in comic prose."},{"name":"Prologue and Epilogue","group":"The people","desc":"The speakers who frame the play. The Prologue promises no jokes or fights but sad, true and noble scenes; the Epilogue hopes the good women in the audience will like a play that showed them such a good woman."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"The Prologue promises a serious, true play about great people brought low. In the palace Norfolk describes to Buckingham the splendour of the meeting of the English and French kings, which Buckingham missed through illness. Buckingham learns that Wolsey arranged it, and he, Abergavenny and Norfolk attack the Cardinal's pride and the ruinous cost. Wolsey passes, glaring, and asks for the examination of Buckingham's surveyor. Buckingham means to go to the King and accuse Wolsey of treason, but Norfolk urges caution. Brandon arrests Buckingham and Abergavenny for high treason. Buckingham sees that his surveyor has been bribed.","key":["The Prologue sets the pattern: 'How soon this mightiness meets misery.'","Buckingham's hatred of Wolsey is about blood: a butcher's son should not outrank the nobility.","The arrest comes the moment Buckingham decides to act, showing how far ahead Wolsey is."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why did Buckingham miss the meeting of the kings in France?","o":["He was in prison","He was ill","He refused to go","He was in Ireland"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Who arranged the Field of the Cloth of Gold, according to Norfolk?","o":["The King","Buckingham","Cardinal Wolsey","The French king"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Who arrests Buckingham?","o":["Lovell","Surrey","Brandon, with a sergeant-at-arms","Wolsey himself"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the Prologue prepare the audience for?","o":["A merry comedy full of songs, jests and dancing","A war play with great battles fought in France","A serious play about great people brought low","A love story that ends in a royal wedding"],"a":2,"b":4,"p":"implication"},{"q":"How does Buckingham realise he has been betrayed?","o":["He reads a letter written in Wolsey's own hand","He is told by Norfolk at the palace door","He sees that his surveyor has been bribed","He finds that Abergavenny has confessed all"],"a":2,"b":2,"p":"information"}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"The King thanks Wolsey for uncovering a conspiracy. Queen Katherine kneels to tell him that a tax of a sixth of every man's goods has driven the clothworkers to near rebellion. The King had not heard of it; Wolsey says he acted only with the judges' approval. The King cancels it and pardons those who refused, and Wolsey quietly has the pardon spread as his own doing. Buckingham's surveyor tells how Buckingham hoped for the crown if the King died childless, trusted a monk's prophecy, and talked of stabbing the King as his father meant to stab Richard III. Katherine warns that the surveyor was dismissed by Buckingham, but the King declares Buckingham a traitor and orders his trial.","key":["Katherine is the first to speak for the people and against Wolsey.","Wolsey takes credit for the pardon he opposed: a small, exact proof of his double dealing.","The case against Buckingham rests on a hostile witness, which the play lets us doubt."],"quiz":[{"q":"What part of each man's goods does the new tax demand?","o":["A tenth","A sixth","A third","Half"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Katherine distrust the surveyor?","o":["He is French and spies for the King of France at court","He lost his post after the tenants complained","He is Wolsey's cousin","He is a monk"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Who made the prophecy that Buckingham would govern England?","o":["Nicholas Hopkins (Henton), a Carthusian monk","Archbishop Cranmer, in a sermon at Saint Paul's in London","John de la Car","A gypsy"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Wolsey's handling of the pardon reveal?","o":["He takes credit for mercy while avoiding blame for the tax","He is a humble man who gives all of the glory to the King","He wants the clothworkers to be punished very harshly","He has opposed the tax from the very beginning of it"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"Why does Katherine doubt the surveyor's evidence?","o":["He is Katherine's own servant","He is too old to remember","He was abroad at the time","Buckingham had dismissed him"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"purpose"}]},"a1s3":{"sum":"The Lord Chamberlain and Lord Sandys mock the courtiers who came home from France with foreign clothes and manners. Lovell brings news of a proclamation ordering these gallants to give up their French fashions or leave court. All three are going to a great supper at Wolsey's house, and they agree that the Cardinal is generous, as a churchman ought to be.","key":["A short comic scene that shows the cost of the French visit in a lighter key.","Sandys's bawdy jokes prepare for his flirting with Anne.","The praise of Wolsey's generosity sets up the luxury of the banquet."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the new proclamation target?","o":["Gambling at cards and dice in the taverns of London","Gallants who copy French fashions","Catholic priests who preach against the King","Tavern keepers"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Where are the three lords going?","o":["To the Tower","To a supper at Wolsey's house","To France","To Blackfriars"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What is Sandys's 'colt's tooth'?","o":["A real tooth that has troubled him since he was a boy","His youthful appetite for pleasure","His horse","His sword"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What do the lords' jokes about French fashions show?","o":["The court is shallow and swayed by fashion","The lords are planning a war against France","The King has banned all French books","The lords want to travel to France soon"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"Why is the lords' praise of Wolsey's generosity ironic?","o":["He is known to be a poor and simple country priest","His wealth and display are part of his pride","He gives nearly all of his money to the King","He has refused to host any suppers at all this year"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"implication"}]},"a1s4":{"sum":"At Wolsey's supper in York Place, Sandys sits between the ladies and kisses Anne Bullen. Cannons announce a party of 'foreign' visitors dressed as shepherds; they are the King and his friends in masks. The King dances with Anne. Wolsey guesses which masker is the King and offers him his chair; the King unmasks, asks who Anne is, kisses her, and leads the company to the banquet in the next room.","key":["Henry and Anne meet at Wolsey's own party, so the Cardinal sets up the marriage that ruins him.","'The fairest hand I ever touched! O beauty, Till now I never knew thee!'","The masque shows a court of pleasure; the next scene opens on a condemned man."],"quiz":[{"q":"How are the King and his friends disguised?","o":["As monks","As shepherds","As Frenchmen in armour","As servants"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"Who kisses Anne before the King arrives?","o":["Lovell","Guildford","Sandys","Wolsey"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Whose daughter is Anne, according to the Chamberlain?","o":["Sir Thomas Bullen's","The Duke of Norfolk's","Sir Henry Guildford's","Lord Sandys's"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"view"},{"q":"Why is Wolsey's supper a turning point?","o":["The King meets Anne there","Wolsey is arrested there","Buckingham is tried there","Katherine falls ill there"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"implication"},{"q":"What does the King's masked entrance suggest about him?","o":["He fears being seen in public","He distrusts Wolsey's guests","He wants to spy on the lords","He enjoys play and disguise"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"inference"}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"Two Gentlemen meet in a Westminster street. The first tells how Buckingham was tried, pleaded not guilty, faced his surveyor, chancellor, confessor and the monk Hopkins, and was condemned by his peers. They agree Wolsey is behind it, as he was behind Surrey's posting to Ireland. Buckingham passes on his way to execution. He forgives everyone, blesses the King, compares his fate with his father's, who was betrayed by a servant to Richard III, and warns the crowd against trusting friends. The Gentlemen then share a new rumour: the King is to separate from Katherine, Cardinal Campeius has arrived, and Wolsey is blamed.","key":["Buckingham's farewell begins the play's run of noble falls and forgiving speeches.","'Both fell by our servants, by those men we loved most.'","The talk of the divorce links Buckingham's fall to the next one."],"quiz":[{"q":"How does Buckingham plead at his trial?","o":["Guilty","Not guilty","He refuses to plead","He asks for mercy"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"Who betrayed Buckingham's father?","o":["His wife, the Duchess","His servant Banister","Henry VII","Wolsey"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Who has just arrived to deal with the King's 'separation'?","o":["Cranmer, from Cambridge","Cardinal Campeius","The Emperor himself","The French king's envoy"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Buckingham's farewell speech warn the crowd about?","o":["The danger of loving the King far too much","The danger of travelling to France in wartime","The danger of ever speaking to the Cardinal","The danger of trusting friends and servants"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"implication"},{"q":"How does Buckingham's fate echo his father's?","o":["Both died in battle in France","Both were pardoned at the end","Both were made Lord Protector","Both were betrayed by servants"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"information"}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"The Lord Chamberlain reads a letter: Wolsey's men took the horses he had bought. Norfolk and Suffolk say the King is troubled by his marriage to his brother's widow, and they blame Wolsey for planting the doubt in order to break with the Emperor and marry the King to the French king's sister. The King, interrupted, is angry with the dukes but welcomes Wolsey and Campeius, who brings Rome's commission. Wolsey introduces Gardiner, his own man, as the King's new secretary. Campeius hears that Gardiner's predecessor, Doctor Pace, was driven mad by Wolsey's envy. The King appoints Blackfriars for the hearing.","key":["The King's 'conscience' is mocked at once: 'his conscience has crept too near another lady.'","Wolsey plants Gardiner at the King's side.","'But conscience, conscience! O, 'tis a tender place, and I must leave her.'"],"quiz":[{"q":"What did Wolsey's men take from the Lord Chamberlain?","o":["His seal","His horses","His land","His barge"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Who becomes the King's new secretary?","o":["Cromwell","Gardiner","Lovell","Denny"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Where will the divorce be heard?","o":["Westminster Hall","Blackfriars","Dunstable","York Place"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What do the dukes believe about Wolsey's role in the divorce?","o":["He planted the King's doubts for his own policy","He is trying hard to save the Queen's marriage","He has no interest in the matter at all","He has been ordered to do it by the Pope"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the story of Doctor Pace suggest about Wolsey?","o":["Envy makes him dangerous to rivals","He rewards all his loyal servants well","He is a kind and very caring master","He is truly afraid of all clever men"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"inference"}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"Anne pities Katherine, who after so many years as queen is to be thrown off, and swears she would not be a queen for all the world. The Old Lady laughs at her. The Lord Chamberlain brings the King's message: Anne is to be Marchioness of Pembroke with a thousand pounds a year. Aside, he thinks a jewel may come from her to light the whole island. The Old Lady envies Anne's luck after her own sixteen years of begging. Anne asks her to say nothing to the Queen.","key":["Anne's protest is gentle and sincere, but the scene sets her pity against her rise.","The Chamberlain's 'gem to lighten all this isle' points to Elizabeth.","The Old Lady's bawdy jokes are the plain truth about ambition and the court."],"quiz":[{"q":"What title does the King give Anne?","o":["Duchess of Norfolk and Suffolk","Marchioness of Pembroke","Countess of Kent","Lady of the Bedchamber"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Anne say she would not be 'for all the riches under heaven'?","o":["Married","A queen","A duchess","A nun"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How long has the Old Lady been begging at court?","o":["Six years","Sixteen years","Twenty years","Forty years"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What is ironic about Anne's pity for Katherine?","o":["She pities Katherine but has already married the King in secret","She weeps for Katherine but plans to leave the court for good","She defends Katherine to the Old Lady, who is Katherine's enemy","She swears she would never be queen, and is soon made a marchioness"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"implication"},{"q":"What does the Old Lady's view of Anne show?","o":["She is sceptical of Anne's modesty","She thinks Anne is truly a living saint","She hates Anne and wants her gone from court","She hopes that Anne will marry Wolsey"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"view"}]},"a2s4":{"sum":"The court sits at Blackfriars with the two Cardinals as judges. Called, Katherine kneels to the King: she is a stranger without impartial judges, she has been a faithful wife for twenty years, and their wise fathers judged the marriage lawful. She refuses Wolsey as her judge, calls him her enemy, appeals to the Pope and leaves, refusing to return. The King praises her as 'the queen of earthly queens', clears Wolsey of starting the matter, and explains how the French ambassador's question about Mary's legitimacy and the deaths of his sons troubled his conscience. Campeius adjourns the court. Aside, the King scorns the delays of Rome and longs for Cranmer.","key":["Katherine's speech is the emotional centre of the first half: dignified, legal and personal.","Henry praises her only after she has left.","His aside about 'this dilatory sloth and tricks of Rome' looks forward to the break with Rome and to Cranmer."],"quiz":[{"q":"How long has Katherine been Henry's wife, she says?","o":["Ten years, no more, and she has been a true wife every day of them","Upwards of twenty years","Thirty years","Five years"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"To whom does Katherine appeal?","o":["The Emperor","The Pope","Parliament","Her father"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Who first raised the doubt about Mary's legitimacy, according to the King?","o":["Wolsey","The Bishop of Bayonne, the French ambassador","Cranmer","Campeius"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Katherine defend herself at Blackfriars?","o":["She produces a letter from the Emperor proving her innocence","She accuses the King of wanting to marry the Old Lady instead","She appeals to her loyalty, her lack of fair judges and the Pope","She asks the court to give her time to go back home to Spain"],"a":2,"b":2,"p":"question"},{"q":"What does the King give as the source of his doubt?","o":["His love for Anne, which he openly confesses in the court","The Pope's letter forbidding the marriage to continue","Wolsey's advice, which he says he followed from the first","His conscience, troubled by the French ambassador's question"],"a":3,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"Katherine, sewing with her women, asks for a song: 'Orpheus with his lute'. The two Cardinals arrive. She refuses to see them privately and refuses Latin. They advise her to put her case in the King's hands rather than risk disgrace in court. She accuses them of seeking her ruin: they have angels' faces but false hearts. She recalls her loyalty, swears only death will take her title, and compares herself to a dying lily in a land with no pity. Wolsey and Campeius persist, and at last she wearily asks for their counsel.","key":["The song's power to kill 'care and grief of heart' fails as soon as the Cardinals come in.","'Ye turn me into nothing': Katherine's fury is her strongest self-defence.","She yields only in words: she never gives up the name of queen."],"quiz":[{"q":"What song does Katherine's woman sing?","o":["Greensleeves, an old love song","Orpheus with his lute","Hark, hark, the lark","Willow"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Katherine refuse Wolsey's Latin?","o":["She cannot read Latin and is ashamed to say so before the lords","A strange tongue makes her case look suspicious","She is angry with Rome","Campeius speaks only Italian"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What flower does Katherine compare herself to?","o":["The rose","The lily","The violet","The daisy"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"Why does Katherine refuse to speak in Latin?","o":["She wants the truth heard openly in English","She has never learned to speak Latin well","She thinks Latin is the language of witches","She does not trust Campeius's translation"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Katherine's lily image say about her?","o":["She is fading in a land without pity","She is pure and will soon bloom again","She is proud and should be cut down","She is from Spain, where lilies grow"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"concept"}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"The lords rejoice that Wolsey's letter asking the Pope to delay the divorce, because the King loves Anne, has reached the King. The King has already married Anne in secret, Campeius has fled, and Cranmer's opinions have won the universities. Wolsey, alone, plans to marry the King to the French king's sister. The King enters with an inventory of Wolsey's wealth, found among state papers. He tests Wolsey's loyalty, hands him the inventory and the letter to the Pope, and leaves frowning. Wolsey sees that he is ruined. The lords demand the great seal, list his crimes, and pronounce a praemunire: all his goods are forfeit. Alone, he says farewell to his greatness; then he tells Cromwell of the peace he feels, hears of More, Cranmer and Anne, and urges him to serve the King and fling away ambition.","key":["Wolsey falls by his own hand: he put the secret papers in the King's packet himself.","'I shall fall like a bright exhalation in the evening, And no man see me more.'","'Had I but served my God with half the zeal I served my king, he would not in mine age have left me naked to mine enemies.'"],"quiz":[{"q":"What two papers does the King give Wolsey?","o":["A death warrant for Katherine and a pardon for Buckingham's son","An inventory of his wealth and his letter to the Pope","Katherine's letter and Anne's","The charges and the seal"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Who is made Lord Chancellor in Wolsey's place?","o":["Cromwell","Sir Thomas More","Gardiner","Norfolk"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Wolsey tell Cromwell to fling away?","o":["His sword","Ambition","His robe","Fear"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What brings about Wolsey's fall?","o":["His own papers reach the King by mistake","Katherine proves his treason in open court","The Pope excommunicates him from Rome","Buckingham's ghost accuses him in a dream"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Wolsey change after his fall?","o":["He becomes angrier and plots revenge","He flees to France to join the Emperor","He blames Cromwell for everything wrong","He gains self-knowledge and humility"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"information"}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"The two Gentlemen meet again, this time for Anne's coronation. They read the list of lords who claim offices by custom, and hear that Katherine, who ignored Cranmer's summons to Dunstable, has been divorced and lies sick at Kimbolton. The procession passes and they name the lords and admire Anne. A third Gentleman describes the crowded Abbey, the people's joy, and Cranmer crowning Anne. York Place is now the King's Whitehall. Gardiner, now Bishop of Winchester, dislikes Cranmer, but Cromwell, rising fast, is Cranmer's friend.","key":["A public, joyful scene that answers the sad street scene of 2.1.","'These are stars indeed, and sometimes falling ones.'","The talk of Gardiner and Cromwell sets up Act 5."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where was Katherine's marriage declared void?","o":["Blackfriars","Dunstable","Rome","Kimbolton"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What is York Place now called?","o":["Hampton Court","Whitehall","Greenwich","Westminster"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"Who is said to be Cranmer's loyal friend?","o":["Bishop Gardiner","Thomas Cromwell","The Duke of Suffolk","The Earl of Surrey"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What contrast does the coronation scene draw?","o":["Anne's triumph against Katherine's sickness and divorce","The King's wealth against the poverty of all the lords","London's joy against the anger of the people of York","Cranmer's power against the weakness of Cromwell"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does the Gentlemen's gossip about Gardiner and Cranmer prepare?","o":["A peace between all the lords","A new rivalry at court","A war with Spain","The King's death"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"implication"}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"At Kimbolton the sick Katherine hears from Griffith how Wolsey, arrested at York, fell ill and died at Leicester Abbey, repentant. She lists his faults; Griffith answers with his virtues, his learning and his colleges, and Katherine honours him in his ashes. She sleeps and sees a vision of six spirits in white who hold a garland over her head, and wakes asking where they have gone. Capucius brings comfort from the King, too late. She gives him a letter asking the King to care for their daughter, her women and her servants, and asks to be buried like a queen.","key":["Katherine forgives Wolsey: the play's lesson of charity in its gentlest form.","The vision promises her a heavenly reward that the court denied her.","'Although unqueened, yet like a queen, and daughter to a king, inter me.'"],"quiz":[{"q":"Where did Wolsey die?","o":["In the Tower of London","Leicester Abbey","In his palace at York","At Kimbolton"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What do the spirits in Katherine's vision hold over her?","o":["A crown","A garland","A sword","A candle"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Who brings the King's comfort to Katherine?","o":["Thomas Cromwell, the King's new secretary","Capucius, the Emperor's ambassador","Griffith, her own gentleman usher","Campeius"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Griffith change Katherine's view of Wolsey?","o":["He reminds her of Wolsey's virtues","He proves that Wolsey loved her","He tells her Wolsey is still alive","He shows her Wolsey's will"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"view"},{"q":"What does Katherine's vision suggest?","o":["She is haunted by guilt for her sins","She will soon be made Queen again","She is blessed and at peace at death","She is going mad in her sickness"],"a":2,"b":4,"p":"inference"}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"At one in the morning Gardiner learns from Lovell that the Queen is in labour. He wishes Anne, Cranmer and Cromwell dead, and says he has persuaded the council to summon Cranmer as a heretic. The King, waiting, sends Suffolk to bed and has Cranmer brought. He tells him of the charges and that he must go to the Tower; Cranmer welcomes a trial. Amazed at his innocence, the King warns him of perjured witnesses and gives him a ring to show the council if they try to imprison him. The Old Lady announces a 'boy', then a girl, and is given a hundred marks, which she thinks too little.","key":["The King now acts on his own judgement, for the first time without Wolsey.","Cranmer's tears prove his honesty to the King.","The birth arrives in the same night as the plot: Elizabeth and Cranmer are tied together."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the King give Cranmer?","o":["A letter","His ring","A sword","A pardon"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Gardiner call Cranmer?","o":["A traitor to the King and to his own church","An arch heretic and a rank weed","A fool who reads too many books","A Frenchman in English clothes"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"How much does the King give the Old Lady?","o":["A hundred marks","A thousand pounds","Forty pence","Nothing"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the King's gift of a ring to Cranmer show?","o":["He wants Cranmer to marry","He doubts Cranmer's honesty","He is paying for a prayer","He protects those he trusts"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does the Old Lady's complaint about a hundred marks show?","o":["Joy at the birth of a son and heir","Fear of the angry King's temper","Pity for the weak and tired Queen","Greed even at a royal birth"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"inference"}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"Cranmer, summoned to the council, is kept waiting at the door among servants. Doctor Butts sees it and fetches the King, who watches from a window above and calls it knavery.","key":["A short scene of humiliation that the King sees with his own eyes.","Cranmer accepts it patiently, as he will accept the council's charges.","The King watching from above is like providence over the court."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who sees Cranmer waiting at the door?","o":["Cromwell","Doctor Butts","Norfolk","Gardiner"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"view"},{"q":"Where does the King watch from?","o":["Behind a curtain in the council room","A window above","The street","The Tower"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the King call the council's treatment of Cranmer?","o":["Justice","Knavery","Wisdom","Folly"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"Why does Cranmer's waiting at the door anger the King?","o":["Cranmer is late for the council meeting again","It is a deliberate insult to his councillor","The servants have refused to give him any food","Cranmer has forgotten to bring the King's ring"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Who fetches the King to see the insult?","o":["Doctor Butts","Cromwell","Gardiner","Suffolk"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a5s3":{"sum":"The council charges Cranmer with spreading heresies. Gardiner says reform must be swift, like taming a horse. Cranmer denies stirring unrest and asks to face his accusers; he is told that, as a councillor, no one dares accuse him, so he must go to the Tower. Cromwell defends him and quarrels with Gardiner. As the guard comes, Cranmer shows the King's ring. The lords are dismayed. The King enters, scorns Gardiner's flattery, rebukes them for keeping Cranmer at the door, orders them to embrace him, and asks Cranmer to be godfather to his daughter.","key":["The ring reverses the pattern of the play: this time the accused man is saved.","'Do my Lord of Canterbury a shrewd turn, and he is your friend for ever.'","The King turns a faction fight into enforced unity: 'As I have made ye one, lords, one remain.'"],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can no one accuse Cranmer while he is free?","o":["He is a councillor","He is a bishop abroad","He is ill","He is the King's cousin"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Who defends Cranmer against Gardiner?","o":["Suffolk","Cromwell","Surrey","The Lord Chancellor"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"question"},{"q":"What does the King ask Cranmer to be?","o":["Lord Chancellor of England","Godfather to his daughter","Ambassador to Rome","Keeper of the seal"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why are the lords dismayed by the ring?","o":["It proves that Cranmer is a thief and a liar","It shows the King is on Cranmer's side","It once belonged to the dead Queen Katherine","It means that Cranmer will soon be made King"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does the King's treatment of Gardiner show?","o":["He loves Gardiner most","He sees through flattery","He fears the council","He agrees with the charge"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"inference"}]},"a5s4":{"sum":"At the palace gate the Porter and his Man fail to keep back the huge crowd that has come to see the christening. The Man describes his fight with a red-nosed brazier, a haberdasher's wife and a gang of apprentices. The Lord Chamberlain scolds them and orders them to clear a way for the procession.","key":["The only prose scene of comic common people in the play.","The crowd's eagerness shows the people's joy in the new princess.","The Porter's jokes about playhouses and the suburbs are aimed at the audience."],"quiz":[{"q":"What have the crowd come to see?","o":["An execution on Tower Hill","The christening","The coronation of Queen Anne","A bear-baiting on the Bankside"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the Porter call for to beat the crowd?","o":["Guns and muskets","A dozen crab-tree staves","Dogs","Water"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"Who scolds the Porter?","o":["The King himself, from the window","The Lord Chamberlain","Archbishop Cranmer","The Duke of Norfolk"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the Porter's scene add before the christening?","o":["A secret plot against the infant","A solemn prayer for the Queen","News of war with France","Comic noise from the common people"],"a":3,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the crowd's size show?","o":["Anger at the King's new marriage to Anne","Fear of the plague in the city of London","Hunger after a long and very cold winter","Public excitement over the royal birth"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"inference"}]},"a5s5":{"sum":"The christening procession enters and Garter proclaims the Princess Elizabeth. The King kisses the child. Cranmer, inspired, prophesies her reign: she will be a pattern to all princes, loved and feared, and in her days every man will eat in safety under his own vine; from her ashes, like the phoenix, will rise an heir as great, who will make new nations. She will die a virgin, mourned by the world. The King says Cranmer has made him a man and declares a holiday. The Epilogue hopes the good women will like the play.","key":["The play ends not with a fall but with a birth and a prophecy.","The phoenix heir is James I, king when the play was first staged.","Henry's 'never before this happy child did I get anything' ironically recalls Katherine's dead sons and the reign that followed."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who proclaims the Princess's name and titles?","o":["Archbishop Cranmer","Garter King-at-Arms","The Duke of Norfolk","The King himself"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What bird does Cranmer use for the heir who will follow Elizabeth?","o":["The eagle","The phoenix","The dove","The pelican"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How will Elizabeth die, according to Cranmer?","o":["In battle against the Spanish","A virgin, old and mourned","Young, before she can rule","Murdered by her enemies at court"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Cranmer's prophecy matter to Shakespeare's audience?","o":["It warns them of a war that is coming in the very next year","It reminds them that Henry was a cruel and a wicked king","It predicts the end of the world within their own lifetime","It praises Elizabeth and James, whose reigns they knew"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"implication"},{"q":"What does the phoenix image promise?","o":["Elizabeth will be burned for her beliefs","The King will be born again in his daughter","England will be destroyed and rebuilt by fire","An heir as great will rise from her ashes"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"implication"}]}},"notes":{"1":"Prologues usually asked an audience to laugh. This one promises the opposite: a serious play, true to history, about great people brought down. 'A fellow in a long motley coat guarded with yellow' is the stage fool. The play was first performed in 1613, perhaps under the title 'All is True'.","5":"The 'two lights of men' are Henry VIII and Francis I of France, who met in 1520 at the Field of the Cloth of Gold, between Guisnes (English) and Ardres (French) near Calais. The historical Buckingham did attend; Shakespeare and Fletcher keep him away so that Norfolk can describe it.","8":"Bevis of Hampton was a hero of medieval romance whose deeds were thought impossible; the splendour in France made even his story believable.","15":"A 'keech' is a lump of animal fat rolled up by a butcher. It is a sneer at Wolsey's size and at his father's trade: Wolsey was the son of an Ipswich butcher.","28":"The 'rock' Norfolk advises Buckingham to avoid is Wolsey himself, who enters at this moment.","36":"'A beggar's book outworths a noble's blood': Buckingham means that learning, which has raised the low-born Wolsey, now counts for more than noble birth.","46":"The Emperor Charles V, Katherine's nephew, visited England in 1520. Buckingham claims he bribed Wolsey to break the new peace with France, which threatened him.","51":"Buckingham's titles show how great he is: Duke of Buckingham and Earl of Hereford, Stafford and Northampton. His descent from Edward III gave him a distant claim to the throne.","57":"Lord Montacute (Montague) was Buckingham's son-in-law. John de la Car and Gilbert Peck were Buckingham's confessor and chancellor; they will testify against him.","62":"The surveyor (estate manager) was a trusted servant; Buckingham believes Wolsey has bribed him.","66":"Stage direction: the King enters leaning on Wolsey's shoulder, a picture of how close the Cardinal stands to power.","78":"The 'Amicable Grant' of 1525 demanded a sixth of the goods of laymen to pay for war in France. It caused riots among Suffolk clothworkers, and Henry withdrew it.","82":"Wolsey makes sure the people will think the pardon came from him, though he imposed the tax. The audience alone hears this.","97":"The Rose was Buckingham's London house in the parish of St Lawrence Poultney. The 'holy monk' is Nicholas Hopkins of the Carthusian priory at Henton (Hinton).","98":"Katherine knows the surveyor lost his post when Buckingham's tenants complained, and so had a motive for revenge.","104":"Sir William Bulmer left the King's service to wear Buckingham's livery, and the King was angry. Retaining another lord's sworn servant could look like gathering private power.","106":"Buckingham's father, Henry Stafford, helped Richard III to the throne and then rebelled against him in 1483. He was betrayed and executed at Salisbury (see Richard III).","118":"Pepin and Clotharius were early kings of the Franks. The returning courtiers put on such grand French faces that their noses might have advised ancient French kings.","125":"A proclamation of 1519 did order young courtiers with French manners away from court.","127":"'Cum privilegio' (with privilege) was printed on books licensed by the crown; here it mocks the gallants' licence to be foolish elsewhere.","144":"The banquet takes place at York Place, Wolsey's London house as Archbishop of York. Henry later took it over and renamed it Whitehall (4.1).","171":"Small cannons (chambers) are fired to announce the masquers. At the first performance of the play in 1613 a cannon set the thatch of the Globe theatre alight and burned it down.","182":"Henry and his friends come as masquers dressed as shepherds, pretending to be foreign visitors. Masques were a favourite Tudor court entertainment.","197":"Henry probably first met Anne Boleyn later than 1520; the play compresses history to make Wolsey's own feast the place of the meeting.","200":"Sir Thomas Bullen (Boleyn) was made Viscount Rochford in 1525. Anne served as one of Katherine's ladies-in-waiting.","223":"Buckingham was tried by his peers in May 1521 with the Duke of Norfolk presiding. 'Viva voce' means in person, by spoken word.","231":"The Earl of Kildare, Deputy of Ireland, was removed and imprisoned. Surrey replaced him, which kept Surrey away from his father-in-law's trial.","238":"The executed nobleman's farewell speech was a recognised genre: the condemned man accepted the law, forgave enemies and prayed for the King. 'The long divorce of steel' is the axe that separates head from body.","243":"'Edward Bohun': having lost his titles, Buckingham names himself after an ancestor. Henry VII restored the family's honours after Bosworth.","252":"The 'scruple' is a doubt of conscience: that Henry's marriage to his brother Arthur's widow was against the law of God (Leviticus 20:21).","253":"The Emperor did not make Wolsey Archbishop of Toledo, a rich Spanish see. The Gentlemen believe Wolsey pushes the divorce to punish Charles through his aunt.","259":"The Chamberlain's letter is the only prose in the play before the Porter scene.","268":"Fortune was pictured as blind, turning a wheel. Norfolk calls Wolsey Fortune's eldest son, raising and lowering men as he likes.","270":"The 'French king's sister' is Marguerite, Duchess of Alencon, Wolsey's choice of a new queen.","294":"Wolsey presents the divorce as a matter for the whole of Christendom and the learned clergy of Europe.","296":"Lorenzo Campeggio (Campeius) was sent by Pope Clement VII in 1528 to try the case jointly with Wolsey.","306":"Richard Pace was the King's secretary before Gardiner. He was sent on embassies abroad and later suffered a mental breakdown.","316":"Blackfriars, the Dominican priory in London, where the legatine court sat in 1529. Shakespeare's company played in the Blackfriars theatre nearby.","320":"Anne is talking with the Old Lady as the scene begins: 'Not for that neither' answers something we have not heard.","331":"The Old Lady's jokes are bawdy: to 'queen it' plays on 'quean' (whore), and 'a three-pence bowed' (bent) is next to worthless.","335":"'Emballing' means being given the royal orb (ball) at a coronation, with a sexual double meaning.","341":"Anne was created Marquess (here 'Marchioness') of Pembroke in 1532, in her own right, with lands worth a thousand pounds a year.","343":"The 'gem to lighten all this isle' looks forward to Elizabeth I.","355":"The long stage direction reproduces the ceremony at Blackfriars from Holinshed's Chronicles almost word for word.","365":"Katherine's speech follows the account in Holinshed closely. She was first married to Henry's elder brother Arthur, who died in 1502. Her father Ferdinand of Aragon and Henry VII had the marriage approved by papal dispensation.","374":"Katherine appeals from the legatine court to the Pope directly, refusing Wolsey as judge.","384":"The Bishop of Bayonne (Tarbes in some sources) came in 1527 to discuss a French marriage for Princess Mary. Katherine had several sons who died at birth or in infancy; only Mary survived.","388":"Lincoln was Henry's confessor, John Longland. Canterbury in 1529 was William Warham, not Cranmer.","390":"Henry's aside shows him impatient with Rome. Cranmer was then abroad consulting universities on the divorce.","395":"Orpheus, the mythical Greek singer, could move trees, rocks and the sea with his music.","407":"Wolsey begins in Latin: 'So great is our integrity of mind towards you, most serene queen'. Katherine insists on English.","445":"In history Wolsey's ruin came in 1529 from his failure to get the divorce, not from a misdirected letter. The play combines two stories from Holinshed: one about Wolsey's letters and one about another bishop's inventory sent by mistake.","461":"Cranmer had gathered opinions from universities in Europe that the marriage was invalid. 'Princess Dowager' was Katherine's title as Arthur's widow.","481":"Wolsey calls Anne a 'spleeny Lutheran', a hot-headed Protestant. The historical Anne did favour religious reform.","488":"The inventory story is taken from Holinshed, where it concerns Thomas Ruthall, Bishop of Durham, who sent the King the wrong book.","510":"The great seal was the symbol of the Lord Chancellor's office. Asher House is Esher, the Bishop of Winchester's house in Surrey.","518":"Surrey calls Wolsey a 'scarlet sin' from the red of his cardinal's robes.","522":"The sacring bell rang at the consecration in the Mass. Surrey's 'brown wench' is a slur on Wolsey's private life; the historical Wolsey had a mistress and children.","528":"A papal legate had authority over all English bishops. Many of these charges come from the articles brought against Wolsey in Parliament in 1529.","529":"'Ego et Rex meus': 'I and my king'. Latin puts the speaker first, but the nobles took it as an insult to the King.","533":"Wolsey was said to have hoped to become Pope.","536":"Praemunire was the offence of upholding a foreign (papal) authority against the crown. The penalty was loss of all goods and of the King's protection.","539":"Wolsey's speech uses the image of a tree killed by frost and a boy swimming on bladders (inflated pig bladders used as floats).","550":"Sir Thomas More became Lord Chancellor in October 1529. He was executed in 1535 for refusing to accept Henry as head of the Church, which the audience would know.","552":"Cranmer was made Archbishop of Canterbury in 1533.","554":"Henry married Anne secretly in January 1533. The play moves this before Wolsey's fall in 1529.","557":"Cromwell rose to be Henry's chief minister. He was executed in 1540: Wolsey's 'fling away ambition' has an irony the audience would feel.","571":"Certain lords claimed the right by custom to serve at a coronation: Suffolk as High Steward, Norfolk as Earl Marshal.","573":"Katherine's marriage was declared void by Cranmer at Dunstable in May 1533. Ampthill is near Dunstable; Kimbolton is in Huntingdonshire.","579":"The coronation procession stage direction follows Holinshed's account of Anne's coronation on 1 June 1533. 'Collars of SS' were chains of office.","587":"The Cinque Ports were five south-coast ports whose barons had the right to carry the canopy over the monarch at a coronation.","590":"The Second Gentleman's 'falling stars' hints at the falls of the play, and at Anne's own execution in 1536.","604":"Henry took over York Place after Wolsey's fall and renamed it Whitehall.","607":"John Stokesley, Bishop of London, and Stephen Gardiner, Bishop of Winchester.","622":"Wolsey was arrested at Cawood near York by the Earl of Northumberland in November 1530 and died at Leicester Abbey on his way south.","625":"Simony is the buying and selling of church offices.","628":"Wolsey founded a college at Ipswich, which closed after his fall, and Cardinal College at Oxford, refounded by Henry as Christ Church.","633":"The vision is one of the play's masque-like spectacles. Katherine died at Kimbolton in January 1536, after Elizabeth's birth, though the play puts her death before it.","663":"The 'young daughter' is Mary, later Queen Mary I.","673":"Primero was a card game, a kind of early poker.","681":"Gardiner and other conservative bishops did try to bring Cranmer before the council in the 1540s; the play moves the story to 1533.","719":"The story of the ring is from Foxe's Book of Martyrs, a Protestant account that praises Cranmer.","726":"Elizabeth was born on 7 September 1533 at Greenwich.","766":"The Lord Chancellor in 1533 was Thomas Audley.","767":"'Upper Germany' recalls the Peasants' War of 1524 to 1525, blamed by conservatives on Reformation preaching.","807":"Godparents traditionally gave christening spoons. The old Duchess of Norfolk and the Marchioness of Dorset were Elizabeth's godmothers.","814":"Paris Garden was a bear-baiting ring on the south bank of the Thames, near the Globe.","821":"Samson, Sir Guy of Warwick and the giant Colbrand were heroes of strength from the Bible and popular romance.","825":"Moorfields, outside the city wall, was used for drilling the trained bands. 'Some strange Indian with the great tool' is a bawdy joke about Native Americans brought to London to be shown.","827":"Tower Hill was a place of public execution; Limehouse was a rough dockside district. 'Limbo Patrum' is slang for the lockup.","838":"Garter King-at-Arms was the chief herald; this proclamation follows Holinshed.","848":"Saba is the Queen of Sheba, who came to test Solomon's wisdom. 'Every man shall eat in safety under his own vine' echoes the Bible (Micah 4:4). The phoenix, a bird that rises from its own ashes, stands for James I, who succeeded Elizabeth in 1603.","850":"Elizabeth died in 1603, aged sixty-nine, unmarried: the 'virgin queen'.","853":"The Epilogue jokes that only women will like the play, because it showed them such a good woman (Katherine)."},"gloss":{"ague":"fever","suns of glory":"the two kings","clinquant":"glittering","cherubins":"angels, cherubs","censure":"criticism, judgement","discourser":"teller of a story","certes":"certainly","keech":"lump of fat","niggard":"miser","privity":"private knowledge, consent","minister":"supply, provide; agent","aboded":"foretold","flaw'd":"broken","attach'd":"arrested","potency":"power","chaf'd":"angry, irritated","choler":"anger","vouch":"assertion","subtle":"cunning","colour":"pretext, excuse","trow":"believe","device":"plot, trickery","practice":"plotting","spann'd":"measured out","level":"line of fire","grievance":"distress","commissions":"official orders, warrants","clothiers":"cloth manufacturers","spinsters":"women who spin thread","carders":"workers who comb wool","fullers":"workers who clean and thicken cloth","exactions":"heavy demands for money","pestilent":"poisonous, harmful","primer":"more urgent","traduc'd":"slandered","stint":"stop, hold back","cope":"meet, encounter","censurers":"critics","precedent":"earlier example","lop":"small branches","conception":"idea, plan","malignant":"malicious","Chartreux":"Carthusian (monk)","commonalty":"common people","rank":"gross, foul","period":"goal, end","spavin":"swelling in a horse's leg","springhalt":"stringhalt, twitching disease of horses","remnants":"leftover scraps","blist'red":"puffed","physic":"medicine","plainsong":"simple melody","comptrollers":"masters of ceremonies","bevy":"company (of ladies)","running banquet":"quick snack; a hasty affair","beholding":"indebted","gamester":"player, gambler","moiety":"half","viva voce":"by word of mouth, in person","attainder":"condemnation for treason","envious":"malicious","perniciously":"deadly, to destruction","monument":"tomb","furniture":"fittings","rub":"obstacle","fain":"gladly","scruple":"doubt of conscience","list":"likes, wishes","conclave":"college of cardinals","unpartial":"impartial","holy fox":"cunning churchman","temporal":"worldly","avaunt":"order to be gone","sufferance":"suffering","glist'ring":"glittering","cheveril":"kid leather, easily stretched","emballing":"being given the royal orb","hap":"luck","vouchsafe":"be willing to; grant","pomp":"splendour","consistory":"church court","spleen":"malice, bad temper","abhor":"loathe; protest against","gainsay":"deny","dilatory":"delaying","dowager":"widow with a title from her late husband","truant":"idler","cordial":"heart-comforting medicine","professors":"those who profess religion","carriage":"behaviour","mere":"utter, complete","coasts":"goes round about","inventory":"detailed list of possessions","husband":"manager, steward","requite":"repay","allegiant":"loyal","exhalation":"meteor, shooting star","letters-patents":"royal documents granting rights","jaded":"bullied, treated like an old horse","sacring bell":"bell rung at the consecration in Mass","legate":"the Pope's representative","item":"also (used in lists)","praemunire":"crime of upholding papal authority against the crown","chattels":"movable possessions","bladders":"inflated pig bladders used as swimming floats","Lucifer":"the fallen angel, Satan","chronicler":"historian","pageants":"public shows","sceptre":"rod held by a monarch","Cinque-ports":"five south-coast ports with special privileges","broiling":"roasting (in the crowd)","rankness":"smell, excess","shrouds":"ropes of a ship's mast","press":"crowd","preferr'd":"promoted","stomach":"pride, arrogance","simony":"buying or selling of church posts","covent":"convent, community of monks","saucy":"insolent","model":"image, copy","unqueen'd":"no longer queen","primero":"a card game","grubb'd":"dug up","remark'd":"noticed, prominent","convented":"summoned","suff'rance":"suffering","holidame":"holiness; by my holidame = by Our Lady","indurance":"imprisonment, delay","precipice":"cliff edge","pursuivants":"junior heralds, messengers","lackeys":"footmen","purgation":"clearing of guilt","sectary":"member of a sect, heretic","faith":"religious belief","commendations":"greetings, praise","gossips":"godparents","prodigal":"generous to excess","staves":"staffs, clubs","crab-tree":"crab-apple wood","chine":"joint of meat","cuckold":"man whose wife is unfaithful","brazier":"brass-worker","fire-drake":"fiery dragon; meteor","porringer":"bowl; here a round cap","truncheoners":"men armed with clubs","beadles":"parish officers who whipped offenders","bombards":"large leather jugs of drink","camlet":"fine cloth","pales":"fence","Saba":"the Queen of Sheba","phoenix":"mythical bird reborn from its ashes","construction":"interpretation","Bevis":"hero of romance","conscience":"inner sense of right and wrong; also, inward knowledge","stranger":"foreigner","indifferent":"impartial","fair conceit":"good opinion","tender":"offer","sooth":"truth","spleeny":"hot-headed","dew":"blessing (like rain)","malicious":"spiteful","chiding":"roaring, raging","lewdness":"foolishness, vice","ravenous":"greedy","mule":"riding animal of a churchman","abbot":"head of an abbey","fellowship":"companionship","wag":"move, stir","knavery":"villainy","hither":"here","anon":"soon","prithee":"please","betwixt":"between","whence":"from where","wont":"used to; wonted = usual","durst":"dared","ere":"before","wherefore":"why","marry":"indeed (a mild oath, by the Virgin Mary)","forsooth":"truly, indeed","beshrew":"curse","troth":"faith, truth","moe":"more","perforce":"by force, necessarily","sirrah":"fellow (said to an inferior)","withal":"with it, as well","gall":"bitterness","founts":"springs","hulling":"drifting with sails furled","maz'd":"bewildered","amity":"friendship","exclamation":"loud complaint","appliance":"remedy","temp'rance":"self-control","mettle":"spirit, energy","countenance":"face, expression; favour","approbation":"approval","mincing":"affected prudishness","aspect":"look, appearance","colt's tooth":"youthful lust","state-statues":"statues of statesmen","nought":"nothing; worthless","dog-days":"hottest days of summer","under the line":"at the equator","combustion":"uproar","limbo patrum":"prison (slang)","gaping":"bawling","ruminate":"brood on"}};
const PARTS={"1":[[0,5,"I have not come to make you laugh this time. We now present serious, weighty scenes, sad and lofty, full of state affairs and grief, noble scenes that will bring tears to your eyes."],[6,11,"Those of you who can feel pity may shed a tear here if you like; the subject deserves it. Those who pay hoping to see something true will find truth here too. Those who come only for a spectacle or two, and are willing to let the play pass,"],[12,21,"I promise will get their shilling's worth in two short hours. Only those who come to hear a merry, bawdy play, the clash of shields, or a clown in a long motley coat trimmed with yellow will be disappointed. For, gentle audience, if we mixed our chosen true story with such foolery and fighting, we would throw away our own wits and our aim of showing only what is true, and no thinking person would stay our friend."],[22,31,"So, for goodness' sake, and since you are known as the best and most discerning audience in the city, be as sad as we want to make you. Imagine you see the real people of our noble story as if they were alive; see them great, followed by crowds of thousands of sweating friends; then see how in a moment this greatness meets misery. And if you can be merry then, I'll say a man can weep on his wedding day."]],"8":[[0,5,"Then you missed the sight of earthly glory. You could say that until then splendour had been single, but now it married something greater than itself. Each day outdid the one before, until the last one gathered up all the earlier wonders as its own."],[6,14,"One day the French, all glittering, all in gold like pagan gods, outshone the English; the next, the English made Britain look like India: every man standing there shone like a gold mine. Their little pages were like cherubs, all gilded; the ladies too, not used to work, almost sweated under the weight of their finery, so that the effort of carrying it made them blush as if painted. One night's masque was called beyond compare,"],[15,25,"and the next night's made it look poor and foolish. The two kings, equal in splendour, were now one the best, now the other, depending on who was in sight; whichever you were looking at got the praise, and when both were present people said they saw only one, and no critic dared say a word against either. When these two suns, as they called them, had their heralds challenge the nobles to joust, they performed beyond belief, so that old fabulous legends, now seen to be possible, were believed, and even the tale of Bevis was credited."]],"16":[[0,9,"Surely, sir, he has qualities that drive him to these ends. He isn't supported by noble ancestry, whose favour marks out the way for heirs; he isn't called on for great services to the crown; he isn't connected to powerful allies. Like a spider spinning a web out of itself, he shows us that the force of his own merit makes his way, a gift from heaven that buys him the place next to the King."]],"28":[[0,8,"If your Grace pleases, the government has noticed the private quarrel between you and the Cardinal. I advise you, and take this from a heart that wishes you honour and safety, to weigh the Cardinal's malice and his power together, and to consider that whatever his great hatred wants done, he has someone in his power to do it."],[9,14,"You know his nature, that he is revengeful, and I know his sword has a sharp edge; it is long, and you might say it reaches far, and where it won't reach he throws it. Keep my advice close; you'll find it healthy. Look, here comes the rock I advise you to steer around."]],"41":[[0,6,"Be careful. Don't heat the furnace for your enemy so hot that it scorches you. We can overshoot what we are running at by running too fast, and lose by overrunning. Don't you know that the fire that makes liquid boil over seems to increase it but actually wastes it? Be careful."],[7,10,"I say again, there is no English soul better able to guide you than yourself, if you would quench, or just cool, the fire of passion with the sap of reason."]],"44":[[0,10,"I'll say it to the King, and make my claim as firm as a rocky shore. Listen. This holy fox, or wolf, or both, for he is as greedy as he is cunning, and as ready for mischief as he is able to carry it out, his mind and his position infecting each other, just to show off his splendour in France as well as at home, urged the King our master into this last expensive treaty, the meeting that swallowed so much treasure and broke like a glass in the rinsing."]],"46":[[0,6,"Please, let me go on, sir. This cunning Cardinal drew up the terms of the alliance just as he liked, and they were ratified as soon as he said \"Let it be so,\" with as much use as giving a crutch to a dead man. But our cardinal-count did it, so it must be good, for worthy Wolsey, who can't make mistakes, did it."],[7,15,"Now this follows, which I take to be a puppy of the old bitch treason: Charles the Emperor, pretending to visit his aunt the Queen, though that was only his excuse, came to whisper with Wolsey. He feared that the meeting between England and France might, through their friendship, do him harm, since threats to him peeped out of this alliance."],[16,25,"He deals secretly with our Cardinal, and, as I believe, and I'm sure of it, for the Emperor paid before he promised, so his request was granted before it was asked, when the way had been made and paved with gold, the Emperor asked that Wolsey turn the King's course and break that peace. Let the King know, as he soon will from me, that this is how the Cardinal buys and sells the King's honour as he likes, for his own profit."]],"72":[[0,11,"I am being petitioned, not by a few people, and those honest ones, that your subjects are in great distress. Commissions have been sent among them that have cracked the heart of their loyalty. In this, although they pour bitter reproaches on you, my good Lord Cardinal, as the one who urged these demands, yet even the King our master, whose honour heaven keep from stain, does not escape rude language, language that breaks the bounds of loyalty and almost appears as open rebellion."]],"76":[[0,9,"No, my lord? You know no more than others? But you devise things that everyone then knows, which are harmful to those who would rather not know them but are forced to become familiar with them. These demands, which my sovereign wants to hear about, are poisonous even to hear of, and bearing them breaks the back under the load. They say you devised them; if not, you are blamed too harshly."]],"78":[[0,6,"I am too bold in testing your patience, but your promised pardon gives me courage. The subjects' grievance comes from commissions that force each man to pay a sixth of all he owns, to be collected without delay, and the reason given is your wars in France."],[7,13,"This makes mouths bold; tongues spit out their duty, and cold hearts freeze their allegiance. Their curses now live where their prayers used to, and it has come to this: their willing obedience has become a slave to every angry impulse. I wish your Highness would consider it quickly, for there is no more urgent business."]],"80":[[0,7,"As for me, I went no further in this than casting a single vote, and that only with the learned approval of the judges. If I am slandered by ignorant tongues that know neither my abilities nor me, yet set themselves up as historians of what I do, let me say that this is simply the fate of high office, the thorny thicket that virtue must go through."],[8,20,"We must not hold back necessary actions for fear of facing malicious critics, who always follow like greedy fish behind a newly fitted ship but gain nothing except useless longing. What we often do best, sick or weak interpreters say is not ours or not good; what we do worst, because it appeals to cruder minds, is often praised as our best act. If we stand still for fear our actions will be mocked or criticised, we should take root where we sit, or sit like statues of statesmen."]],"81":[[0,6,"Things done well and carefully have nothing to fear; things done without precedent are to be feared in their results. Do you have a precedent for this commission? I believe not. We must not tear our subjects away from our laws and fix them on our mere will. A sixth of everything? A contribution to make men tremble!"],[7,14,"Why, it is as if we took from every tree its branches, bark and part of the trunk; though we leave the root, the tree so hacked will have its sap drunk by the air. Send our letters to every county where this is disputed, with a free pardon for every man who has refused this commission. Please see to it; I leave it in your care."]],"86":[[0,8,"It grieves many. The gentleman is learned and a remarkably fine speaker, more gifted by nature than anyone; his education is such that he could teach great teachers and never need help from outside himself. Yet see: when such noble gifts are badly used and the mind once becomes corrupt, they turn into vicious forms ten times uglier than they were ever beautiful."],[9,18,"This man, so complete, who was counted among the wonders, whom we listened to almost entranced so that an hour of his speech seemed a minute, has, my lady, turned the graces that were once his into monstrous habits and has become as black as if smeared in hell. Sit beside us; you shall hear things about him, from this man who was his trusted servant, that would sadden honour itself. Tell him to repeat the plots he already described, which we cannot be too little moved by, nor hear too much of."]],"97":[[0,8,"Not long before your Highness went to France, the Duke, at the Rose in the parish of Saint Lawrence Poultney, asked me what the Londoners were saying about the French journey. I replied that people feared the French would prove treacherous, endangering the King. At once the Duke said that was indeed the fear, and he suspected it would prove the truth of certain words spoken by a holy monk."],[9,20,"\"He has often sent to me,\" he said, \"asking me to allow my chaplain, John de la Car, a convenient hour to hear something important from him. After he had made my chaplain swear solemnly, under the seal of confession, to tell what he said to no living creature but me, he said slowly and with grave confidence: 'Neither the King nor his heirs, tell the Duke, shall prosper. Tell him to work to win the love of the common people. The Duke shall govern England.'\""]],"100":[[0,9,"On my soul, I'll speak only the truth. I told my lord the Duke that the monk might have been deceived by the devil's tricks, and that it was dangerous for him to brood on this so long that it led him into some plot, which, if he believed it, it was very likely to do. He answered, \"Nonsense, it can do me no harm,\" and added that if the King had died in his last illness, the Cardinal's and Sir Thomas Lovell's heads would have come off."]],"127":[[0,12,"By the terms of it, they must either give up the scraps of folly and feathers they picked up in France, with all the honourable bits of ignorance that go with them, such as duelling and fireworks and insulting better men than themselves with foreign wisdom; renounce completely their faith in tennis, tall stockings, short puffed breeches and those other badges of travel, and behave like honest men again; or else pack off to their old playmates. There, I suppose, they may, \"with privilege,\" wear out the last of their lewdness and be laughed at."]],"223":[[0,11,"I'll tell you briefly. The great Duke came to the bar, where he kept pleading not guilty to the charges and offered many sharp arguments to defeat the law. The King's attorney, on the other side, pressed the statements, proofs and confessions of various witnesses, whom the Duke asked to have brought to his face to speak in person. Then his surveyor appeared against him, Sir Gilbert Peck his chancellor, and John Car his confessor, along with that devil of a monk, Hopkins, who caused this mischief."]],"238":[[0,6,"All you good people who have come this far out of pity for me, hear what I say, and then go home and forget me. Today I have received a traitor's sentence and must die under that name; yet heaven be my witness, and if I have a conscience, let it sink me as the axe falls if I am not loyal!"],[7,13,"I bear the law no grudge for my death; on the evidence it has done only justice. But I wish those who sought it were better Christians. Whoever they are, I forgive them with all my heart; but let them take care not to glory in mischief, nor build their evil on great men's graves, for then my innocent blood will cry out against them."],[14,23,"I never hope for more life in this world, nor will I beg for it, although the King has more mercy than I dare commit faults. You few who loved me and dare to weep for Buckingham, his noble friends and companions, whom it is the only bitterness, the only death, to leave, go with me like good angels to my end; and as the long divorce of the steel falls on me, make your prayers one sweet offering and lift my soul to heaven. Lead on, in God's name."]],"240":[[0,6,"Sir Thomas Lovell, I forgive you as freely as I would be forgiven. I forgive everyone. There cannot be so many offences against me that I cannot make peace with them; no dark hatred shall mark my grave. Give my respects to his Grace, and if he speaks of Buckingham, please tell him you met him half in heaven."],[7,12,"My vows and prayers are still the King's, and until my soul leaves me they will cry for blessings on him. May he live longer than I have time to count his years! May his rule be always loved and loving! And when old Time leads him to his end, may goodness and he share one monument!"]],"243":[[0,6,"No, Sir Nicholas, leave it; my former rank would only mock me now. When I came here I was Lord High Constable and Duke of Buckingham; now I'm poor Edward Bohun. Yet I am richer than my low accusers, who never knew what truth meant. I seal my truth now, and with this blood I'll make them groan for it one day."],[7,20,"My noble father, Henry of Buckingham, who first raised an army against the usurper Richard, fled for help to his servant Banister when he was in trouble, was betrayed by that wretch, and died without trial; God's peace be with him! Henry the Seventh, who came after, truly pitying my father's loss, like a most royal prince restored me to my honours and made my name noble again out of the ruins. Now his son, Henry the Eighth, has taken life, honour, name and everything that made me happy from the world for ever at one stroke. I had a trial, and I must say a noble one, which makes me a little happier than my wretched father."],[21,31,"Yet in this our fortunes are the same: we both fell through our servants, through the men we loved most, a most unnatural and faithless service. Heaven has a purpose in everything; yet you who hear me, take this as certain from a dying man: where you are generous with your love and advice, be sure you are not careless. Those you make friends of and give your hearts to, once they see the least bump in your fortunes, fall away from you like water and are never found again except where they mean to sink you."],[32,36,"All good people, pray for me! I must leave you now. The last hour of my long, weary life has come. Farewell! And when you want to tell a sad story, tell how I fell. I have finished; and God forgive me!"]],"270":[[0,5,"How holy he is in all his business, and how zealous! Now that he has broken the alliance between us and the Emperor, the Queen's great nephew, he dives into the King's soul and scatters there dangers, doubts, pangs of conscience, fears and despair, all about his marriage."],[6,13,"And to rescue the King from all of these, he advises a divorce: losing her who has hung like a jewel round his neck for twenty years without ever losing her shine; her who loves him with the perfect love angels have for good men; her who, even when the heaviest blow of fortune falls, will bless the King. Isn't this a pious course?"]],"294":[[0,6,"Your Grace has given an example of wisdom above all princes by freely submitting your doubt to the judgement of all Christendom. Who can be angry now? What malice can reach you? The Spanish, tied to her by blood and favour, must now admit, if they have any goodness, that the trial is just and noble."],[7,12,"All the clergy, I mean the learned ones, in Christian kingdoms are free to give their opinions. Rome, the nurse of judgement, invited by your noble self, has sent us one voice to speak for all, this good man, this just and learned priest, Cardinal Campeius, whom I present to your Highness once more."]],"320":[[0,10,"Not for that either. Here's the pain that pinches: his Highness has lived so long with her, and she is so good a lady that no tongue could ever speak of dishonour in her; on my life, she never knew how to do harm. Now, after so many years on the throne, always growing in majesty and splendour, which are a thousand times more bitter to lose than they are sweet to gain at first, after all this, to send her packing is a pity that would move a monster."]],"327":[[0,9,"Curse me, I would, and risk my virginity for it; and so would you, for all this flavour of hypocrisy. You, who have such lovely woman's features, have a woman's heart too, which has always wanted high rank, wealth and power; which, to tell the truth, are blessings; and, for all your primness, your soft, stretchy kid-leather conscience would take in those gifts if you cared to stretch it."]],"365":[[0,8,"Sir, I ask you to do me right and justice and to take pity on me, for I am a very poor woman and a foreigner, born outside your kingdom, with no impartial judge here and no assurance of fair friendship or fair proceedings. Alas, sir, how have I offended you? What cause has my behaviour given for your displeasure, that you should proceed like this to cast me off and take your good grace from me?"],[9,21,"Heaven be my witness, I have been a true and humble wife to you, always obedient to your will, always afraid of provoking your dislike, yes, following your expression, glad or sorry as I saw it turn. When did I ever contradict your wishes, or not make them mine too? Which of your friends have I not tried to love, even knowing he was my enemy? Which friend of mine who had earned your anger did I go on liking? No, I let him know he was dismissed."],[22,31,"Sir, remember that I have been your wife in this obedience for more than twenty years, and have been blessed with many children by you. If in all that time you can report and prove anything against my honour, my marriage vows, or my love and duty to your sacred person, then in God's name turn me away, let the foulest contempt shut the door on me, and hand me over to the harshest justice."],[32,44,"If you please, sir, the King your father was known as a most prudent prince, of excellent, unmatched intelligence and judgement; my father Ferdinand, King of Spain, was thought the wisest prince who had reigned for many years before. There is no question that they gathered wise councillors from each kingdom to debate this business, and these judged our marriage lawful. So I humbly beg you, sir, to spare me until I can be advised by my friends in Spain, whose counsel I will beg for. If not, in God's name, let your will be done!"]],"372":[[0,10,"I will, when you are humble; no, even before, or God will punish me. I believe, persuaded by strong evidence, that you are my enemy, and I object that you shall not be my judge, for it is you who have blown on this coal between my lord and me; may God's dew put it out! So I say again: I utterly loathe you, and from my soul I refuse you as my judge, you whom once more I consider my most malicious enemy and no friend at all to truth."]],"373":[[0,10,"I declare you are not speaking like yourself; you have always been charitable and shown a gentle nature and a wisdom beyond a woman's. Madam, you wrong me. I have no malice against you, and intend no injustice to you or anyone. How far I have gone, and how much further I shall go, is authorised by a commission from the papal court, the whole court of Rome. You accuse me of blowing on this coal. I deny it."],[11,21,"The King is here. If he knows that I am denying what I did, how rightly he could punish my falsehood, as much as you have wounded my truth! If he knows I am innocent of what you say, he knows I am not guilty of wronging you. So it is in his power to cure me, and the cure is to remove these thoughts from you. Before his Highness speaks on this, I beg you, gracious madam, to take back what you said and say it no more."]],"374":[[0,10,"My lord, my lord, I am a simple woman, far too weak to stand against your cunning. You are mild and humble in speech; you mark your position and calling, to all appearances, with meekness and humility; but your heart is crammed with arrogance, malice and pride. Through luck and his Highness's favours you have skipped lightly over the low steps and now sit so high that powerful men are your servants, and your words, like household servants, do whatever you tell them."],[11,16,"I must tell you, you care more for your own honour than for your high spiritual calling. So again I refuse you as my judge, and here, before you all, I appeal to the Pope, to bring my whole case before his Holiness and be judged by him."]],"382":[[0,10,"Go your way, Kate. Any man in the world who claims he has a better wife should not be trusted in anything, for lying about that. If your rare qualities, your sweet gentleness, your saintly meekness, your wifely self-command, obedient while commanding, and your other royal and devout qualities could speak for you, you alone would be the queen of earthly queens. She is nobly born, and she has behaved toward me with true nobility."]],"383":[[0,12,"Most gracious sir, I humbly ask your Highness to declare in the hearing of everyone here, for where I was robbed and bound I must be set free there, though not at once fully compensated, whether I ever raised this business with your Highness or put any doubt in your way that might lead you to question your marriage; or whether I ever said to you, except to thank God for so royal a lady, the smallest word that might harm her present position or touch her good person."]],"384":[[0,11,"My Lord Cardinal, I excuse you; yes, on my honour, I clear you of it. You don't need telling that you have many enemies who don't know why they are your enemies, but like village dogs bark when the others do; some of these have made the Queen angry. You are excused. But would you like to be cleared further? You have always wished this business to stay asleep, never wanted it stirred, but often, often blocked the moves toward it. On my honour, I speak for my good Lord Cardinal on this point and so far clear him."],[12,26,"Now, as to what moved me to it, I'll take some time and your attention; so mark the cause. It came like this; listen. My conscience first felt a tenderness, a doubt and a prick, at certain words spoken by the Bishop of Bayonne, then the French ambassador, who had been sent here to negotiate a marriage between the Duke of Orleans and our daughter Mary. In the course of this business, before a firm decision, the Bishop asked for a delay in which he could tell the King his master whether our daughter was legitimate, considering our marriage to the widow who was once our brother's wife."],[27,41,"This delay shook my conscience to its heart, entered me with a splitting force and made my whole breast tremble; it forced such a way in that many bewildered thoughts crowded in after this warning. First, I thought I was not in Heaven's favour, which had ordered nature that if my lady's womb conceived a male child by me it should do no more to keep it alive than the grave does for the dead; for her sons either died in the womb or shortly after this world had given them air. So I thought this was a judgement on me: that my kingdom, worthy of the best heir in the world, should not be made glad by one from me."],[42,54,"Then I considered the danger my kingdoms were in through my lack of an heir, and that gave me many groaning pangs. So, drifting on the wild sea of my conscience, I steered toward this remedy, which is why we are here together; that is, I meant to set right my conscience, which I then felt was very sick and still is not well, with the help of all the reverend fathers of the land and learned doctors. I began first in private with you, my Lord of Lincoln. You remember how I sweated under my burden when I first raised it with you."]],"388":[[0,8,"Then I raised it with you, my Lord of Canterbury, and got your permission to call this present hearing. I left no reverend person in this court unasked, but went ahead with the agreement of each, under your signatures and seals. So go on; for it is no dislike in the world of the good Queen's person, but the sharp, thorny points of the reasons I have given, that drives this forward."],[9,13,"Only prove our marriage lawful, and on my life and royal dignity we are content to spend the rest of our mortal life with her, Katherine our queen, rather than with the most perfect creature in the world."]],"395":[[0,5,"Orpheus with his lute made trees and frozen mountain tops bow down when he sang. Plants and flowers always sprang up to his music, as if sun and showers had made an everlasting spring there."],[6,11,"Everything that heard him play, even the waves of the sea, hung their heads and then lay still. In sweet music there is such art that care and heartache, hearing it, fall asleep or die."]],"406":[[0,10,"Say it here. Nothing I have done yet, on my conscience, needs a corner to hide in. I wish all other women could say this with as clear a soul as I do! My lords, I don't care, and in this I am happier than many, if my actions were judged by every tongue and seen by every eye, with malice and low opinion set against them; I know my life is so blameless. If your business is about me and my position as a wife, out with it boldly. Truth loves plain dealing."]],"409":[[0,11,"Noble lady, I am sorry that my honesty and my service to his Majesty and you should cause such deep suspicion when only good faith was meant. We don't come to accuse you or to stain the honour every good tongue blesses, nor to lead you into sorrow in any way; you have too much, good lady. We come to learn your mind on the serious dispute between the King and you, and to give you, as free and honest men, our sincere opinions and comfort for your cause."]],"411":[[0,6,"(Aside) To betray me. (Aloud) My lords, I thank you both for your good will. You speak like honest men; God grant you prove to be! But how to give you a quick answer on such a serious matter, so close to my honour, and closer to my life, I fear, with my weak wits, and to such grave and learned men, I really don't know."],[7,13,"I was working among my maids, little expecting, God knows, such men or such business. For the sake of what I have been, for I feel the last throes of my greatness, good your Graces, let me have time and counsel for my cause. Alas, I am a woman, friendless and hopeless!"]],"413":[[0,9,"In England they are of little use to me. Can you believe, lords, that any Englishman would dare give me advice, or be known as my friend against his Highness's wishes, even if he had become desperate enough to be honest, and still live as a subject? No, indeed. My friends, those who truly weigh my troubles, those I can trust, don't live here; they are, like all my other comforts, far away in my own country, lords."]],"420":[[0,9,"The more shame on you! I thought you were holy men, on my soul, two reverend cardinal virtues; but I fear you are cardinal sins and hollow hearts. Mend them, for shame, my lords! Is this your comfort, the medicine you bring a miserable lady, a woman lost among you, laughed at and scorned? I won't wish you half my miseries; I have more charity than that. But remember, I warned you. Take care, for heaven's sake, take care that the whole weight of my sorrows doesn't fall on you at once."]],"422":[[0,10,"You turn me into nothing. A curse on you and on all such false believers! Would you have me, if you have any justice or pity, if you are anything more than churchmen's robes, put my sick case into the hands of the man who hates me? Alas, he has already banished me from his bed, and from his love long ago! I am old, my lords, and the only bond I have with him now is my obedience. What can happen to me worse than this misery? All your efforts bring me this kind of curse."]],"424":[[0,8,"Have I lived so long, let me speak for myself, since virtue finds no friends, as a wife, a true one? A woman, I dare say without boasting, never once branded with suspicion? Have I always met the King with all my love? Loved him next to Heaven? Obeyed him? Out of fondness, almost worshipped him? Almost forgotten my prayers to please him? And am I rewarded like this? It isn't right, lords."],[9,12,"Show me a woman faithful to her husband, one who never dreamed of any joy beyond his pleasure, and to that woman, when she has done her utmost, I will still add one honour more: a great patience."]],"428":[[0,10,"I wish I had never set foot on this English soil, or felt the flattery that grows on it! You have angels' faces, but Heaven knows your hearts. What will become of me now, wretched lady? I am the unhappiest woman alive. Alas, poor girls, where are your fortunes now! Shipwrecked in a kingdom where there is no pity, no friends, no hope; no relatives weep for me; I'm hardly allowed a grave. Like the lily that was once mistress of the field and flourished, I'll hang my head and die."]],"429":[[0,8,"If your Grace could only be brought to see that our purposes are honest, you would feel more comfort. Why should we wrong you, good lady, and for what reason? Alas, our positions and the nature of our calling are against it; we are meant to cure such sorrows, not to sow them. For goodness' sake, think what you are doing: how you may harm yourself, yes, and lose the King's acquaintance completely by behaving like this."],[9,14,"The hearts of princes kiss obedience, they love it so much; but against stubborn spirits they swell and grow as terrible as storms. I know you have a gentle, noble temper, a soul as smooth as a calm sea; please think of us as what we claim to be, peacemakers, friends and servants."]],"431":[[0,9,"Do what you will, my lords, and please forgive me if I have behaved rudely; you know I am a woman, without the wit to give a proper answer to men like you. Please give my respects to his Majesty; he still has my heart, and shall have my prayers as long as I live. Come, reverend fathers, give me your advice. She who now begs little thought, when she first set foot here, that she would pay so dearly for her honours."]],"481":[[0,10,"(Aside) The late queen's lady-in-waiting, a knight's daughter, to become her mistress's mistress, the Queen's queen! This candle doesn't burn clearly; I must snuff it, and then out it goes. What if I know she is virtuous and deserving? I also know she is a hot-headed Lutheran, and it isn't good for our cause that she should lie in the arms of our hard-to-manage King. And again, a heretic has sprung up, an arch-heretic, Cranmer, who has crawled into the King's favour and is his oracle."]],"488":[[0,9,"That may well be; there's a mutiny in his mind. This morning he sent me state papers to read, as I had asked; and do you know what I found there, put in, on my conscience, by mistake? An inventory, listing the various pieces of his silver plate, his treasure, rich fabrics and household ornaments, which I find valued so high that it goes far beyond what a subject should own."]],"501":[[0,8,"My sovereign, I admit your royal favours, showered on me daily, have been more than my most careful efforts could repay, and they were beyond any man's efforts. My efforts have always fallen short of my wishes, yet matched my abilities. My own aims have been mine only in that they always pointed to the good of your most sacred person and the profit of the state."],[9,13,"For your great favours heaped on me, a poor undeserving man, I can give nothing back but loyal thanks, my prayers to heaven for you, and my loyalty, which has always grown and always will, until death, that winter, kills it."]],"502":[[0,11,"A fair answer. It shows a loyal and obedient subject. The honour of loyalty is its own reward, just as the shame of disloyalty is its punishment. I take it that, since my hand has opened bounty to you, my heart dropped love and my power rained honour on you more than on anyone, so your hand and heart, your brain and every power you have should, besides your general bond of duty, be more devoted to me, as your friend in a particular love, than to anyone."]],"503":[[0,9,"I declare that I have always worked for your Highness's good more than my own; and that I am, and have been, and will be, though all the world should break its duty to you and throw it out of their souls, though dangers were as thick as thought could make them and appeared in more horrible forms, yet my duty, like a rock against the raging flood, will break the onrush of this wild river and stand unshaken, yours."]],"508":[[0,5,"What can this mean? What sudden anger is this? How have I earned it? He left me frowning, as if ruin leapt from his eyes. That's how the enraged lion looks at the bold hunter who has wounded him, before he destroys him. I must read this paper; the story of his anger, I fear."],[6,12,"That's it: this paper has ruined me. It's the account of all that world of wealth I have gathered for my own ends, in fact to win the papacy and pay my friends in Rome. Oh, carelessness fit for a fool to fall by! What spiteful devil made me put this vital secret in the packet I sent the King?"],[13,19,"Is there no way to cure this? No new trick to knock it out of his head? I know it will anger him greatly; yet I know a way that, if it works, will get me off again in spite of fortune. What's this? \"To the Pope!\" The letter, as I live, with all the business I wrote to his Holiness. Well then, farewell!"],[20,24,"I have reached the highest point of all my greatness, and from that full noon of my glory I now hurry to my setting. I shall fall like a shooting star in the evening, and no one will see me again."]],"513":[[0,9,"Until I find more than will and words to do it, I mean your malice, know, you meddling lords, I dare and must refuse. Now I feel what coarse metal you are made of: envy. How eagerly you follow my disgrace, as if it fed you! And how smooth and gleeful you look at anything that may bring my ruin! Follow your envious ways, men of malice! You have Christian authority for them, and no doubt in time they will find their proper rewards."],[10,14,"That seal you demand so violently, the King, my master and yours, gave me with his own hand and told me to enjoy it, with the office and honours, for life; and to confirm his goodness, he bound it by letters patent. Now who'll take it?"]],"518":[[0,10,"Your ambition, you scarlet sin, robbed this mourning land of noble Buckingham, my father-in-law. The heads of all your fellow cardinals, together with you and all your best qualities, weren't worth one of his hairs. A plague on your scheming! You sent me as deputy to Ireland, far from helping him, far from the King, far from everyone who might have shown mercy for the crime you charged him with, while your great goodness, out of holy pity, absolved him with an axe."]],"519":[[0,11,"This, and everything else this talkative lord can blame me for, I answer is completely false. The Duke got what he deserved by law. His noble jury and his foul case can witness how free I was of any private malice in his death. If I liked long speeches, lord, I would tell you that you have as little honesty as honour, and that in loyalty and truth toward the King, my always royal master, I dare match a sounder man than Surrey can be, and all who love his follies."]],"522":[[0,5,"Yes, that goodness of gathering all the land's wealth into one place, your own hands, Cardinal, by extortion; the goodness of your intercepted letters that you wrote to the Pope against the King. Since you provoke me, your goodness shall be made well known."],[6,13,"My Lord of Norfolk, as you are truly noble, as you care for the common good, the state of our despised nobility and our children, who will hardly be gentlemen if he lives, bring out the grand total of his sins, the charges gathered from his life. I'll startle you worse than the sacring bell at Mass did when the brown-skinned girl lay kissing in your arms, Lord Cardinal."]],"539":[[0,7,"So farewell to the little good you wish me. Farewell, a long farewell, to all my greatness! This is the state of man: today he puts out the tender leaves of hope; tomorrow he blossoms and wears his blushing honours thick on him; the third day comes a frost, a killing frost, and just when he thinks, the good trusting man, that his greatness is surely ripening, it nips his root, and then he falls, as I do."],[8,14,"Like little reckless boys who swim on inflated bladders, I have ventured many summers in a sea of glory, but far out of my depth. My swollen pride finally burst under me, and now has left me, weary and old with service, to the mercy of a rough current that must hide me for ever."],[15,22,"Empty pomp and glory of this world, I hate you! I feel my heart opened anew. Oh, how wretched is the poor man who depends on princes' favours! Between the smile we long for, the sweet look of princes, and the ruin they can bring, there are more pangs and fears than wars or women cause; and when he falls, he falls like Lucifer, never to hope again."]],"545":[[0,9,"Why, well; never so truly happy, my good Cromwell. I know myself now, and I feel within me a peace above all earthly honours, a calm and quiet conscience. The King has cured me, I humbly thank his Grace, and out of pity has taken from these shoulders, these ruined pillars, a load that would sink a navy: too much honour. Oh, it's a burden, Cromwell, a burden too heavy for a man who hopes for heaven!"]],"555":[[0,6,"That was the weight that pulled me down. Oh, Cromwell, the King has outstripped me! In that one woman I have lost all my glories for ever. No sun will ever usher in my honours again, or gild the noble crowds that waited on my smiles. Go, leave me, Cromwell!"],[7,15,"I am a poor fallen man, unworthy now to be your lord and master. Go to the King, that sun, which I pray may never set! I have told him what you are and how loyal. He will promote you; some small memory of me will move him, I know his noble nature, not to let your promising service die too. Good Cromwell, don't neglect him; take your chance now and provide for your own future safety."]],"557":[[0,10,"Cromwell, I didn't think I would shed a tear in all my miseries; but your honest loyalty has forced me to weep like a woman. Let's dry our eyes, and hear me this far, Cromwell: when I am forgotten, as I shall be, and sleep in dull cold marble where I must never be mentioned again, say I taught you. Say that Wolsey, who once walked the paths of glory and sounded all the depths and shallows of honour, found you a way to rise out of his wreck, a sure and safe one, though your master missed it."],[11,21,"Just note my fall and what ruined me. Cromwell, I charge you, throw away ambition! By that sin the angels fell; how then can man, made in his Maker's image, hope to gain by it? Love yourself last. Cherish the hearts that hate you; corruption gains no more than honesty. Always carry gentle peace in your right hand to silence envious tongues. Be just and fear nothing. Let everything you aim at be for your country, your God and truth; then if you fall, Cromwell, you fall a blessed martyr!"],[22,29,"Serve the King! And please, lead me in. Take an inventory there of everything I own, to the last penny; it's the King's. My robe and my honesty before Heaven are all I now dare call my own. Oh Cromwell, Cromwell! If I had only served my God with half the zeal I served my king, He would not have left me naked to my enemies in my old age."]],"573":[[0,11,"I can tell you that too. The Archbishop of Canterbury, with other learned and reverend fathers of his order, recently held a court at Dunstable, six miles from Ampthill where the Princess was staying. She was summoned to it many times but did not appear. To be brief, because she did not appear and because of the King's recent doubt of conscience, by the general agreement of all these learned men she was divorced and her marriage declared void. Since then she has been moved to Kimbolton, where she now lies ill."]],"601":[[0,8,"As well as I can. The rich stream of lords and ladies, having brought the Queen to a place prepared in the choir, drew back a little from her, while her Grace sat down to rest for half an hour or so in a rich chair of state, freely showing the beauty of her person to the people. Believe me, sir, she is the finest woman who ever lay beside a man."],[9,19,"When the people got a full view of her, such a noise went up as the rigging makes at sea in a hard storm, as loud and in as many tunes. Hats, cloaks, even doublets I think, flew into the air; and if their faces had been loose, they'd have lost them today. I never saw such joy. Heavily pregnant women with less than half a week to go would shake the crowd like battering rams in old wars and make people reel in front of them. No man alive could say \"This is my wife\" there; they were all so strangely woven into one piece."]],"603":[[0,12,"At last her Grace rose and with modest steps came to the altar, where she knelt and, like a saint, raised her lovely eyes to heaven and prayed devoutly. Then she rose again and bowed to the people, and the Archbishop of Canterbury nobly gave her all the royal marks of a queen: the holy oil, Edward the Confessor's crown, the rod, the dove of peace and all such emblems. When that was done, the choir, with all the finest musicians in the kingdom, sang the \"Te Deum\" together. Then she left, and with the same full ceremony walked back to York Place, where the feast is being held."]],"624":[[0,6,"At last, travelling in easy stages, he came to Leicester and lodged in the abbey, where the reverend abbot and all his monks received him with honour. He said to the abbot: \"Oh, father abbot, an old man broken by the storms of state has come to lay his weary bones among you; give him a little earth, for charity!\""],[7,13,"So he went to bed, where his sickness kept pursuing him fiercely; and three nights later, about eight o'clock, the hour he himself had predicted would be his last, full of repentance, constant prayer, tears and sorrow, he gave his honours back to the world and his blessed soul to heaven, and slept in peace."]],"625":[[0,8,"So may he rest; may his faults lie lightly on him! Yet let me say this much of him, Griffith, and still with charity. He was a man of boundless pride, always ranking himself with princes; one who by his scheming bound the whole kingdom. He thought selling church offices fair play; his own opinion was his law; in the King's presence he would tell lies, and he was always two-faced in his words and meaning."],[9,13,"He never showed pity except where he meant to destroy. His promises were mighty, as he was then; but his performance was nothing, as he is now. He was immoral in his body, and set the clergy a bad example."]],"628":[[0,9,"This Cardinal, though from humble stock, was undoubtedly made for great honour from his cradle. He was a scholar, a mature and good one; extremely wise, well-spoken and persuasive; haughty and sour to those who didn't love him, but sweet as summer to those who sought him out. And though he was never satisfied in getting wealth, which was a sin, yet in giving, madam, he was most princely."],[10,20,"Let those twin colleges of learning he founded always bear witness for him, Ipswich and Oxford! One of them fell with him, unwilling to outlive the good man who made it; the other, though unfinished, is already so famous, so excellent in learning and still so growing, that Christendom will always speak of his virtue. His fall heaped happiness on him, for then, and not before, he knew himself and found the blessing of being small; and, to add greater honour to his old age than any man could give him, he died fearing God."]],"629":[[0,6,"After my death I want no other herald, no other reporter of my life's actions to keep my honour from decay, than an honest chronicler like Griffith. With your devout truthfulness and fairness, you have made me now honour in his ashes the man I most hated while he lived. Peace be with him!"],[7,11,"Patience, stay close to me, and lay me lower; I won't trouble you much longer. Good Griffith, have the musicians play me that sad tune I named my funeral bell, while I sit thinking about the heavenly harmony I am going to."]],"663":[[0,7,"In it I have commended to his goodness the image of our chaste love, his young daughter, may the dews of heaven fall thick with blessings on her, begging him to give her a virtuous upbringing. She is young, and of a noble, modest nature; I hope she will turn out well. And I ask him to love her a little for her mother's sake, who loved him, heaven knows how dearly."],[8,16,"My next poor request is that his noble Grace take pity on my wretched women, who have followed me faithfully for so long through good fortune and bad. There is not one of them, I dare swear, and I should not lie now, who will not deserve a really good husband, for virtue and true beauty of soul, for chastity and good behaviour; let him be a nobleman; and surely the men who get them will be happy."],[17,27,"The last is for my menservants, who are very poor, though poverty could never draw them away from me: that they may have their wages properly paid, and something extra to remember me by. If Heaven had been pleased to give me longer life and the means, we would not have parted like this. That is all it contains. And, my good lord, by whatever you love most in this world, as you wish Christian peace to departed souls, be a friend to these poor people and urge the King to do me this last justice."]],"665":[[0,6,"Thank you, honest lord. Remember me in all humility to his Highness. Tell him the one who troubled him so long is now passing out of this world; tell him I blessed him in death, for so I will. My eyes are growing dim. Farewell, my lord. Griffith, farewell. No, Patience, you mustn't leave me yet."],[7,13,"I must go to bed; call in more women. When I am dead, good girl, let me be treated with honour. Scatter maiden flowers over me, so the whole world may know I was a chaste wife to my grave. Embalm me, then lay me out. Though I am no longer queen, bury me like a queen and a king's daughter. I can say no more."]],"681":[[0,7,"Yes, yes, Sir Thomas, there are those who dare, and I myself have risked speaking my mind about him. Indeed, sir, I can tell you, today I think I have convinced the lords of the council that he is, for I know he is, and they know he is, a most arch-heretic, a plague infecting the land."],[8,15,"Moved by this, they have raised it with the King, who has listened so far to our complaint that, out of his great grace and royal care, foreseeing the deadly harm our arguments set before him, he has ordered him to be summoned before the council tomorrow morning. He's a rank weed, Sir Thomas, and we must root him out. I'm keeping you too long from your business. Good night, Sir Thomas."]],"713":[[0,8,"Please rise, my good and gracious Lord of Canterbury. Come, you and I must take a walk together; I have news to tell you. Come, come, give me your hand. Ah, my good lord, I grieve at what I'm saying, and am truly sorry to repeat what follows. Recently, and very unwillingly, I have heard many serious, I say, my lord, serious complaints about you."],[9,17,"Having considered them, we and our council have decided that you shall come before us this morning; where, I know, you cannot clear yourself so freely but that, until there is a further trial of the charges you must answer, you must be patient and be content to make your home in our Tower. Since you are one of our council, it is right we proceed this way, or else no witness would dare come forward against you."]],"717":[[0,7,"Don't you know how you stand in the world, with the whole world? Your enemies are many, and not small; their plots must be just as great. And the justice and truth of a case do not always bring the verdict it deserves. How easily corrupt minds could find rogues just as corrupt to swear against you! Such things have been done."],[8,14,"You are powerfully opposed, and with malice just as great. Do you expect better luck with perjured witnesses than your Master, whose minister you are, had while He lived on this wicked earth? Come on, come on! You take a cliff edge for no dangerous leap, and court your own destruction."]],"719":[[0,10,"Cheer up; they shall get no further than we allow. Keep your courage, and see that you appear before them this morning. If, in charging you, they happen to commit you to prison, don't fail to use the best arguments against it, as forcefully as the moment teaches you. If pleading gets you no remedy, give them this ring and make your appeal to us there in front of them."],[11,14,"Look, the good man is weeping! He's honest, on my honour. By God's blessed mother, I swear he is true-hearted, and there's no better soul in my kingdom. Go now, and do as I've told you."]],"744":[[0,9,"(Aside) That's Butts, the King's physician. How intently he looked at me as he passed! Heaven grant he isn't sounding out my disgrace! Surely this has been arranged on purpose by some who hate me, God change their hearts, I never sought their malice, to snuff out my honour; otherwise they'd be ashamed to make me, a fellow councillor, wait at the door among boys, grooms and footmen. But their wishes must be carried out, and I'll wait patiently."]],"751":[[0,10,"Ha! It's him indeed. Is this the honour they pay one another? It's a good thing there's still one above them. I'd have thought they had enough honesty among them, or at least good manners, not to let a man of his rank, so close to our favour, dance attendance on their lordships' pleasure, and at the door too, like a messenger with letters. By holy Mary, Butts, there's villainy here. Leave them alone, and draw the curtain shut; we'll hear more soon."]],"766":[[0,6,"My good Lord Archbishop, I'm very sorry to sit here now and see that chair stand empty. But we are all men, frail by nature and subject to the weakness of the flesh; few are angels. Out of that frailty and lack of wisdom, you, who ought most to teach us, have behaved wrongly, and not a little,"],[7,11,"toward the King first and then his laws, by filling the whole kingdom, through your teaching and your chaplains, as we are informed, with new opinions, varied and dangerous, which are heresies and, if not reformed, may prove deadly."]],"767":[[0,7,"And that reform must be quick too, my noble lords. People who tame wild horses don't walk them about in their hands to make them gentle; they stop their mouths with hard bits and spur them until they obey their training. If, out of our softness and childish pity for one man's honour, we allow this infectious sickness, farewell to all medicine!"],[8,11,"And what follows then? Riots and uproars, with the whole state infected, as our neighbours in upper Germany can bitterly witness, still freshly pitied in our memories."]],"768":[[0,9,"My good lords, until now, in the whole course of my life and office, I have worked, with no small study, to make my teaching and the firm exercise of my authority go one way, and safely; and my aim was always to do good. There is no man alive, I say it with an honest heart, my lords, who hates more, or works harder against, those who disturb the public peace, both in his private conscience and his office, than I do."],[10,16,"I pray Heaven the King may never find a heart with less loyalty in it! Men who feed on envy and crooked malice dare bite the best. I beg your lordships that, in this matter of justice, my accusers, whoever they are, may stand face to face with me and freely make their charges."]],"771":[[0,6,"Ah, my good Lord of Winchester, I thank you. You are always my good friend; if you get your way, I'll find your lordship both judge and jury, you're so merciful. I see your aim; it's my ruin. Love and meekness, my lord, suit a churchman better than ambition. Win back straying souls with gentleness; cast none away."],[7,11,"Lay all the weight you can on my patience; I have as little doubt that I will clear myself as you have conscience in doing daily wrongs. I could say more, but respect for your office keeps me modest."]],"801":[[0,7,"You were always good at sudden compliments, Bishop of Winchester. But know that I have not come to hear such flattery now, and in my presence; it is too thin and bare to hide offences. You can't reach me; you play the fawning spaniel and think you can win me with a wagging tongue; but whatever you take me for, I'm sure you have a cruel and bloody nature."],[8,11,"(To Cranmer) Good man, sit down. Now let me see the proudest of them, the boldest, so much as wag his finger at you. By all that's holy, he would do better to starve than even think once that this place doesn't suit you."]],"803":[[0,6,"No, sir, it does not please me. I thought I had men of some understanding and wisdom on my council, but I find none. Was it sensible, lords, to let this man, this good man, few of you deserve that title, this honest man, wait like a lice-ridden footboy at the chamber door, and a man as great as you?"],[7,13,"Why, what a disgrace this was! Did my commission tell you to forget yourselves so far? I gave you power to try him as a councillor, not as a servant. I see some of you, more from malice than honesty, would try him to the utmost if you had the means; which you shall never have while I live."]],"805":[[0,5,"Well, well, my lords, respect him. Accept him and treat him well; he deserves it. I'll say this much for him: if a prince can be indebted to a subject, I am to him, for his love and service. Make no more fuss, but all embrace him."],[6,9,"Be friends, for shame, my lords! My Lord of Canterbury, I have a request you mustn't refuse me: there is a fair young girl who still needs baptising; you must be her godfather and answer for her."]],"826":[[0,8,"The christening spoons will be bigger, sir. There's a fellow near the door who must be a brass-worker by his face, for on my conscience twenty dog-days of heat rule in his nose; everyone standing round him is at the equator and needs no other penance. I hit that fiery dragon on the head three times, and three times his nose fired at me; he stands there like a mortar ready to blow us up. There was a haberdasher's wife of little sense near him who abused me till her decorated hat fell off her head, for starting such a fire in the state."],[9,16,"I missed the shooting star once and hit that woman, who shouted \"Clubs!\", and I saw from far off about forty club-carrying apprentices, the flower of the Strand where she lodged, come to her rescue. They charged; I held my ground; at last they came at me with broomsticks; I still defied them; when suddenly a line of boys behind them, skirmishers, let fly such a shower of pebbles that I was glad to withdraw my honour and let them win the fort. The devil was among them, I'm sure."]],"831":[[0,9,"As I live, if the King blames me for this, I'll put you all in the stocks, and quickly, and slap heavy fines on your heads for neglect. You're lazy rogues, and here you lie drinking from leather jugs when you should be doing your job. Listen! The trumpets are sounding; they've already come from the christening. Go, push into the crowd and clear a way for the procession to pass properly, or I'll find a Marshalsea prison to keep you amused for the next two months."]],"848":[[0,8,"Let me speak, sir, for Heaven now commands me; and let no one think my words flattery, for they will find them true. This royal infant, may Heaven always watch over her, though still in her cradle, already promises this land a thousand thousand blessings, which time will bring to ripeness. She shall be, though few now living will see that goodness, a model to all princes of her time and all who come after."],[9,18,"The Queen of Sheba was never more eager for wisdom and virtue than this pure soul will be. All the royal graces that shape such a mighty person as this, with all the virtues that go with goodness, shall be doubled in her. Truth shall nurse her; holy and heavenly thoughts shall always guide her. She shall be loved and feared; her own people shall bless her; her enemies shall shake like a field of flattened corn and hang their heads in sorrow. Good grows with her."],[19,24,"In her days every man shall eat in safety what he plants under his own vine, and sing merry songs of peace to all his neighbours. God shall be truly known; and those around her shall learn from her the perfect ways of honour, and claim their greatness by those, not by birth."],[25,35,"Nor shall this peace die with her; but as when the miraculous bird, the virgin phoenix, dies, her ashes create a new heir as wonderful as herself, so she shall leave her blessedness to one who, when heaven calls her from this cloud of darkness, shall rise like a star from the sacred ashes of her honour, as great in fame as she was, and stay fixed there. Peace, plenty, love, truth and awe, which served this chosen infant, shall then be his and grow round him like a vine."],[36,41,"Wherever the bright sun of heaven shines, his honour and the greatness of his name shall be there and found new nations. He shall flourish and, like a mountain cedar, spread his branches over all the plains around him. Our children's children shall see this and bless Heaven."]],"851":[[0,5,"Oh, Lord Archbishop, you have made me a man now! Before this happy child I never fathered anything. This comforting prophecy has pleased me so much that when I am in heaven I shall want to see what this child does, and praise my Maker."],[6,13,"I thank you all. To you, my good Lord Mayor, and you, good aldermen, I am much obliged; I have received great honour by your presence, and you shall find me grateful. Lead the way, lords. You must all see the Queen, and she must thank you, or she'll be ill. Let no man think he has business at home today, for everyone shall stay. This little one shall make it a holiday."]],"853":[[0,6,"It's ten to one this play can never please everyone here. Some come to relax and sleep through an act or two, but we fear we have woken those with our trumpets, so clearly they'll say it's worthless; others come to hear the city mocked mercilessly and cry \"That's witty!\", which we haven't done either."],[7,13,"So I fear all the praise we are likely to hear for this play at present lies only in the kind judgement of good women, for we showed them such a woman. If they smile and say it will do, I know that before long all the best men will be ours; for it's bad luck if the men hold back when their ladies tell them to clap."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"fall":["The fall of greatness","Buckingham, Katherine and Wolsey fall one after another: Fortune's wheel, farewells and the meaning of a fall."],"conscience":["Conscience and the divorce","The King's 'scruple' about his brother's widow: real guilt, convenient excuse, or both?"],"pride":["Ambition, pride and envy","Wolsey's rise from Ipswich, the nobles' contempt for a butcher's son, and the envy that brings men down."],"church":["The Church, Rome and reform","Cardinals and bishops, a legate from Rome, the 'tricks of Rome', and Cranmer's gentler faith."],"mercy":["Patience, charity and forgiveness","The fallen forgive: Buckingham, Wolsey, Katherine and Cranmer, and the call to pity a falling man."],"truth":["Truth, rumour and report","'All is True': hostile witnesses, street gossip, honest chroniclers and the truth of history."],"kingship":["Kingship and favour","A king's favour raises and ruins; Henry grows from Wolsey's puppet to a king who acts alone."],"birth":["Providence, Anne and Elizabeth","The play moves towards a birth: Anne's rise, the coronation, the christening and Cranmer's prophecy."]},"quotes":[{"id":"1","who":"PROLOGUE","text":"How soon this mightiness meets misery","themes":["fall"],"note":"The pattern of the whole play in one line."},{"id":"1","who":"PROLOGUE","text":"A man may weep upon his wedding-day","themes":["fall"],"note":"A paradox: joy and grief together, like the play's ending."},{"id":"8","who":"NORFOLK","text":"Till this time pomp was single, but now married","themes":["pride"],"note":"The splendour of the two kings is described as a marriage."},{"id":"8","who":"NORFOLK","text":"All clinquant, all in gold, like heathen gods","themes":["pride"],"note":"The display in France is dazzling and a little pagan."},{"id":"15","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"The devil speed him! no man's pie is freed","themes":["pride"],"note":"Buckingham's first outburst against Wolsey."},{"id":"15","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"From his ambitious finger","themes":["pride"],"note":"Wolsey's ambition is greedy, reaching into everyone's business."},{"id":"15","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"That such a keech can with his very bulk","themes":["pride"],"note":"A sneer at Wolsey's fat body and his butcher father."},{"id":"16","who":"NORFOLK","text":"Out of his self-drawing web, he gives us note","themes":["pride"],"note":"Wolsey has made himself, like a spider spinning its own web."},{"id":"16","who":"NORFOLK","text":"A place next to the King","themes":["pride","kingship"],"note":"Wolsey's position: second only to the King."},{"id":"17","who":"ABERGAVENNY","text":"A new hell in himself","themes":["pride"],"note":"Wolsey's pride is so great it is a hell of its own."},{"id":"20","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"Have broke their backs with laying manors on 'em","themes":["pride"],"note":"Nobles wore their estates on their backs to impress the French."},{"id":"28","who":"NORFOLK","text":"The state takes notice of the private difference","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Nothing at this court is private."},{"id":"28","who":"NORFOLK","text":"That he's revengeful, and I know his sword","themes":["pride"],"note":"Norfolk warns of Wolsey's long reach."},{"id":"36","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"This butcher's cur is venom-mouth'd","themes":["pride"],"note":"Buckingham sneers at Wolsey's low birth."},{"id":"36","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"A beggar's book","themes":["pride","church"],"note":"Learning, not birth, now wins power."},{"id":"36","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"Outworths a noble's blood","themes":["pride"],"note":"Buckingham's complaint: birth no longer counts."},{"id":"39","who":"NORFOLK","text":"Requires slow pace at first","themes":["pride"],"note":"Haste ruins the hasty; a warning Buckingham ignores."},{"id":"40","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"This Ipswich fellow's insolence","themes":["pride"],"note":"Wolsey's origins as an insult."},{"id":"41","who":"NORFOLK","text":"Heat not a furnace for your foe so hot","themes":["pride"],"note":"Do not let your hatred burn yourself."},{"id":"44","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"This holy fox,","themes":["church","pride"],"note":"Buckingham's image of Wolsey: a cunning beast in holy clothes."},{"id":"46","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"Does buy and sell his honour as he pleases","themes":["church","truth"],"note":"Buckingham accuses Wolsey of selling the King's honour."},{"id":"52","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"The net has fall'n upon me! I shall perish","themes":["fall"],"note":"Buckingham sees at once that he is trapped."},{"id":"52","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"Under device and practice","themes":["truth"],"note":"He will die by plots, not by truth."},{"id":"54","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"Which makes my whit'st part black. The will of Heaven","themes":["fall","mercy"],"note":"His innocence is stained by the charge."},{"id":"62","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"I am the shadow of poor Buckingham","themes":["fall"],"note":"He is already only a shadow of himself."},{"id":"66","who":"KING HENRY","text":"My life itself, and the best heart of it","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The King's gratitude to Wolsey for 'saving' him."},{"id":"69","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Never name to us, you have half our power","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry's love for Katherine at the start."},{"id":"70","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"That you would love yourself, and in that love","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Katherine asks the King to care for his own honour."},{"id":"78","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"Tongues spit their duties out, and cold hearts freeze","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The tax kills loyalty."},{"id":"80","who":"WOLSEY","text":"'Tis but the fate of place, and the rough brake","themes":["pride"],"note":"Wolsey presents criticism as the price of high office."},{"id":"80","who":"WOLSEY","text":"We should take root here where we sit, or sit","themes":["truth"],"note":"If we fear criticism we will never act."},{"id":"81","who":"KING HENRY","text":"And stick them in our will. Sixth part of each?","themes":["kingship"],"note":"A king must rule by law, not will."},{"id":"82","who":"WOLSEY","text":"That through our intercession this revokement","themes":["truth","pride"],"note":"Wolsey takes credit for the pardon."},{"id":"86","who":"KING HENRY","text":"They turn to vicious forms, ten times more ugly","themes":["fall"],"note":"Gifts misused turn ugly."},{"id":"91","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"Deliver all with charity","themes":["mercy","truth"],"note":"Katherine asks for fairness to Buckingham."},{"id":"98","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"You charge not in your spleen a noble person","themes":["truth","mercy"],"note":"Katherine warns the witness against spite."},{"id":"106","who":"SURVEYOR","text":"The part my father meant to act upon","themes":["truth"],"note":"The surveyor ties Buckingham to his father's plot."},{"id":"107","who":"KING HENRY","text":"A giant traitor!","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The King has made up his mind."},{"id":"112","who":"KING HENRY","text":"He's traitor to th' height","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The King condemns Buckingham before trial."},{"id":"116","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","text":"Is't possible the spells of France should juggle","themes":["pride"],"note":"The court has caught French fashions."},{"id":"126","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","text":"And never see the Louvre","themes":["pride"],"note":"An English courtier can be wise without France."},{"id":"137","who":"LOVELL","text":"That churchman bears a bounteous mind indeed","themes":["church"],"note":"Wolsey's generosity."},{"id":"187","who":"KING HENRY","text":"The fairest hand I ever touch'd! O beauty","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry's first sight of Anne."},{"id":"197","who":"KING HENRY","text":"You are a churchman, or, I'll tell you, Cardinal","themes":["kingship","church"],"note":"Henry teases Wolsey about his feast."},{"id":"201","who":"KING HENRY","text":"By heaven, she is a dainty one. Sweetheart","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry is already captivated by Anne."},{"id":"225","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","text":"Was either pitied in him or forgotten","themes":["fall"],"note":"Buckingham's eloquence did not save him."},{"id":"230","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","text":"The Cardinal is the end of this","themes":["pride"],"note":"The street blames Wolsey."},{"id":"234","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","text":"The mirror of all courtesy","themes":["fall"],"note":"Buckingham as a model of noble behaviour."},{"id":"238","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"Hear what I say, and then go home and lose me","themes":["fall"],"note":"Buckingham asks to be forgotten."},{"id":"238","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"Nor build their evils on the graves of great men","themes":["mercy"],"note":"A warning to his enemies."},{"id":"238","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"And, as the long divorce of steel falls on me","themes":["fall"],"note":"The axe as a divorce: an image that looks forward to Katherine."},{"id":"240","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"As I would be forgiven. I forgive all","themes":["mercy"],"note":"Buckingham echoes the Lord's Prayer."},{"id":"240","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"You met him half in heaven. My vows and prayers","themes":["mercy","kingship"],"note":"He dies blessing the King."},{"id":"243","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"And Duke of Buckingham; now, poor Edward Bohun","themes":["fall"],"note":"Stripped of all names but his own."},{"id":"243","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"Fell by our servants, by those men we lov'd most","themes":["fall","truth"],"note":"Betrayal by servants unites father and son."},{"id":"243","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"Like water from ye; never found again","themes":["fall"],"note":"False friends vanish when fortune turns."},{"id":"243","who":"BUCKINGHAM","text":"Speak how I fell. I have done; and God forgive me","themes":["fall"],"note":"His last request: tell the story of the fall."},{"id":"252","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","text":"possess'd him with a scruple","themes":["conscience"],"note":"The divorce begins as a 'scruple', a doubt of conscience."},{"id":"254","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","text":"Will have his will, and she must fall","themes":["fall"],"note":"The next fall is announced."},{"id":"266","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","text":"It seems the marriage with his brother's wife","themes":["conscience"],"note":"The public reason for the divorce."},{"id":"267","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"Has crept too near another lady","themes":["conscience"],"note":"Suffolk's joke: conscience means desire."},{"id":"268","who":"NORFOLK","text":"This is the Cardinal's doing, the king-cardinal","themes":["pride","church"],"note":"Wolsey seems to rule the King."},{"id":"268","who":"NORFOLK","text":"That blind priest, like the eldest son of Fortune","themes":["pride"],"note":"Wolsey turns the wheel like Fortune."},{"id":"270","who":"NORFOLK","text":"That, like a jewel, has hung twenty years","themes":["conscience"],"note":"Katherine's long marriage."},{"id":"271","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","text":"This bold bad man","themes":["pride"],"note":"The lords' verdict on Wolsey."},{"id":"273","who":"NORFOLK","text":"From princes into pages. All men's honours","themes":["pride"],"note":"Wolsey's power threatens the nobility."},{"id":"274","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"To him that made him proud, the Pope","themes":["church"],"note":"Suffolk blames Rome for Wolsey's pride."},{"id":"286","who":"KING HENRY","text":"The quiet of my wounded conscience","themes":["conscience"],"note":"The King's 'wounded conscience'."},{"id":"294","who":"WOLSEY","text":"Rome, the nurse of judgement","themes":["church"],"note":"Wolsey's praise of Rome."},{"id":"313","who":"WOLSEY","text":"We live not to be grip'd by meaner persons","themes":["church","pride"],"note":"Wolsey's contempt for those below him."},{"id":"316","who":"KING HENRY","text":"But, conscience, conscience!","themes":["conscience"],"note":"Henry's mixture of desire and guilt."},{"id":"316","who":"KING HENRY","text":"O, 'tis a tender place; and I must leave her","themes":["conscience"],"note":"He leaves Katherine for conscience's sake."},{"id":"322","who":"ANNE BULLEN","text":"As soul and body's severing","themes":["fall"],"note":"Losing greatness is like dying."},{"id":"324","who":"ANNE BULLEN","text":"And range with humble livers in content","themes":["fall"],"note":"Better to be humble and content."},{"id":"324","who":"ANNE BULLEN","text":"And wear a golden sorrow","themes":["fall"],"note":"Greatness as a golden grief."},{"id":"326","who":"ANNE BULLEN","text":"I would not be a queen","themes":["birth"],"note":"Anne's protest, soon to be proved wrong."},{"id":"327","who":"OLD LADY","text":"Affected eminence, wealth, sovereignty","themes":["birth"],"note":"The Old Lady's view of women's ambition."},{"id":"343","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","text":"Beauty and honour in her are so mingled","themes":["birth"],"note":"The Chamberlain's verdict on Anne."},{"id":"343","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","text":"But from this lady may proceed a gem","themes":["birth"],"note":"A first hint of Elizabeth."},{"id":"346","who":"OLD LADY","text":"I have been begging sixteen years in court","themes":["fall"],"note":"The Old Lady's envy of Anne's luck."},{"id":"365","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"I am a most poor woman, and a stranger","themes":["conscience"],"note":"Katherine's opening plea."},{"id":"365","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"I have been to you a true and humble wife","themes":["conscience"],"note":"Her claim of loyalty."},{"id":"365","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"Upward of twenty years, and have been blest","themes":["conscience"],"note":"Twenty years of marriage."},{"id":"370","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"We are a queen, or long have dream'd so, certain","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Katherine's pride in her rank."},{"id":"372","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"You are mine enemy, and make my challenge","themes":["pride"],"note":"Katherine accuses Wolsey."},{"id":"372","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"Have blown this coal betwixt my lord and me","themes":["pride"],"note":"Wolsey fanned the divorce."},{"id":"374","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"You're meek and humble-mouth'd","themes":["pride","church"],"note":"Katherine sees through Wolsey's manner."},{"id":"374","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"Is cramm'd with arrogancy, spleen, and pride","themes":["pride"],"note":"Her charge against Wolsey's heart."},{"id":"382","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Go thy ways, Kate","themes":["kingship","conscience"],"note":"Henry's tender farewell."},{"id":"382","who":"KING HENRY","text":"The queen of earthly queens. She's noble born","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry praises Katherine."},{"id":"384","who":"KING HENRY","text":"My conscience first receiv'd a tenderness","themes":["conscience"],"note":"Henry's account of his doubts."},{"id":"384","who":"KING HENRY","text":"This was a judgement on me; that my kingdom","themes":["conscience"],"note":"He reads the deaths as God's sentence."},{"id":"384","who":"KING HENRY","text":"The wild sea of my conscience, I did steer","themes":["conscience"],"note":"His conscience as a stormy sea."},{"id":"388","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Prove but our marriage lawful, by my life","themes":["conscience"],"note":"He claims he would stay with her if the marriage were lawful."},{"id":"390","who":"KING HENRY","text":"This dilatory sloth and tricks of Rome","themes":["church"],"note":"Henry turns against Rome."},{"id":"390","who":"KING HENRY","text":"My learn'd and well-beloved servant, Cranmer","themes":["church"],"note":"Cranmer named for the first time."},{"id":"395","who":"GENTLEWOMAN","text":"~Killing care and grief of heart","themes":["mercy"],"note":"Music's power over grief."},{"id":"401","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"But all hoods make not monks","themes":["church"],"note":"A hood does not make a monk."},{"id":"406","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"Truth loves open dealing","themes":["truth"],"note":"Katherine refuses secrecy."},{"id":"411","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"Alas, I am a woman, friendless, hopeless!","themes":["conscience"],"note":"Katherine alone in a foreign country."},{"id":"418","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"That no king can corrupt","themes":["church"],"note":"Heaven is the only fair judge."},{"id":"420","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"But cardinal sins and hollow hearts I fear ye","themes":["church","pride"],"note":"A pun on cardinal (chief) sins."},{"id":"422","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"Ye turn me into nothing. Woe upon ye","themes":["fall"],"note":"Katherine's fury."},{"id":"424","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"Yet will I add an honour,—a great patience","themes":["mercy"],"note":"Patience as her highest honour."},{"id":"426","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"Shall e'er divorce my dignities","themes":["conscience"],"note":"She will never give up being queen."},{"id":"428","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"Ye have angels' faces, but Heaven knows your hearts","themes":["church"],"note":"The cardinals' fair looks and false hearts."},{"id":"428","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"Shipwreck'd upon a kingdom, where no pity","themes":["fall"],"note":"Her life in England as a shipwreck."},{"id":"428","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"I'll hang my head and perish","themes":["fall"],"note":"The lily image."},{"id":"429","who":"WOLSEY","text":"The hearts of princes kiss obedience","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Wolsey's warning: kings love obedience."},{"id":"431","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"She should have bought her dignities so dear","themes":["fall"],"note":"The price of being queen."},{"id":"445","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"A creature of the Queen's, Lady Anne Bullen","themes":["pride"],"note":"Wolsey's letter that ruins him."},{"id":"455","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"Will fall some blessing to this land, which shall","themes":["birth"],"note":"Suffolk expects blessings from Anne."},{"id":"477","who":"WOLSEY","text":"Anne Bullen! No; I'll no Anne Bullens for him","themes":["pride"],"note":"Wolsey's scorn of Anne."},{"id":"481","who":"WOLSEY","text":"A spleeny Lutheran","themes":["church"],"note":"Wolsey fears Anne's Protestant leanings."},{"id":"481","who":"WOLSEY","text":"An heretic, an arch one, Cranmer","themes":["church"],"note":"Wolsey sees Cranmer as the new threat."},{"id":"486","who":"KING HENRY","text":"What piles of wealth hath he accumulated","themes":["pride"],"note":"The King discovers Wolsey's greed."},{"id":"490","who":"KING HENRY","text":"His thinkings are below the moon, not worth","themes":["church"],"note":"Wolsey's thoughts are earthly."},{"id":"497","who":"KING HENRY","text":"And yet words are no deeds. My father lov'd you","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The King tests Wolsey's words against deeds."},{"id":"502","who":"KING HENRY","text":"To me, your friend, than any","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The King claims friendship as well as rule."},{"id":"503","who":"WOLSEY","text":"As doth a rock against the chiding flood","themes":["pride"],"note":"Wolsey's last boast of loyalty."},{"id":"508","who":"WOLSEY","text":"This paper has undone me. 'Tis the account","themes":["fall"],"note":"Wolsey reads his ruin."},{"id":"508","who":"WOLSEY","text":"I have touch'd the highest point of all my greatness","themes":["fall"],"note":"The top of Fortune's wheel."},{"id":"508","who":"WOLSEY","text":"Like a bright exhalation in the evening","themes":["fall"],"note":"Wolsey as a falling star."},{"id":"513","who":"WOLSEY","text":"Of what coarse metal ye are moulded, envy","themes":["pride"],"note":"Wolsey accuses the lords of envy."},{"id":"518","who":"SURREY","text":"Thou scarlet sin, robb'd this bewailing land","themes":["pride"],"note":"Surrey's attack."},{"id":"520","who":"SURREY","text":"Farewell nobility! Let his Grace go forward","themes":["pride"],"note":"The nobles' pride hurt by a churchman."},{"id":"529","who":"NORFOLK","text":"\"Ego et Rex meus\"","themes":["pride","kingship"],"note":"I and my King: Wolsey puts himself first."},{"id":"534","who":"LORD CHAMBERLAIN","text":"Press not a falling man too far! 'tis virtue","themes":["mercy"],"note":"The Chamberlain's call for pity."},{"id":"539","who":"WOLSEY","text":"Farewell! a long farewell, to all my greatness!","themes":["fall"],"note":"The most famous line of the play."},{"id":"539","who":"WOLSEY","text":"This is the state of man: to-day he puts forth","themes":["fall"],"note":"Wolsey's lesson about human life."},{"id":"539","who":"WOLSEY","text":"The third day comes a frost, a killing frost","themes":["fall"],"note":"The sudden end of greatness."},{"id":"539","who":"WOLSEY","text":"This many summers in a sea of glory","themes":["fall","pride"],"note":"Wolsey swam out of his depth."},{"id":"539","who":"WOLSEY","text":"But far beyond my depth. My high-blown pride","themes":["pride"],"note":"Pride as a swollen bladder that bursts."},{"id":"539","who":"WOLSEY","text":"Is that poor man that hangs on princes' favours!","themes":["fall","kingship"],"note":"The danger of depending on kings."},{"id":"539","who":"WOLSEY","text":"And when he falls, he falls like Lucifer","themes":["fall"],"note":"The comparison with the fallen angel."},{"id":"545","who":"WOLSEY","text":"I know myself now; and I feel within me","themes":["mercy"],"note":"Wolsey's self-knowledge."},{"id":"545","who":"WOLSEY","text":"A still and quiet conscience. The King has cur'd me","themes":["mercy"],"note":"The fall cures Wolsey's soul."},{"id":"551","who":"WOLSEY","text":"May have a tomb of orphans' tears wept on 'em!","themes":["mercy"],"note":"Wolsey's generous wish for More."},{"id":"555","who":"WOLSEY","text":"There was the weight that pull'd me down","themes":["birth","fall"],"note":"Anne is the weight that sank Wolsey."},{"id":"555","who":"WOLSEY","text":"That sun, I pray, may never set!","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Wolsey's blessing on the King."},{"id":"557","who":"WOLSEY","text":"Say, Wolsey, that once trod the ways of glory","themes":["fall"],"note":"Wolsey wants to be remembered as a teacher."},{"id":"557","who":"WOLSEY","text":"Cromwell, I charge thee, fling away ambition!","themes":["pride"],"note":"The lesson of his fall."},{"id":"557","who":"WOLSEY","text":"By that sin fell the angels; how can man, then","themes":["pride"],"note":"Ambition was Lucifer's sin."},{"id":"557","who":"WOLSEY","text":"Love thyself last. Cherish those hearts that hate thee","themes":["mercy"],"note":"Christian advice."},{"id":"557","who":"WOLSEY","text":"Corruption wins not more than honesty","themes":["truth"],"note":"Honesty pays as well as corruption."},{"id":"557","who":"WOLSEY","text":"Had I but serv'd my God with half the zeal","themes":["church","kingship"],"note":"Wolsey's great regret."},{"id":"559","who":"WOLSEY","text":"My hopes in heaven do dwell","themes":["church"],"note":"Wolsey turns to heaven."},{"id":"586","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","text":"Thou hast the sweetest face I ever look'd on","themes":["birth"],"note":"Anne admired by the crowd."},{"id":"586","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","text":"I cannot blame his conscience","themes":["conscience"],"note":"A joke: no one can blame Henry now."},{"id":"590","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","text":"And sometimes falling ones","themes":["fall"],"note":"Stars that fall: the play's pattern."},{"id":"601","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","text":"Could say \"This is my wife\" there; all were woven","themes":["birth"],"note":"The crowd's joy unites everyone."},{"id":"609","who":"THIRD GENTLEMAN","text":"Cranmer will find a friend will not shrink from him","themes":["church"],"note":"Cromwell will protect Cranmer."},{"id":"619","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"That the great child of honour, Cardinal Wolsey","themes":["fall"],"note":"Katherine's ironic title for Wolsey."},{"id":"624","who":"GRIFFITH","text":"An old man, broken with the storms of state","themes":["fall","church"],"note":"Wolsey's words at Leicester Abbey."},{"id":"624","who":"GRIFFITH","text":"Give him a little earth for charity!","themes":["church"],"note":"His humble request."},{"id":"625","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"Of an unbounded stomach, ever ranking","themes":["church","pride"],"note":"Katherine's portrait of Wolsey's pride."},{"id":"625","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"Simony was fair-play","themes":["church"],"note":"Wolsey sold church offices."},{"id":"626","who":"GRIFFITH","text":"Men's evil manners live in brass; their virtues","themes":["mercy","truth"],"note":"We remember faults, forget virtues."},{"id":"628","who":"GRIFFITH","text":"He was a scholar, and a ripe and good one","themes":["church"],"note":"Griffith's praise of Wolsey."},{"id":"628","who":"GRIFFITH","text":"But to those men that sought him, sweet as summer","themes":["church"],"note":"Wolsey's kindness to friends."},{"id":"628","who":"GRIFFITH","text":"Those twins of learning that he rais'd in you","themes":["church"],"note":"His colleges at Ipswich and Oxford."},{"id":"628","who":"GRIFFITH","text":"And found the blessedness of being little","themes":["mercy"],"note":"The fall as a blessing."},{"id":"629","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"But such an honest chronicler as Griffith","themes":["mercy"],"note":"Katherine wants a truthful historian."},{"id":"629","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"Now in his ashes honour. Peace be with him!","themes":["mercy"],"note":"Katherine forgives Wolsey."},{"id":"637","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"They promis'd me eternal happiness","themes":["mercy"],"note":"The vision's promise."},{"id":"656","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"'Tis like a pardon after execution","themes":["fall"],"note":"The King's comfort comes too late."},{"id":"663","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"The model of our chaste loves, his young daughter","themes":["birth"],"note":"Mary, the child of the first marriage."},{"id":"663","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"To love her for her mother's sake, that lov'd him","themes":["mercy"],"note":"Katherine's last love for Henry."},{"id":"665","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"Say his long trouble now is passing","themes":["mercy","kingship"],"note":"Katherine calls herself the King's trouble."},{"id":"665","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"I was a chaste wife to my grave. Embalm me","themes":["fall"],"note":"Her last claim of chastity."},{"id":"665","who":"QUEEN KATHERINE","text":"A queen, and daughter to a king, inter me","themes":["kingship","fall"],"note":"She dies a queen in her own eyes."},{"id":"677","who":"GARDINER","text":"I wish it grubb'd up now","themes":["birth"],"note":"Gardiner wishes Anne dead."},{"id":"679","who":"GARDINER","text":"Till Cranmer, Cromwell, her two hands, and she","themes":["church"],"note":"Gardiner's hatred of the reformers."},{"id":"681","who":"GARDINER","text":"A most arch heretic, a pestilence","themes":["church"],"note":"Gardiner's charge against Cranmer."},{"id":"681","who":"GARDINER","text":"He's a rank weed, Sir Thomas","themes":["church"],"note":"Gardiner's image for heresy."},{"id":"714","who":"CRANMER","text":"Most throughly to be winnowed, where my chaff","themes":["truth"],"note":"Cranmer welcomes a trial."},{"id":"716","who":"CRANMER","text":"The good I stand on is my truth and honesty","themes":["truth"],"note":"Cranmer's trust in truth."},{"id":"717","who":"KING HENRY","text":"The justice and the truth o' the question carries","themes":["truth"],"note":"Truth does not always win in court."},{"id":"717","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Whose minister you are, whiles here He liv'd","themes":["church"],"note":"Cranmer compared to Christ."},{"id":"719","who":"KING HENRY","text":"They shall no more prevail than we give way to","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The King's power protects Cranmer."},{"id":"719","who":"KING HENRY","text":"He's honest, on mine honour. God's blest mother","themes":["truth"],"note":"The King sees Cranmer's honesty."},{"id":"721","who":"KING HENRY","text":"He has strangled his language in his tears","themes":["truth"],"note":"Tears speak for Cranmer."},{"id":"726","who":"OLD LADY","text":"And of a lovely boy. The God of Heaven","themes":["birth"],"note":"The Old Lady's first lie."},{"id":"726","who":"OLD LADY","text":"Promises boys hereafter. Sir, your queen","themes":["birth"],"note":"A girl now, boys later."},{"id":"744","who":"CRANMER","text":"This is of purpose laid by some that hate me","themes":["church","truth"],"note":"Cranmer sees the plot."},{"id":"751","who":"KING HENRY","text":"'Tis well there's one above 'em yet. I had thought","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The King above the council, like providence."},{"id":"751","who":"KING HENRY","text":"By holy Mary, Butts, there's knavery","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The King sees malice."},{"id":"767","who":"GARDINER","text":"Which reformation must be sudden too","themes":["church"],"note":"Gardiner wants swift punishment."},{"id":"767","who":"GARDINER","text":"But stop their mouth with stubborn bits and spur 'em","themes":["church"],"note":"Gardiner's image of breaking horses."},{"id":"768","who":"CRANMER","text":"Defacers of a public peace, than I do","themes":["church","truth"],"note":"Cranmer's defence."},{"id":"771","who":"CRANMER","text":"Become a churchman better than ambition","themes":["church","mercy"],"note":"Cranmer's rebuke to Gardiner."},{"id":"771","who":"CRANMER","text":"Win straying souls with modesty again","themes":["church","mercy"],"note":"Cranmer's gentle religion."},{"id":"773","who":"CROMWELL","text":"To load a falling man","themes":["mercy"],"note":"Cromwell repeats the play's lesson of pity."},{"id":"779","who":"CROMWELL","text":"Would you were half so honest!","themes":["truth"],"note":"Cromwell's retort."},{"id":"792","who":"CRANMER","text":"To a most noble judge, the King my master","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Cranmer's appeal to the King."},{"id":"795","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"'Twould fall upon ourselves","themes":["fall"],"note":"The plotters' stone falls back on them."},{"id":"798","who":"CROMWELL","text":"Ye blew the fire that burns ye","themes":["truth"],"note":"Cromwell's verdict on the plot."},{"id":"801","who":"KING HENRY","text":"You were ever good at sudden commendations","themes":["kingship","truth"],"note":"The King sees through flattery."},{"id":"801","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Thou hast a cruel nature and a bloody","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The King's judgement on Gardiner."},{"id":"803","who":"KING HENRY","text":"I had thought I had had men of some understanding","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The King rebukes his council."},{"id":"805","who":"KING HENRY","text":"You must be godfather, and answer for her","themes":["kingship","birth"],"note":"Cranmer's reward."},{"id":"810","who":"KING HENRY","text":"A shrewd turn, and he is your friend for ever","themes":["mercy"],"note":"Cranmer's forgiving nature."},{"id":"810","who":"KING HENRY","text":"As I have made ye one, lords, one remain","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The King unites the council."},{"id":"814","who":"PORTER","text":"do you take the court for Paris-garden?","themes":["birth"],"note":"The christening crowd behaves like a bear-pit audience."},{"id":"845","who":"KING HENRY","text":"With this kiss take my blessing: God protect thee!","themes":["birth"],"note":"The King's blessing."},{"id":"848","who":"CRANMER","text":"Let none think flattery, for they'll find 'em truth","themes":["truth"],"note":"Cranmer claims prophecy, not flattery."},{"id":"848","who":"CRANMER","text":"Upon this land a thousand thousand blessings","themes":["birth"],"note":"Elizabeth's promise."},{"id":"848","who":"CRANMER","text":"A pattern to all princes living with her","themes":["birth","kingship"],"note":"Elizabeth as a model ruler."},{"id":"848","who":"CRANMER","text":"She shall be lov'd and fear'd: her own shall bless her","themes":["birth"],"note":"Love and fear together."},{"id":"848","who":"CRANMER","text":"In her days every man shall eat in safety","themes":["birth"],"note":"Peace and plenty in her reign."},{"id":"848","who":"CRANMER","text":"And by those claim their greatness, not by blood","themes":["birth"],"note":"Merit over birth: the reverse of Buckingham's complaint."},{"id":"848","who":"CRANMER","text":"The bird of wonder dies, the maiden phoenix","themes":["birth"],"note":"Elizabeth reborn in James I."},{"id":"848","who":"CRANMER","text":"Shall be, and make new nations. He shall flourish","themes":["birth","kingship"],"note":"James and new colonies."},{"id":"850","who":"CRANMER","text":"An aged princess; many days shall see her","themes":["birth"],"note":"Elizabeth's long life."},{"id":"850","who":"CRANMER","text":"A most unspotted lily shall she pass","themes":["birth"],"note":"The virgin queen; the lily recalls Katherine."},{"id":"851","who":"KING HENRY","text":"Thou hast made me now a man! Never, before","themes":["birth"],"note":"Henry's joy."},{"id":"851","who":"KING HENRY","text":"This little one shall make it holiday","themes":["birth"],"note":"The play ends in holiday."},{"id":"853","who":"EPILOGUE","text":"'Tis ten to one this play can never please","themes":["truth"],"note":"The Epilogue's modesty."},{"id":"853","who":"EPILOGUE","text":"The merciful construction of good women","themes":["mercy"],"note":"The play hopes for women's kindness."}]};
const EX={"intro":["Henry VIII (The Famous History of the Life of King Henry the Eighth) is a play about the falls of great people at the court of Henry VIII between 1520 and 1533. It was first performed in 1613, probably under the title All is True, and was written with John Fletcher. It is the last of the Histories in the 1623 First Folio.","The great fall one after another. The proud Duke of Buckingham, who hates the low-born Cardinal Wolsey, is arrested and executed on the word of a dismissed servant. Queen Katherine of Aragon, Henry's wife for twenty years, is put on trial when the King's 'conscience' about marrying his brother's widow coincides with his love for Anne Bullen. Wolsey himself, who has ruled the court, is ruined when his secret letter to the Pope and the inventory of his wealth fall into the King's hands. Each of them makes a farewell speech, and each forgives.","In the last act the pattern breaks. The Protestant Archbishop Cranmer is accused of heresy by Gardiner and the council, but the King, who now acts for himself, saves him with a ring. Anne gives birth to a daughter, and at her christening Cranmer prophesies the glory of Queen Elizabeth and of her heir, James I, the king in whose reign the play was first staged."],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The Famous History of the Life of King Henry the Eighth (Folio title); first performed as All is True"],["Written","1613, almost certainly with John Fletcher. Performed at the Globe on 29 June 1613, when a cannon fired in 1.4 set the thatch alight and the theatre burned down. First printed in the 1623 First Folio"],["Genre","History: the last of the Histories in the 1623 First Folio. Its masques, visions and joyful ending also link it to the late romances"],["Setting","London and Westminster (the palace, York Place, Blackfriars, the Abbey, the streets) and Kimbolton, from 1520 to 1533"],["Protagonist","No single hero: Buckingham, Katherine and Wolsey each take the centre in turn, with the King above them"],["Antagonists","Wolsey for the first half; Gardiner and the council against Cranmer in Act 5"],["Inciting incident","Buckingham's arrest (1.1), the first of the falls"],["Turning point","The King's discovery of Wolsey's inventory and letter (3.2)"],["Climax","Wolsey's fall and farewell (3.2)"],["Resolution","Cranmer saved by the King's ring; Elizabeth christened and prophesied"],["Time span","Thirteen years of history (1520 to 1533) compressed and rearranged"],["Major conflicts","Nobility against Wolsey; Katherine against Wolsey and the divorce; Gardiner against Cranmer; Rome against the King"],["Themes","The fall of greatness, conscience, ambition and envy, the Church and reform, patience and forgiveness, truth and rumour, royal favour, providence and Elizabeth"]],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"Buckingham hears of the Field of the Cloth of Gold and rages at Wolsey","id":15},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Buckingham arrested for high treason","id":51},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Katherine pleads against the tax","id":78},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Wolsey takes credit for the pardon","id":82},{"s":"a1s2","t":"The surveyor accuses Buckingham","id":89},{"s":"a1s4","t":"The King meets Anne at Wolsey's banquet","id":187},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Buckingham condemned","id":219},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Buckingham's farewell","id":243},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Rumour of the divorce","id":252},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Norfolk and Suffolk blame Wolsey for the King's scruple","id":268},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Campeius arrives; Gardiner made secretary","id":302},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Anne made Marchioness of Pembroke","id":341},{"s":"a2s4","t":"Katherine's plea at Blackfriars","id":365},{"s":"a2s4","t":"Katherine refuses Wolsey and appeals to the Pope","id":374},{"s":"a2s4","t":"The King explains his conscience","id":384},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Katherine and the Cardinals","id":420},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Wolsey's letter to the Pope discovered","id":445},{"s":"a3s2","t":"The inventory and the letter given to Wolsey","id":508},{"s":"a3s2","t":"The lords strip Wolsey of the seal and his goods","id":536},{"s":"a3s2","t":"'Farewell! a long farewell, to all my greatness!'","id":539},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Wolsey's advice to Cromwell","id":557},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Katherine divorced; Anne crowned","id":603},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Wolsey's death reported; Katherine forgives him","id":629},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Katherine's vision and last letter","id":663},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Gardiner plots against Cranmer","id":681},{"s":"a5s1","t":"The King gives Cranmer his ring","id":719},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Elizabeth born","id":726},{"s":"a5s3","t":"Cranmer shows the ring; the King rebukes the council","id":792},{"s":"a5s4","t":"The crowd at the christening","id":826},{"s":"a5s5","t":"Cranmer's prophecy of Elizabeth","id":848}],"irony":{"1":"The Prologue promises truth, but the play rearranges history freely: Anne marries Henry before Wolsey falls, and Katherine dies before Elizabeth is born.","16":"Norfolk praises Wolsey's self-made merit, which the nobles hate precisely because it is self-made.","34":"Wolsey promises to humble Buckingham's 'big look'; by Act 3 his own look will be humbled.","41":"Norfolk warns Buckingham not to heat a furnace that scorches himself; Wolsey's own fire will burn him.","62":"Buckingham blames his surveyor's betrayal; Wolsey will be betrayed by his own careless papers.","82":"Wolsey spreads the news that the pardon came from him, the tax's author.","137":"The lords praise Wolsey's bounty on their way to the very feast where he loses the King to Anne.","187":"'O beauty, till now I never knew thee!' The King falls in love at Wolsey's own party, which leads to Wolsey's ruin.","191":"Wolsey offers his chair to the most worthy masker; he will soon lose every chair he holds.","254":"The Gentlemen assume the Cardinal will 'have his will' over Katherine; in fact the divorce will destroy him.","289":"'This priest has no pride in him?' A sarcastic aside about the proudest man at court.","298":"Wolsey urges that Katherine should have scholars to argue for her, while she believes he is her enemy.","326":"Anne swears she would not be a queen; within two acts she is crowned.","343":"The Chamberlain foresees a 'gem' from Anne; the audience knows it is Elizabeth.","388":"The King says he would happily stay with Katherine if the marriage were proved lawful, while he is already in love with Anne.","445":"Wolsey's letter to stop the divorce comes too late: the King has already married Anne.","503":"Wolsey swears his loyalty stands like a rock; the King hands him the papers that prove the opposite.","557":"Wolsey tells Cromwell to fling away ambition; the audience knew Cromwell rose to power and was executed in 1540.","726":"'And of a lovely boy': the Old Lady's flattery turns into the truth that the girl will be greater than any boy.","851":"'Never, before this happy child, did I get anything': Henry forgets his living daughter Mary."},"devices":{"fall":"Fortune, falling and the wheel","light":"Sun, stars and light","fire":"Fire, coals and furnaces","beast":"Beasts and hunting","plant":"Flowers, trees and weeds","sea":"The sea, storms and water"},"dev":{"1":[["fall","The Prologue states the theme of falling greatness.","How soon this mightiness meets misery"]],"5":[["light","The two kings as two suns.","Those suns of glory, those two lights of men"]],"15":[["light","Wolsey's bulk blocks the King's sunlight from the earth.","Take up the rays o' th' beneficial sun"]],"16":[["beast","Wolsey spins his career from himself like a spider.","spider-like"]],"36":[["beast","Wolsey as a butcher's dog.","This butcher's cur is venom-mouth'd"]],"39":[["beast","Anger as a hot-blooded horse.","A full hot horse"]],"41":[["fire","Revenge as an overheated furnace.","Heat not a furnace for your foe so hot"]],"44":[["beast","Wolsey as fox and wolf.","This holy fox,"]],"62":[["light","A cloud darkens Buckingham's sun.","By dark'ning my clear sun"]],"81":[["plant","Taxation as stripping a tree.","From every tree lop, bark, and part o' the timber"]],"268":[["fall","Wolsey as Fortune's son, turning the wheel.","like the eldest son of Fortune"]],"270":[["light","Katherine as a jewel that never lost her lustre.","yet never lost her lustre"]],"370":[["fire","Tears turned to sparks.","I'll turn to sparks of fire"]],"372":[["fire","Wolsey has blown on the coal of the quarrel.","Have blown this coal betwixt my lord and me"]],"384":[["sea","The King's conscience as a wild sea.","The wild sea of my conscience, I did steer"]],"428":[["plant","Katherine as a dying lily.","Like the lily,"],["sea","England as a shipwreck.","Shipwreck'd upon a kingdom, where no pity"]],"429":[["sea","Princes' anger as storms.","They swell, and grow as terrible as storms"]],"481":[["light","Anne as a candle Wolsey must snuff.","This candle burns not clear: 'tis I must snuff it"]],"503":[["sea","Wolsey's loyalty as a rock against the flood.","As doth a rock against the chiding flood"]],"508":[["beast","The King as a wounded lion.","So looks the chafed lion"],["light","Wolsey as a falling star at evening.","Like a bright exhalation in the evening"],["fall","The highest point and the setting.","I have touch'd the highest point of all my greatness"]],"539":[["plant","Man as a tree killed by frost.","The third day comes a frost, a killing frost"],["sea","Swimming beyond his depth.","This many summers in a sea of glory"],["fall","Falling like Lucifer.","And when he falls, he falls like Lucifer"]],"555":[["light","The King as a sun that should never set.","That sun, I pray, may never set!"]],"590":[["light","Ladies as stars, sometimes falling.","These are stars indeed"]],"624":[["sea","Wolsey broken by storms of state.","An old man, broken with the storms of state"]],"626":[["sea","Virtues written in water.","their virtues"]],"681":[["plant","Cranmer as a weed to be rooted out.","He's a rank weed, Sir Thomas"]],"714":[["plant","Cranmer's trial as winnowing chaff from grain.","Most throughly to be winnowed, where my chaff"]],"767":[["beast","Heretics as wild horses to be broken.","But stop their mouth with stubborn bits and spur 'em"]],"795":[["fall","The stone they set rolling falls back on them.","When we first put this dangerous stone a-rolling"]],"798":[["fire","The plotters burned by their own fire.","Ye blew the fire that burns ye"]],"848":[["beast","The phoenix rising from its ashes.","The bird of wonder dies, the maiden phoenix"],["plant","Elizabeth's heir as a mountain cedar.","And, like a mountain cedar, reach his branches"],["light","The heir rises like a star.","Shall star-like rise as great in fame as she was"]],"850":[["plant","Elizabeth as a spotless lily.","A most unspotted lily shall she pass"]]},"stage":{"palace":"A room in the royal palace at Westminster or Greenwich, where the court waits on the King","council":"The council chamber, with a table under the cloth of state","hall":"A great hall: Wolsey's York Place for the banquet, or Blackfriars for the trial","street":"A street in Westminster, where Gentlemen meet to watch and gossip","chamber":"A private room: the Queen's apartments in London, or Kimbolton where Katherine lies sick","gate":"A door or gate: the lobby outside the council, or the palace yard where the crowd presses"},"stagerules":[["lobby","gate"],["council","council"],["yard","gate"],["hall","hall"],["street","street"],["kimbolton|queen's apartments","chamber"],["palace|ante-chamber|gallery","palace"]],"place":{"a1s1":"An antechamber in the palace, London","a1s2":"The council chamber, London","a1s3":"An antechamber in the palace","a1s4":"A hall in York Place, Wolsey's London house","a2s1":"A street in Westminster","a2s2":"An antechamber in the palace","a2s3":"An antechamber of the Queen's rooms","a2s4":"A hall in Blackfriars","a3s1":"The Queen's rooms, London","a3s2":"An antechamber to the King's rooms","a4s1":"A street in Westminster, on the coronation route","a4s2":"Kimbolton, Katherine's sickroom","a5s1":"A gallery in the palace, at night","a5s2":"The lobby outside the council chamber","a5s3":"The council chamber","a5s4":"The palace yard, at the gate","a5s5":"The palace, after the christening at Greenwich"},"arc":{"Wolsey's power":[9,9,8,9,9,10,9,9,8,1,0,0,0,0,0,0,0],"Anne's rise":[0,0,0,3,3,4,6,6,6,8,10,9,9,9,9,9,10]},"arcnote":"Wolsey begins at the height of his power: he has organised the meeting of the kings, arrests Buckingham and controls the King's ear. He peaks in 2.2, when he brings in the papal legate and places his own man, Gardiner, beside the King. Katherine's attack at Blackfriars and in her rooms dents him, and in 3.2 he falls in a single scene: the letter, the inventory, the lords, the praemunire. By 4.2 he is dead. Anne enters the story at Wolsey's own feast in 1.4, rises with the King's gift of a title in 2.3, is secretly married by 3.2, crowned in 4.1, and gives birth to Elizabeth in 5.1. Her peak comes at the christening. The two lines cross in 3.2, where Wolsey says of her: 'There was the weight that pulled me down.'","iago":[["a1s1","Buckingham, who hates Wolsey, is arrested before he can accuse him: the first fall, and the Prologue's pattern of 'mightiness' meeting 'misery'."],["a1s2","The surveyor's evidence condemns Buckingham in the King's mind before any trial. Katherine's doubts go unheard."],["a2s1","Buckingham is condemned by his peers, forgives everyone, blesses the King and goes to the block. The talk turns at once to the next fall: the Queen's."],["a2s2","The King's 'conscience' about his brother's widow is blamed on Wolsey; Campeius arrives to judge the marriage."],["a2s3","As Katherine's fall begins, Anne is raised to Marchioness of Pembroke: one rises as another falls."],["a2s4","At Blackfriars Katherine pleads, refuses Wolsey as judge and leaves the court. The King praises her and explains why he must leave her."],["a3s1","Katherine fights the Cardinals in her own rooms, then gives way. 'Like the lily... I'll hang my head and perish.'"],["a3s2","Wolsey's letter and inventory reach the King. The lords strip him of the seal and his goods. 'Farewell! a long farewell, to all my greatness!'"],["a3s2","Wolsey finds peace in his fall and warns Cromwell to 'fling away ambition'. Anne is now queen and Cranmer archbishop."],["a4s1","Katherine is divorced at Dunstable while Anne is crowned in the Abbey: the Queen's fall and the new queen's rise in one scene."],["a4s2","Wolsey dies repentant; Katherine forgives him, sees a vision of heaven and prepares to die 'like a queen'."],["a5s1","Gardiner tries to make Cranmer the next to fall. The King gives Cranmer his ring."],["a5s3","The council sends Cranmer to the Tower; he shows the ring, and the King rebukes his enemies. For the first time, a fall is stopped."],["a5s5","Instead of a fall, a birth: Elizabeth is christened, and Cranmer prophesies her glory and her heir's."],["a5s5","The Epilogue asks for applause. The pattern of the play has moved from the wheel of Fortune to the providence of a new reign."]],"rel":{"1":[["BUCKINGHAM","WOLSEY","hate"],["WOLSEY","BUCKINGHAM","hate"],["NORFOLK","BUCKINGHAM","trust"],["KING HENRY","WOLSEY","trust"],["QUEEN KATHERINE","WOLSEY","hate"],["SURVEYOR","BUCKINGHAM","deceive"],["KING HENRY","ANNE BULLEN","love"],["QUEEN KATHERINE","KING HENRY","love"]],"2":[["BUCKINGHAM","KING HENRY","love"],["NORFOLK","WOLSEY","hate"],["SUFFOLK","WOLSEY","hate"],["KING HENRY","WOLSEY","trust"],["GARDINER","WOLSEY","trust"],["QUEEN KATHERINE","WOLSEY","hate"],["KING HENRY","QUEEN KATHERINE","love"],["ANNE BULLEN","QUEEN KATHERINE","love"],["KING HENRY","CRANMER","trust"]],"3":[["QUEEN KATHERINE","WOLSEY","hate"],["QUEEN KATHERINE","CAMPEIUS","hate"],["WOLSEY","ANNE BULLEN","hate"],["WOLSEY","CRANMER","hate"],["SURREY","WOLSEY","hate"],["KING HENRY","WOLSEY","hate"],["WOLSEY","KING HENRY","deceive"],["CROMWELL","WOLSEY","love"],["WOLSEY","CROMWELL","love"],["KING HENRY","ANNE BULLEN","love"]],"4":[["QUEEN KATHERINE","WOLSEY","hate"],["GRIFFITH","WOLSEY","trust"],["QUEEN KATHERINE","GRIFFITH","trust"],["QUEEN KATHERINE","KING HENRY","love"],["GARDINER","CRANMER","hate"],["CROMWELL","CRANMER","trust"]],"5":[["GARDINER","CRANMER","hate"],["GARDINER","CROMWELL","hate"],["CROMWELL","CRANMER","trust"],["KING HENRY","CRANMER","trust"],["CRANMER","KING HENRY","love"],["KING HENRY","GARDINER","hate"],["CRANMER","GARDINER","love"]]},"context":[["Date and the Globe fire","Performed at the Globe on 29 June 1613 under the title All is True. During the masque in 1.4 a cannon fired as a salute set the thatch alight and the theatre burned to the ground in an hour; nobody was hurt, though one man's breeches caught fire. The Globe was rebuilt in 1614."],["Authorship","Since James Spedding in 1850, most scholars have thought the play was written with John Fletcher, who succeeded Shakespeare as the King's Men's main playwright. Studies of verse and word choice usually give Shakespeare 1.1, 1.2, 2.3, 2.4, 3.2 up to Wolsey's exit of the King, and 5.1, and Fletcher most of the rest, including Wolsey's farewell, Katherine's death and Cranmer's prophecy. The 1623 Folio printed it as Shakespeare's."],["Sources","Most of the history comes, often line by line, from Raphael Holinshed's Chronicles (1587), including Katherine's speech at Blackfriars and the coronation procession. The plot against Cranmer comes from John Foxe's Actes and Monuments (the 'Book of Martyrs'), a Protestant work."],["The real Henry VIII","Henry VIII (reigned 1509 to 1547) married Katherine of Aragon, his brother Arthur's widow, in 1509. Having no surviving son, he sought an annulment from 1527. When Pope Clement VII refused, Henry broke with Rome, made himself head of the Church of England, and married Anne Boleyn in 1533. He had six wives in all."],["The real Wolsey","Thomas Wolsey (about 1473 to 1530), son of an Ipswich butcher, became Archbishop of York, cardinal, papal legate and Lord Chancellor. He fell in 1529 for failing to obtain the annulment, was charged under praemunire, arrested for treason in 1530 and died at Leicester Abbey on his way to London."],["History rearranged","The play compresses and reorders events: Buckingham's execution (1521), the Blackfriars trial (1529), Wolsey's fall (1529), Anne's coronation (1533), Elizabeth's birth (1533) and Katherine's death (1536) are made to follow closely, with Katherine dying before Elizabeth is born. The plot against Cranmer happened in the 1540s."],["Religion and the Reformation","The play never shows the break with Rome directly, but the King's scorn for 'the tricks of Rome', Wolsey's fall, Cranmer's rise and his prophecy of a Protestant reign all point to it. Its audience knew that More, Cromwell, Anne and Cranmer would all be executed."],["James I and Princess Elizabeth","Cranmer's prophecy of a phoenix heir who will 'make new nations' praises James I and the new colony in Virginia. In 1613 James's daughter, also called Elizabeth, married Frederick, the Elector Palatine, and the play may have been planned for the wedding season."],["Pageantry and masque","The play has more spectacle than any other Shakespeare history: processions, a masque, a trial, a coronation, a vision and a christening, many of them described in long stage directions. These reflect the taste of the Jacobean court and the indoor Blackfriars theatre."],["The Folio","Henry VIII is the last of the ten Histories in the 1623 First Folio, after Richard III, though it was written about twenty years later. Its full Folio title is The Famous History of the Life of King Henry the Eight."],["A late play","It was one of Shakespeare's last works, after The Tempest. Like the romances, it ends with forgiveness, a child and hope for the future; unlike them, its people are real and its 'miracle' is the birth of a historical queen."]],"critics":{"fall":[["Frank Kermode, 'What is Shakespeare's Henry VIII About?' (1948)","Paraphrased: Kermode reads the play as a series of 'falls of princes' in the medieval tradition: Buckingham, Katherine and Wolsey each fall, each is given a set piece of farewell, and the King stands above them as a figure of justice and mercy."],["Lee Bliss, 'The Wheel of Fortune and the Maiden Phoenix of Shakespeare's King Henry the Eighth' (1975)","Paraphrased: Bliss argues the play sets the turning wheel of Fortune against the providential image of the phoenix: the first four acts show endless rise and fall, and the last act promises a reign that escapes the cycle."]],"conscience":[["R. A. Foakes, introduction to the Arden edition (1957)","Paraphrased: Foakes sees the play as deliberately leaving Henry's motives open: the King's conscience may be genuine, but the audience is given enough jokes and asides to doubt it."],["Gordon McMullan, introduction to the Arden Third Series edition (2000)","Paraphrased: McMullan argues that the play's alternative title, All is True, is ironic: the play keeps asking what can be known, and the King's conscience is one of many 'truths' that are reported but never proved."]],"pride":[["Samuel Johnson, notes on the play (1765)","Paraphrased: Johnson admired Katherine above everything in the play, and found Wolsey's farewell and his speeches to Cromwell moving; he thought the play's power came and went with Katherine."],["Hugh M. Richmond, 'King Henry VIII' in the Shakespeare in Performance series (1994)","Paraphrased: Richmond notes how often productions made Wolsey the star role, a magnificent prelate whose fall is the play's emotional peak."]],"church":[["Gordon McMullan, Arden Third Series (2000)","Paraphrased: McMullan reads the play as a Reformation play, written when Protestant hopes centred on Prince Henry and Princess Elizabeth; Cranmer, the Protestant martyr from Foxe, is given the last and greatest speech."],["Jay L. Halio, introduction to the Oxford edition (1999)","Paraphrased: Halio stresses how the play balances Catholic and Protestant sympathies: Katherine and Griffith are presented with great respect, and Wolsey is allowed a good death."]],"mercy":[["G. Wilson Knight, 'The Crown of Life' (1947)","Paraphrased: Knight read the play as the culmination of the late romances: its fallen characters move from pride to humility and forgiveness, and the play ends in a vision of national peace."],["Frank Kermode (1948)","Paraphrased: Kermode notes that each fall ends in patience and charity: Buckingham forgives, Wolsey finds peace, Katherine forgives Wolsey, and Cranmer forgives Gardiner."]],"truth":[["James Spedding, 'Who Wrote Shakespeare's Henry VIII?' (1850)","Paraphrased: Spedding argued that the uneven verse and the play's lack of a single design showed two writers, Shakespeare and Fletcher, and that Fletcher wrote the larger share."],["Ivo Kamps, 'Historiography and Ideology in Stuart Drama' (1996)","Paraphrased: Kamps argues the play is sceptical about history-writing itself: events reach us through gossiping Gentlemen, biased witnesses and competing portraits like Katherine's and Griffith's of Wolsey."]]},"symbols":{"Fall":"\\bfall\\w*|\\bfell\\b|\\bfallen\\b","Conscience":"\\bconscience\\w*|\\bscruple\\b","Sun":"\\bsun\\b|\\bstars?\\b|\\blight\\w*","Fire":"\\bfire\\b|\\bfurnace\\b|\\bcoal\\b|\\bburn\\w*","Truth":"\\btruth\\b|\\btrue\\b|\\bhonest\\w*","Heaven":"\\bheaven\\w*|\\bangels?\\b"},"symbols2":[["Fortune's wheel","Never shown on stage but always present: each great figure rises to the top and falls. Wolsey names it when he calls himself at 'the highest point of all my greatness'."],["The great seal","The Lord Chancellor's badge of office. Wolsey refuses to give it up to the lords; its loss marks his fall."],["The King's ring","The token that saves Cranmer. Unlike the seal, which is lost, the ring carries the King's personal trust."],["The lily","Katherine compares herself to a dying lily; Cranmer calls the infant Elizabeth 'a most unspotted lily'. The first queen's flower passes to the new princess."],["The phoenix","The bird that is reborn from its ashes stands for the succession from Elizabeth to James I."],["Conscience","The King's 'tender place': the official cause of the divorce, mocked by the lords and tested by the audience."],["Pageants and processions","Masque, trial, coronation, vision and christening: the court displays itself in spectacle, and each spectacle hides private grief or joy."],["The inventory","Wolsey's list of his own wealth, sent by mistake: the paper that brings down the most powerful man in England."]],"solo":{"390":1,"477":1,"481":1,"508":1,"539":1,"744":1},"why":["Use it as the thesis of any essay on the falls: every great figure in the play meets misery.","Shows the play's double mood: it ends in a christening, but tears are never far away.","The marriage image makes political display sound like love, a first hint of the royal marriages to come.","The splendour is almost pagan, which colours Wolsey's role as its organiser.","Buckingham's anger begins the play's quarrel between old nobility and a new man.","Ambition is the vice Wolsey will confess at his fall.","Insults Wolsey through his body and his father's trade: class contempt in a single word.","Wolsey made himself; Buckingham cannot forgive a man without ancestors.","Defines Wolsey's power exactly: he stands between the nobles and the King.","Abergavenny's hyperbole shows how far hatred of Wolsey runs among the nobles.","The cost of display falls on the nobility, giving their hatred a material cause.","Nothing at this court is private; everyone is watched.","Norfolk sees Wolsey's power clearly and warns of revenge.","Low-born, savage and poisonous: Buckingham's image of the Cardinal.","Learning has replaced birth as the road to power, a social change Buckingham resents.","The old nobility's complaint in four words.","Norfolk's patience is the wisdom Buckingham lacks.","Wolsey's birthplace becomes a sneer.","A warning that revenge burns the revenger, which is exactly what happens to Buckingham.","The fox image fixes Wolsey as cunning in holy dress.","Accuses Wolsey of selling the King's honour, the charge that will be proved in Act 3.","Buckingham sees at once that he is caught in a trap.","Suggests the arrest is a plot, not justice.","His innocence turned black by a charge, like a dye.","Buckingham already sees himself as a shadow of the great man he was.","Opens the King's part: he owes his life, he thinks, to Wolsey.","The King's early love for Katherine, before the divorce.","Katherine speaks for the King's honour and the people's good.","The tax kills loyalty; Katherine's report shows the danger to the crown.","Wolsey turns criticism into proof of his virtue.","A clever defence of action despite criticism; Wolsey is persuasive.","The King's best moment of rule: law must govern, not will.","Wolsey takes credit for a pardon he did not want: the clearest proof of his double dealing.","The King condemns Buckingham's gifts as corrupted, before the trial.","Katherine asks for charity in judgement, a key word of the play.","Katherine sees the witness's motive, and is ignored.","Connects Buckingham to his father's plot against Richard III.","The King's verdict comes before any trial.","The King has made up his mind on a dismissed servant's evidence.","The French visit has infected the court with fashion.","English courtiers can be wise without French polish.","Wolsey's generosity is real, which complicates the hostile picture.","The King's first sight of Anne turns the play towards the divorce.","Henry's joke shows his easy friendship with Wolsey before the fall.","Henry is already captivated; desire comes before 'conscience'.","Eloquence cannot save a man the King has condemned.","The common people blame Wolsey for Buckingham's death.","Buckingham's popularity with the people.","Begins the noble farewell speech that each falling figure will make.","A warning to his enemies, without naming them.","The axe called a 'divorce' links Buckingham's fall to Katherine's.","Christian forgiveness: the first of the play's acts of charity.","He dies blessing the King who condemns him.","His fall is measured in lost names and titles.","Betrayal by servants joins father and son.","False friends vanish at the first sign of trouble.","His last wish: to be remembered as a story of a fall.","The divorce enters the play as a doubt of conscience, a word the play will keep testing.","Announces the next fall before it happens.","The official reason for the divorce.","Suffolk's joke exposes the real reason.","Quote it in an essay on ambition, pride and envy or the church, rome and reform.","Wolsey as Fortune's son, turning the wheel of the play.","Katherine's twenty years of faithful marriage.","The lords' verdict on Wolsey in three words.","Wolsey's power threatens to reduce princes to pages.","Suffolk links Wolsey's pride to Rome.","The King's guilt is real, but also convenient.","Wolsey praises Rome, the court that will fail the King.","Wolsey's contempt for lesser men.","Desire and guilt mixed in one cry.","The King leaves Katherine for 'conscience'.","Losing greatness is a kind of death.","Anne's praise of a humble life is soon contradicted by her rise.","Greatness as a golden grief, true of every great figure in the play.","Anne's protest, which history and the play reverse.","The Old Lady's cynical view of ambition, which may be right.","The Chamberlain sees Anne as a match for the King.","The first hint of Elizabeth.","The Old Lady's envy shows how arbitrary favour is.","Katherine begins as a foreigner without fair judges.","Her claim of loyalty is the heart of her defence.","Twenty years and many children: the marriage is real.","Pride in her royal birth: she will not be humbled.","She names Wolsey as her enemy openly.","Wolsey has fanned the fire of the divorce, she says.","She sees through Wolsey's humble manner.","Her charge against his heart: pride, malice and arrogance.","The King's tenderness for Katherine as she goes.","Henry's praise of Katherine, spoken only after she has left.","Henry's account of his conscience, which the audience may doubt.","He reads the deaths of his sons as God's judgement.","Quote it in an essay on conscience and the divorce.","He claims he would stay with Katherine if the marriage were lawful.","The King's impatience with Rome foreshadows the break.","Cranmer named for the first time, linked to the King's comfort.","Music cannot kill Katherine's grief for long.","Appearances deceive: holy dress is not holiness.","Katherine's honesty against the cardinals' secrecy.","Quote it in an essay on conscience and the divorce.","Quote it in an essay on the church, rome and reform.","A pun on 'cardinal' that turns their title against them.","Katherine's fury at being made nothing.","Patience as her greatest honour.","She will never give up the name of queen.","Quote it in an essay on the church, rome and reform.","Quote it in an essay on the fall of greatness.","The lily image will return in Cranmer's prophecy of Elizabeth.","Wolsey's political wisdom: kings love obedience.","Quote it in an essay on the fall of greatness.","The letter that ruins Wolsey names Anne.","Quote it in an essay on providence, anne and elizabeth.","Wolsey's scorn of Anne shows he has misread the King.","Quote it in an essay on the church, rome and reform.","Wolsey sees Cranmer as the coming threat.","The King discovers Wolsey's greed for himself.","The King's irony: Wolsey's thoughts are earthly.","The King tests Wolsey: words against deeds.","The King claims friendship as well as service.","Wolsey's last boast, said with the papers waiting.","Wolsey sees his ruin in his own handwriting.","The top of Fortune's wheel, the play's central image.","Quote it in an essay on the fall of greatness.","Quote it in an essay on ambition, pride and envy.","Surrey's revenge for Buckingham.","Quote it in an essay on ambition, pride and envy.","Wolsey seems to put himself before the King.","The Chamberlain's call for pity: the play's moral voice.","Quote it in an essay on the fall of greatness.","Wolsey's general lesson about human life.","Quote it in an essay on the fall of greatness.","Quote it in an essay on the fall of greatness or ambition, pride and envy.","Quote it in an essay on ambition, pride and envy.","Quote it in an essay on the fall of greatness or kingship and favour.","Quote it in an essay on the fall of greatness.","Wolsey's new self-knowledge.","Quote it in an essay on patience, charity and forgiveness.","Wolsey's generous wish for his successor.","Quote it in an essay on providence, anne and elizabeth or the fall of greatness.","Wolsey's blessing on the King who ruined him.","Quote it in an essay on the fall of greatness.","The lesson of his fall: ambition destroys.","Quote it in an essay on ambition, pride and envy.","Christian advice that Wolsey never followed.","Quote it in an essay on truth, rumour and report.","Wolsey's great regret: he served the King before God.","Wolsey turns from the court to heaven.","Anne admired by the crowd at her coronation.","A joke: no one can blame Henry's conscience now.","Quote it in an essay on the fall of greatness.","Quote it in an essay on providence, anne and elizabeth.","Cromwell will protect Cranmer, preparing Act 5.","Quote it in an essay on the fall of greatness.","Wolsey's words at Leicester Abbey: a broken old man.","His humble request for a grave.","Quote it in an essay on the church, rome and reform or ambition, pride and envy.","Quote it in an essay on the church, rome and reform.","We remember faults and forget virtues.","Griffith's praise of Wolsey's learning.","Wolsey's kindness to his friends.","Quote it in an essay on the church, rome and reform.","Quote it in an essay on patience, charity and forgiveness.","Quote it in an essay on patience, charity and forgiveness.","Quote it in an essay on patience, charity and forgiveness.","The vision's promise of reward.","Quote it in an essay on the fall of greatness.","Quote it in an essay on providence, anne and elizabeth.","Quote it in an essay on patience, charity and forgiveness.","Quote it in an essay on patience, charity and forgiveness or kingship and favour.","Quote it in an essay on the fall of greatness.","Quote it in an essay on kingship and favour or the fall of greatness.","Quote it in an essay on providence, anne and elizabeth.","Quote it in an essay on the church, rome and reform.","Quote it in an essay on the church, rome and reform.","Quote it in an essay on the church, rome and reform.","Quote it in an essay on truth, rumour and report.","Quote it in an essay on truth, rumour and report.","Quote it in an essay on truth, rumour and report.","Quote it in an essay on the church, rome and reform.","Quote it in an essay on kingship and favour.","Quote it in an essay on truth, rumour and report.","Quote it in an essay on truth, rumour and report.","Quote it in an essay on providence, anne and elizabeth.","Quote it in an essay on providence, anne and elizabeth.","Quote it in an essay on the church, rome and reform or truth, rumour and report.","Quote it in an essay on kingship and favour.","Quote it in an essay on kingship and favour.","Quote it in an essay on the church, rome and reform.","Quote it in an essay on the church, rome and reform.","Quote it in an essay on the church, rome and reform or truth, rumour and report.","Quote it in an essay on the church, rome and reform or patience, charity and forgiveness.","Quote it in an essay on the church, rome and reform or patience, charity and forgiveness.","Quote it in an essay on patience, charity and forgiveness.","Quote it in an essay on truth, rumour and report.","Quote it in an essay on kingship and favour.","Quote it in an essay on the fall of greatness.","Quote it in an essay on truth, rumour and report.","Quote it in an essay on kingship and favour or truth, rumour and report.","Quote it in an essay on kingship and favour.","Quote it in an essay on kingship and favour.","Quote it in an essay on kingship and favour or providence, anne and elizabeth.","Quote it in an essay on patience, charity and forgiveness.","Quote it in an essay on kingship and favour.","Quote it in an essay on providence, anne and elizabeth.","The King's blessing on Elizabeth.","Quote it in an essay on truth, rumour and report.","Elizabeth's promise of blessings.","Quote it in an essay on providence, anne and elizabeth or kingship and favour.","Quote it in an essay on providence, anne and elizabeth.","Quote it in an essay on providence, anne and elizabeth.","Quote it in an essay on providence, anne and elizabeth.","Quote it in an essay on providence, anne and elizabeth.","Quote it in an essay on providence, anne and elizabeth or kingship and favour.","Quote it in an essay on providence, anne and elizabeth.","Quote it in an essay on providence, anne and elizabeth.","Quote it in an essay on providence, anne and elizabeth.","Quote it in an essay on providence, anne and elizabeth.","Quote it in an essay on truth, rumour and report.","Quote it in an essay on patience, charity and forgiveness."],"insults":[{"id":"15","f":"keech","who":"Buckingham","n":"A lump of butcher's fat: Wolsey's size and his father's trade together."},{"id":"36","f":"This butcher's cur is venom-mouth'd","who":"Buckingham","n":"Wolsey as a butcher's dog with a poisonous bite."},{"id":"40","f":"This Ipswich fellow's insolence","who":"Buckingham","n":"Wolsey's birthplace used as an insult."},{"id":"44","f":"This holy fox","who":"Buckingham","n":"Cunning in holy clothes."},{"id":"17","f":"A new hell in himself","who":"Abergavenny","n":"Wolsey's pride is a hell of its own."},{"id":"268","f":"That blind priest","who":"Norfolk","n":"Wolsey turns men's fortunes like blind Fortune."},{"id":"271","f":"This bold bad man","who":"Lord Chamberlain","n":"The plainest verdict on Wolsey."},{"id":"420","f":"cardinal sins and hollow hearts","who":"Katherine","n":"A pun that turns the Cardinals' title against them."},{"id":"428","f":"Ye have angels' faces, but Heaven knows your hearts","who":"Katherine","n":"Fair looks, false hearts."},{"id":"516","f":"Thou art a proud traitor, priest","who":"Surrey","n":"A noble's contempt for a churchman: 'thou' and 'priest'."},{"id":"517","f":"Proud lord, thou liest!","who":"Wolsey","n":"Wolsey answers 'thou' with 'thou'."},{"id":"518","f":"Thou scarlet sin","who":"Surrey","n":"Wolsey's red robes made into his sin."},{"id":"520","f":"jaded by a piece of scarlet","who":"Surrey","n":"The nobles ridden like tired horses by a red robe."},{"id":"521","f":"All goodness","who":"Wolsey","n":"Wolsey claims Surrey cannot stomach goodness."},{"id":"537","f":"my little good Lord Cardinal","who":"Norfolk","n":"A mocking farewell."},{"id":"625","f":"Of an unbounded stomach","who":"Katherine","n":"Wolsey's limitless pride."},{"id":"646","f":"You are a saucy fellow","who":"Katherine","n":"Katherine keeps her royal dignity to the end."},{"id":"681","f":"He's a rank weed","who":"Gardiner","n":"Heresy as a weed to be rooted out."},{"id":"772","f":"you are a sectary","who":"Gardiner","n":"Gardiner calls Cranmer a heretic."},{"id":"776","f":"Ye are not sound","who":"Gardiner","n":"He turns on Cromwell too."},{"id":"779","f":"Would you were half so honest!","who":"Cromwell","n":"Cromwell's counter-attack."},{"id":"801","f":"Thou hast a cruel nature and a bloody","who":"King Henry","n":"The King's judgement on Gardiner."},{"id":"814","f":"Ye rude slaves","who":"Porter","n":"Comic abuse of the crowd."},{"id":"816","f":"be hang'd, ye rogue","who":"Porter","n":"The Porter's threats are all bark."},{"id":"823","f":"good master puppy","who":"Porter","n":"Mock politeness."},{"id":"829","f":"These lazy knaves","who":"Lord Chamberlain","n":"The Chamberlain blames the Porters."}],"pronouns":[{"id":"382","f":"Go thy ways, Kate","n":"Henry uses tender 'thy' and a pet name, but only after Katherine has left."},{"id":"201","f":"I were unmannerly to take you out","n":"Henry uses polite 'you' to Anne at first meeting."},{"id":"516","f":"Thou art a proud traitor, priest","n":"Surrey uses contemptuous 'thou' to the Cardinal."},{"id":"517","f":"Proud lord, thou liest!","n":"Wolsey throws 'thou' back."},{"id":"555","f":"Go, get thee from me, Cromwell!","n":"Wolsey's 'thee' to his servant is affectionate."},{"id":"557","f":"Cromwell, I charge thee, fling away ambition!","n":"The intimate 'thee' of a dying master's advice."},{"id":"715","f":"Thy truth and thy integrity is rooted","n":"The King switches to 'thy' to show Cranmer his friendship."},{"id":"801","f":"Thou hast a cruel nature and a bloody","n":"The King moves from 'you' to 'thou' as he turns on Gardiner."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["Buckingham reaches the King and exposes Wolsey","Buckingham is arrested before he can"],"a1s2":["The King believes Katherine and spares Buckingham","The King orders Buckingham's trial"],"a1s3":["The lords stay away from Wolsey's feast","They go, and the King comes too, in disguise"],"a1s4":["The King forgets Anne after the party","Anne is soon given a title and the divorce begins"],"a2s1":["Buckingham curses the King on the scaffold","He forgives everyone and blesses the King"],"a2s2":["The King refuses the divorce","A court is set up at Blackfriars"],"a2s3":["Anne refuses the King's gifts","She accepts the title of Marchioness"],"a2s4":["Katherine accepts the court","She walks out and appeals to the Pope"],"a3s1":["Katherine agrees to the divorce","She attacks the Cardinals, then wearily asks their counsel"],"a3s2":["Wolsey survives","Wolsey falls in one scene"],"a4s1":["Katherine attends the coronation","Katherine is divorced and lies sick at Kimbolton"],"a4s2":["Katherine dies cursing Wolsey","She forgives him and dies like a queen"],"a5s1":["Cranmer flees","The King gives him a ring"],"a5s2":["Cranmer is let straight in","He is kept waiting at the door"],"a5s3":["Cranmer goes to the Tower","The ring saves him"],"a5s4":["The crowd is cleared","The Porters fail, and the procession forces its way"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Buckingham and Wolsey","pts":["Buckingham is a great noble by birth; Wolsey is a butcher's son who rose by ability.","Each accuses the other of treason, and each falls.","Buckingham is ruined by a servant's evidence; Wolsey by his own papers.","Both make long farewells, forgive and find peace."],"ids":[243,539]},{"t":"Katherine and Anne","pts":["Katherine is a king's daughter and an experienced queen; Anne is a knight's daughter and a maid of honour.","Katherine speaks at length and fights; Anne speaks little and only in Acts 1 and 2.","Katherine falls as Anne rises: 2.3 and 4.1 set them side by side.","Katherine gives the King Mary; Anne gives him Elizabeth."],"ids":[365,326]},{"t":"Wolsey and Cranmer","pts":["Both are the King's chief churchmen.","Wolsey is proud and worldly; Cranmer is humble and tearful.","Wolsey works against the King's marriage; Cranmer supports it.","Wolsey falls; Cranmer is saved by the King's ring."],"ids":[557,771]},{"t":"Katherine and Griffith on Wolsey","pts":["Two portraits of the same man.","Katherine lists pride, lying and simony; Griffith lists learning, generosity and a holy death.","Katherine accepts both and forgives.","The scene shows how history depends on who tells it."],"ids":[625,628]},{"t":"Gardiner and Cromwell","pts":["Both were Wolsey's men.","Gardiner becomes a conservative bishop and Cranmer's enemy; Cromwell becomes a statesman and Cranmer's friend.","At the council they quarrel openly.","Cromwell keeps Wolsey's last advice; Gardiner follows his old ambition."],"ids":[681,779]}],"analysis":{"KING HENRY":["Henry begins the play almost as Wolsey's creature. He enters leaning on the Cardinal's shoulder, thanks him for saving his life, and condemns Buckingham on a dismissed servant's word before any trial: 'He's traitor to th' height.' Yet he can act well: when Katherine tells him of the tax, he cancels it at once with a firm principle, 'We must not rend our subjects from our laws, And stick them in our will.'","The divorce makes his motives the play's great question. He tells the Blackfriars court that a French bishop's question and the deaths of his sons troubled his 'wounded conscience', but the lords joke that his conscience 'has crept too near another lady', and we have seen him fall for Anne. The play lets both be true.","In Act 3 he destroys Wolsey coolly, testing his professions of loyalty before handing him the papers. In Act 5 he acts entirely on his own judgement: he warns Cranmer, gives him the ring, watches the council from above and humbles them. He ends the play as a father blessing his child, the source of order rather than a pawn of favourites."],"WOLSEY":["Wolsey is the play's great figure for its first three acts. The nobles hate him as a low-born upstart, a 'butcher's cur' and 'Ipswich fellow', and accuse him of pride, greed and treason. He arrests Buckingham, levies a tax without precedent and takes credit for its repeal, plants Gardiner beside the King, and is blamed for the divorce.","He is also generous, witty and able: his banquet is splendid, his defence of necessary action is persuasive, and he offers his chair to the masked King. His plan, to replace Katherine with a French princess, is a statesman's plan, and he is undone because the King wants Anne instead and because he puts his secret papers in the wrong packet.","His fall is the play's climax and its best-known writing: 'Farewell! a long farewell, to all my greatness!' In defeat he finds peace, self-knowledge and faith, and gives Cromwell honest advice. Griffith's account of his death, and Katherine's forgiveness, complete the portrait of a proud man redeemed by his fall."],"QUEEN KATHERINE":["Katherine is the play's moral centre. She speaks first for the taxed clothworkers and warns the King not to trust Buckingham's surveyor; she sees Wolsey clearly when no one else does.","At Blackfriars she is magnificent: a foreigner without fair judges, she reminds the King of twenty years of obedience, refuses Wolsey as her judge, appeals to the Pope and leaves the court. In her rooms she turns her fury on both Cardinals: 'Ye have angels' faces, but heaven knows your hearts.'","Divorced and dying, she still insists on her title, and her last scene shows patience and charity: she forgives Wolsey after hearing Griffith's account, sees a vision of heaven, and asks the King to care for their daughter and her servants. Johnson thought the play's genius came and went with her."],"BUCKINGHAM":["Buckingham is the first to fall. Proud of his blood, he cannot bear that a butcher's son should outrank the nobility, and his anger rushes him towards the King despite Norfolk's warnings.","Whether he is guilty is left uncertain. The evidence comes from a servant he dismissed, and Katherine warns against spite; but the surveyor's details (the monk's prophecy, the dagger, his father's plan against Richard III) are specific.","His farewell in 2.1 sets the pattern for every later fall: he accepts the law, forgives his enemies, blesses the King, and warns the crowd that friends 'fall away like water' at the first sign of ruin."],"CRANMER":["Cranmer is first mentioned as the King's 'learned and well-beloved servant', whose opinions from the universities support the divorce. Wolsey calls him an arch-heretic; he crowns Anne.","In Act 5 he is the one man who does not fall. Gardiner and the council try to send him to the Tower as a heretic, but his innocence moves the King to give him a ring, and his tears prove his honesty.","His last speech, the prophecy of Elizabeth's reign and of the phoenix heir who will follow her, turns the play from a chain of falls into a vision of providence. His gentleness is the opposite of Wolsey's pride."],"ANNE BULLEN":["Anne is the hinge of the plot but speaks little. The King meets her at Wolsey's banquet and is captivated at once.","In 2.3 she pities Katherine and swears she would not be a queen; the Old Lady mocks her, and the King's gift of a title follows. The play leaves her motives unclear.","After 2.3 she is only seen: crowned in a silent procession, praised by the crowd, and giving birth offstage. She matters as the mother of Elizabeth."],"GARDINER":["Gardiner begins as Wolsey's secretary, placed beside the King to serve his old master, and rises to be Bishop of Winchester.","In Act 5 he is the villain: he wishes Anne, Cranmer and Cromwell dead, persuades the council that Cranmer is a heretic, and insists on sending him to the Tower.","The King exposes his flattery and orders him to embrace Cranmer. His defeat shows how the King's favour has turned against the old conservative party."]},"motifs":[["Fortune's wheel and the fall","Every great figure rises and falls in turn, and each fall is marked by a farewell: Buckingham, Katherine and Wolsey.",[0,24,56,121,128,149]],["Conscience","The King's 'tender place': the official reason for the divorce, mocked by the lords and doubted by the audience.",[57,60,67,70,92]],["Pageantry and display","From the Field of the Cloth of Gold to the christening, the court shows itself in spectacle, and the play asks what lies behind the show.",[2,3,150]],["Sun and stars","Kings are suns, favourites stars, and falls are shooting stars; Wolsey ends as 'a bright exhalation in the evening'.",[122,139,149]],["The lily and the phoenix","Katherine as a dying lily, Elizabeth as an unspotted lily and the phoenix whose ashes produce a new ruler.",[107,214,211]],["Charity and patience","Each fallen character forgives, and the play keeps asking for pity for 'a falling man'.",[51,127,103,163,201]]],"bigq":[["Is Henry's conscience genuine?",["He gives a detailed account of his doubts at Blackfriars","The lords joke that his conscience has crept near another lady","He met Anne in Act 1, long before the trial","The play lets both readings stand"],[57,60,70,92]],["Is Wolsey a villain?",["He arrests Buckingham and takes credit for a pardon he opposed","He is generous, witty and able","He falls through carelessness, not just malice","His death is repentant and Griffith praises him"],[32,41,128,158]],["Was Buckingham guilty?",["The evidence comes from a dismissed servant","Katherine warns against spite","The details are specific and damning","He forgives but does not confess"],[35,36,51]],["Who is the play's hero?",["Katherine, for her courage and charity","Wolsey, whose fall is its climax","The King, who grows into his role","Elizabeth, the goal of the whole play"],[105,128,202,207]],["Is 'All is True' an honest title?",["The play follows Holinshed closely in many speeches","It rearranges dates and makes Katherine die before Elizabeth's birth","It shows rumour, biased witnesses and competing portraits","Cranmer's prophecy is 'truth' because the audience knows it came true"],[157,162,205]],["Does the ending resolve the play?",["Cranmer's rescue breaks the pattern of falls","The prophecy turns history into providence","The audience knew Anne, Cromwell and Cranmer would be executed","The King's joy forgets Katherine and Mary"],[179,209,215]]],"examq":[["How does the play present the fall of great people?",[0,56,128,107,134]],["How is Wolsey presented?",[19,32,87,141,158]],["How is Katherine presented?",[81,86,105,103,170]],["How does the play present kingship?",[31,38,108,133,202]],["How does the play present religion and the Church?",[94,101,145,174,191]],["How does the play prepare for Elizabeth?",[78,111,183,207,211]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short quotations, built into my own sentences","Explained what the quotation suggests, not just what it says","Zoomed in on a single word or image","Named a method (farewell speech, imagery, pageant, aside, prophecy, parallel scenes) and its effect","Linked to context where it helps the argument (Holinshed, the Reformation, James I, Fletcher)","Considered more than one interpretation, especially of Henry's conscience and Wolsey","Referred to the beginning, middle and end of the play","Ended with a conclusion that answers the question"],"models":[[128,"Wolsey's farewell is the climax of the play's pattern of falls.","The repetition of 'farewell' and the adjective 'long' make the moment slow and final.","The image of the frost that 'nips his root' turns a political defeat into a law of nature.","His comparison with Lucifer links ambition to the first sin, preparing for his advice to Cromwell."],[105,"Katherine's line sets appearance against truth.","'Angels' faces' admits the Cardinals' holy look, while 'hearts' accuses what lies beneath.","She appeals over their heads to Heaven, the only judge she trusts.","The line connects to the play's concern with how far anyone can be known."],[70,"Henry's cry is the centre of the debate about his motives.","The repeated word sounds sincere, but follows directly on his regret at leaving 'so sweet a bedfellow'.","Audiences can take it as real guilt or as self-deception.","The ambiguity is the point: the play never settles it."],[51,"Buckingham's forgiveness establishes the play's model of a good fall.","The echo of the Lord's Prayer makes him a Christian example.","It contrasts with his anger in Act 1, showing the fall has changed him.","Each later fallen figure repeats the pattern."],[214,"Cranmer's prophecy ends with an image of purity.","The lily recalls Katherine's 'Like the lily' in 3.1, so the old queen's flower passes to the new princess.","'Unspotted' praises Elizabeth's virginity, a key part of her public image.","The prophecy turns history into providence, with the audience knowing it came true."],[157,"Griffith's proverb comments on the play's whole method.","Brass is permanent, water vanishes: we remember bad deeds, not good ones.","He corrects Katherine's portrait of Wolsey with a kinder one.","The scene suggests that history is partial, which questions the title All is True."]],"lang":{"un":[["539","Farewell! a long farewell, to all my greatness!","Goodbye for ever to all my power and glory."],["557","Corruption wins not more than honesty","Being dishonest gets you no further than being honest."],["626","Men's evil manners live in brass; their virtues","People's bad deeds are remembered for ever; their good ones are quickly forgotten."],["401","But all hoods make not monks","Wearing holy clothes doesn't make you holy."],["848","She shall be lov'd and fear'd: her own shall bless her","Her people will love and respect her."],["243","Speak how I fell. I have done; and God forgive me","Tell my story as a story of a fall."]],"pun":[["420","But cardinal sins and hollow hearts I fear ye","cardinal","pun","Cardinal (a churchman) and cardinal (chief, deadly) sins."],["335","In faith, for little England","England","bawdy","A joke on 'emballing', being given the royal orb or being taken to bed."],["331","A three-pence bow'd would hire me","bow'd","bawdy","A bent coin, and a hint at bending."],["44","Or wolf, or both","wolf","image","Wolsey's name sounds like 'wolf'."],["15","That such a keech can with his very bulk","keech","sneer","A butcher's lump of fat, aimed at the butcher's son."],["825","Or have we some strange Indian with the","Indian","bawdy","A joke about Native Americans displayed in London."]],"st":[["539","Farewell! a long farewell, to all my greatness!",[["Farewell","Stress falls on the first and last: FARE-well, a long fare-WELL."],["greatness","A heavy final word, said slowly."]]],["428","Ye have angels' faces, but Heaven knows your hearts.",[["angels'","Light, mocking."],["hearts","The hard final stress lands the accusation."]]],["382","Go thy ways, Kate.",[["Kate","A short, tender name: the King's only use of it."]]]]},"dir":[["243","Speak how I fell","How should Buckingham deliver his farewell?",[["Calmly, almost serene","He has accepted death and forgiven everyone."],["With barely controlled bitterness","His servants betrayed him; the anger is still there."],["Weeping, to the crowd","A popular hero moving the people to pity."]]],["365","Sir, I desire you do me right and justice","How should Katherine plead at Blackfriars?",[["Kneeling, quiet and dignified","Her power is moral: she shames the court by calm."],["Rising in anger as she goes on","Her grief turns to the 'sparks of fire' she promises."],["Speaking only to Henry, ignoring the court","It is a personal appeal, wife to husband."]]],["508","This paper has undone me","How should Wolsey react to the papers?",[["Slowly, reading as the truth sinks in","The audience watches him realise."],["In sudden panic","The great man loses control for once."],["With a wry, almost amused acceptance","He sees the joke of his own carelessness."]]],["539","Farewell! a long farewell, to all my greatness!","How should Wolsey's farewell be staged?",[["Alone in a bare space, stripped of his robes","The fall made visible."],["Still in full scarlet","The splendour of the robes makes the words ironic."],["To the audience, as a confession","He shares his lesson with us."]]],["633","Spirits of peace","How should Katherine's vision be staged?",[["As a full masque with dancers","Spectacle, as the stage direction asks."],["Only through Katherine's reaction","We see her joy, not the spirits."],["With light and music alone","A simple, holy effect."]]],["848","Let me speak, sir","How should Cranmer's prophecy be delivered?",[["As an inspired vision, almost in a trance","Heaven speaks through him."],["As a public speech to the audience","Flattering James I directly."],["Gently, holding the baby","A private blessing that becomes national."]]]],"ev":{"ctx":[["8","845"],["539","619","625"],["266","316","384"],["266","384","390"],["40","268","628"],["455","845","850"],["390","481","850"],["848","850"],["8","637","845"],["845","850"],["637","850"]],"cr":{"fall":[["238","539","619"],["268","539","557"]],"conscience":[["266","316","384"],["384","406","625"]],"pride":[["374","539","625"],["539","557","268"]],"church":[["390","481","850"],["619","625","628"]],"mercy":[["240","545","637"],["240","545","629"]],"truth":[["539","625"],["625","628","629"]]}}};
